{"topics":[],"grammarPoints":[],"lessons":[],"exercises":[],"phrases":{"format":1,"phrases":[{"id":"phrase.a-bit-of-a-worry","text":"a bit of a worry","kind":"collocation","form":"a bit of a worry","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Describes something that causes a slight degree of anxiety or apprehension.","example":"The unexpected delay in the shipment is a bit of a worry for our production schedule.","plain":"The unexpected delay in the shipment is slightly concerning for our production schedule.","note":"A common and understated way to express mild concern.","audio":"audio/0e243bd8a0bf9070.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-common-misconception-is-that","text":"A common misconception is that","kind":"frame","form":"A common misconception is that","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a widely held but incorrect belief that the speaker intends to correct.","example":"A common misconception is that qualitative research lacks scientific rigor.","plain":"Many people wrongly believe that qualitative research lacks scientific rigor.","note":"Useful for clarifying complex topics and correcting errors.","audio":"audio/3f0a3d787b9573a7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-constructive-suggestion","text":"a constructive suggestion","kind":"collocation","form":"a constructive suggestion","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A helpful idea aimed at improving something.","example":"I have a constructive suggestion regarding how we might streamline the reporting process.","plain":"I have a helpful idea regarding how we might streamline the reporting process.","note":"Emphasizes the positive intent of the feedback, focusing on improvement.","audio":"audio/c142d0652a401c17.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-credit-to","text":"a credit to","kind":"chunk","form":"a credit to","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To be someone or something that brings respect or honor to a group or organization.","example":"Your dedication to client satisfaction is a credit to the entire sales team.","plain":"Your dedication to client satisfaction brings respect and honor to the entire sales team.","note":"Formal and strong praise, often used when someone represents a group well.","audio":"audio/fff8976a257aa875.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-critical-factor","text":"A critical factor","kind":"collocation","form":"a critical factor","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To refer to an extremely important element or consideration.","example":"Customer satisfaction is a critical factor in our long-term success.","plain":"Customer satisfaction is an extremely important element in our long-term success.","note":"Highlights an element that is decisive for an outcome.","audio":"audio/894446848a930b4e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-different-approach","text":"a different approach","kind":"collocation","form":"a different approach","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"An alternative method or way of doing something.","example":"For the next phase, we might need to consider a different approach to resource allocation.","plain":"For the next phase, we might need to consider an alternative method to resource allocation.","note":"Suggests an alternative without directly criticizing the current one.","audio":"audio/d138cea55a48fc0e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-feeling-of-being-put-out","text":"a feeling of being put out","kind":"collocation","form":"a feeling of being put out","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Describes mild annoyance or irritation, often because of an inconvenience or slight.","example":"I had a feeling of being put out when they changed the schedule without telling me.","plain":"I felt slightly annoyed when they changed the schedule without telling me.","note":"A common, understated way to express mild irritation or feeling inconvenienced.","audio":"audio/1dca7fc24d99f7d8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-genuine-surprise","text":"a genuine surprise","kind":"collocation","form":"a genuine surprise","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Describes something truly unexpected or astonishing.","example":"Her promotion was a genuine surprise to everyone, as she'd only been there a year.","plain":"Her promotion was truly unexpected to everyone, as she'd only been there a year.","note":"Used to convey authentic surprise, often when something positive or unexpected happens.","audio":"audio/214111085ea39e69.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-good-cultural-addition","text":"a good cultural addition","kind":"collocation","form":"a good cultural addition","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To bring new and positive perspectives or qualities that enhance a company's existing culture.","example":"We're not just looking for a fit, but someone who can be a good cultural addition.","plain":"We're not just looking for a fit, but someone who can bring new positive qualities to our culture.","note":"Modern take on cultural fit, emphasizing diversity of thought.","audio":"audio/377558f228949347.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-good-cultural-fit","text":"a good cultural fit","kind":"collocation","form":"a good cultural fit","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To align well with the values, norms, and environment of a company or team.","example":"We're looking for someone who is not only skilled but also a good cultural fit.","plain":"We're looking for someone who is not only skilled but also aligns with our company culture.","note":"Very common in modern hiring.","audio":"audio/a217bbd35ac0a8d8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-good-starting-point","text":"a good starting point","kind":"collocation","form":"a good starting point","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A suitable initial basis from which to begin a process or discussion.","example":"Your initial proposal is a good starting point, and we can build on it from there.","plain":"Your initial proposal is a suitable initial basis, and we can build on it from there.","note":"Positive framing, acknowledges effort while implying further work is needed.","audio":"audio/ad2e3123f1c408e9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-huge-relief","text":"a huge relief","kind":"collocation","form":"a huge relief","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Describes a feeling of great comfort and reassurance after a period of worry or stress.","example":"Hearing that the flight wasn't cancelled was a huge relief, as I really needed to get home.","plain":"Hearing that the flight wasn't cancelled made me feel much better, as I really needed to get home.","note":"Commonly used to express strong positive feelings after a negative situation is avoided.","audio":"audio/ef758ba4a087d080.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-key-distinction","text":"A key distinction","kind":"collocation","form":"A key distinction","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"An important difference that helps to clarify or separate two concepts or ideas.","example":"A key distinction between the two approaches lies in their methodological frameworks.","plain":"An important difference between the two approaches lies in their methodological frameworks.","note":"Used to highlight a crucial difference for analytical clarity.","audio":"audio/558e774f9b36b4df.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-non-starter","text":"a non-starter","kind":"chunk","form":"a non-starter","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Something that is completely unacceptable or impossible to consider from the outset.","example":"Unfortunately, that particular clause is a non-starter for us; we simply cannot agree to it.","plain":"Unfortunately, that particular clause is completely unacceptable for us; we simply cannot agree to it.","note":"Direct and firm. Used to reject a proposal or term decisively.","audio":"audio/695b1bbb3591f0d3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-notable-divergence","text":"A notable divergence","kind":"collocation","form":"a notable divergence","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"A significant difference or deviation from a common path, trend, or expectation.","example":"We observed a notable divergence in outcomes between the control group and the experimental group.","plain":"We observed a significant difference in outcomes between the control group and the experimental group.","note":"Formal way to describe a significant difference in results or paths.","audio":"audio/ac1f02d7f19634f8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-nuanced-understanding","text":"A nuanced understanding","kind":"collocation","form":"a nuanced understanding","level":"C1","situations":["academic","interviews","feedback"],"meaning":"A deep and subtle comprehension that recognizes complexities and fine distinctions.","example":"Developing a nuanced understanding of the issue requires considering multiple perspectives.","plain":"Developing a deep understanding of the issue requires considering multiple perspectives.","note":"Implies sophistication and thoroughness in analysis.","audio":"audio/930b7354507dfbf0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-potential-pitfall","text":"a potential pitfall","kind":"collocation","form":"a potential pitfall","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A possible hidden danger or difficulty.","example":"We need to be aware of a potential pitfall regarding data privacy in this new system.","plain":"We need to be aware of a possible hidden danger regarding data privacy in this new system.","note":"Highlights a risk in a professional, cautious manner.","audio":"audio/902c4e4f33fcb081.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-sense-of-disappointment","text":"a sense of disappointment","kind":"collocation","form":"a sense of disappointment","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Describes a feeling of sadness or dissatisfaction when expectations are not met.","example":"There was a sense of disappointment when the project deadline was extended again.","plain":"People felt sad when the project deadline was extended again.","note":"A common way to describe a general feeling of dissatisfaction or regret.","audio":"audio/40ae5ffc940d796f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-slight-concern","text":"a slight concern","kind":"collocation","form":"a slight concern","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A minor worry or issue that needs to be addressed.","example":"I have a slight concern about the timeline for the next phase of the project.","plain":"I have a minor worry about the timeline for the next phase of the project.","note":"Softens the impact of raising an issue, implying it's not a major problem yet.","audio":"audio/2baf1044e4546ace.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-sticking-point","text":"a sticking point","kind":"collocation","form":"a sticking point","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","negotiation"],"meaning":"A point of disagreement that prevents progress in a negotiation or discussion.","example":"The payment schedule seems to be a sticking point for both parties.","plain":"The payment schedule seems to be a point of disagreement for both parties.","note":"Highlights a specific issue that is causing difficulty in reaching an agreement.","audio":"audio/898f837183b66d6f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-striking-difference","text":"A striking difference","kind":"collocation","form":"a striking difference","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"A very noticeable or surprising contrast between two things.","example":"There is a striking difference in public perception between the two political parties.","plain":"There is a very noticeable difference in public perception between the two political parties.","note":"Used to emphasize a significant and clear contrast.","audio":"audio/4bca01d1ab0ae852.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-tentative-conclusion","text":"A tentative conclusion","kind":"collocation","form":"a tentative conclusion","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"A preliminary finding or judgment that is not yet final or fully confirmed.","example":"We can draw a tentative conclusion, but further research is needed to validate it.","plain":"We can draw a preliminary conclusion, but further research is needed to validate it.","note":"Emphasizes that the conclusion is provisional and subject to change.","audio":"audio/aabdfa44ec969198.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-win-win-situation","text":"a win-win situation","kind":"collocation","form":"a win-win situation","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation","persuading"],"meaning":"A scenario where all parties involved benefit from the outcome.","example":"Our goal is to create a win-win situation for both companies.","plain":"Our goal is to create a situation where both companies benefit.","note":"Emphasizes a collaborative and mutually beneficial approach to negotiation.","audio":"audio/686a98f919a8b8b6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.absolute-delight","text":"absolute delight","kind":"collocation","form":"absolute delight","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Describes a feeling of extreme happiness and joy about a particular event or outcome.","example":"Winning the award brought her absolute delight after years of hard work.","plain":"Winning the award made her extremely happy after years of hard work.","note":"A strong expression of joy, often used for significant positive events.","audio":"audio/8cf2e5d48a6f9c1c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.according-to-x","text":"According to X","kind":"chunk","form":"According to the latest report","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"States that the information or idea comes from a particular source or person.","example":"According to the latest report, global temperatures continue to rise at an alarming rate.","plain":"The latest report says global temperatures continue to rise at an alarming rate.","note":"A standard, neutral way to introduce cited information.","audio":"audio/522d3be218510666.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.action-item","text":"action item","kind":"collocation","form":"action item","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A specific task assigned to someone to be completed.","example":"Let's list the action items and assign owners before we close.","plain":"Let's list the tasks and assign owners before we close.","note":"Standard business term for a task resulting from a meeting.","audio":"audio/98481899bf47b3c3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.address-a-point","text":"address a point","kind":"collocation","form":"address a point","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To deal with or respond to a specific issue or comment.","example":"Before we move on, I'd like to address a point Sarah raised earlier.","plain":"Before we move on, I want to respond to Sarah's earlier comment.","note":"Common in formal and semi-formal discussions.","audio":"audio/4d018617c4c61208.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.address-an-issue","text":"address an issue","kind":"collocation","form":"address an issue","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To deal with a problem or difficulty directly and purposefully.","example":"We need to address an issue with data security before it escalates into a major incident.","plain":"We need to deal with a problem with data security before it escalates into a major incident.","note":"Implies taking action to resolve a problem. Professional and direct.","audio":"audio/dd3766dc6dc1649b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.adjourn-the-meeting","text":"adjourn the meeting","kind":"collocation","form":"adjourn the meeting","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To formally end a meeting.","example":"I propose we adjourn the meeting and reconvene next week.","plain":"I propose we formally end the meeting and reconvene next week.","note":"Formal way to end a meeting.","audio":"audio/ed5f7e5e575bbe10.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.against-all-odds","text":"Against all odds","kind":"chunk","form":"Against all odds","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Describes achieving something despite facing extreme difficulties or unlikelihood.","example":"Against all odds, they managed to finish the marathon.","plain":"Despite facing many difficulties, they managed to finish the marathon.","note":"Highlights a remarkable achievement in challenging circumstances.","audio":"audio/07d6e5460ea84cf0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.align-our-efforts","text":"Align our efforts","kind":"collocation","form":"align our efforts","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To coordinate actions and work together towards a common goal or strategy.","example":"We need to align our efforts to ensure we're all working towards the same strategic goals.","plain":"We need to coordinate our actions to ensure we're all working towards the same strategic goals.","note":"Used in team or organisational settings to promote efficiency and synergy. Strategic.","audio":"audio/e7d2fe5eafdb6dd4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.allocate-resources","text":"allocate resources","kind":"collocation","form":"allocate resources","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To distribute available assets or personnel for tasks.","example":"We need to carefully allocate resources for the upcoming project phases.","plain":"We need to carefully distribute assets for the upcoming project phases.","note":"Standard business term for managing assets.","audio":"audio/16b3f0b8fc743bd1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.allocate-time-for","text":"allocate time for","kind":"collocation","form":"allocate time for","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To assign a specific period for discussing a particular item.","example":"We should allocate time for a Q&A session at the end of the presentation.","plain":"We should assign a specific period for a Q&A session.","note":"Used when planning or managing the meeting schedule.","audio":"audio/dffe1ed6663d0476.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.allow-me-to-clarify","text":"allow me to clarify","kind":"frame","form":"Allow me to clarify","level":"C1","situations":["explaining","calls"],"meaning":"Please let me explain something more clearly or provide additional detail.","example":"I think there's a misunderstanding, so allow me to clarify my previous statement.","plain":"I believe there's a misunderstanding, so let me explain my last point better.","note":"Used when you perceive confusion or want to ensure your message is understood precisely.","audio":"audio/1373c3581bd58c20.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.allow-me-to-rephrase","text":"Allow me to rephrase","kind":"frame","form":"Allow me to rephrase","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To politely ask permission to say something again in different words.","example":"Allow me to rephrase: our primary goal is to enhance customer satisfaction.","plain":"Let me say that again in different words: our primary goal is to enhance customer satisfaction.","note":"A slightly more formal version of \"let me rephrase that.\"","audio":"audio/c0d0ae676b5aaa7b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.anything-exciting-happening","text":"anything exciting happening","kind":"chunk","form":"Anything exciting happening","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A casual way to ask if there's any interesting news or events in someone's life.","example":"Hey, long time no see! Anything exciting happening with you lately?","plain":"Hey, long time no see! Is there any interesting news with you lately?","note":"An informal way to invite someone to share recent positive news.","audio":"audio/043184588eefcf82.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.anyway-getting-back-to","text":"Anyway, getting back to","kind":"frame","form":"Anyway, getting back to","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Redirecting the conversation to a previous topic after a digression.","example":"Anyway, getting back to our plans for the weekend, what did you decide?","plain":"So, returning to our plans for the weekend, what did you decide?","note":"Used to steer the conversation back to the main point after a tangent.","audio":"audio/43e3f98e041be3a2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.apologies-i-misspoke","text":"Apologies, I misspoke","kind":"chunk","form":"Apologies, I misspoke","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To apologise for saying something incorrectly or unintentionally.","example":"Apologies, I misspoke; the figures are from last quarter, not this one.","plain":"Sorry, I said that incorrectly; the figures are from last quarter, not this one.","note":"A polite and concise way to acknowledge a verbal error.","audio":"audio/5c0dba35165ef74e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.are-we-assuming-that","text":"Are we assuming that","kind":"frame","form":"Are we assuming that","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Questions a foundational belief or premise of an argument, inviting re-evaluation.","example":"Are we assuming that all team members have the necessary skills for this new task?","plain":"Do we assume that all team members have the necessary skills for this new task?","note":"Challenges an unspoken assumption, opening the door for a different conclusion.","audio":"audio/314b790cbf645428.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.are-we-on-the-same-page","text":"Are we on the same page","kind":"chunk","form":"are we on the same page","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To confirm that everyone involved has the same understanding of a situation or plan.","example":"Before we proceed, let's just check: are we on the same page regarding the project timeline?","plain":"Before we proceed, let's just check: do we all understand the project timeline in the same way?","note":"Very common in professional settings to ensure alignment.","audio":"audio/4dc8fbad0731cde5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.are-you-following-me","text":"Are you following me","kind":"chunk","form":"are you following me","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To check if the listener understands and is keeping up with the explanation.","example":"The new policy changes are quite complex, so please tell me, are you following me?","plain":"The new policy changes are quite complex, so please tell me if you understand.","note":"Common, direct way to check comprehension during a detailed explanation.","audio":"audio/5dd93a631ee83f79.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.arrange-transport","text":"arrange transport","kind":"collocation","form":"arrange transport","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To make plans for how to travel from one place to another.","example":"The hotel can arrange transport to the airport for guests.","plain":"The hotel can organize how guests will travel to the airport.","note":"Common phrase for organizing travel logistics.","audio":"audio/7d0c8e8df73a9e75.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.as-a-gesture-of-goodwill","text":"as a gesture of goodwill","kind":"chunk","form":"as a gesture of goodwill","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Indicates an action taken to show friendliness or a desire for a positive relationship.","example":"As a gesture of goodwill, we're willing to absorb some of the initial setup costs.","plain":"We're willing to absorb some of the initial setup costs to show our good intentions.","note":"Often used when making a small concession to build trust or improve relations.","audio":"audio/3421e7d29f4da7c9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.as-a-rule-of-thumb","text":"as a rule of thumb","kind":"chunk","form":"as a rule of thumb","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to introduce a practical, approximate principle or guideline, not a strict rule.","example":"As a rule of thumb, always double-check your work before submitting it to the client.","plain":"Always double-check your work before submitting it to the client.","note":"Implies practical advice based on experience, not a formal regulation.","audio":"audio/3db585c960ce08ab.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.as-a-starting-point","text":"As a starting point","kind":"chunk","form":"As a starting point","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces the initial idea or basis for a discussion, analysis, or plan.","example":"As a starting point, we need to define the scope of the research project clearly.","plain":"First, we need to define the scope of the research project clearly.","note":"Used to establish the initial step or premise.","audio":"audio/26e273d17d15d0d1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.as-far-as-i-know","text":"as far as I know","kind":"chunk","form":"as far as I know","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to indicate that the information being given is based on the speaker's current knowledge, which may be incomplete.","example":"As far as I know, the meeting is still scheduled for Tuesday morning.","plain":"The meeting is still scheduled for Tuesday morning.","note":"A common way to qualify information, showing awareness of potential gaps in knowledge.","audio":"audio/3fc98871ccfa77fe.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.as-i-was-saying","text":"as I was saying","kind":"chunk","form":"As I was saying","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","presenting","calls"],"meaning":"To resume a point or explanation after an interruption or brief pause.","example":"As I was saying before the connection dropped, the project deadline is next Friday.","plain":"Before the call disconnected, I was explaining that the project deadline is next Friday.","note":"Used to smoothly transition back to your original point after an interruption.","audio":"audio/66777908174bf486.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.as-it-happens","text":"as it happens","kind":"chunk","form":"as it happens","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to introduce a piece of information that is relevant or surprising in the current context.","example":"I was just thinking about that. As it happens, I have some news regarding the budget.","plain":"I was just thinking about that. I have some news regarding the budget.","note":"Often used to reveal a coincidence or a piece of timely information.","audio":"audio/2b0a5710138a7cd5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.as-luck-would-have-it","text":"As luck would have it","kind":"chunk","form":"As luck would have it","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Introduces a fortunate or unfortunate coincidence or turn of events.","example":"I forgot my umbrella, and as luck would have it, it started raining.","plain":"I forgot my umbrella, and coincidentally, it started raining.","note":"Can be used for both good and bad luck, often with a touch of irony.","audio":"audio/8a218379ed5e21cb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.as-x-points-out","text":"As X points out","kind":"frame","form":"As Professor Davies points out","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a statement or idea by attributing it to a specific person or source.","example":"As Professor Davies points out, the historical context is crucial for understanding this phenomenon.","plain":"Professor Davies says the historical context is crucial for understanding this phenomenon.","note":"A common way to cite an authority or reference a previous statement.","audio":"audio/bd079ba78fcff5e2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.assess-the-impact","text":"assess the impact","kind":"collocation","form":"assess the impact","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To evaluate the effect or influence of something, often a change or decision.","example":"Before proceeding, we need to assess the impact of these proposed changes on our existing infrastructure.","plain":"Before proceeding, we need to evaluate the effect of these proposed changes on our existing infrastructure.","note":"A critical step in decision-making and risk management. Formal and analytical.","audio":"audio/ba2d51e512d6bd33.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.at-the-heart-of-the-matter","text":"At the heart of the matter","kind":"chunk","form":"At the heart of the matter","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To refer to the most crucial or central aspect of a complex situation or problem.","example":"At the heart of the matter is our need to adapt to changing market demands quickly.","plain":"The most important thing is our need to adapt to changing market demands quickly.","note":"Similar to 'the core of the issue', often used when simplifying a complex situation. Formal.","audio":"audio/005c7bf947d945e5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.at-this-juncture","text":"at this juncture","kind":"chunk","form":"at this juncture","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"At this particular point in time or stage of the discussion.","example":"At this juncture, we're not prepared to make any further concessions.","plain":"At this point, we're not prepared to make any further concessions.","note":"Slightly formal, used to emphasize the current stage of a negotiation.","audio":"audio/33be9d9503c0d8e1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.be-up-against","text":"be up against","kind":"collocation","form":"are up against","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To be faced with a difficult situation or opponent.","example":"We are up against a very tight deadline for this project, so efficiency is key.","plain":"We are faced with a very tight deadline for this project, so efficiency is key.","note":"Implies facing a challenge or competition.","audio":"audio/f48976973ec80680.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.bear-with-me","text":"bear with me","kind":"chunk","form":"bear with me","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To ask someone to be patient while you do something.","example":"Please bear with me for a moment while I pull up the relevant data.","plain":"Please be patient for a moment while I pull up the relevant data.","note":"Polite request for patience, often when there's a slight delay.","audio":"audio/47b8c6703391b72c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.before-i-conclude","text":"Before I conclude","kind":"frame","form":"Before I conclude","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To introduce a final point or thought before ending the presentation.","example":"Before I conclude, I want to thank everyone for their hard work.","plain":"Before ending, I want to thank everyone for their hard work.","note":"Signals that the presentation is nearing its end.","audio":"audio/ba932a70558f0c71.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.before-we-close","text":"Before we close","kind":"frame","form":"Before we close","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To introduce final remarks or actions before ending the meeting.","example":"Before we close, let's confirm who is responsible for each action item.","plain":"Before we end, let's confirm who is responsible for each action item.","note":"A standard phrase for the final stage of a meeting.","audio":"audio/02d0b1394a524211.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.before-we-conclude","text":"Before we conclude","kind":"chunk","form":"Before we conclude","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"Before ending the meeting.","example":"Before we conclude, are there any final questions or points?","plain":"Before we end the meeting, are there any final questions or points?","note":"Standard phrase for inviting last-minute input.","audio":"audio/ed36ec563a505eff.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.benefit-everyone-involved","text":"Benefit everyone involved","kind":"collocation","form":"benefit everyone involved","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To state that a proposed action or outcome will have positive results for all parties.","example":"This new initiative is designed to benefit everyone involved, from staff to clients.","plain":"This new initiative is designed to help all parties, from staff to clients.","note":"A persuasive phrase to show inclusivity and positive outcomes for all stakeholders. Positive.","audio":"audio/3dff3ce1c2b2f6dc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.bite-the-bullet","text":"bite the bullet","kind":"chunk","form":"bite the bullet","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To face a difficult or unpleasant situation with courage.","example":"We might just have to bite the bullet and invest in the new, expensive software.","plain":"We might just have to face the difficult situation and invest in the new, expensive software.","note":"Informal, implies accepting an unavoidable hardship.","audio":"audio/bc8f2d30cab28ff6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.break-the-ice","text":"break the ice","kind":"collocation","form":"break the ice","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"To initiate conversation in a new or awkward social situation, making people feel more comfortable.","example":"It's always a bit tricky to break the ice at these networking events, isn't it?","plain":"It's always a bit tricky to start conversations at these networking events.","note":"Commonly used when meeting new people; implies a slight initial awkwardness.","audio":"audio/b337bee4db87a99f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.bring-us-back-to","text":"bring us back to","kind":"frame","form":"bring us back to","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To redirect the discussion to the main topic.","example":"If I could just bring us back to the budget discussion for a moment.","plain":"If I could just redirect us to the budget discussion for a moment.","note":"Polite way to steer a conversation back to the agenda.","audio":"audio/d5f44b4ae58f15e6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.building-on-the-idea","text":"Building on the idea","kind":"collocation","form":"Building on the idea","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Continues a discussion by developing or expanding upon a previously mentioned concept.","example":"Building on the idea of collaborative learning, we designed a new peer-mentoring program.","plain":"Expanding on the idea of collaborative learning, we designed a new peer-mentoring program.","note":"Shows progression of thought and connection to prior points.","audio":"audio/3be87294ec9ca211.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.by-the-same-token","text":"By the same token","kind":"chunk","form":"by the same token","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation","academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a related point that is equally true or valid, often reinforcing a previous argument.","example":"The study highlights the benefits of exercise; by the same token, it underscores the risks of inactivity.","plain":"The study highlights the benefits of exercise; similarly, it underscores the risks of inactivity.","note":"Used to add a parallel or supporting argument.","audio":"audio/4e0d680ca81a6313.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.by-the-way","text":"by the way","kind":"chunk","form":"By the way","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to introduce a new topic or a piece of information that is not directly related to the current one.","example":"I'm heading to the coffee machine. By the way, did you finish that report?","plain":"I'm heading to the coffee machine. Did you finish that report?","note":"A very common and informal way to change the subject or add an afterthought.","audio":"audio/9c9d282c44419c2d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.by-way-of-background","text":"By way of background","kind":"chunk","form":"By way of background","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To provide introductory information necessary for understanding the main topic.","example":"By way of background, our company was founded on principles of sustainability.","plain":"As an introduction, our company was founded on principles of sustainability.","note":"Sets the context for the discussion that follows.","audio":"audio/5e1c249947983c2e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.call-into-question","text":"call into question","kind":"collocation","form":"call into question","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To cause doubts about something.","example":"These new findings call into question the validity of our initial assumptions.","plain":"These new findings cause doubts about the validity of our initial assumptions.","note":"Used when new information challenges existing beliefs.","audio":"audio/9462d7007ed476c7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.can-you-see-how-that-works","text":"Can you see how that works","kind":"frame","form":"Can you see how that works","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To check if the listener understands the mechanism or process being described.","example":"The new software integrates with the old system. Can you see how that works?","plain":"The new software integrates with the old system. Do you understand the process?","note":"Useful when explaining a system, process, or interaction.","audio":"audio/c2d38188af9b1565.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.cant-complain-really","text":"can't complain really","kind":"chunk","form":"Can't complain really","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A slightly more emphatic version of 'can't complain', indicating things are genuinely good.","example":"How's business? Can't complain really, things are picking up.","plain":"How's business? Things are good, things are picking up.","note":"A common, positive, and understated response, often used in British English.","audio":"audio/6178ed52781f1cce.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.catch-up-with","text":"catch up with","kind":"collocation","form":"catch up with","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"To meet someone you know after a period of time and talk about what has happened.","example":"It was great to catch up with you at the conference; we should do it again soon.","plain":"It was great to talk to you at the conference; we should do it again soon.","note":"Implies a desire to reconnect and share recent life updates.","audio":"audio/ae514ae5d865d4bd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.circle-back-to","text":"circle back to","kind":"chunk","form":"circle back to","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To return to a previous topic later in the discussion.","example":"That's a good point, but let's circle back to it after we've covered the main items.","plain":"That's a good point, but let's return to it after we've covered the main items.","note":"Useful for managing tangents and keeping discussions on track.","audio":"audio/97ecf2606a512dbf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.clear-customs","text":"clear customs","kind":"collocation","form":"clear customs","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To successfully pass through the inspection of goods and luggage at a border.","example":"It took us over an hour to clear customs at the busy airport.","plain":"It took us over an hour to get through the border inspection at the busy airport.","note":"Refers to the process of passing through border control.","audio":"audio/f0b9c9224cc6e3da.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.clear-up-any-confusion","text":"clear up any confusion","kind":"collocation","form":"clear up any confusion","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To explain something to remove misunderstandings.","example":"I'd like to clear up any confusion regarding the new policy changes.","plain":"I'd like to explain the new policy changes to remove any misunderstandings.","note":"Polite and direct way to address misunderstandings.","audio":"audio/53f0e3fea192f9a6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.come-across-as","text":"come across as","kind":"collocation","form":"come across as","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To give a particular impression or seem to have a certain quality.","example":"Sometimes your emails can come across as a little abrupt, which might be misunderstood.","plain":"Sometimes your emails seem a little abrupt, which might be misunderstood.","note":"Useful for giving feedback on communication style or perception. Neutral tone.","audio":"audio/2fb0d5f64790f34e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.come-back-to","text":"come back to","kind":"chunk","form":"come back to","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To return to a topic or issue later in the discussion.","example":"That's an interesting thought, but let's come back to it after we've covered the main agenda.","plain":"That's interesting, but let's discuss it later after the main agenda.","note":"Similar to \"circle back to\" but perhaps slightly less formal.","audio":"audio/3901f75d2a28ce7f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.common-ground","text":"common ground","kind":"collocation","form":"common ground","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"Areas of agreement or shared interests between different parties.","example":"Despite our differences, I believe we can find common ground on this issue.","plain":"Despite our differences, I believe we can find areas of agreement on this issue.","note":"Useful in negotiations or conflict resolution to identify points of consensus.","audio":"audio/7c2413eb2bf8aafb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.common-objective","text":"Common objective","kind":"collocation","form":"common objective","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To refer to a goal or aim that is shared by all members of a group or all parties involved.","example":"Despite our different roles, our common objective is to deliver the project on time.","plain":"Despite our different roles, our shared goal is to deliver the project on time.","note":"Used to unite people around a single purpose. Professional and unifying.","audio":"audio/a85c7325120434a8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.consensus-decision","text":"consensus decision","kind":"collocation","form":"consensus decision","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A decision reached by general agreement among all members.","example":"We aim for a consensus decision on all major strategic points.","plain":"We aim for a decision reached by general agreement on all major strategic points.","note":"Emphasizes agreement rather than simple majority.","audio":"audio/267bca237dc3c125.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.consistent-with-findings","text":"Consistent with findings","kind":"collocation","form":"consistent with findings","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Describes results or observations that match or support previously established research.","example":"The experimental data is consistent with findings from similar studies conducted abroad.","plain":"The experimental data matches findings from similar studies conducted abroad.","note":"Used to show that new research supports existing knowledge.","audio":"audio/000ba03ca8a89df3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.contrary-to-popular-belief","text":"Contrary to popular belief","kind":"chunk","form":"Contrary to popular belief","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Introduces a fact or idea that goes against what most people think.","example":"Contrary to popular belief, multitasking often reduces overall productivity.","plain":"Most people believe multitasking helps, but it often reduces overall productivity.","note":"Used to challenge a widely held but incorrect assumption.","audio":"audio/3a353db221dac1c8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.conversely-it-could-be-argued","text":"Conversely, it could be argued","kind":"frame","form":"Conversely, it could be argued","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces an opposing argument or a different perspective on the same issue.","example":"Conversely, it could be argued that strict regulations stifle innovation in emerging markets.","plain":"On the other hand, one could argue that strict regulations stifle innovation in emerging markets.","note":"A formal way to present a counter-argument.","audio":"audio/9361caa82cbdf299.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.correct-me-if-im-wrong","text":"Correct me if I'm wrong","kind":"frame","form":"Correct me if I'm wrong","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","small-talk"],"meaning":"To invite correction if one's understanding or statement is inaccurate.","example":"Correct me if I'm wrong, but didn't we agree on a different approach last week?","plain":"Didn't we agree on a different approach last week? Please tell me if I'm wrong.","note":"A humble way to challenge or question something, inviting input.","audio":"audio/12e5b7f146b3ffc6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-be-argued","text":"Could be argued","kind":"collocation","form":"could be argued","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a possible interpretation or viewpoint, inviting discussion rather than stating a fact.","example":"It could be argued that the policy had unintended consequences for the local economy.","plain":"One might say the policy had unintended consequences for the local economy.","note":"Often used in academic debate or when presenting a controversial idea.","audio":"audio/b52de009891fdbab.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-i-get-a-late-checkout","text":"Could I get a late checkout","kind":"chunk","form":"Could I get a late checkout","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Requesting permission to leave your hotel room later than the standard time.","example":"My flight is in the evening, so could I get a late checkout, please?","plain":"My flight is in the evening, so can I leave my room later than usual?","note":"A common and polite request at hotels, often subject to availability.","audio":"audio/e46b7cb96bc95fdf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-it-be-that","text":"Could it be that","kind":"frame","form":"Could it be that","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Suggests an alternative explanation or possibility, often to challenge an assumption politely.","example":"Could it be that we're overlooking a simpler solution to this complex problem?","plain":"Perhaps there's a simpler solution to this complex problem that we're missing.","note":"Poses a question to open up new avenues of thought, rather than directly refuting.","audio":"audio/08fc81f9c8fd4731.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-we-explore-the-idea","text":"Could we explore the idea","kind":"frame","form":"Could we explore the idea","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Suggests investigating a new concept or possibility, inviting collaborative consideration.","example":"Could we explore the idea of remote work options for some of the team members?","plain":"Let's explore the idea of remote work options for some of the team members.","note":"A collaborative and open-ended way to introduce a new concept for discussion.","audio":"audio/3f3cd4c1aead5892.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-we-find-a-solution","text":"Could we find a solution","kind":"chunk","form":"Could we find a solution","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Asking to work together to resolve a problem.","example":"This flight delay is causing problems; could we find a solution together?","plain":"This flight delay is causing problems; can we solve this together?","note":"Collaborative and constructive, used when seeking a resolution.","audio":"audio/e862b1a1758b97b3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-we-perhaps","text":"Could we perhaps","kind":"frame","form":"Could we perhaps","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To make a very polite and tentative suggestion.","example":"Could we perhaps consider a phased implementation instead of a full rollout?","plain":"Should we consider a phased implementation instead of a full rollout?","note":"A very soft and diplomatic way to suggest an alternative.","audio":"audio/ebd82967d9a0d952.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-you-clarify","text":"Could you clarify","kind":"frame","form":"Could you clarify","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","travel"],"meaning":"To ask for a clearer explanation or more specific information.","example":"Could you clarify what you mean by 'optimizing workflows' in this context?","plain":"Please explain what you mean by 'optimizing workflows' in this context.","note":"A direct and polite request for more detail or precision.","audio":"audio/7893a95fd5c2b371.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-you-clarify-what-you-mean","text":"Could you clarify what you mean","kind":"chunk","form":"Could you clarify what you mean","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Politely asking for a more precise explanation of a statement.","example":"I'm a bit confused; could you clarify what you mean by \"streamlining operations\"?","plain":"I'm confused; can you explain what you mean by \"streamlining operations\"?","note":"A common and polite request for a clearer explanation.","audio":"audio/4cc7905849013069.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-you-direct-me-to","text":"Could you direct me to","kind":"frame","form":"Could you direct me to","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to ask for instructions on how to get to a specific place.","example":"Could you direct me to the nearest subway station from here, please?","plain":"Please tell me how to get to the nearest subway station.","note":"Standard polite request for directions.","audio":"audio/33312159c6b4b668.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-you-elaborate","text":"could you elaborate","kind":"chunk","form":"Could you elaborate","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","calls"],"meaning":"Please provide more details or further explanation on a particular point.","example":"That's an interesting idea; could you elaborate on how it would work?","plain":"That's interesting; can you give more details on how it would function?","note":"A polite and professional request for more information or a deeper explanation.","audio":"audio/615a820d40bbcc72.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-you-expand-on-that","text":"Could you expand on that","kind":"chunk","form":"Could you expand on that","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Politely asking for more details or information about a topic.","example":"That's an interesting idea; could you expand on that a little more?","plain":"That's interesting; could you give more details about it?","note":"A polite way to invite someone to elaborate.","audio":"audio/4245403deefaef62.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-you-explain-that-again","text":"Could you explain that again","kind":"chunk","form":"Could you explain that again","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Politely asking for a repetition of an explanation.","example":"I apologize, but I missed that; could you explain that again?","plain":"Sorry, I missed that; can you explain it once more?","note":"A direct and polite request for re-explanation.","audio":"audio/8cc8a6b11e5dddab.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-you-give-an-example","text":"Could you give an example","kind":"frame","form":"could you give an example","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To ask for a specific instance to clarify a general statement.","example":"That's an interesting theory; could you give an example of how it applies?","plain":"That's an interesting theory; could you provide a specific instance of how it applies?","note":"Helps to make abstract ideas more concrete.","audio":"audio/edfa05fe884abba9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-you-give-me-a-hand","text":"Could you give me a hand","kind":"chunk","form":"Could you give me a hand","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite and informal way to ask for assistance with a task.","example":"Could you give me a hand with these suitcases? They're quite heavy.","plain":"Please help me with these heavy suitcases.","note":"Common informal request for physical help.","audio":"audio/45b0bb1fe484e767.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-you-let-me-know","text":"Could you let me know","kind":"frame","form":"Could you let me know","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to ask for information or confirmation.","example":"Could you let me know if the flight is still on schedule for this evening?","plain":"Please tell me if the flight is still on schedule.","note":"Very common polite request. Use when asking for information you expect someone to have.","audio":"audio/02c954ee6869d569.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-you-look-into-this-for-me","text":"Could you look into this for me","kind":"chunk","form":"Could you look into this for me","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Asking someone to investigate a problem or situation.","example":"My flight was cancelled; could you look into this for me, please?","plain":"My flight was cancelled; could you investigate this for me, please?","note":"A polite request for assistance in resolving an issue.","audio":"audio/058d2a4ae5887582.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-you-make-it","text":"Could you make it","kind":"frame","form":"could you make it","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Asking if someone is able to attend an event or meet at a specific time.","example":"The party starts at seven; could you make it then?","plain":"The party starts at seven; can you be there then?","note":"Common for checking attendance or punctuality. Polite and direct.","audio":"audio/70d3da199e2d4565.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-you-point-me-in-the-right-direction","text":"Could you point me in the right direction","kind":"chunk","form":"Could you point me in the right direction","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Asking for help to find a specific place or way.","example":"Excuse me, could you point me in the right direction for the train station?","plain":"Excuse me, could you tell me how to get to the train station?","note":"A polite way to ask for directions from a stranger.","audio":"audio/19d89fb6f1fd5914.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-you-possibly","text":"Could you possibly","kind":"frame","form":"Could you possibly","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A very polite and tentative way to make a request, suggesting it might be difficult.","example":"Could you possibly tell me where the nearest currency exchange office is located?","plain":"Please tell me where the nearest currency exchange is.","note":"Adds an extra layer of politeness, implying you understand it might be an inconvenience.","audio":"audio/192e98463d18d947.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-you-possibly-tell-me","text":"Could you possibly tell me","kind":"frame","form":"Could you possibly tell me","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A very polite and tentative way to ask for information.","example":"Could you possibly tell me if there's a luggage storage facility at this station?","plain":"Please tell me if there's luggage storage here.","note":"Adds extra politeness, similar to \"I was wondering if\".","audio":"audio/2a0a244e8a10e651.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-you-run-that-by-me-again","text":"Could you run that by me again","kind":"chunk","form":"Could you run that by me again","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Politely asking for something to be repeated or re-explained.","example":"I was distracted for a second; could you run that by me again?","plain":"I was distracted; can you repeat that for me?","note":"A common and informal way to ask for repetition or clarification.","audio":"audio/c9d3e59eea4d1e1b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-you-spell-that-out","text":"could you spell that out","kind":"chunk","form":"Could you spell that out","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Please explain that in very clear, simple terms, or literally spell a word.","example":"I'm not familiar with that acronym; could you spell that out for me?","plain":"I don't know that acronym; can you explain it clearly?","note":"Can mean to explain simply or to literally spell a word. Context clarifies.","audio":"audio/cfb53a5214383b99.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-you-walk-me-through","text":"Could you walk me through","kind":"frame","form":"Could you walk me through","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Asking someone to explain a process or concept step-by-step.","example":"I'm new to this system; could you walk me through the setup process?","plain":"I'm new to this system; can you explain the setup process step-by-step?","note":"A polite request for a detailed, guided explanation.","audio":"audio/42653dd46600b1f3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.cover-the-main-points","text":"cover the main points","kind":"collocation","form":"cover the main points","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To discuss the most important aspects of a topic.","example":"Before we go into detail, let's quickly cover the main points of the project update.","plain":"Let's quickly discuss the most important aspects of the project update.","note":"Useful for structuring a discussion or presentation.","audio":"audio/bdadd9660db91784.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.cut-corners","text":"cut corners","kind":"chunk","form":"cut corners","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To do something in the easiest, quickest, or cheapest way, often sacrificing quality.","example":"We can't afford to cut corners on quality control, even with tight deadlines.","plain":"We can't afford to do things in the cheapest way on quality control, even with tight deadlines.","note":"Implies a negative consequence of taking shortcuts.","audio":"audio/5c4d7cdf5ffbe6e5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.cut-to-the-chase","text":"cut to the chase","kind":"chunk","form":"cut to the chase","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To get straight to the main point without unnecessary details.","example":"Let's cut to the chase; what's the bottom line on this budget proposal?","plain":"Let's get to the main point; what's the bottom line on this budget proposal?","note":"Informal but common, used when time is short or patience is thin.","audio":"audio/7adb624092072220.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.delegate-tasks","text":"delegate tasks","kind":"collocation","form":"delegate tasks","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To assign specific duties or responsibilities to other people.","example":"To manage the workload effectively, I need to delegate tasks to other team members.","plain":"To manage the workload effectively, I need to assign specific duties to other team members.","note":"A core management skill. Implies trust and efficient distribution of work.","audio":"audio/f5acccc1cd4b0e13.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.devise-a-plan","text":"devise a plan","kind":"collocation","form":"devise a plan","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To invent or create a detailed scheme or method for doing something.","example":"We need to devise a plan to address the unexpected budget shortfall by the end of the week.","plain":"We need to create a detailed scheme to address the unexpected budget shortfall by the end of the week.","note":"Implies careful thought and creativity in planning. Formal and professional.","audio":"audio/3fc7173a64491ea1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.dive-deeper-into","text":"dive deeper into","kind":"chunk","form":"dive deeper into","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To explore a topic in more detail.","example":"Now that we've covered the basics, let's dive deeper into the specific challenges we face.","plain":"Now we've covered the basics, let's explore the specific challenges in more detail.","note":"Suggests a thorough exploration; often used when transitioning to specifics.","audio":"audio/e79d9b3864d298d0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.do-you-get-the-picture","text":"Do you get the picture","kind":"chunk","form":"Do you get the picture","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To ask if the listener fully understands the situation or the overall context.","example":"We're short on staff, and the deadline is tomorrow. Do you get the picture?","plain":"We're short on staff, and the deadline is tomorrow. Do you understand the situation?","note":"Slightly informal, implies understanding the full implications of a situation.","audio":"audio/e78707c3e98ba24c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.do-you-happen-to-have","text":"Do you happen to have","kind":"frame","form":"Do you happen to have","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite and indirect way to ask if someone possesses something.","example":"Do you happen to have a spare adapter I could borrow for my phone charger?","plain":"Do you have a spare adapter?","note":"Use when you're not sure if the person will have what you're asking for.","audio":"audio/10331455d31f195f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.do-you-happen-to-know","text":"do you happen to know","kind":"frame","form":"Do you happen to know","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to ask if someone has specific information, implying they might not.","example":"Do you happen to know if the coffee machine is working today?","plain":"Is the coffee machine working today?","note":"Softens a direct question, making it less demanding and more conversational.","audio":"audio/cbb0fb170445bbfe.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.do-you-have-any-availability-for","text":"Do you have any availability for","kind":"frame","form":"Do you have any availability for","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Asking if there are open slots or rooms for a specific time or date.","example":"Do you have any availability for a single room next Tuesday night?","plain":"Is a single room available next Tuesday night?","note":"Common when trying to book a hotel, flight, or appointment.","audio":"audio/5728a4d5038f3171.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.do-you-have-any-questions","text":"do you have any questions","kind":"chunk","form":"Do you have any questions","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I'm asking if you need clarification or further information on what has been discussed.","example":"I've explained the new policy; do you have any questions about it?","plain":"I've explained the new policy; do you need more information?","note":"A standard closing question after presenting information, inviting engagement.","audio":"audio/66414455b56d44c9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.do-you-see-what-i-mean","text":"Do you see what I mean","kind":"chunk","form":"Do you see what I mean","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To ask if the listener understands the point or implication being made.","example":"The budget is tight, so we need to prioritize. Do you see what I mean?","plain":"The budget is tight, so we need to prioritize. Do you understand my point?","note":"A common, slightly informal way to check for agreement or understanding of a subtle point.","audio":"audio/5a92aa822e5c92f0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.does-that-make-sense-to-you","text":"Does that make sense to you","kind":"chunk","form":"Does that make sense to you","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To confirm if the explanation is logical and clear from the listener's perspective.","example":"We'll start with the basics, then move to advanced topics. Does that make sense to you?","plain":"We'll start with the basics, then move to advanced topics. Is that clear and logical for you?","note":"A polite and personal way to check understanding, inviting feedback.","audio":"audio/fa57391d0d269c45.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.does-that-resonate-with-you","text":"Does that resonate with you","kind":"chunk","form":"Does that resonate with you","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To ask if an idea or statement feels right or relevant to the listener's experience or beliefs.","example":"The idea of flexible working hours seems popular. Does that resonate with you and your team?","plain":"The idea of flexible working hours seems popular. Does that feel right or relevant to you and your team?","note":"More about emotional or experiential understanding than factual comprehension.","audio":"audio/dc33ad35e16fb6cc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.does-that-work-for-you","text":"Does that work for you","kind":"chunk","form":"does that work for you","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Asking if a proposed time, date, or plan is convenient for the other person.","example":"I was thinking of Tuesday evening; does that work for you?","plain":"I was thinking of Tuesday evening; is that convenient for you?","note":"Standard phrase for confirming arrangements. Very versatile and polite.","audio":"audio/67d06c2eea763a03.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.dont-get-me-wrong","text":"don't get me wrong","kind":"chunk","form":"Don't get me wrong","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to clarify a previous statement, ensuring it's not misunderstood or taken negatively.","example":"I like the new office, don't get me wrong, but the commute is a nightmare.","plain":"I like the new office, but the commute is a nightmare.","note":"Helps to balance a negative comment with a positive one, or prevent misinterpretation.","audio":"audio/d6a25e5f5af0adaf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.dont-worry-about-a-thing","text":"Don't worry about a thing","kind":"chunk","form":"Don't worry about a thing","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Reassuring someone, especially a guest, that they don't need to be concerned or help.","example":"\"Can I help with the dishes?\" \"No, don't worry about a thing, I'll do them later.\"","plain":"\"Can I help with the dishes?\" \"No, don't be concerned, I'll do them later.\"","note":"A kind and hospitable phrase to put guests at ease.","audio":"audio/58d72f3b30ff5090.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.draw-a-line","text":"draw a line","kind":"collocation","form":"draw a line","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To set a firm limit or boundary beyond which one is unwilling to go or compromise.","example":"We have to draw a line on the budget; we can't exceed this amount.","plain":"We have to set a firm limit on the budget; we can't exceed this amount.","note":"Signals a non-negotiable point, often used to hold firm on a position.","audio":"audio/aff0878aa67866eb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.draw-a-parallel","text":"Draw a parallel","kind":"collocation","form":"draw a parallel","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To point out a similarity or connection between two different things or situations.","example":"We can draw a parallel between the historical development of these two regions.","plain":"We can show a similarity between the historical development of these two regions.","note":"Used to make comparisons and illustrate relationships.","audio":"audio/cb43b88850432966.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.drawing-on-xs-work","text":"Drawing on X's work","kind":"chunk","form":"Drawing on Dr. Lee's work","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Indicates that one's own ideas or analysis are informed by or based on another's research.","example":"Drawing on Dr. Lee's work, we developed a new framework for analyzing social networks.","plain":"Using Dr. Lee's work, we developed a new framework for analyzing social networks.","note":"A formal way to acknowledge intellectual debt and build upon existing scholarship.","audio":"audio/d88e1b8db05b4840.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.drill-down-into","text":"drill down into","kind":"collocation","form":"drill down into","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To examine something in more detail.","example":"We need to drill down into the sales figures to understand the dip.","plain":"We need to examine the sales figures in more detail to understand the dip.","note":"Implies a thorough, in-depth investigation.","audio":"audio/31b0940dd9be8270.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.echoes-the-sentiment","text":"Echoes the sentiment","kind":"collocation","form":"echoed the sentiment","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Expresses a similar feeling or opinion to one that has already been stated.","example":"Her concluding remarks echoed the sentiment of many participants regarding the need for reform.","plain":"Her concluding remarks expressed a similar feeling to many participants regarding the need for reform.","note":"Suggests a shared feeling or opinion, often in a formal context.","audio":"audio/5085fe70f3fd6847.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.emphasize-the-importance","text":"emphasize the importance","kind":"collocation","form":"emphasize the importance","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To stress the significance or value of something.","example":"I want to emphasize the importance of timely communication in this project.","plain":"I want to stress the significance of timely communication in this project.","note":"Used to make sure a particular point is understood as crucial.","audio":"audio/55e270e2ad84043a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.experience-delays","text":"experience delays","kind":"collocation","form":"experience delays","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To encounter unexpected postponements or slowdowns in travel.","example":"Unfortunately, we often experience delays when flying during peak season.","plain":"Unfortunately, our flights are often postponed when flying during peak season.","note":"Common phrase for travel disruptions.","audio":"audio/9d753b0b9fe9f92e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.face-the-music","text":"face the music","kind":"chunk","form":"face the music","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To accept the unpleasant consequences of one's actions.","example":"We made a mistake, and now we have to face the music with the client.","plain":"We made a mistake, and now we have to accept the unpleasant consequences with the client.","note":"Informal, implies accepting blame or criticism.","audio":"audio/1b25b82e26900564.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.fancy-getting-together","text":"Fancy getting together","kind":"chunk","form":"fancy getting together","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"An informal and friendly way to suggest meeting up.","example":"It's been a while; fancy getting together for a drink soon?","plain":"It's been a while; would you like to meet for a drink soon?","note":"Common in British English, but understood in American English. Casual and warm.","audio":"audio/b7857b838302864e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.fancy-meeting-you-here","text":"fancy meeting you here","kind":"chunk","form":"Fancy meeting you here","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"An informal expression of surprise at seeing someone unexpectedly in a particular place.","example":"Well, fancy meeting you here! I didn't expect to see you at this event.","plain":"I'm surprised to see you here! I didn't expect to see you at this event.","note":"Common in British English; expresses pleasant surprise at an unexpected encounter.","audio":"audio/bc2a01b285603fe9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.feel-free-to-bring","text":"Feel free to bring","kind":"frame","form":"Feel free to bring","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Giving permission or encouraging someone to bring something, like a guest or an item.","example":"Feel free to bring a friend along to the barbecue if you like.","plain":"You can bring a friend along to the barbecue if you like.","note":"A welcoming phrase used by a host to make guests feel comfortable.","audio":"audio/c22b2e2beb5a6e49.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.fill-me-in-on","text":"Fill me in on","kind":"collocation","form":"fill me in on","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Asking someone to provide details or updates about a situation or recent events.","example":"It's been ages, so please fill me in on everything you've been up to.","plain":"It's been ages, so please tell me all the details about what you've been doing.","note":"Informal and friendly, used when catching up with someone you haven't seen in a while.","audio":"audio/a8e580f01e681ddb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.finally-i-want-to-leave-you-with","text":"Finally, I want to leave you with","kind":"frame","form":"Finally, I want to leave you with","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To present a final thought or call to action at the end of a talk.","example":"Finally, I want to leave you with the thought that collaboration is key.","plain":"Lastly, I want to share one final thought: collaboration is key.","note":"A common closing phrase to make a lasting impression.","audio":"audio/50288641286ca15c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.fingers-crossed","text":"fingers crossed","kind":"chunk","form":"Fingers crossed","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to express hope that something will happen as desired.","example":"I've got an interview tomorrow, so fingers crossed!","plain":"I've got an interview tomorrow, so I hope it goes well!","note":"A common, informal expression of hope or good luck.","audio":"audio/f48afebf8eead415.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.float-an-idea","text":"float an idea","kind":"collocation","form":"float an idea","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To suggest an idea informally to see how people react.","example":"I'd like to float an idea for improving our team's daily stand-ups.","plain":"I'd like to suggest an idea informally for improving our team's daily stand-ups.","note":"Informal, used to test the waters with a new concept.","audio":"audio/049e1f0f0b97af11.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.for-lack-of-a-better-word","text":"For lack of a better word","kind":"chunk","form":"for lack of a better word","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Indicates that the chosen word isn't perfect but is the closest available.","example":"The atmosphere was, for lack of a better word, tense.","plain":"The atmosphere was tense, though that might not be the perfect word.","note":"Used when searching for the most precise vocabulary.","audio":"audio/be3c8e2dc35a4cf8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.for-the-greater-good","text":"for the greater good","kind":"chunk","form":"for the greater good","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"For the benefit of the majority or the community as a whole.","example":"Sometimes we must make difficult decisions for the greater good of the entire organization.","plain":"Sometimes we must make difficult decisions for the benefit of the entire organization.","note":"A slightly formal or philosophical phrase, often used when advocating for sacrifice.","audio":"audio/91bcd909132a5c3e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.for-the-sake-of-argument","text":"for the sake of argument","kind":"chunk","form":"For the sake of argument","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","negotiation"],"meaning":"Used to introduce a hypothetical situation or point for discussion, without necessarily agreeing with it.","example":"For the sake of argument, let's say we agree to your price; what about the timeline?","plain":"Let's assume we agree to your price; what about the timeline?","note":"Allows exploration of possibilities without commitment, useful for testing scenarios.","audio":"audio/3dc535edc9ffafab.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.from-a-practical-standpoint","text":"From a practical standpoint","kind":"chunk","form":"From a practical standpoint","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To introduce an argument based on feasibility or real-world application.","example":"From a practical standpoint, this solution is the most cost-effective and easiest to implement.","plain":"This solution is the most cost-effective and easiest to implement in reality.","note":"Signals a shift to discussing the tangible, workable aspects of a proposal.","audio":"audio/52857b2b77fe0135.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.from-my-standpoint","text":"from my standpoint","kind":"frame","form":"From my standpoint","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To introduce your personal viewpoint or position.","example":"From my standpoint, the risks outweigh the potential benefits at this stage.","plain":"In my opinion, the risks outweigh the potential benefits at this stage.","note":"Similar to \"from my perspective,\" slightly more formal.","audio":"audio/a212f491c2b71b96.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.funnily-enough","text":"Funnily enough","kind":"chunk","form":"Funnily enough","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Introduces a surprising or coincidental detail in a story.","example":"Funnily enough, I ran into him just yesterday at the airport.","plain":"Surprisingly, I ran into him just yesterday at the airport.","note":"Adds a touch of humor or irony to a narrative.","audio":"audio/4a7f6a41876b3c8b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.further-research-is-needed","text":"Further research is needed","kind":"collocation","form":"further research is needed","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"States that more investigation or study is required to fully understand a topic.","example":"While promising, further research is needed to confirm the long-term effects of the intervention.","plain":"More research is needed to confirm the long-term effects of the intervention.","note":"A standard phrase in academic papers to acknowledge limitations and future directions.","audio":"audio/068eeaed5141af57.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.gain-insight","text":"gain insight","kind":"collocation","form":"gain insight","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To acquire a deep and accurate understanding of something.","example":"Analyzing the customer feedback helped us gain insight into their evolving needs.","plain":"Analyzing the customer feedback helped us acquire a deep understanding of their evolving needs.","note":"Refers to achieving a valuable understanding. Often the goal of analysis or research.","audio":"audio/c717641e14a37de0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.gain-traction","text":"gain traction","kind":"collocation","form":"gain traction","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To start to become popular, accepted, or make progress.","example":"The new proposal is starting to gain traction among the senior management team.","plain":"The new proposal is starting to become accepted among the senior management team.","note":"Suggests increasing momentum or acceptance. Often used for ideas, products, or initiatives.","audio":"audio/099401a6ab71a2ca.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.get-a-sense-of","text":"get a sense of","kind":"collocation","form":"get a sense of","level":"C1","situations":["travel","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To develop an understanding or impression of something.","example":"I'm trying to get a sense of the team's overall workload before assigning new tasks.","plain":"I'm trying to develop an understanding of the team's overall workload before assigning new tasks.","note":"Used when trying to gauge a situation or feeling.","audio":"audio/4b2e20ac8009ce4c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.get-bogged-down","text":"get bogged down","kind":"collocation","form":"get bogged down","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To become stuck or delayed in a process, often by excessive detail or minor issues.","example":"Let's try not to get bogged down in too many technical specifics during this high-level discussion.","plain":"Let's try not to become stuck in too many technical specifics during this high-level discussion.","note":"Warns against losing focus on the main goal by getting caught up in minutiae. Informal.","audio":"audio/dfe5769f2af1cd21.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.get-buy-in","text":"get buy-in","kind":"chunk","form":"get buy-in","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To gain agreement or support from others.","example":"We need to present a strong case to get buy-in from senior management.","plain":"We need to present a strong case to gain agreement from senior management.","note":"Informal but widely used in business for gaining approval.","audio":"audio/178b8294a1f0bb26.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.get-buy-in-from","text":"get buy-in from","kind":"collocation","form":"get buy-in from","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To gain agreement or support from others for a plan or idea.","example":"We need to get buy-in from all stakeholders before we can proceed with the changes.","plain":"We need to gain agreement from all stakeholders before we can proceed with the changes.","note":"Essential for successful implementation, especially in organizations.","audio":"audio/2932339fab8a177a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.get-down-to-business","text":"get down to business","kind":"chunk","form":"get down to business","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To start focusing on the main, serious topics of the meeting.","example":"Now that everyone's here, let's get down to business and discuss the Q3 results.","plain":"Everyone is here, so let's start discussing the Q3 results.","note":"Signals a shift from informal chat to serious work.","audio":"audio/5873aac02118a242.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.get-sidetracked","text":"get sidetracked","kind":"chunk","form":"get sidetracked","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To deviate from the main topic of discussion.","example":"Let's try not to get sidetracked by minor details; we have a lot to cover.","plain":"Let's try not to deviate from the main topic by minor details; we have a lot to cover.","note":"Used to keep a discussion focused.","audio":"audio/81d6916fdd5b1ab0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.get-sidetracked-by","text":"get sidetracked by","kind":"collocation","form":"get sidetracked by","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To become distracted from the main topic or goal.","example":"Let's try not to get sidetracked by minor details and focus on the core issue.","plain":"Let's try not to become distracted by minor details and focus on the core issue.","note":"Used to keep discussions focused.","audio":"audio/41a77dde2ad6769c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.get-the-full-picture","text":"get the full picture","kind":"collocation","form":"get the full picture","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To understand all the relevant facts about a situation.","example":"We need more data to get the full picture of why sales have declined.","plain":"We need more data to understand all the relevant facts of why sales have declined.","note":"Used when seeking comprehensive understanding.","audio":"audio/f907b019abd8880c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.get-the-go-ahead","text":"get the go-ahead","kind":"chunk","form":"get the go-ahead","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To receive permission to proceed with something.","example":"We're waiting to get the go-ahead from legal before finalizing the contract.","plain":"We're waiting to receive permission from legal before finalizing the contract.","note":"Informal, similar to \"get the green light.\"","audio":"audio/4711fe03663dc85e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.get-the-green-light","text":"get the green light","kind":"chunk","form":"get the green light","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To receive permission to proceed with something.","example":"We're just waiting to get the green light from management before launching the pilot.","plain":"We're just waiting to receive permission from management before launching the pilot.","note":"Informal, refers to approval to start.","audio":"audio/ba73f26e5adce5d8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.get-the-message-across","text":"get the message across","kind":"chunk","form":"get the message across","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To communicate an idea or point effectively.","example":"It's crucial to get the message across clearly to all team members.","plain":"It's crucial to communicate the idea effectively to all team members.","note":"Focuses on successful communication.","audio":"audio/b9733a56d89b5c9d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.get-to-the-root-of","text":"get to the root of","kind":"chunk","form":"get to the root of","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To discover the fundamental cause of a problem.","example":"We need to get to the root of why these errors are occurring so frequently.","plain":"We need to discover the fundamental cause of why these errors are occurring so frequently.","note":"Used when diagnosing deep-seated problems.","audio":"audio/6996a1f3c8898905.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.get-your-thoughts-on","text":"get your thoughts on","kind":"frame","form":"get your thoughts on","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To ask someone for their opinion or perspective on a topic.","example":"Before we decide, I'd like to get your thoughts on the proposed marketing strategy.","plain":"Before we decide, I'd like to hear your opinion on the marketing strategy.","note":"A polite way to invite participation and gather input.","audio":"audio/51a447ecea1cb983.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.getting-back-to","text":"getting back to","kind":"frame","form":"Getting back to","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to return to a previous topic after a digression.","example":"Anyway, getting back to the budget, have we finalized the figures?","plain":"Anyway, about the budget, have we finalized the figures?","note":"A clear signal that you want to refocus the conversation.","audio":"audio/39d4a0cca3834c00.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.give-some-ground","text":"give some ground","kind":"collocation","form":"give some ground","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To make concessions or yield on some points during a negotiation.","example":"We're prepared to give some ground on the payment schedule if you can commit now.","plain":"We're prepared to make some concessions on the payment schedule if you can commit now.","note":"Suggests flexibility and a willingness to compromise on certain aspects.","audio":"audio/523bbdef05dd5374.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.give-someone-the-benefit-of-the-doubt","text":"Give someone the benefit of the doubt","kind":"collocation","form":"give him the benefit of the doubt","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"To believe someone's statement, even if there are reasons to be skeptical.","example":"He seemed suspicious, but I decided to give him the benefit of the doubt.","plain":"He seemed suspicious, but I decided to trust him.","note":"Implies a willingness to trust despite uncertainty.","audio":"audio/7a4a934ccd6a16f5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.going-forward","text":"going forward","kind":"chunk","form":"Going forward","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"From this point onwards; in the future.","example":"Going forward, we'll schedule these check-ins bi-weekly.","plain":"From now on, we'll schedule these check-ins bi-weekly.","note":"Interchangeable with \"moving forward.\"","audio":"audio/68a523fe2bee4cdb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.hash-out-the-details","text":"hash out the details","kind":"chunk","form":"hash out the details","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To discuss something thoroughly to reach an agreement or understanding.","example":"We need to hash out the details of the new proposal before presenting it to the board.","plain":"We need to discuss the details of the new proposal thoroughly before presenting it to the board.","note":"Informal, implies a detailed and often lengthy discussion.","audio":"audio/6f4e3913bcab2489.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.have-a-quick-chat","text":"have a quick chat","kind":"collocation","form":"have a quick chat","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To have a short, informal conversation, often to discuss something specific.","example":"Could we have a quick chat about your recent performance review later today?","plain":"Could we talk briefly about your recent performance review later today?","note":"Common in workplace settings to initiate a discussion, often about feedback.","audio":"audio/7899dfb57410c1d0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.have-i-explained-that-clearly","text":"Have I explained that clearly","kind":"chunk","form":"Have I explained that clearly","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To ask the listener if the speaker's explanation was easy to understand.","example":"I know this concept is tricky. Have I explained that clearly, or would you like me to try again?","plain":"I know this concept is tricky. Was my explanation easy to understand, or should I try again?","note":"Shows humility and a willingness to re-explain, focusing on the speaker's delivery.","audio":"audio/78c6fdfe6e85372b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.have-some-reservations","text":"have some reservations","kind":"collocation","form":"have some reservations","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To have doubts or concerns about a particular aspect of a proposal or agreement.","example":"I have some reservations about the proposed budget allocation for marketing.","plain":"I have some doubts about the proposed budget allocation for marketing.","note":"A polite way to express concerns without outright rejecting an idea.","audio":"audio/cc3fc91831ac4658.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.he-made-it-sound-like","text":"He made it sound like","kind":"frame","form":"He made it sound like","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Reports someone's words or tone, implying a particular impression was given.","example":"He made it sound like the project was impossible, but I knew we could do it.","plain":"He implied the project was impossible, but I knew we could do it.","note":"Often used to convey a speaker's interpretation of another's words.","audio":"audio/7b57d6ae2c75007c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.he-stressed-the-importance-of","text":"He stressed the importance of","kind":"frame","form":"He stressed the importance of","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Reports someone emphasizing the significance of something.","example":"He stressed the importance of clear communication within the team.","plain":"He emphasized that clear communication within the team was important.","note":"Formal, used to convey a strong recommendation or warning.","audio":"audio/2321cdfc2e1e5c05.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.he-was-adamant-that","text":"He was adamant that","kind":"frame","form":"He was adamant that","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Reports someone's firm and unyielding insistence on a point.","example":"He was adamant that the deadline could not be extended under any circumstances.","plain":"He insisted strongly that the deadline could not be extended.","note":"Indicates strong conviction and refusal to change one's mind.","audio":"audio/d5430c5f10e10473.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.he-was-quick-to-point-out","text":"He was quick to point out","kind":"chunk","form":"He was quick to point out","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Reports that someone promptly highlighted an important fact or detail.","example":"I suggested a solution, but he was quick to point out its flaws.","plain":"I suggested a solution, but he immediately highlighted its flaws.","note":"Suggests a prompt or eager correction or clarification.","audio":"audio/4a917055179df4d8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.he-went-on-to-say","text":"He went on to say","kind":"chunk","form":"He went on to say","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Continues reporting what someone said after an initial statement.","example":"She mentioned the project was behind schedule, and she went on to say that we needed to work overtime.","plain":"She mentioned the project was behind schedule, and she added that we needed to work overtime.","note":"Useful for reporting a sequence of statements from one person.","audio":"audio/7510b1f71da33505.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.he-went-so-far-as-to-say","text":"He went so far as to say","kind":"frame","form":"He went so far as to say","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Reports someone making a strong or extreme statement, often surprising.","example":"He went so far as to say that the entire project was a waste of resources.","plain":"He even said that the entire project was a waste of resources.","note":"Used to highlight an unusually strong or bold statement.","audio":"audio/bbc8f3bb6183ae15.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.hear-me-out","text":"hear me out","kind":"chunk","form":"hear me out","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To listen to someone completely before responding.","example":"Just hear me out for a moment before you jump to conclusions about the budget.","plain":"Just listen to me for a moment before you jump to conclusions about the budget.","note":"Used when you anticipate interruption or disagreement. Slightly informal.","audio":"audio/bb860beef77da164.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.how-about-we","text":"How about we","kind":"frame","form":"How about we","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A gentle way to suggest an activity or plan to someone.","example":"How about we grab a coffee next week to discuss the project?","plain":"Let's grab a coffee next week to discuss the project.","note":"Very common, friendly way to propose an idea. Can be used in many contexts.","audio":"audio/f877af416b04f232.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.how-are-things-shaping-up","text":"How are things shaping up","kind":"chunk","form":"How are things shaping up","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Asking about the progress or development of a situation or plan.","example":"How are things shaping up with your move to the new apartment?","plain":"How is your move to the new apartment progressing?","note":"A natural way to inquire about ongoing plans or projects.","audio":"audio/f07c994fae7e6438.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.how-do-we-address-the-fact-that","text":"How do we address the fact that","kind":"frame","form":"How do we address the fact that","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to ask for solutions or strategies to deal with a specific, often problematic, reality.","example":"How do we address the fact that our current software is no longer compatible with new operating systems?","plain":"How do we deal with the reality that our current software is no longer compatible with new operating systems?","note":"Prompts for practical solutions to an acknowledged problem. Action-oriented.","audio":"audio/081dcc3b2e8d86ed.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.how-do-we-go-about","text":"How do we go about","kind":"frame","form":"How do we go about","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To ask for the method or process for achieving something.","example":"How do we go about getting approval for these new system requirements?","plain":"What is the method for getting approval for these new system requirements?","note":"Used to inquire about the procedure or steps.","audio":"audio/c849b8d5d7c59396.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.how-do-you-feel-about","text":"how do you feel about","kind":"frame","form":"How do you feel about","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A direct but polite way to ask for someone's opinion or emotional response to something.","example":"How do you feel about the new changes to the company policy?","plain":"What's your opinion on the new changes to the company policy?","note":"Invites a more personal or emotional response than 'what do you think'.","audio":"audio/d47236f07240b140.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.how-have-things-been","text":"How have things been","kind":"chunk","form":"How have things been","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A general inquiry about someone's life and circumstances.","example":"It's great to see you! How have things been since we last met?","plain":"It's good to see you! What has been happening in your life?","note":"A common, friendly way to start a conversation and catch up with someone.","audio":"audio/81b49ad147f3c455.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-agree-up-to-a-point","text":"I agree up to a point","kind":"chunk","form":"I agree up to a point","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing","academic"],"meaning":"Expresses partial agreement, indicating that one agrees with some aspects but not entirely.","example":"I agree up to a point that the methodology is sound, but the interpretation needs more nuance.","plain":"I partially agree that the methodology is sound, but the interpretation needs more nuance.","note":"A diplomatic way to express qualified agreement.","audio":"audio/2d09b5feee70c493.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-almost-forgot","text":"I almost forgot","kind":"chunk","form":"I almost forgot","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to introduce a piece of information that suddenly came to mind and is relevant.","example":"I almost forgot, the client called earlier and left a message for you.","plain":"The client called earlier and left a message for you.","note":"Informal, used to add a piece of information that was nearly overlooked.","audio":"audio/fba282fc700d870a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-also-wanted-to-mention","text":"I also wanted to mention","kind":"frame","form":"I also wanted to mention","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Introduces an additional point or piece of information.","example":"I also wanted to mention the excellent work done by the design team on this project.","plain":"I wanted to mention the excellent work done by the design team on this project.","note":"A common and polite way to contribute an extra point to a discussion.","audio":"audio/fba77a913753d278.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-appreciate-that","text":"I appreciate that","kind":"chunk","form":"I appreciate that","level":"C1","situations":["feedback","reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses gratitude or acknowledgement for someone's effort, understanding, or offer.","example":"I appreciate that you stayed late to help me finish the report.","plain":"I'm grateful that you stayed late to help me finish the report.","note":"A polite and common way to show thanks or acknowledge a helpful gesture.","audio":"audio/800f224a73bf5949.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-appreciate-the-concern","text":"I appreciate the concern","kind":"collocation","form":"I appreciate the concern","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Acknowledges and values someone's worry or apprehension about a particular issue.","example":"I appreciate the concern about the project's timeline, and we are actively addressing it.","plain":"I understand your worry about the project's timeline, and we are actively addressing it.","note":"A polite and professional way to respond to a worry or criticism.","audio":"audio/68a1fa77c7cd6bf7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-appreciate-you-raising-that","text":"I appreciate you raising that","kind":"chunk","form":"I appreciate you raising that","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To thank someone for bringing up a particular point or question.","example":"I appreciate you raising that point about potential risks; it's vital to consider.","plain":"Thank you for bringing up that point about potential risks; it's vital to consider.","note":"A polite and professional way to acknowledge a contribution.","audio":"audio/6f76a41c2d87d44b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-appreciate-you-telling-me","text":"I appreciate you telling me","kind":"chunk","form":"I appreciate you telling me","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Expresses gratitude for being informed about something, especially something difficult.","example":"I appreciate you telling me about the potential risks involved with this new strategy.","plain":"Thank you for informing me about the potential risks involved with this new strategy.","note":"Shows gratitude for honesty and transparency, even if the news is challenging.","audio":"audio/551d093c9d2b9ec2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-appreciate-your-perspective","text":"I appreciate your perspective","kind":"frame","form":"I appreciate your perspective","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Acknowledges and values the other person's viewpoint before offering a different one.","example":"I appreciate your perspective on the matter, but I'm not sure it fully accounts for the risks.","plain":"I value your viewpoint, but I'm not sure it fully accounts for the risks.","note":"A polite and respectful way to acknowledge someone's opinion before disagreeing.","audio":"audio/3f2d851c568b24f5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-appreciate-your-time","text":"I appreciate your time","kind":"chunk","form":"I appreciate your time","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I am grateful for the time you have spent speaking with me or attending this meeting.","example":"Thank you for joining; I appreciate your time and valuable insights.","plain":"Thanks for joining; I'm grateful for your time and helpful insights.","note":"A formal and polite expression of gratitude, especially at the end of a professional interaction.","audio":"audio/aee4fea3b0f61c73.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-braced-myself-for","text":"I braced myself for","kind":"chunk","form":"I braced myself for","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Prepared oneself mentally or physically for something difficult or unpleasant.","example":"I braced myself for the difficult conversation I knew I had to have with my boss.","plain":"I prepared myself for the difficult conversation with my boss.","note":"Shows anticipation of a challenging event.","audio":"audio/2e2132b075cf19b1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-can-assure-you-that","text":"I can assure you that","kind":"frame","form":"I can assure you that","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Introduces a statement with strong conviction, aiming to build trust or certainty.","example":"I can assure you that we are doing everything possible to resolve this issue.","plain":"We are doing everything possible to resolve this issue.","note":"Used to give strong reassurance or guarantee.","audio":"audio/ba0f506f00bfc56a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-can-certainly-help-with","text":"I can certainly help with","kind":"frame","form":"I can certainly help with","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To offer assistance with a specific task or area.","example":"I can certainly help with drafting the initial report if needed.","plain":"I can help draft the initial report if needed.","note":"A polite and confident offer of support.","audio":"audio/f2e121279feb03fb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-can-imagine","text":"I can imagine","kind":"chunk","form":"I can imagine","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Expresses empathy or understanding of someone's experience or feelings.","example":"That sounds incredibly stressful; I can imagine how relieved you must have been.","plain":"That sounds incredibly stressful; I understand how relieved you must have been.","note":"A common softener to show you relate to someone's situation.","audio":"audio/bf771b324c6104b5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-can-see-the-logic","text":"I can see the logic","kind":"frame","form":"I can see the logic","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Acknowledges the rational basis of an argument, even if you have reservations about its practicality or outcome.","example":"I can see the logic in reducing overheads, but it might negatively impact staff morale.","plain":"I understand the reasoning for reducing overheads, but it might negatively impact staff morale.","note":"Shows you've considered the argument, making your subsequent disagreement more reasoned.","audio":"audio/c8fedaaeda79b6e3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-can-see-your-point","text":"I can see your point","kind":"frame","form":"I can see your point","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Acknowledges understanding of someone else's perspective, even if you don't fully agree.","example":"I can see your point about the workload, but we still need to prioritize this task.","plain":"I understand your perspective about the workload, but we still need to prioritize this task.","note":"A diplomatic phrase used to show understanding in a discussion or disagreement.","audio":"audio/7ce22947a7911684.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-can-tell-you-that","text":"I can tell you that","kind":"frame","form":"I can tell you that","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Emphasizes the certainty or importance of the information being shared.","example":"I can tell you that the negotiations were extremely difficult.","plain":"I assure you that the negotiations were extremely difficult.","note":"Adds weight and conviction to a statement.","audio":"audio/a33631641fe96537.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-can-totally-relate","text":"I can totally relate","kind":"chunk","form":"I can totally relate","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Expresses strong empathy or understanding for someone else's experience.","example":"Your story about moving abroad? I can totally relate to that feeling.","plain":"Your story about moving abroad? I completely understand that feeling.","note":"Informal and enthusiastic, shows strong connection to another's experience.","audio":"audio/d268c03ad4037b96.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-cant-help-but-feel","text":"I can't help but feel","kind":"frame","form":"I can't help but feel","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses an unavoidable or strong personal emotion or opinion about a situation.","example":"I can't help but feel a little sad that the project is finally over.","plain":"I feel a little sad that the project is finally over, and I can't avoid it.","note":"Used to convey a strong, often persistent, emotional reaction or intuition.","audio":"audio/2bb6d42fb5710210.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-cant-imagine-how","text":"I can't imagine how","kind":"frame","form":"I can't imagine how","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses deep empathy, indicating that you understand the difficulty of someone's experience.","example":"I can't imagine how stressful it must be to manage two young children and a full-time job.","plain":"I don't know how stressful it must be to manage two young children and a full-time job.","note":"A strong expression of empathy, often used when someone is describing a challenging situation.","audio":"audio/d5cf9bea61eefd70.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-cant-quite-make-out","text":"I can't quite make out","kind":"frame","form":"I can't quite make out","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Indicating difficulty understanding what someone is saying due to poor audio.","example":"I can't quite make out what you're saying; the sound is very faint.","plain":"I can't understand what you're saying because the sound is very faint.","note":"A polite way to express difficulty hearing or understanding.","audio":"audio/499d1c2496bd5d45.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-cannot-stress-enough","text":"I cannot stress enough","kind":"frame","form":"I cannot stress enough","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To strongly emphasise the importance or urgency of something.","example":"I cannot stress enough the need for clear communication within the team.","plain":"I want to strongly emphasise the need for clear communication within the team.","note":"Very strong emphasis, used for critical points.","audio":"audio/d0ad5bdcf386744f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-concede-that","text":"I concede that","kind":"frame","form":"I concede that","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Admits that a particular point or argument made by another person is valid or correct.","example":"I concede that the initial data collection had some limitations, which we have since rectified.","plain":"I admit that the initial data collection had some limitations, which we have since rectified.","note":"A formal way to acknowledge a weakness in one's own argument or a strength in another's.","audio":"audio/65851e69ecc44725.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-concur","text":"I concur","kind":"chunk","form":"I concur","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To formally express agreement with an opinion or decision.","example":"After reviewing the data, I concur with the team's recommendation.","plain":"After reviewing the data, I agree with the team's recommendation.","note":"More formal than \"I agree\", often used in professional settings.","audio":"audio/e18c614cec102ea9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-concur-on-the-main-points","text":"I concur on the main points","kind":"chunk","form":"I concur on the main points","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses agreement with the core ideas, while implying potential disagreement on specifics.","example":"I concur on the main points of your strategy, but I have some concerns about the budget allocation.","plain":"I agree with the main points of your strategy, but I have some concerns about the budget allocation.","note":"A formal way to show general agreement, often used in professional settings.","audio":"audio/e4e932113585708d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-concur-with","text":"I concur with","kind":"chunk","form":"I concur with","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"A formal way to express agreement, often used before stating a point of divergence.","example":"I concur with the need for efficiency, yet we must not compromise on quality.","plain":"I agree with the need for efficiency, but we must not compromise on quality.","note":"More formal than 'I agree'. Often used in professional or academic contexts.","audio":"audio/c1601e902af7df6f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-dont-quite-follow","text":"I don't quite follow","kind":"chunk","form":"I don't quite follow","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to indicate that you don't fully understand what someone is saying.","example":"I don't quite follow your reasoning for that decision; could you elaborate a bit?","plain":"I don't understand your reasoning for that decision; could you elaborate a bit?","note":"A polite and common way to ask for clarification without sounding rude.","audio":"audio/42079384a201e064.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-dont-quite-see-it-that-way","text":"I don't quite see it that way","kind":"chunk","form":"I don't quite see it that way","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"A polite and direct way to express a different opinion or perspective.","example":"I don't quite see it that way; I think there are other factors at play here.","plain":"I have a different opinion; I think there are other factors at play here.","note":"A clear but softened statement of disagreement, focusing on perspective.","audio":"audio/36cd3ea1fc800433.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-gathered-from-her-that","text":"I gathered from her that","kind":"frame","form":"I gathered from her that","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Reports an inference made from someone's words or actions.","example":"I gathered from her that she wasn't happy with the decision.","plain":"I understood from her that she wasn't happy with the decision.","note":"Used when the information wasn't explicitly stated but implied.","audio":"audio/3d2a0fb92a4c8962.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-got-the-impression-that","text":"I got the impression that","kind":"frame","form":"I got the impression that","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Reports a conclusion drawn from someone's words or behavior, rather than direct speech.","example":"I got the impression that she wasn't entirely happy with the new arrangements.","plain":"I felt she wasn't entirely happy with the new arrangements.","note":"A softer way to report an observation or inference.","audio":"audio/74b22d99690dfa60.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-had-a-wonderful-time","text":"I had a wonderful time","kind":"chunk","form":"I had a wonderful time","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing sincere enjoyment and gratitude after an event or visit.","example":"Thank you for inviting me; I had a wonderful time at your dinner party.","plain":"Thank you for inviting me; I really enjoyed myself at your dinner party.","note":"A standard and warm phrase used by a guest to thank a host.","audio":"audio/a524ef3816f2b83c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-different-take","text":"I have a different take","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a different take","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Stating that you have a different interpretation or opinion on a subject.","example":"On the matter of market trends, I have a different take than the report suggests.","plain":"On the matter of market trends, I have a different opinion than the report suggests.","note":"Informal but professional. Implies a unique perspective based on your own analysis.","audio":"audio/1beaf3c3fb4d173c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-prior-engagement","text":"I have a prior engagement","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a prior engagement","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Politically stating that one already has a commitment or appointment.","example":"I'm so sorry, but I have a prior engagement that evening and can't make it.","plain":"I'm so sorry, but I already have something planned that evening.","note":"A formal and polite way to decline an invitation without giving too many details.","audio":"audio/cd608cfd402dcc59.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-every-confidence","text":"I have every confidence","kind":"frame","form":"I have every confidence","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Expresses strong belief in a positive outcome or someone's ability.","example":"I have every confidence that you'll successfully lead this new initiative.","plain":"I strongly believe that you'll successfully lead this new initiative.","note":"Formal and strong reassurance, often used by superiors.","audio":"audio/e5bae2d06b1f9846.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-firm-plans","text":"I have firm plans","kind":"collocation","form":"I have firm plans","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Indicating that one has definite, unchangeable commitments.","example":"I'd love to come, but I have firm plans that evening.","plain":"I want to come, but I already have definite commitments for that evening.","note":"A polite but clear way to decline an invitation, suggesting the plans cannot be changed.","audio":"audio/d44ea43fd927ef29.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-some-reservations","text":"I have some reservations","kind":"collocation","form":"I have some reservations","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","disagreeing"],"meaning":"Indicating that you have doubts or concerns about a plan or proposal.","example":"I have some reservations about launching the product without further testing.","plain":"I have some doubts about launching the product without further testing.","note":"Sounds professional and thoughtful. Suitable for business or academic discussions.","audio":"audio/6796440586db7a34.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-some-reservations-about","text":"I have some reservations about","kind":"frame","form":"I have some reservations about","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing","reacting","problem-solving"],"meaning":"Expresses specific doubts or concerns regarding a particular aspect of a plan or idea.","example":"I have some reservations about the proposed budget cuts, especially for essential services.","plain":"I have some doubts about the proposed budget cuts, especially for essential services.","note":"A clear but polite way to state specific concerns without outright rejecting an idea.","audio":"audio/8e071c7c8391bf82.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-to-admit","text":"I have to admit","kind":"chunk","form":"I have to admit","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Introduces a confession or an acknowledgment of something, often reluctantly.","example":"I have to admit, I was a bit nervous before the presentation.","plain":"I confess, I was a bit nervous before the presentation.","note":"Used to share a personal feeling or fact, sometimes with slight hesitation.","audio":"audio/36303a2d22eb6038.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-kid-you-not","text":"I kid you not","kind":"chunk","form":"I kid you not","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Emphasizes that what the speaker is saying is true, even if surprising.","example":"He actually wore a clown suit to the meeting, I kid you not!","plain":"He actually wore a clown suit to the meeting, it's true!","note":"Informal, adds emphasis and a touch of disbelief to a statement.","audio":"audio/3c398ec078462932.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-know-exactly-what-you-mean","text":"I know exactly what you mean","kind":"chunk","form":"I know exactly what you mean","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Expresses strong agreement or shared experience with someone's point.","example":"Dealing with bureaucracy can be so frustrating; I know exactly what you mean.","plain":"Dealing with bureaucracy can be so frustrating; I understand your feeling completely.","note":"A strong expression of empathy and shared understanding.","audio":"audio/12ab1bed9250e80f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-largely-agree","text":"I largely agree","kind":"chunk","form":"I largely agree","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses general agreement while leaving room for minor points of disagreement or nuance.","example":"I largely agree with your assessment, though I have a minor point on the timeline.","plain":"I mostly agree with your assessment, but I have a minor point on the timeline.","note":"Signals overall alignment, making any subsequent disagreement seem less significant.","audio":"audio/05f1b3bdd380c700.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-mean-its-not","text":"I mean, it's not","kind":"frame","form":"I mean, it's not","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Clarifying or softening a previous statement, often by rephrasing or adding context.","example":"I mean, it's not that I don't want to go, I just have too much work.","plain":"I don't want to go, but I just have too much work.","note":"A common filler and softener, used to explain or qualify.","audio":"audio/fc116ec468cbe73c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-mostly-agree","text":"I mostly agree","kind":"chunk","form":"I mostly agree","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Indicates a high level of agreement, but implies there are still some minor differences.","example":"I mostly agree with the strategy, but I'm not convinced about the marketing budget allocation.","plain":"I agree with most of the strategy, but I'm not convinced about the marketing budget allocation.","note":"Similar to 'I largely agree', it sets a cooperative tone before a slight divergence.","audio":"audio/9c617f0053959528.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-must-be-off","text":"I must be off","kind":"chunk","form":"I must be off","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"An informal and direct way to say you need to leave.","example":"Well, I must be off now, I have an appointment. Great to see you!","plain":"Well, I need to leave now, I have an appointment. Great to see you!","note":"Slightly more direct than 'I should get going', but still polite.","audio":"audio/0acf6fe239a1c629.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-need-to-extend-my-stay","text":"I need to extend my stay","kind":"chunk","form":"I need to extend my stay","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Stating your desire to remain in accommodation for a longer period.","example":"My plans changed, so I need to extend my stay by two more nights.","plain":"My plans changed, so I want to stay two more nights.","note":"Common request at hotels, often requiring checking availability.","audio":"audio/15fc57f599902d09.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-need-to-run","text":"I need to run","kind":"chunk","form":"I need to run","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I need to leave now because I have another commitment or need to go somewhere.","example":"It's been great talking, but I need to run to my next appointment.","plain":"It was good talking, but I must leave for my next appointment.","note":"A common, informal way to politely excuse yourself from a call or meeting.","audio":"audio/1aa39889913a2161.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-need-to-speak-to-a-manager","text":"I need to speak to a manager","kind":"chunk","form":"I need to speak to a manager","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Requesting to talk to someone in authority to resolve a problem.","example":"Since the issue isn't resolved, I need to speak to a manager.","plain":"Since the issue isn't resolved, I want to talk to a manager.","note":"Used when frontline staff cannot or will not resolve an issue.","audio":"audio/e95133f059ba9c85.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-really-appreciate-you","text":"I really appreciate you","kind":"frame","form":"I really appreciate you","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing sincere gratitude for someone's actions or presence.","example":"I really appreciate you coming all this way for my birthday.","plain":"I am very grateful that you came all this way for my birthday.","note":"A warm and heartfelt way to show thanks.","audio":"audio/b988594daa0bac63.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-really-appreciate-your-assistance","text":"I really appreciate your assistance","kind":"chunk","form":"I really appreciate your assistance","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Expressing sincere gratitude for help received.","example":"Thank you so much; I really appreciate your assistance with my luggage.","plain":"Thank you so much; I am very grateful for your help with my luggage.","note":"A more formal and stronger expression of thanks than \"thank you\".","audio":"audio/68c3f1a23954b00c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-regret-to-inform-you","text":"I regret to inform you","kind":"frame","form":"I regret to inform you","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","storytelling"],"meaning":"A formal way to deliver bad news, expressing sorrow or reluctance.","example":"I regret to inform you that your application for the grant was not successful this time.","plain":"Your application for the grant was not successful this time.","note":"Very formal and serious, used for official bad news.","audio":"audio/3fd772b850850701.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-remember-one-time","text":"I remember one time","kind":"chunk","form":"I remember one time","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Introduces a personal anecdote or past event.","example":"I remember one time I almost missed my flight because of a traffic jam.","plain":"Once, I almost missed my flight because of a traffic jam.","note":"Common, informal way to start a personal story.","audio":"audio/807782a600ebdedf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-respectfully-disagree","text":"I respectfully disagree","kind":"chunk","form":"I respectfully disagree","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","feedback"],"meaning":"To express disagreement in a polite and formal manner.","example":"I respectfully disagree with the assessment of the project's overall success.","plain":"I politely disagree with the assessment of the project's overall success.","note":"A formal and diplomatic way to state a differing opinion, maintaining professionalism.","audio":"audio/1c8e38fa4e30cc26.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-second-that","text":"I second that","kind":"chunk","form":"I second that","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To formally express agreement with a proposal, allowing it to be discussed or voted on.","example":"Sarah's idea is excellent; I second that proposal.","plain":"Sarah's idea is excellent; I agree with that proposal.","note":"Formal, used to support a motion or proposal.","audio":"audio/718955dd6054a7fe.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-seem-to-have-misplaced","text":"I seem to have misplaced","kind":"chunk","form":"I seem to have misplaced","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Politely stating that you have lost something, implying it's temporary.","example":"Oh dear, I seem to have misplaced my boarding pass.","plain":"Oh dear, I think I've lost my boarding pass.","note":"Softer than \"I've lost,\" suggesting it might be found soon.","audio":"audio/0aca72475c7ba6bf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-tend-to-think-that","text":"I tend to think that","kind":"frame","form":"I tend to think that","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To express a personal opinion or inclination in a gentle, less assertive way.","example":"I tend to think that a gradual rollout would be less disruptive.","plain":"I usually believe that a gradual rollout would be less disruptive.","note":"A softer way to state a personal belief or preference.","audio":"audio/b2ec1853346b10ce.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-think-thats-everything","text":"I think that's everything","kind":"chunk","form":"I think that's everything","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I believe all necessary topics have been covered and there's nothing more to discuss.","example":"Unless anyone has anything else, I think that's everything for today's meeting.","plain":"If no one has more to add, I believe we've finished for today's meeting.","note":"A common way to signal the end of a discussion, inviting final contributions.","audio":"audio/ee007ca073ae714b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-think-we-should-prioritize","text":"I think we should prioritize","kind":"frame","form":"I think we should prioritize","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to suggest focusing on certain tasks or goals as being more important than others.","example":"I think we should prioritize fixing the critical bugs before adding new features.","plain":"I believe we should focus on fixing the critical bugs before adding new features.","note":"A direct way to propose a strategic focus. Useful for managing workload and resources.","audio":"audio/c3f1227d0d65e636.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-think-were-aligned-on","text":"I think we're aligned on","kind":"frame","form":"I think we're aligned on","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"States areas of shared understanding before gently introducing a point of difference.","example":"I think we're aligned on the importance of customer satisfaction, but not on the best way to achieve it.","plain":"We agree on the importance of customer satisfaction, but not on the best way to achieve it.","note":"Builds rapport by focusing on common ground before addressing differences.","audio":"audio/72ee51d60aec8c82.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-think-were-done-here","text":"I think we're done here","kind":"chunk","form":"I think we're done here","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I believe we have completed our business or discussion for this call/meeting.","example":"Unless anyone has anything else, I think we're done here.","plain":"If there's nothing else, I believe our discussion is complete.","note":"A direct way to signal the end of a discussion, inviting final comments.","audio":"audio/fefb96f4b6acb1c1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-think-weve-covered-the-main-points","text":"I think we've covered the main points","kind":"chunk","form":"I think we've covered the main points","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Expressing the belief that all essential topics have been discussed.","example":"I think we've covered the main points for today's agenda.","plain":"I believe we have discussed all the important topics for today's agenda.","note":"Used to signal readiness to conclude a discussion.","audio":"audio/a9d0d231bafb5ed9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-totally-get-it","text":"I totally get it","kind":"chunk","form":"I totally get it","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses complete understanding and empathy for someone's feelings or situation.","example":"I totally get it; it's frustrating when your hard work isn't recognized.","plain":"I completely understand; it's frustrating when your hard work isn't recognized.","note":"An informal but widely used phrase to show strong empathy and comprehension.","audio":"audio/4ec2be6e48dc1351.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-understand-completely","text":"I understand completely","kind":"chunk","form":"I understand completely","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To fully grasp and acknowledge what someone is saying.","example":"I understand completely why you're concerned about the project's timeline.","plain":"I fully grasp why you're concerned about the project's timeline.","note":"Shows empathy and active listening, reassuring the speaker.","audio":"audio/e7e14460ab508eb6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-understand-where-youre-coming-from","text":"I understand where you're coming from","kind":"frame","form":"I understand where you're coming from","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation","disagreeing"],"meaning":"Shows empathy and comprehension of the other person's position before presenting a counter-argument.","example":"I understand where you're coming from with the need for speed, but quality is paramount.","plain":"I understand your point about needing speed, but quality is paramount.","note":"A common softener, indicating you've listened and understood, even if you don't agree.","audio":"audio/4fe02b433cf274c7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-understand-your-hesitation","text":"I understand your hesitation","kind":"chunk","form":"I understand your hesitation","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To acknowledge someone's reluctance or doubt about a proposal, showing empathy.","example":"I understand your hesitation about committing to such a large project without more data.","plain":"I know you're reluctant to commit to such a large project without more data.","note":"A polite and empathetic way to address someone's doubts or concerns. Reassuring.","audio":"audio/cbd016836cb4599c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-understand-your-position","text":"I understand your position","kind":"collocation","form":"I understand your position","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Expresses comprehension of someone's viewpoint or stance on an issue.","example":"I understand your position on the ethical implications, and it's something we're taking seriously.","plain":"I comprehend your viewpoint on the ethical implications, and it's something we're taking seriously.","note":"A polite and diplomatic way to acknowledge another's perspective.","audio":"audio/40ae366951b52058.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-value-your-input","text":"I value your input","kind":"chunk","form":"I value your input","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Expresses that one considers someone's contributions or opinions to be important.","example":"I value your input on how we can streamline our internal processes more efficiently.","plain":"I consider your opinions important on how we can streamline our internal processes more efficiently.","note":"A professional and respectful way to acknowledge someone's contribution to a discussion.","audio":"audio/e05d55c78f98a376.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-acknowledge","text":"I want to acknowledge","kind":"frame","form":"I want to acknowledge","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To formally recognize and appreciate someone's contribution or effort.","example":"I want to acknowledge the significant effort you put into this challenging project.","plain":"I want to recognize the significant effort you put into this challenging project.","note":"A professional way to give credit where it's due, often for hard work.","audio":"audio/687a7bef5d7c8c22.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-address","text":"I want to address","kind":"frame","form":"I want to address","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To deal with or give attention to a specific issue, concern, or question.","example":"I want to address the concerns raised during the last team meeting about workload.","plain":"I want to deal with the concerns raised during the last team meeting about workload.","note":"A direct and professional way to indicate that a problem or issue will be discussed.","audio":"audio/70f627cf5eecdb19.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-assure-you","text":"I want to assure you","kind":"frame","form":"I want to assure you","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express a strong desire to convince someone of the truth or certainty of something.","example":"I want to assure you that we are fully committed to meeting the project deadline.","plain":"I promise you that we are fully committed to meeting the project deadline.","note":"Used to convey sincerity and commitment, often to alleviate concerns. Direct and earnest.","audio":"audio/5188a183ebcc17ca.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-be-clear","text":"I want to be clear","kind":"frame","form":"I want to be clear","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Used to introduce a direct and unambiguous statement, often to avoid misunderstanding.","example":"I want to be clear that this isn't a criticism of your effort, but the outcome.","plain":"I want to state clearly that this isn't a criticism of your effort, but the outcome.","note":"Similar to 'make it clear,' but often used when delivering sensitive or corrective feedback.","audio":"audio/27d96223ecbb5b70.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-be-honest","text":"I want to be honest","kind":"frame","form":"I want to be honest","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Introduces a truthful and direct statement, often about a difficult reality.","example":"I want to be honest about the challenges we face with the current budget constraints.","plain":"I want to be truthful about the challenges we face with the current budget constraints.","note":"Signals that direct, unvarnished information is coming. Builds trust.","audio":"audio/a841c54db863e505.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-be-transparent","text":"I want to be transparent","kind":"frame","form":"I want to be transparent","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Expresses a commitment to openness and full disclosure of information.","example":"I want to be transparent about the reasons behind the recent organizational changes.","plain":"I want to be open about the reasons behind the recent organizational changes.","note":"Used to foster trust and understanding, especially when delivering difficult news.","audio":"audio/86389e82974823cf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-bring-up","text":"I want to bring up","kind":"frame","form":"I want to bring up","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To introduce a topic for discussion, often one that is important or sensitive.","example":"I want to bring up a point regarding the efficiency of our current software tools.","plain":"I want to mention a point regarding the efficiency of our current software tools.","note":"A common way to introduce a new topic or a concern in a meeting or conversation.","audio":"audio/85991e7ccfff715e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-challenge-you","text":"I want to challenge you","kind":"frame","form":"I want to challenge you","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Expresses a desire to push someone to develop or perform at a higher level.","example":"I want to challenge you to think outside the box for this particular problem.","plain":"I want to push you to think creatively for this particular problem.","note":"Used to motivate growth and development, often implying a belief in their potential.","audio":"audio/c8175d913202b628.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-check-in","text":"I want to check in","kind":"frame","form":"I want to check in","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To briefly connect with someone to see how they are doing or to get an update.","example":"I want to check in with you about how you're feeling about your current workload.","plain":"I want to talk briefly with you about how you're feeling about your current workload.","note":"A caring and informal way to initiate a conversation, often about well-being or progress.","audio":"audio/35178df32475024d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-clarify","text":"I want to clarify","kind":"frame","form":"I want to clarify","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To make something clearer or more precise, often to avoid misunderstanding.","example":"I want to clarify the expectations for your role in the upcoming initiative.","plain":"I want to make the expectations for your role in the upcoming initiative clearer.","note":"Used to ensure mutual understanding, especially regarding roles or responsibilities.","audio":"audio/74094a29624dc102.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-discuss","text":"I want to discuss","kind":"frame","form":"I want to discuss","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To talk about a specific topic in detail, often to exchange ideas or resolve issues.","example":"I want to discuss the feedback I received about your recent client presentation.","plain":"I want to talk about the feedback I received about your recent client presentation.","note":"A straightforward way to initiate a conversation about feedback, positive or negative.","audio":"audio/9f99a4ba7b4c16dd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-draw-attention-to","text":"I want to draw attention to","kind":"frame","form":"I want to draw attention to","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To specifically point out something important for others to notice.","example":"I want to draw attention to the innovative solutions proposed by the junior team members.","plain":"I want to specifically point out the innovative solutions proposed by the junior team members.","note":"Professional and direct, used to emphasize a specific point or achievement.","audio":"audio/09f1780ff366c932.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-emphasize","text":"I want to emphasize","kind":"frame","form":"I want to emphasize","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","feedback"],"meaning":"To stress the importance of a particular point or idea.","example":"I want to emphasize the importance of clear communication in our remote team setup.","plain":"I want to stress the importance of clear communication in our remote team setup.","note":"Used to make a point stand out as crucial. Can be used for both praise and criticism.","audio":"audio/91274e4de16059c7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-empower-you","text":"I want to empower you","kind":"frame","form":"I want to empower you","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Expresses a desire to give someone the authority or confidence to do something.","example":"I want to empower you to take more initiative in your projects and decision-making.","plain":"I want to give you the authority to take more initiative in your projects and decision-making.","note":"Used to encourage autonomy and confidence, often in a leadership context.","audio":"audio/13a9c2a53a25b125.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-encourage-you","text":"I want to encourage you","kind":"frame","form":"I want to encourage you","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Expresses a desire to motivate or inspire someone to do something.","example":"I want to encourage you to share your innovative ideas more often in team meetings.","plain":"I want to motivate you to share your innovative ideas more often in team meetings.","note":"A positive way to suggest a change in behavior or to foster a positive habit.","audio":"audio/d258539667818d80.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-ensure","text":"I want to ensure","kind":"frame","form":"I want to ensure","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Expresses a desire to make certain that something happens or is understood.","example":"I want to ensure that everyone feels comfortable sharing their ideas in team meetings.","plain":"I want to make sure that everyone feels comfortable sharing their ideas in team meetings.","note":"A common phrase for setting expectations or clarifying intentions, often in a leadership role.","audio":"audio/0632eb4086cbf9b5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-follow-up","text":"I want to follow up","kind":"frame","form":"I want to follow up","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To continue a previous discussion or action, often to review progress or feedback.","example":"I want to follow up on the feedback we discussed last week regarding your presentation.","plain":"I want to continue our discussion from last week regarding your presentation feedback.","note":"Professional way to revisit a topic, ensuring accountability or continued support.","audio":"audio/22c2820605785f5d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-get-your-thoughts","text":"I want to get your thoughts","kind":"frame","form":"I want to get your thoughts","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A polite way to ask for someone's opinion or perspective on a matter.","example":"I want to get your thoughts on the proposed changes to the marketing strategy.","plain":"I want to hear your opinions on the proposed changes to the marketing strategy.","note":"Invites collaboration and shows respect for others' opinions. Inclusive.","audio":"audio/4b833bcbbf40bff3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-hear-your-perspective","text":"I want to hear your perspective","kind":"frame","form":"I want to hear your perspective","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A polite and inclusive way to ask for someone's point of view.","example":"I want to hear your perspective on how the team dynamics are evolving.","plain":"I want to hear your point of view on how the team dynamics are evolving.","note":"Encourages open communication and shows value for diverse viewpoints.","audio":"audio/0afc4c602d01a014.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-highlight","text":"I want to highlight","kind":"frame","form":"I want to highlight","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To draw special attention to a specific point, achievement, or issue.","example":"I want to highlight the innovative solution you proposed for the technical problem.","plain":"I want to emphasize the innovative solution you proposed for the technical problem.","note":"Used to bring a specific positive (or sometimes negative) aspect to the forefront.","audio":"audio/f4ae07b67d8d0baf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-make-it-clear","text":"I want to make it clear","kind":"frame","form":"I want to make it clear","level":"C1","situations":["presenting","feedback"],"meaning":"To state something explicitly and unambiguously, leaving no room for misinterpretation.","example":"I want to make it clear that your contributions are highly valued here.","plain":"I want to state clearly that your contributions are highly valued here.","note":"Used to ensure a message is understood precisely, often for reassurance or firm feedback.","audio":"audio/9d821ebc848cffbb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-make-sure","text":"I want to make sure","kind":"frame","form":"I want to make sure","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Expresses a desire to confirm understanding or ensure a specific outcome.","example":"I want to make sure we're all on the same page regarding the next steps for the project.","plain":"I want to confirm we're all in agreement regarding the next steps for the project.","note":"A common phrase for checking alignment or understanding in a team setting.","audio":"audio/072e46a85dd6509d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-praise","text":"I want to praise","kind":"frame","form":"I want to praise","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To express strong approval or admiration for someone's actions or qualities.","example":"I want to praise your initiative in taking on that challenging client account.","plain":"I want to express strong approval for your initiative in taking on that challenging client account.","note":"Direct and strong positive feedback, suitable for formal or informal settings.","audio":"audio/d93a506663b6adfc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-provide","text":"I want to provide","kind":"frame","form":"I want to provide","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To give or supply something, often information or assistance.","example":"I want to provide some constructive feedback on your recent report's structure.","plain":"I want to give some constructive feedback on your recent report's structure.","note":"A professional way to introduce feedback, emphasizing its helpful intent.","audio":"audio/3b49eec2e872d5a8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-raise","text":"I want to raise","kind":"frame","form":"I want to raise","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To introduce a question, concern, or issue for consideration.","example":"I want to raise a question about the feasibility of the proposed timeline.","plain":"I want to ask a question about the feasibility of the proposed timeline.","note":"Professional and direct, often used for formal concerns or questions.","audio":"audio/c46ae1b0b09c432f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-recognize","text":"I want to recognize","kind":"frame","form":"I want to recognize","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To formally express appreciation or give credit for someone's contribution or effort.","example":"I want to recognize the exceptional effort put in by the entire project team.","plain":"I want to formally express appreciation for the exceptional effort put in by the entire project team.","note":"Professional and positive, used to give credit or show appreciation.","audio":"audio/245d29ecc1048f68.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-stress","text":"I want to stress","kind":"frame","form":"I want to stress","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To strongly emphasize a point, indicating its critical importance.","example":"I want to stress that this feedback is intended to be constructive and supportive.","plain":"I want to strongly emphasize that this feedback is intended to be constructive and supportive.","note":"A direct way to highlight the significance of a statement, often to clarify intent.","audio":"audio/16306c070cfd9547.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-support-you","text":"I want to support you","kind":"frame","form":"I want to support you","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Expresses a commitment to providing assistance, encouragement, or resources.","example":"I want to support you in achieving your professional development goals this year.","plain":"I want to assist you in achieving your professional development goals this year.","note":"Emphasizes a supportive role, often when discussing career growth or challenges.","audio":"audio/bbf9950f9d7ea3ab.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-underscore","text":"I want to underscore","kind":"frame","form":"I want to underscore","level":"C1","situations":["presenting","feedback"],"meaning":"To stress the importance of something, making it very clear.","example":"I want to underscore the critical need for timely data submission this quarter.","plain":"I want to stress the critical need for timely data submission this quarter.","note":"Formal and strong, used to highlight the extreme importance of a point.","audio":"audio/90b6cf001d58f08e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-understand-better","text":"I want to understand better","kind":"frame","form":"I want to understand better","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Expresses a desire for more clarity or insight into a situation or behavior.","example":"I want to understand better what led to the delay in the project delivery.","plain":"I want to gain more insight into what led to the delay in the project delivery.","note":"A non-confrontational way to seek information, often when addressing a problem.","audio":"audio/de8ffb6fac9ba2ae.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-wanted-to-follow-up-on","text":"I wanted to follow up on","kind":"frame","form":"I wanted to follow up on","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Introducing a return to a previous topic or action point.","example":"I wanted to follow up on the discussion we had about the new project.","plain":"I wanted to revisit our discussion about the new project.","note":"Used to bring attention back to a specific item from a past conversation.","audio":"audio/382eb1c08c53081c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-wanted-to-mention-that","text":"I wanted to mention that","kind":"chunk","form":"I wanted to mention that","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Introducing a point or piece of information one wishes to share.","example":"Before we finish, I wanted to mention that the report is due next week.","plain":"Before we finish, I wanted to say that the report is due next week.","note":"A polite way to introduce a new, often important, piece of information.","audio":"audio/88e144f1ba7026bd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-was-just-about-to-say","text":"I was just about to say","kind":"frame","form":"I was just about to say","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to express agreement or indicate that you were thinking the same thing.","example":"You're right, we should definitely try that. I was just about to say the same thing!","plain":"You're right, we should definitely try that. I was thinking the same thing!","note":"Shows strong agreement and a sense of shared thought.","audio":"audio/44f2f34d387ebd71.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-was-just-thinking-of-you","text":"I was just thinking of you","kind":"chunk","form":"I was just thinking of you","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing that someone was recently on your mind, often when you unexpectedly meet them.","example":"What a coincidence! I was just thinking of you the other day.","plain":"What a coincidence! I was thinking about you the other day.","note":"A warm and friendly phrase, often used when bumping into someone.","audio":"audio/f5eb483dd49eadda.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-was-under-the-impression-that","text":"I was under the impression that","kind":"frame","form":"I was under the impression that","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Used to express mild surprise or confusion when a belief turns out to be incorrect.","example":"I was under the impression that the meeting was scheduled for Tuesday, not Wednesday.","plain":"I thought the meeting was scheduled for Tuesday, not Wednesday.","note":"A polite way to correct a misunderstanding or express mild annoyance at a change.","audio":"audio/e63d27297c0e0379.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-was-wondering-if-youd","text":"I was wondering if you'd","kind":"frame","form":"I was wondering if you'd","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A very polite and indirect way to make a request or extend an invitation.","example":"I was wondering if you'd be free to grab a coffee sometime soon.","plain":"Would you be free to grab a coffee sometime soon?","note":"Softens the request, making it less demanding. Good for sensitive topics.","audio":"audio/c856b25e60d9fc92.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-wish-i-could","text":"I wish I could","kind":"frame","form":"I wish I could","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing regret that you are unable to accept an invitation or do something.","example":"I wish I could join you for the concert, but I have to work late.","plain":"I regret that I cannot join you for the concert, as I have to work late.","note":"Conveys genuine desire to participate despite inability.","audio":"audio/2ef4ab1538cf58d8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-wish-to-emphasize","text":"I wish to emphasize","kind":"frame","form":"I wish to emphasize","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To highlight the importance of a particular point.","example":"I wish to emphasize the importance of teamwork for this project's success.","plain":"I want to highlight the importance of teamwork for this project's success.","note":"A more formal and strong way to stress a point; sounds very deliberate.","audio":"audio/7025f2d0fa94425c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-wont-be-able-to-make-it","text":"I won't be able to make it","kind":"chunk","form":"I won't be able to make it","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A common and polite way to state that you cannot attend an event.","example":"Thanks for the invitation, but I won't be able to make it to the party.","plain":"Thanks for the invitation, but I cannot come to the party.","note":"Standard and clear way to decline attendance.","audio":"audio/63034a115de87116.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-wonder-if","text":"I wonder if","kind":"frame","form":"I wonder if","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Introduces a suggestion or alternative idea tentatively, inviting discussion rather than stating a fact.","example":"I wonder if we could explore a different vendor for these materials to cut costs.","plain":"Perhaps we could explore a different vendor for these materials to cut costs.","note":"A very polite way to suggest an alternative or raise a doubt, making it less confrontational.","audio":"audio/a7e269ae6b8925d1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-wonder-if-we-could-try","text":"I wonder if we could try","kind":"frame","form":"I wonder if we could try","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Proposes an experimental or alternative action in a tentative and polite manner.","example":"I wonder if we could try a smaller pilot project first before rolling it out company-wide.","plain":"Perhaps we could try a smaller pilot project first before rolling it out company-wide.","note":"A very soft way to suggest a different course of action, often implying caution.","audio":"audio/de8bd0fc50d1fa18.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-would-argue-that","text":"I would argue that","kind":"frame","form":"I would argue that","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","disagreeing","academic","presenting","persuading"],"meaning":"Introduces a strong but still polite assertion or contention.","example":"I would argue that the long-term benefits outweigh the short-term implementation costs.","plain":"I believe the long-term benefits outweigh the short-term implementation costs.","note":"A formal and assertive yet polite way to present a strong opinion.","audio":"audio/447063edf420b26f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-would-respectfully-suggest-that","text":"I would respectfully suggest that","kind":"frame","form":"I would respectfully suggest that","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Introduces an alternative idea or opinion in a very polite and deferential manner.","example":"I would respectfully suggest that we reconsider the timing of this announcement.","plain":"I think we should reconsider the timing of this announcement.","note":"A very formal and polite way to offer a different idea, often used in formal settings.","audio":"audio/d1225964a19cfd7e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-wouldnt-go-so-far-as-to-say","text":"I wouldn't go so far as to say","kind":"frame","form":"I wouldn't go so far as to say","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Expresses disagreement with an extreme statement, while not fully disagreeing.","example":"I wouldn't go so far as to say it was a failure, but it wasn't a success.","plain":"I wouldn't say it was a failure, but it wasn't a success.","note":"A polite way to express partial disagreement or temper a strong claim.","audio":"audio/b7ef8f4ae319ea14.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-wouldnt-go-that-far","text":"I wouldn't go that far","kind":"chunk","form":"I wouldn't go that far","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","disagreeing","socialising"],"meaning":"Gently disagreeing with an extreme statement or strong opinion.","example":"\"That was the worst movie ever!\" \"I wouldn't go that far, but it wasn't great.\"","plain":"\"That was the worst movie ever!\" \"I don't agree completely, but it wasn't great.\"","note":"A polite way to express partial disagreement or temper an exaggeration.","audio":"audio/732e57f40ab8e26f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-wouldnt-have-thought","text":"I wouldn't have thought","kind":"frame","form":"I wouldn't have thought","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses surprise or disbelief about something, implying it goes against one's expectations.","example":"I wouldn't have thought he'd be interested in that kind of research.","plain":"I didn't expect him to be interested in that kind of research.","note":"Used to convey mild surprise or to indicate that something was unexpected.","audio":"audio/24d9bfb3a418a4f4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-wouldnt-say-no-to","text":"I wouldn't say no to","kind":"frame","form":"I wouldn't say no to","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","storytelling"],"meaning":"Expresses a desire for something, often food, drink, or an opportunity.","example":"After that long presentation, I wouldn't say no to a cup of coffee.","plain":"After that long presentation, I would like a cup of coffee.","note":"Informal, polite way to express a wish or acceptance.","audio":"audio/4634315a6dd89503.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-appreciate-it-if-you-could","text":"I'd appreciate it if you could","kind":"frame","form":"I'd appreciate it if you could","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A very polite way to make a request or suggest a solution.","example":"I'd appreciate it if you could arrange a taxi for us to the airport.","plain":"Please arrange a taxi for us to the airport.","note":"Highly polite and formal, often used when asking for a favour or service.","audio":"audio/2004b6751fad90d4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-be-delighted-to","text":"I'd be delighted to","kind":"chunk","form":"I'd be delighted to","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A formal and very polite way to accept an invitation.","example":"\"We'd love you to join us.\" \"Thank you, I'd be delighted to.\"","plain":"\"We'd love you to join us.\" \"Thank you, I would be very happy to.\"","note":"More formal than \"I'd love to,\" suitable for more formal invitations.","audio":"audio/599421c63aa5b2f3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-be-happy-to","text":"I'd be happy to","kind":"chunk","form":"I'd be happy to","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing willingness or pleasure to do something.","example":"I'd be happy to bring a dessert if that would be helpful for the party.","plain":"I would gladly bring a dessert if that would help for the party.","note":"A polite and helpful way to offer assistance or accept a request.","audio":"audio/9c291af67ab467d5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-be-willing-to","text":"I'd be willing to","kind":"frame","form":"I'd be willing to","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Indicates a readiness to perform an action or make a concession.","example":"I'd be willing to adjust the payment terms if you can guarantee a larger volume.","plain":"I would adjust the payment terms if you can guarantee a larger volume.","note":"A polite way to offer a concession, often conditional.","audio":"audio/070f0b6629062080.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-bring-to-your-attention","text":"I'd like to bring to your attention","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to bring to your attention","level":"C1","situations":["travel","presenting"],"meaning":"To formally highlight a specific piece of information or issue.","example":"I'd like to bring to your attention a critical security vulnerability we discovered.","plain":"I want to tell you about a critical security vulnerability we discovered.","note":"A formal and polite way to introduce an important point or concern.","audio":"audio/6f026e55a563cbfc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-cancel-my-booking","text":"I'd like to cancel my booking","kind":"chunk","form":"I'd like to cancel my booking","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Stating your intention to revoke a reservation you made.","example":"Due to unforeseen circumstances, I'd like to cancel my booking.","plain":"Due to unforeseen circumstances, I want to cancel my reservation.","note":"Standard phrase for cancelling any type of reservation.","audio":"audio/032674c053b3da57.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-challenge","text":"I'd like to challenge","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to challenge","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","disagreeing"],"meaning":"Politely questioning or disputing a statement or assumption.","example":"I'd like to challenge the assumption that our customers prefer cheaper options.","plain":"I want to question the assumption that our customers prefer cheaper options.","note":"More direct than 'raise a concern', but still professional. Used to dispute a premise.","audio":"audio/ff9b8430467647ff.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-challenge-that","text":"I'd like to challenge that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'd like to challenge that","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Politely expressing an intention to question or dispute a statement.","example":"With respect, I'd like to challenge that assumption about market growth.","plain":"Respectfully, I want to question that assumption about market growth.","note":"A formal and assertive way to express disagreement or doubt.","audio":"audio/a56581fa2e4cc569.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-challenge-that-assumption","text":"I'd like to challenge that assumption","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to challenge that assumption","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Politely questioning the validity of a premise or belief.","example":"I'd like to challenge that assumption that all employees prefer working from home.","plain":"I want to question the belief that all employees prefer working from home.","note":"Direct but professional. Used to dispute a foundational idea in an argument.","audio":"audio/a9bd337178770eca.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-challenge-the-premise","text":"I'd like to challenge the premise","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to challenge the premise","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Politely questioning the fundamental assumption or basis of an argument.","example":"I'd like to challenge the premise that our current system is inherently flawed.","plain":"I want to question the basic assumption that our current system is inherently flawed.","note":"Formal and analytical. Used to dispute the foundation of an argument.","audio":"audio/ffdd70c5a298ca11.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-chime-in","text":"I'd like to chime in","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to chime in","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To politely add your opinion or comment to a discussion.","example":"I'd like to chime in here with a thought on the marketing strategy.","plain":"I'd like to add my opinion here with a thought on the marketing strategy.","note":"Informal but common way to contribute to a conversation.","audio":"audio/4905de451e078f12.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-commend","text":"I'd like to commend","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to commend","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A formal way to praise someone publicly for their achievements or efforts.","example":"I'd like to commend the entire team for their dedication and hard work this quarter.","plain":"I want to praise the entire team for their dedication and hard work this quarter.","note":"Formal and often used in public recognition or official feedback.","audio":"audio/afb7e51c21ddcf5c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-confirm-my-reservation","text":"I'd like to confirm my reservation","kind":"chunk","form":"I'd like to confirm my reservation","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To verify the details of a booking you have made.","example":"Before we leave, I'd like to confirm my reservation for the evening tour.","plain":"Before we leave, I want to check my booking for the evening tour.","note":"Standard phrase for checking bookings for hotels, tours, flights.","audio":"audio/a2ec6ea401eb311c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-double-check","text":"I'd like to double-check","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to double-check","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To politely state that you want to verify information again to ensure accuracy.","example":"I'd like to double-check the departure time for my train to Paris tomorrow.","plain":"I want to confirm the train departure time.","note":"Implies a quick, final verification.","audio":"audio/e246ff50ce55ca7f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-enquire-about","text":"I'd like to enquire about","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to enquire about","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A formal and polite way to ask for information about something.","example":"I'd like to enquire about the possibility of upgrading my room for an extra charge.","plain":"I want to ask about upgrading my room.","note":"More formal than \"ask about\". Suitable for official settings like hotels or airlines.","audio":"audio/9ab8994581d56394.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-express","text":"I'd like to express","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to express","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A polite way to state one's feelings or opinions.","example":"I'd like to express my gratitude for your valuable input during this challenging period.","plain":"I want to state my gratitude for your valuable input during this challenging period.","note":"Professional and versatile, used to convey various sentiments.","audio":"audio/85a3dfedb09fb7d6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-express-a-concern","text":"I'd like to express a concern","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to express a concern","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Politely stating that you have a worry or apprehension about something.","example":"I'd like to express a concern about the tight deadline for this critical project.","plain":"I want to state my worry about the tight deadline for this critical project.","note":"Formal and professional. Used to voice worries in a constructive way.","audio":"audio/3ef839a2669f8b32.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-hand-over-to","text":"I'd like to hand over to","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to hand over to","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To formally transfer the speaking turn or responsibility to another person.","example":"I'd like to hand over to John, who will present the financial projections.","plain":"I will now let John present the financial projections.","note":"Common when transitioning between speakers or topics.","audio":"audio/9ee161e45ae9ccd6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-hear-more","text":"I'd like to hear more","kind":"chunk","form":"I'd like to hear more","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Expressing a desire for additional information or elaboration.","example":"That sounds promising; I'd like to hear more about the project details.","plain":"That sounds promising; I want to hear more about the project details.","note":"A polite way to encourage someone to continue explaining.","audio":"audio/dd0b6b9584e5aaa0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-highlight","text":"I'd like to highlight","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to highlight","level":"C1","situations":["presenting","persuading"],"meaning":"To draw particular attention to a specific piece of information.","example":"I'd like to highlight the innovative features of our new product.","plain":"I want to draw attention to the innovative features of our new product.","note":"A polite way to emphasise an important detail.","audio":"audio/63dcd88028dd1ecf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-make-a-complaint","text":"I'd like to make a complaint","kind":"chunk","form":"I'd like to make a complaint","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Formally stating your intention to register a grievance.","example":"I'd like to make a complaint about the noise from the room next door.","plain":"I want to complain about the noise from the room next door.","note":"Direct and formal way to initiate a complaint process.","audio":"audio/9aa7fbdb98217aa8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-offer-a-caveat","text":"I'd like to offer a caveat","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to offer a caveat","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Introducing a warning or a condition that limits the scope of a statement or plan.","example":"I'd like to offer a caveat regarding the projected sales figures; they depend on market stability.","plain":"I want to give a warning regarding the projected sales figures; they depend on market stability.","note":"Formal and precise. Used to qualify a statement or proposal, often implying a potential issue.","audio":"audio/578e3ffa73830d9d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-offer-a-counterpoint","text":"I'd like to offer a counterpoint","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to offer a counterpoint","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Politely introducing an opposing argument or a different perspective.","example":"I'd like to offer a counterpoint to the idea that remote work reduces productivity.","plain":"I want to present an opposing argument to the idea that remote work reduces productivity.","note":"Formal and academic. Signals a reasoned, opposing argument.","audio":"audio/75dab757107d22e4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-offer-a-different-angle","text":"I'd like to offer a different angle","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to offer a different angle","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Introducing a new perspective or way of looking at a problem or situation.","example":"I'd like to offer a different angle on how we can attract new talent.","plain":"I want to give a different way of looking at how we can attract new talent.","note":"Collaborative and creative. Encourages a broader discussion.","audio":"audio/f73e1296f8355ea8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-offer-a-different-approach","text":"I'd like to offer a different approach","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to offer a different approach","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Proposing an alternative method or strategy for dealing with a situation.","example":"I'd like to offer a different approach to managing the project's stakeholders.","plain":"I want to give a different method for managing the project's stakeholders.","note":"Constructive and collaborative. Focuses on finding a better way.","audio":"audio/85cc1e84025dc5f9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-offer-a-different-interpretation","text":"I'd like to offer a different interpretation","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to offer a different interpretation","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Presenting an alternative understanding or meaning of data, events, or statements.","example":"I'd like to offer a different interpretation of the survey results.","plain":"I want to give a different understanding of the survey results.","note":"Formal and analytical. Used to challenge an existing interpretation with your own.","audio":"audio/e8fbee5d99741fe6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-offer-a-different-perspective","text":"I'd like to offer a different perspective","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to offer a different perspective","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Introducing an alternative viewpoint or way of looking at a situation.","example":"I'd like to offer a different perspective on the challenges we face.","plain":"I want to give a different way of looking at the challenges we face.","note":"Constructive and collaborative. Focuses on broadening the discussion.","audio":"audio/fe47eef664d54aa3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-offer-a-different-perspective-on","text":"I'd like to offer a different perspective on","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to offer a different perspective on","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Introducing an alternative viewpoint or way of looking at a specific topic.","example":"I'd like to offer a different perspective on the recent changes in company policy.","plain":"I want to give a different way of looking at the recent changes in company policy.","note":"Collaborative and thoughtful. Encourages a broader understanding of an issue.","audio":"audio/32ee88cd40fd3e1f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-offer-a-different-solution","text":"I'd like to offer a different solution","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to offer a different solution","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Proposing an alternative answer or resolution to a problem.","example":"I'd like to offer a different solution that might be more sustainable long-term.","plain":"I want to give a different answer that might be more sustainable long-term.","note":"Constructive and problem-solving oriented. Focuses on finding a better outcome.","audio":"audio/32595de45b024e8a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-offer-a-different-viewpoint","text":"I'd like to offer a different viewpoint","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to offer a different viewpoint","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Introducing an alternative opinion or way of seeing a situation.","example":"I'd like to offer a different viewpoint on the current market volatility.","plain":"I want to give a different opinion on the current market volatility.","note":"Collaborative and thoughtful. Encourages a broader discussion.","audio":"audio/fc09365ed1f7cf68.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-offer-a-perspective","text":"I'd like to offer a perspective","kind":"chunk","form":"I'd like to offer a perspective","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Politely introducing one's own viewpoint on a matter.","example":"If I may, I'd like to offer a perspective on the budget allocation.","plain":"If I may, I want to share my view on the budget allocation.","note":"A formal and polite way to contribute to a discussion.","audio":"audio/0b8c0a08a1b5cb30.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-offer-a-qualification","text":"I'd like to offer a qualification","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to offer a qualification","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Adding a condition or limitation to a statement, often to express a subtle disagreement.","example":"I'd like to offer a qualification to that statement: it only applies under ideal conditions.","plain":"I want to add a condition to that statement: it only applies under ideal conditions.","note":"Formal and precise. Used to refine or limit a previous statement, often to avoid overgeneralization.","audio":"audio/887f8feb786b86eb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-offer-a-word-of-caution","text":"I'd like to offer a word of caution","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to offer a word of caution","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Politely giving a warning or advice about potential risks or difficulties.","example":"I'd like to offer a word of caution regarding the speed of implementation.","plain":"I want to give a warning regarding the speed of implementation.","note":"Formal and thoughtful. Used to highlight potential problems in a diplomatic way.","audio":"audio/5fa87419a313083b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-preface-this-by-saying","text":"I'd like to preface this by saying","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to preface this by saying","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To make an introductory remark before stating the main point.","example":"I'd like to preface this by saying that these are preliminary findings.","plain":"I want to start by saying that these are preliminary findings.","note":"Often used to set expectations or provide context for what's coming.","audio":"audio/c81f68fbcb7ea791.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-present-a-different-view","text":"I'd like to present a different view","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to present a different view","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Formally introducing an alternative opinion or perspective on a topic.","example":"I'd like to present a different view on the potential impact of these changes.","plain":"I want to show a different opinion on the potential impact of these changes.","note":"Formal and constructive. Focuses on broadening the discussion with new insights.","audio":"audio/e42798e2eeb688ef.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-propose-a-motion","text":"I'd like to propose a motion","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to propose a motion","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To formally suggest a course of action for a vote or decision.","example":"I'd like to propose a motion to approve the revised project plan.","plain":"I suggest we vote to approve the revised project plan.","note":"Very formal, typically used in structured meetings with voting procedures.","audio":"audio/29ba0941dfc8a900.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-propose-that","text":"I'd like to propose that","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to propose that","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","persuading"],"meaning":"To formally suggest a plan or course of action.","example":"I'd like to propose that we form a small task force to investigate this further.","plain":"I suggest we form a small task force to investigate this further.","note":"A formal way to make a suggestion or recommendation.","audio":"audio/43133a56e5ce4dd9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-put-forward","text":"I'd like to put forward","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to put forward","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing","persuading"],"meaning":"Proposing an idea or argument for consideration, often as an alternative.","example":"I'd like to put forward a different proposal for the team's structure.","plain":"I want to propose a different plan for the team's structure.","note":"Formal and constructive. Used to introduce a new idea or counter-proposal.","audio":"audio/15084c97c14b2d66.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-put-it-another-way","text":"I'd like to put it another way","kind":"chunk","form":"I'd like to put it another way","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Rephrasing an idea or argument, often to present a different emphasis or perspective.","example":"I'd like to put it another way: perhaps we should focus on long-term sustainability.","plain":"I want to say it differently: perhaps we should focus on long-term sustainability.","note":"Used to clarify your own point or to gently reframe the discussion.","audio":"audio/a177d2db45e0bda5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-question","text":"I'd like to question","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to question","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Politely expressing doubt or asking for justification about a statement or decision.","example":"I'd like to question the effectiveness of the new software implementation.","plain":"I want to ask about the effectiveness of the new software implementation.","note":"More direct than 'raise a concern', but still professional. Used to seek clarification or justification.","audio":"audio/2e7d41fecde5db94.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-raise-a-concern","text":"I'd like to raise a concern","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to raise a concern","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing","travel"],"meaning":"To politely state that you have a problem or worry.","example":"I'd like to raise a concern about the cleanliness of our hotel room.","plain":"I want to mention a problem with the cleanliness of our hotel room.","note":"Formal and diplomatic, used when making a complaint or pointing out an issue.","audio":"audio/64f98a0bf6f58d3a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-raise-a-counter-argument","text":"I'd like to raise a counter-argument","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to raise a counter-argument","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Politely introducing an opposing argument to challenge a previous statement.","example":"I'd like to raise a counter-argument regarding the cost-effectiveness of this proposal.","plain":"I want to present an opposing argument regarding the cost-effectiveness of this proposal.","note":"Formal and analytical. Used to introduce a reasoned objection.","audio":"audio/364bdfab5baf1434.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-raise-a-point-of-clarification","text":"I'd like to raise a point of clarification","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to raise a point of clarification","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Politely asking for more detail or explanation on a specific aspect.","example":"I'd like to raise a point of clarification regarding the budget allocation for marketing.","plain":"I want to ask for clarification regarding the budget allocation for marketing.","note":"Formal and precise. Often used to highlight an ambiguity or potential issue.","audio":"audio/7f532bd2da0599a5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-raise-a-potential-issue","text":"I'd like to raise a potential issue","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to raise a potential issue","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Politely bringing up a possible problem or difficulty that needs consideration.","example":"I'd like to raise a potential issue with the proposed software integration.","plain":"I want to mention a possible problem with the proposed software integration.","note":"Formal and proactive. Used to identify risks or complications early on.","audio":"audio/c6de2fe390d58a89.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-raise-a-question","text":"I'd like to raise a question","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to raise a question","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Politely introducing a query that might challenge an assumption or require clarification.","example":"I'd like to raise a question about the feasibility of implementing this by next quarter.","plain":"I want to ask a question about the feasibility of implementing this by next quarter.","note":"A common and polite way to introduce a query, often implying a hidden concern.","audio":"audio/409a2aab66ea897f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-respectfully-disagree","text":"I'd like to respectfully disagree","kind":"chunk","form":"I'd like to respectfully disagree","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"A very polite and formal way to state your disagreement.","example":"I'd like to respectfully disagree with the conclusion drawn in the report.","plain":"I disagree with the conclusion drawn in the report.","note":"Highly formal and courteous. Emphasizes politeness even when disagreeing strongly.","audio":"audio/7ea9fcbcf85d78bb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-suggest-a-compromise","text":"I'd like to suggest a compromise","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to suggest a compromise","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Proposing a middle ground or a solution that satisfies both sides of an argument.","example":"I'd like to suggest a compromise that addresses both budget and quality concerns.","plain":"I want to propose a middle ground that addresses both budget and quality concerns.","note":"Collaborative and diplomatic. Focuses on finding common ground.","audio":"audio/0f1f8ca9d4e5952e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-suggest-a-different-approach-to","text":"I'd like to suggest a different approach to","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to suggest a different approach to","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Proposing an alternative method or strategy for handling a specific task or problem.","example":"I'd like to suggest a different approach to our customer feedback process.","plain":"I want to propose a different method for our customer feedback process.","note":"Constructive and collaborative. Focuses on improving a specific process.","audio":"audio/f173a76a6387dec8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-suggest-a-different-course","text":"I'd like to suggest a different course","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to suggest a different course","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Proposing an alternative plan or direction for action.","example":"I'd like to suggest a different course of action for resolving this conflict.","plain":"I want to propose a different plan for resolving this conflict.","note":"Formal and constructive. Used to offer a new path forward.","audio":"audio/322e809fec6f6d47.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-suggest-a-different-direction","text":"I'd like to suggest a different direction","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to suggest a different direction","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Proposing an alternative path or overall strategy for a project or initiative.","example":"I'd like to suggest a different direction for our next product development cycle.","plain":"I want to propose a different path for our next product development cycle.","note":"Constructive and strategic. Focuses on changing the overall trajectory.","audio":"audio/5999d0c07e9c7afd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-suggest-a-different-focus","text":"I'd like to suggest a different focus","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to suggest a different focus","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Proposing a shift in attention or emphasis to another aspect of a problem.","example":"I'd like to suggest a different focus for our next team meeting agenda.","plain":"I want to propose a different area of attention for our next team meeting agenda.","note":"Constructive and collaborative. Used to redirect discussion or effort.","audio":"audio/e69a4681ea9e0007.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-suggest-a-different-strategy","text":"I'd like to suggest a different strategy","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to suggest a different strategy","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Proposing an alternative plan or method for achieving a goal.","example":"I'd like to suggest a different strategy for entering the new market.","plain":"I want to propose a different plan for entering the new market.","note":"Constructive and forward-looking. Focuses on finding a more effective plan.","audio":"audio/1e8877939743611a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-suggest-a-modification","text":"I'd like to suggest a modification","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to suggest a modification","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Proposing a change or adjustment to an existing plan or idea.","example":"I'd like to suggest a modification to the proposed project timeline.","plain":"I want to propose a change to the proposed project timeline.","note":"Constructive and collaborative. Focuses on improving an existing idea.","audio":"audio/fe2cec65ee986905.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-suggest-a-revised-plan","text":"I'd like to suggest a revised plan","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to suggest a revised plan","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Proposing an updated or altered version of an existing strategy or proposal.","example":"I'd like to suggest a revised plan that incorporates the feedback we received.","plain":"I want to propose an updated plan that incorporates the feedback we received.","note":"Constructive and collaborative. Focuses on improving an existing plan.","audio":"audio/1c31ae3919b06480.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-suggest-otherwise","text":"I'd like to suggest otherwise","kind":"chunk","form":"I'd like to suggest otherwise","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Politely proposing a different idea or conclusion than what was just stated.","example":"While the report suggests a decline, I'd like to suggest otherwise based on recent data.","plain":"While the report suggests a decline, I want to propose a different conclusion based on recent data.","note":"A concise and polite way to introduce a contrasting viewpoint.","audio":"audio/23daf69a5ef143ca.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-take-issue-with","text":"I'd like to take issue with","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to take issue with","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Politely but firmly disagreeing with a specific point or statement.","example":"I'd like to take issue with the claim that our team lacks motivation.","plain":"I disagree with the claim that our team lacks motivation.","note":"More direct than 'raise a concern', but still professional. Used to dispute a specific point.","audio":"audio/5a6b8e5f66f45b18.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-underscore","text":"I'd like to underscore","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to underscore","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","presenting","persuading"],"meaning":"To emphasize the importance of a particular point.","example":"I'd like to underscore the urgency of completing this task by Friday.","plain":"I want to emphasize the urgency of completing this task by Friday.","note":"A formal way to highlight something important.","audio":"audio/9797ded0b6b2b35f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-underscore-that","text":"I'd like to underscore that","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to underscore that","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Emphasizing the importance of a particular point.","example":"I'd like to underscore that client satisfaction is our top priority.","plain":"I want to emphasize that client satisfaction is our top priority.","note":"A formal way to highlight the significance of something.","audio":"audio/ff77335e8bb27480.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-voice-my-concern","text":"I'd like to voice my concern","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to voice my concern","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Formally expressing a worry or apprehension about a situation or decision.","example":"I'd like to voice my concern about the potential impact on our long-term strategy.","plain":"I want to express my worry about the potential impact on our long-term strategy.","note":"Formal and serious. Used when a concern is significant and needs to be heard.","audio":"audio/11a44a2911ce5ff7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-weigh-in","text":"I'd like to weigh in","kind":"chunk","form":"I'd like to weigh in","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Expressing a desire to contribute one's opinion to a discussion.","example":"If there's a moment, I'd like to weigh in on the marketing strategy.","plain":"If there's a moment, I want to share my opinion on the marketing strategy.","note":"A common, slightly informal way to offer your contribution.","audio":"audio/5d18236283c7d06e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-love-for-you-to","text":"I'd love for you to","kind":"frame","form":"I'd love for you to","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing a strong desire for someone to participate in an activity or event.","example":"I'd love for you to join us for the picnic in the park.","plain":"I really want you to join us for the picnic in the park.","note":"A warm and enthusiastic way to extend an invitation.","audio":"audio/578f31b341ba4da0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-say","text":"I'd say","kind":"frame","form":"I'd say","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","interviews"],"meaning":"A casual and polite way to introduce an opinion or an estimate.","example":"I'd say we're looking at about three more weeks to complete this phase.","plain":"I think we're looking at about three more weeks to complete this phase.","note":"Softens an opinion or estimate, making it sound less assertive.","audio":"audio/bac02e5344712d31.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-suggest-we-look-into","text":"I'd suggest we look into","kind":"frame","form":"I'd suggest we look into","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to propose an investigation or further examination of a particular issue.","example":"I'd suggest we look into alternative software providers before making a final decision.","plain":"I propose we investigate alternative software providers before making a final decision.","note":"A polite and proactive way to suggest a course of action, often for problem-solving.","audio":"audio/aee69a95ff72ef9f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-address-that","text":"I'll address that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll address that","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To deal with a problem or issue directly.","example":"Thank you for pointing out the discrepancy; I'll address that with the team immediately.","plain":"Thank you for pointing out the discrepancy; I will deal with that with the team immediately.","note":"Professional and proactive, indicates a commitment to resolve an issue.","audio":"audio/d5f57ccc8d349289.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-be-available-from","text":"I'll be available from","kind":"frame","form":"I'll be available from","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I will be free to speak or meet starting at a particular time.","example":"I have a meeting until 2 PM, so I'll be available from 2:30 PM onwards.","plain":"I have a meeting until 2 PM, so I will be free to talk from 2:30 PM.","note":"Used to specify your availability for a call or meeting, often in messages.","audio":"audio/045e6d57d2fd9c33.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-be-brief","text":"I'll be brief","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll be brief","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to indicate that you intend to speak for only a short time.","example":"I'll be brief, but I wanted to share a quick update on the project's progress.","plain":"I wanted to share a quick update on the project's progress.","note":"Polite and efficient, often used at the start of a short intervention.","audio":"audio/21292c1aa7dc04b0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-be-sure-to-follow-up","text":"I'll be sure to follow up","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll be sure to follow up","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","calls"],"meaning":"I will definitely contact you again to check on progress or provide more information.","example":"Thanks for the information; I'll be sure to follow up with the relevant department.","plain":"Thanks for the information; I will definitely contact the relevant department.","note":"A strong commitment to ensure a follow-up action is taken, often in a professional context.","audio":"audio/b0cc2089b5415386.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-be-sure-to-pass-that-on","text":"I'll be sure to pass that on","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll be sure to pass that on","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I will definitely relay the information you've given me to the correct person.","example":"Thanks for the feedback; I'll be sure to pass that on to the development team.","plain":"Thanks for the feedback; I will definitely give it to the development team.","note":"A strong assurance that a message or feedback will be delivered to the intended recipient.","audio":"audio/91abde14ef5574d9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-bear-that-in-mind","text":"I'll bear that in mind","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll bear that in mind","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To remember a piece of information or advice for future consideration.","example":"Thank you for the feedback on the presentation style; I'll bear that in mind for next time.","plain":"Thank you for the feedback on the presentation style; I will remember that for next time.","note":"Professional and polite, acknowledges feedback without immediate commitment to action.","audio":"audio/045b3ea38fbd4faf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-bring-something-to-share","text":"I'll bring something to share","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll bring something to share","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Offering to contribute food or drink to a social gathering.","example":"Thanks for inviting me to the potluck, I'll bring something to share.","plain":"I'll bring some food or drink for everyone at the potluck.","note":"A polite and common offer when attending a casual meal or party.","audio":"audio/05b8ed6c949ae645.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-check-my-calendar","text":"I'll check my calendar","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll check my calendar","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Promising to look at your schedule to confirm availability for a proposed plan.","example":"That sounds good; I'll check my calendar and get back to you.","plain":"That sounds good; I'll look at my schedule and get back to you.","note":"Standard response when you need to verify your schedule before committing.","audio":"audio/ce63499ff6644ecd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-circulate-the-action-points","text":"I'll circulate the action points","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll circulate the action points","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Stating an intention to distribute a list of tasks agreed upon during the call.","example":"After this call, I'll circulate the action points to everyone involved.","plain":"After this call, I will send the list of tasks to everyone involved.","note":"A common professional commitment for distributing meeting outcomes.","audio":"audio/0537c30f3b01d669.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-confirm-closer-to-the-date","text":"I'll confirm closer to the date","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll confirm closer to the date","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Promising to finalize attendance or details nearer to the event day.","example":"I'm not sure about my work commitments yet, so I'll confirm closer to the date.","plain":"I'm not sure about my work commitments yet, so I will finalize my attendance nearer the date.","note":"A polite way to express uncertainty while keeping an option open.","audio":"audio/158afaf9a1c73931.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-confirm-our-next-meeting","text":"I'll confirm our next meeting","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll confirm our next meeting","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Stating an intention to verify the details of a future appointment.","example":"Before the end of the day, I'll confirm our next meeting time and agenda.","plain":"Before the end of the day, I will verify the details of our next meeting.","note":"A professional commitment to finalize future arrangements.","audio":"audio/f2e1180b2a950a2a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-confirm-with-you","text":"I'll confirm with you","kind":"frame","form":"I'll confirm with you","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I will verify details or arrangements with you to ensure accuracy.","example":"I'll confirm with you the exact time of the meeting once it's set.","plain":"I will verify the exact meeting time with you once it's decided.","note":"A professional way to promise to verify information with someone directly.","audio":"audio/a80393195e5b69b5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-consider-that","text":"I'll consider that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll consider that","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To think carefully about a suggestion or piece of feedback.","example":"That's an interesting perspective on the project; I'll consider that as we move forward.","plain":"That's an interesting perspective on the project; I will think carefully about that as we move forward.","note":"Polite and open, shows willingness to reflect on input without immediate commitment.","audio":"audio/9ec90d8abb5c1c29.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-definitely-be-there","text":"I'll definitely be there","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll definitely be there","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Giving a strong assurance that you will attend an event or meeting.","example":"Don't worry, I'll definitely be there for your presentation.","plain":"Don't worry, I will certainly attend your presentation.","note":"Conveys commitment and reliability.","audio":"audio/7690b02ad35b96d2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-ensure-that","text":"I'll ensure that","kind":"frame","form":"I'll ensure that","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To take responsibility for making certain that something happens.","example":"I'll ensure that all necessary approvals are obtained before proceeding with the launch.","plain":"I will take responsibility for making certain that all necessary approvals are obtained before proceeding with the launch.","note":"Professional and strong, indicates a high level of commitment.","audio":"audio/d8c7983d5f843fd3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-get-back-to-you-by","text":"I'll get back to you by","kind":"frame","form":"I'll get back to you by","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I will contact you again no later than a specified time or date.","example":"I need to consult with my team, but I'll get back to you by Friday with an answer.","plain":"I need to talk to my team, but I will contact you by Friday with an answer.","note":"A professional way to set an expectation for when a response will be provided.","audio":"audio/cd46bb0b088b2b73.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-get-back-to-you-on-that","text":"I'll get back to you on that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll get back to you on that","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I will provide you with an answer or information regarding that specific point later.","example":"I don't have the exact figures now, but I'll get back to you on that by tomorrow.","plain":"I don't have the exact numbers now, but I will give you that information by tomorrow.","note":"Used to defer a specific question or request, promising a future response.","audio":"audio/b6047531854b53fd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-get-back-to-you-shortly","text":"I'll get back to you shortly","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll get back to you shortly","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I will contact you again very soon, usually within a short period of time.","example":"I need to check my calendar, but I'll get back to you shortly with my availability.","plain":"I need to check my schedule, but I will contact you very soon with my availability.","note":"A common professional phrase promising a quick response, often used in voicemails or emails.","audio":"audio/800a315cfde40073.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-get-right-on-it","text":"I'll get right on it","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll get right on it","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To promise to start working on something immediately.","example":"You're right, that needs fixing; I'll get right on it after this call.","plain":"You're right, that needs fixing; I will start working on it immediately after this call.","note":"Informal but common in professional settings, shows urgency and commitment.","audio":"audio/cf47b76373278ac8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-get-right-on-that","text":"I'll get right on that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll get right on that","level":"C1","situations":["feedback","calls"],"meaning":"I will start working on that task or issue immediately.","example":"Thanks for letting me know; I'll get right on that and send you an update soon.","plain":"Thanks for informing me; I will start working on it immediately and update you.","note":"An informal but common phrase to indicate immediate action will be taken.","audio":"audio/f48ba90284fde5ff.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-get-that-to-you","text":"I'll get that to you","kind":"frame","form":"I'll get that to you","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I will send or deliver the specified item or information to you.","example":"I'll get that to you by the end of the day, along with the other documents.","plain":"I will send that to you by the end of the day, with the other documents.","note":"A common phrase to promise delivery of a document, file, or piece of information.","audio":"audio/51406e2239afe14b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-get-the-next-round","text":"I'll get the next round","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll get the next round","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Offering to pay for the next set of drinks or food for the group.","example":"You got the last one, so I'll get the next round.","plain":"You paid last time, so I will pay for the drinks this time.","note":"Common in casual social settings like bars or cafes, showing generosity.","audio":"audio/c74aa212521858ee.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-get-to-that-in-a-moment","text":"I'll get to that in a moment","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll get to that in a moment","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To indicate that a question will be addressed shortly, but not immediately.","example":"That's a relevant point, and I'll get to that in a moment when we discuss the budget.","plain":"That's a relevant point, and I will discuss that soon when we discuss the budget.","note":"Manages expectations and maintains the flow of the presentation.","audio":"audio/33006e3f976bc25e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-give-it-some-thought","text":"I'll give it some thought","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll give it some thought","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To promise to reflect on an idea or suggestion.","example":"Your suggestion for the new marketing campaign is excellent; I'll give it some thought.","plain":"Your suggestion for the new marketing campaign is excellent; I will reflect on it.","note":"Similar to \"I'll consider that,\" but perhaps slightly more informal.","audio":"audio/a474dc64b99311d2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-give-you-a-call","text":"I'll give you a call","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll give you a call","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Promising to phone someone, often to finalize plans or catch up.","example":"I'll give you a call tomorrow to sort out the details for the weekend.","plain":"I will phone you tomorrow to sort out the details for the weekend.","note":"A common, informal way to arrange future communication.","audio":"audio/1551b503c5b5dc49.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-give-you-a-heads-up","text":"I'll give you a heads-up","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll give you a heads-up","level":"C1","situations":["socialising","calls"],"meaning":"I will provide you with advance notice or a warning about something.","example":"If there are any changes to the schedule, I'll give you a heads-up immediately.","plain":"If the schedule changes, I will inform you immediately.","note":"An informal but common phrase for providing advance notice, often about potential issues.","audio":"audio/f2f98d141027d559.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-have-to-pass","text":"I'll have to pass","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll have to pass","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Politically declining an invitation or opportunity.","example":"That sounds like fun, but I'll have to pass on the hiking trip this weekend.","plain":"That sounds like fun, but I cannot go on the hiking trip this weekend.","note":"A casual but clear way to decline, often used among friends.","audio":"audio/c89865a5952352ee.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-have-to-take-a-rain-check","text":"I'll have to take a rain check","kind":"collocation","form":"I'll have to take a rain check","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Politely declining an invitation but suggesting to do it another time.","example":"I can't make it tonight, but I'll have to take a rain check.","plain":"I can't make it tonight, but let's do it another time.","note":"Very common idiom for postponing. Implies genuine interest in meeting later.","audio":"audio/bf3a14e19f105e97.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-just-touch-on","text":"I'll just touch on","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll just touch on","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To mention a topic briefly without going into great detail.","example":"I'll just touch on the budget constraints, as they are not our main focus today.","plain":"I will briefly mention the budget constraints, as they are not our main focus today.","note":"Useful for acknowledging a point without getting sidetracked.","audio":"audio/ea251252a1163847.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-keep-an-eye-out","text":"I'll keep an eye out","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll keep an eye out","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I will watch or look carefully for something you mentioned or are expecting.","example":"Thanks for sending the document; I'll keep an eye out for it in my inbox.","plain":"Thanks for sending the document; I will watch for it in my inbox.","note":"A common phrase to confirm you will be vigilant for an incoming message or item.","audio":"audio/e3ad469c8222067f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-leave-a-message","text":"I'll leave a message","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll leave a message","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I will record a voicemail or write a note for the person I am trying to reach.","example":"Since you're not available, I'll leave a message explaining my query.","plain":"As you're busy, I will record a voicemail explaining my question.","note":"Used when someone is unavailable, indicating you will provide information for them to retrieve.","audio":"audio/418d515e4b628cdd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-leave-it-there","text":"I'll leave it there","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll leave it there","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I will stop discussing this topic or end my contribution to the conversation.","example":"I think I've made my point, so I'll leave it there for now.","plain":"I believe I've explained my point, so I'll stop talking about it for now.","note":"Used to signal that you've finished your contribution to a topic, often after making a strong point.","audio":"audio/d348dbec9dc411db.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-let-you-go","text":"I'll let you go","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll let you go","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","calls"],"meaning":"I am ending the call, giving you permission to disconnect.","example":"I know you're busy, so I'll let you go now. Thanks for your time.","plain":"I know you're busy, so I'm ending the call. Thank you for your time.","note":"A polite way to end a call, implying you are considerate of the other person's time.","audio":"audio/cd8e4d0f91bc462e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-look-into-it","text":"I'll look into it","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll look into it","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I will investigate or research the matter you've raised.","example":"That's a valid concern; I'll look into it and get back to you with a solution.","plain":"That's a valid concern; I will investigate it and contact you with a solution.","note":"A common phrase to promise investigation or research into a problem or question.","audio":"audio/0a02f9e7008f465f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-make-a-note","text":"I'll make a note","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll make a note","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To write down information to remember it for future reference or action.","example":"That's an excellent suggestion for the next meeting agenda; I'll make a note of it.","plain":"That's an excellent suggestion for the next meeting agenda; I will write it down.","note":"Shows attentiveness and commitment to remembering or acting on feedback.","audio":"audio/9ee7e51f7b586a78.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-make-a-point-of","text":"I'll make a point of","kind":"frame","form":"I'll make a point of","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To ensure that one does something specific and deliberately.","example":"I'll make a point of checking in with the team more regularly on progress.","plain":"I will ensure that I check in with the team more regularly on progress.","note":"Shows strong commitment to a specific action, often in response to feedback.","audio":"audio/6a58e6ac2e63d2ce.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-make-an-effort-to","text":"I'll make an effort to","kind":"frame","form":"I'll make an effort to","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To try hard to do something.","example":"I'll make an effort to be more proactive in sharing updates with the team.","plain":"I will try hard to be more proactive in sharing updates with the team.","note":"Shows willingness to improve, often in response to constructive feedback.","audio":"audio/a10b7c925d8d5003.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-make-sure-that","text":"I'll make sure that","kind":"frame","form":"I'll make sure that","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To promise to take responsibility for ensuring something happens.","example":"I'll make sure that the new guidelines are communicated to the entire team.","plain":"I promise to take responsibility for ensuring the new guidelines are communicated to the entire team.","note":"Strong commitment, indicates taking ownership of an action.","audio":"audio/a5a89141743cc28f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-make-the-arrangements","text":"I'll make the arrangements","kind":"collocation","form":"I'll make the arrangements","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Offering to organize the details for an event or meeting.","example":"Don't worry about the restaurant booking, I'll make the arrangements.","plain":"I will book the restaurant, you don't need to worry about it.","note":"A helpful offer when hosting or taking initiative in planning a social gathering.","audio":"audio/7525145d435d79a3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-pass-on-your-message","text":"I'll pass on your message","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll pass on your message","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I will relay the information you've given me to the intended recipient.","example":"He's in a meeting, but I'll pass on your message as soon as he's free.","plain":"He's in a meeting, but I will give him your message when he's available.","note":"Used by an intermediary to assure the caller that their information will be delivered.","audio":"audio/43ac1d7b693c3ae3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-reflect-on-that","text":"I'll reflect on that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll reflect on that","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Promises to think deeply about the feedback received and its implications.","example":"I'll reflect on that feedback and consider how to apply it to my future work.","plain":"I will think deeply about that feedback and consider how to apply it to my future work.","note":"A thoughtful and mature response to feedback, showing a commitment to self-improvement.","audio":"audio/bb598b720acfc2ab.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-send-a-follow-up-email","text":"I'll send a follow-up email","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll send a follow-up email","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Stating an intention to send an email summarizing or continuing the discussion.","example":"I'll send a follow-up email with all the agreed-upon action items.","plain":"I will send an email later with all the agreed-upon action items.","note":"A common professional commitment for post-call communication.","audio":"audio/d83c536790f05f92.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-send-a-quick-note","text":"I'll send a quick note","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll send a quick note","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Stating an intention to send a brief written message.","example":"I'll send a quick note to everyone summarizing our decisions.","plain":"I will send a short message to everyone summarizing our decisions.","note":"A less formal alternative to \"send an email,\" implying brevity.","audio":"audio/559833ee4e4e9c1e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-send-you-the-materials","text":"I'll send you the materials","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll send you the materials","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Stating an intention to transmit relevant documents or resources.","example":"As discussed, I'll send you the materials for the upcoming workshop.","plain":"As discussed, I will send you the documents for the upcoming workshop.","note":"A common commitment to share resources after a discussion.","audio":"audio/a04fb3cdd0c72291.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-send-you-the-report","text":"I'll send you the report","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll send you the report","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Stating an intention to transmit a formal written account of findings.","example":"Once it's finalized, I'll send you the report on the quarterly sales figures.","plain":"Once it's finalized, I will transmit the sales figures report to you.","note":"A common commitment to share a formal report.","audio":"audio/d1d6064c180bbca8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-switch-to-a-different-network","text":"I'll switch to a different network","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll switch to a different network","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Stating an intention to change internet connections to improve stability.","example":"This Wi-Fi isn't working; I'll switch to a different network to improve the call.","plain":"This Wi-Fi is bad; I'll change internet connections to make the call better.","note":"A proactive step to resolve connectivity problems.","audio":"audio/2fafc4ff09efb23f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-take-action-on","text":"I'll take action on","kind":"frame","form":"I'll take action on","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To begin to do something to deal with a problem or situation.","example":"Thank you for highlighting the issue; I'll take action on it first thing tomorrow.","plain":"Thank you for highlighting the issue; I will begin to do something to deal with it first thing tomorrow.","note":"Professional and proactive, indicates a commitment to resolve an issue.","audio":"audio/160b0a1a4b25a803.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-take-responsibility-for","text":"I'll take responsibility for","kind":"frame","form":"I'll take responsibility for","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To accept accountability for an action or outcome.","example":"I'll take responsibility for the oversight in the budget calculation and correct it.","plain":"I will accept accountability for the oversight in the budget calculation and correct it.","note":"Professional and mature, shows ownership of mistakes or tasks.","audio":"audio/db16a80c21c708a6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-take-that-on-board","text":"I'll take that on board","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll take that on board","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To consider and incorporate advice or feedback into one's future actions.","example":"I'll take that on board and consider it for future projects and planning.","plain":"I will consider that advice and incorporate it into future projects and planning.","note":"Common in British English. Shows willingness to learn and adapt. Professional.","audio":"audio/3eab99d41e0c3ea8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-try-calling-from-my-phone","text":"I'll try calling from my phone","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll try calling from my phone","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Suggesting to switch devices for the call, often due to computer issues.","example":"My laptop connection is poor, so I'll try calling from my phone instead.","plain":"My laptop's internet is bad, so I'll use my phone to call.","note":"A practical solution when one device has better connectivity.","audio":"audio/b27fdd5c156b2be6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-walk-you-through","text":"I'll walk you through","kind":"frame","form":"I'll walk you through","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To guide someone step-by-step through a process or explanation.","example":"I'll walk you through the new software interface in the next section.","plain":"I will guide you through the new software interface in the next section.","note":"Implies a detailed, guided explanation.","audio":"audio/d0d953fe9250e0ce.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-a-bit-confused-about","text":"I'm a bit confused about","kind":"frame","form":"I'm a bit confused about","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Politely stating that you are unclear or uncertain about something.","example":"I'm a bit confused about the train schedule; are there any changes?","plain":"I'm unclear about the train schedule; are there any changes?","note":"A polite way to express mild confusion and ask for clarification.","audio":"audio/d33dcc893de526a8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-a-bit-short-on-time","text":"I'm a bit short on time","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm a bit short on time","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To politely indicate that you have limited time available.","example":"I'm a bit short on time, so I need to find the quickest route to the station.","plain":"I don't have much time.","note":"A polite way to explain urgency or inability to linger.","audio":"audio/37e4c484273c852e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-a-bit-taken-aback","text":"I'm a bit taken aback","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm a bit taken aback","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses mild surprise or shock, often implying a slight feeling of being unsettled or confused.","example":"I'm a bit taken aback by their sudden decision to change the entire strategy.","plain":"I'm slightly surprised by their sudden decision to change the entire strategy.","note":"Suggests a reaction of unexpectedness that leaves one momentarily speechless or disoriented.","audio":"audio/d9ed782a32432a26.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-a-bit-unsure","text":"I'm a bit unsure","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm a bit unsure","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling","travel"],"meaning":"Expresses a degree of uncertainty or hesitation about something.","example":"I'm a bit unsure about the best way to proceed with this delicate matter.","plain":"I'm uncertain about the best way to proceed with this delicate matter.","note":"Informal, a softer way to express uncertainty.","audio":"audio/09b1754684068aff.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-a-little-concerned","text":"I'm a little concerned","kind":"collocation","form":"I'm a little concerned","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses mild worry or apprehension about a situation or potential outcome.","example":"I'm a little concerned about the new changes to the company policy.","plain":"I'm slightly worried about the new changes to the company policy.","note":"A polite and common way to voice a moderate level of worry or unease.","audio":"audio/8f26b59b999ba0c4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-a-little-concerned-about","text":"I'm a little concerned about","kind":"frame","form":"I'm a little concerned about","level":"C1","situations":["feedback","reacting","problem-solving"],"meaning":"A gentle way to express worry or unease about a specific situation.","example":"I'm a little concerned about the lack of progress on the new feature this week.","plain":"I'm slightly worried about the lack of progress on the new feature this week.","note":"A common softener for introducing negative feedback or a problem. Empathetic tone.","audio":"audio/1171ba51e1883c99.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-a-little-disappointed","text":"I'm a little disappointed","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm a little disappointed","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses mild unhappiness or dissatisfaction.","example":"I'm a little disappointed that the concert was postponed until next year.","plain":"I'm slightly unhappy that the concert was postponed until next year.","note":"A gentle way to express dissatisfaction without being overly negative.","audio":"audio/8ceaf955221ba216.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-a-little-hesitant","text":"I'm a little hesitant","kind":"frame","form":"I'm a little hesitant","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses reluctance or caution about a proposal, indicating a lack of full support.","example":"I'm a little hesitant to commit to such a large investment without more data.","plain":"I'm reluctant to commit to such a large investment without more data.","note":"Softens disagreement by focusing on personal feelings of caution rather than direct opposition.","audio":"audio/1989ce336a80c079.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-absolutely-delighted","text":"I'm absolutely delighted","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm absolutely delighted","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses extreme happiness and pleasure.","example":"I'm absolutely delighted that you'll be joining our team next month.","plain":"I'm extremely happy that you'll be joining our team next month.","note":"A strong expression of joy, often used for good news or positive events.","audio":"audio/adf27f972c38b593.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-absolutely-delighted-at-the-prospect","text":"I'm absolutely delighted at the prospect","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm absolutely delighted at the prospect","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses extreme happiness and excitement about a future possibility.","example":"I'm absolutely delighted at the prospect of working on this groundbreaking research.","plain":"I'm extremely happy about the possibility of working on this groundbreaking research.","note":"Conveys strong enthusiasm for a future opportunity or event.","audio":"audio/f6770b0d7e827fc5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-absolutely-delighted-that","text":"I'm absolutely delighted that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm absolutely delighted that","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses extreme happiness and pleasure about a specific fact or event.","example":"I'm absolutely delighted that you decided to join us for the celebration.","plain":"I'm extremely happy that you decided to join us for the celebration.","note":"A very enthusiastic way to express joy about a particular situation.","audio":"audio/c31d305b1b5d7655.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-absolutely-delighted-to","text":"I'm absolutely delighted to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm absolutely delighted to","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses extreme happiness and pleasure about doing something.","example":"I'm absolutely delighted to accept your invitation to the conference next month.","plain":"I'm extremely happy to accept your invitation to the conference next month.","note":"A very enthusiastic way to express willingness or pleasure in an action.","audio":"audio/42480a9529b8c9bd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-absolutely-delighted-to-announce","text":"I'm absolutely delighted to announce","kind":"frame","form":"I'm absolutely delighted to announce","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses extreme happiness and pleasure before making a public declaration.","example":"I'm absolutely delighted to announce that our new product line is now available.","plain":"I'm extremely happy to announce that our new product line is now available.","note":"A formal and enthusiastic way to deliver good news, often in presentations.","audio":"audio/2f9e0269544cc0ad.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-absolutely-delighted-with-the-outcome","text":"I'm absolutely delighted with the outcome","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm absolutely delighted with the outcome","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses extreme happiness and satisfaction with a result.","example":"After all the hard work, I'm absolutely delighted with the outcome of the project.","plain":"After all the hard work, I'm extremely happy with the result of the project.","note":"A strong expression of joy and satisfaction for a successful conclusion.","audio":"audio/761cc39f9d51b4d0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-absolutely-thrilled-about","text":"I'm absolutely thrilled about","kind":"frame","form":"I'm absolutely thrilled about","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses intense happiness and excitement regarding a specific topic or event.","example":"I'm absolutely thrilled about the upcoming company retreat to the mountains.","plain":"I'm extremely happy about the upcoming company retreat to the mountains.","note":"Conveys strong enthusiasm for a future event or prospect.","audio":"audio/122884f569730a48.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-absolutely-thrilled-for-you","text":"I'm absolutely thrilled for you","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm absolutely thrilled for you","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses intense happiness and excitement for someone else's good news.","example":"You got the scholarship? I'm absolutely thrilled for you, that's incredible!","plain":"You got the scholarship? I'm extremely happy for you, that's incredible!","note":"A very enthusiastic way to share in someone else's joy.","audio":"audio/4151f90e2da85a7c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-absolutely-thrilled-to-be","text":"I'm absolutely thrilled to be","kind":"frame","form":"I'm absolutely thrilled to be","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses extreme happiness and excitement about being in a certain state or role.","example":"I'm absolutely thrilled to be part of such a dedicated and innovative team.","plain":"I'm extremely happy to be part of such a dedicated and innovative team.","note":"Conveys intense joy about one's current position or involvement.","audio":"audio/e384e6cc6bb405b4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-absolutely-thrilled-to-bits","text":"I'm absolutely thrilled to bits","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm absolutely thrilled to bits","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses extreme happiness and excitement, often informally.","example":"My sister just had a baby, and I'm absolutely thrilled to bits for her!","plain":"My sister just had a baby, and I'm extremely happy for her!","note":"An informal but strong expression of joy, common in British English.","audio":"audio/692d4f6e67f25fe9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-absolutely-thrilled-with","text":"I'm absolutely thrilled with","kind":"frame","form":"I'm absolutely thrilled with","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses extreme excitement and happiness about something specific.","example":"I'm absolutely thrilled with the results of the customer satisfaction survey.","plain":"I'm extremely happy with the results of the customer satisfaction survey.","note":"Conveys intense joy and satisfaction regarding a particular outcome.","audio":"audio/844fc04b1a8fde85.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-i-cant","text":"I'm afraid I can't","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I can't","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite and common way to decline an invitation or request.","example":"I'm afraid I can't make it to the party on Saturday.","plain":"Unfortunately, I cannot come to the party on Saturday.","note":"A standard softener for delivering bad news or a refusal.","audio":"audio/1e3dff8092773c3d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-i-cant-accept-that","text":"I'm afraid I can't accept that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I can't accept that","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Politely but firmly refusing an offer or proposed solution.","example":"A voucher for future travel isn't suitable; I'm afraid I can't accept that.","plain":"A voucher for future travel isn't suitable; I cannot accept that.","note":"A polite but firm refusal, often used in negotiations or complaints.","audio":"audio/9c5693b8ffc7fb3c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-i-cant-agree-to-that","text":"I'm afraid I can't agree to that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I can't agree to that","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"A polite but firm way to refuse a proposal or condition.","example":"I'm afraid I can't agree to that short a turnaround time for development.","plain":"I cannot agree to that short a turnaround time for development.","note":"Softens a direct refusal, maintaining a polite tone.","audio":"audio/99824e3d346af6ec.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-i-cant-help-you","text":"I'm afraid I can't help you","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I can't help you","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite and regretful way to say you are unable to assist someone.","example":"I'm looking for Mr. Smith. I'm afraid I can't help you, he's not in today.","plain":"I'm looking for Mr. Smith. I can't help you, he's not in today.","note":"A polite way to decline a request or indicate inability to assist.","audio":"audio/c04944b0aca0158d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-i-cant-help-you-there","text":"I'm afraid I can't help you there","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I can't help you there","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Politely stating that you are unable to provide assistance.","example":"I'm afraid I can't help you there; I'm also a tourist.","plain":"I cannot help you with that; I'm also a tourist.","note":"A polite way to decline a request for help.","audio":"audio/97fb972ff6bbe18c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-i-dont-have","text":"I'm afraid I don't have","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I don't have","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you lack something requested or needed.","example":"I'm afraid I don't have any local currency on me right now, only euros.","plain":"I don't have any local currency.","note":"Softens a negative response.","audio":"audio/def7ad1e1e63b18c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-i-dont-share","text":"I'm afraid I don't share","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I don't share","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Politely expresses a different opinion or lack of agreement with a viewpoint.","example":"I'm afraid I don't share your optimism about the project's immediate success.","plain":"I don't have the same optimism about the project's immediate success.","note":"A polite and slightly formal way to express a differing opinion or feeling.","audio":"audio/a5b84ab83f8c9143.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-i-have-to-ask","text":"I'm afraid I have to ask","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I have to ask","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Politely introducing a question that might be sensitive or inconvenient.","example":"I'm afraid I have to ask if there's any chance of a room upgrade.","plain":"I need to ask if there's any chance of a room upgrade.","note":"Softens a request or question, showing awareness it might be difficult.","audio":"audio/cf4fac11132cbe18.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-i-have-to-cancel","text":"I'm afraid I have to cancel","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I have to cancel","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite and apologetic way to state that you must withdraw a booking or arrangement.","example":"I'm afraid I have to cancel my hotel booking for next week due to a change in plans.","plain":"I must cancel my hotel booking.","note":"\"I'm afraid\" softens the negative news.","audio":"audio/8a5ff37389901c8b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-i-have-to-insist","text":"I'm afraid I have to insist","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I have to insist","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Firmly stating that you require something to happen, despite resistance.","example":"I'm afraid I have to insist on a full refund for the cancelled tour.","plain":"I must have a full refund for the cancelled tour.","note":"Used when you need to be firm but still want to sound polite.","audio":"audio/163c4aabe8778886.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-i-must","text":"I'm afraid I must","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I must","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite and slightly more formal way to express that you must do something.","example":"I'm afraid I must leave now to catch my train, but it was lovely chatting.","plain":"I must leave now to catch my train, but it was lovely chatting.","note":"A polite and slightly formal way to excuse oneself from a conversation.","audio":"audio/0ecbbd691d3be180.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-im-double-booked","text":"I'm afraid I'm double-booked","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I'm double-booked","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite way to explain that one has two conflicting appointments.","example":"I'm afraid I'm double-booked that evening, so I can't make it.","plain":"Unfortunately, I have two appointments at the same time that evening, so I can't come.","note":"A polite and common way to decline an invitation due to a scheduling conflict.","audio":"audio/a875ad42ca76bd8e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-im-unable-to-assist","text":"I'm afraid I'm unable to assist","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I'm unable to assist","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A formal and polite way to state that you cannot provide the requested help.","example":"I'm afraid I'm unable to assist with that specific request, as it's outside my department.","plain":"I cannot help with that request.","note":"More formal than \"I can't help\". Used in customer service.","audio":"audio/ce703d7db7081caf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-advised","text":"I'm afraid I've been advised","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I've been advised","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to introduce advice or instructions received from another source.","example":"I'm afraid I've been advised to check in at least three hours before my international flight.","plain":"I was told to check in three hours before my flight.","note":"Softens the delivery of a rule or recommendation.","audio":"audio/e12796db9082ffc7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-charged","text":"I'm afraid I've been charged","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I've been charged","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you believe an incorrect amount has been billed to you.","example":"I'm afraid I've been charged for an extra night that I didn't stay here.","plain":"I was charged for an extra night.","note":"Softens a complaint about billing errors.","audio":"audio/2dba281f2c7d1062.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-charged-twice","text":"I'm afraid I've been charged twice","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I've been charged twice","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you believe you have been billed for the same item twice.","example":"I'm afraid I've been charged twice for the same meal at the hotel restaurant.","plain":"I was charged two times for the same meal.","note":"Softens a complaint about a billing error.","audio":"audio/c3d894ccfa7e4a7d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-cut-off","text":"I'm afraid I've been cut off","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I've been cut off","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that a phone call was disconnected unexpectedly.","example":"I'm afraid I've been cut off from our previous conversation; could you repeat that?","plain":"Our phone call was disconnected.","note":"Softens the interruption of a phone call.","audio":"audio/e5c36e3eaf82618a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-delayed","text":"I'm afraid I've been delayed","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I've been delayed","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that your journey or arrival has been held up.","example":"I'm afraid I've been delayed by traffic, so I'll arrive about 20 minutes late.","plain":"I was delayed by traffic.","note":"Softens the news of a delay.","audio":"audio/e46127ddfef6f22b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-expecting","text":"I'm afraid I've been expecting","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I've been expecting","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that something you anticipated has not occurred.","example":"I'm afraid I've been expecting a wake-up call at 6 AM, but it didn't happen.","plain":"I expected a wake-up call at 6 AM.","note":"Softens a complaint about a missed service.","audio":"audio/cfc934e97b1498b3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-given","text":"I'm afraid I've been given","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I've been given","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you received something incorrect or unsuitable.","example":"I'm afraid I've been given the wrong room key; this one doesn't work.","plain":"I received the wrong room key.","note":"Softens a complaint about receiving an incorrect item.","audio":"audio/cc3a3cdb68145a66.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-given-a-room","text":"I'm afraid I've been given a room","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I've been given a room","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to introduce a complaint about the assigned accommodation.","example":"I'm afraid I've been given a room that doesn't match my booking description.","plain":"The room I received does not match my booking.","note":"Softens a complaint about room allocation.","audio":"audio/c442af036e863203.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-given-incorrect-information","text":"I'm afraid I've been given incorrect information","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I've been given incorrect information","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that the details you received were wrong.","example":"I'm afraid I've been given incorrect information about the bus schedule to the airport.","plain":"I received wrong information about the bus schedule.","note":"Softens a complaint about misinformation.","audio":"audio/7dee5311247d4c4b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-hoping-to","text":"I'm afraid I've been hoping to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I've been hoping to","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to express a desire that has not been met or is now unlikely.","example":"I'm afraid I've been hoping to visit the museum today, but it seems to be closed.","plain":"I wanted to visit the museum today.","note":"Softens the expression of disappointment.","audio":"audio/ca55fbd246d79597.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-informed","text":"I'm afraid I've been informed","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I've been informed","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to introduce information that may be unwelcome or contradictory.","example":"I'm afraid I've been informed that my flight is cancelled due to a strike.","plain":"I was told my flight is cancelled.","note":"Softens the delivery of bad news, often from a third party.","audio":"audio/4b391151b7a7e9da.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-looking-for","text":"I'm afraid I've been looking for","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I've been looking for","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to express that you have been searching for something without success.","example":"I'm afraid I've been looking for the bus stop for ages; could you point it out?","plain":"I have been looking for the bus stop for a long time.","note":"Softens a request for help after a long search.","audio":"audio/53e46c9a831f1207.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-misinformed","text":"I'm afraid I've been misinformed","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I've been misinformed","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you were given wrong information.","example":"I'm afraid I've been misinformed about the hotel's pet policy; I was told dogs were allowed.","plain":"I was given wrong information about the pet policy.","note":"More concise than \"given incorrect information\". Softens a complaint.","audio":"audio/86402d778f3a30d5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-overcharged","text":"I'm afraid I've been overcharged","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I've been overcharged","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you believe you have been billed too much.","example":"I'm afraid I've been overcharged for the taxi fare from the airport to the hotel.","plain":"I was charged too much for the taxi.","note":"Softens a complaint about an excessive charge.","audio":"audio/639ad8a928e2b546.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-put-on-hold","text":"I'm afraid I've been put on hold","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I've been put on hold","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to express frustration about waiting on the phone.","example":"I'm afraid I've been put on hold for twenty minutes and still haven't spoken to anyone.","plain":"I have been waiting on hold for twenty minutes.","note":"Softens a complaint about poor phone service.","audio":"audio/f3c35fd3255fa04e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-told","text":"I'm afraid I've been told","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I've been told","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to introduce information that contradicts what someone else has said.","example":"I'm afraid I've been told that breakfast is included, but your colleague says it's not.","plain":"I was told breakfast is included.","note":"Use when there's conflicting information, to gently challenge.","audio":"audio/25b2621c366ca273.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-trying-to","text":"I'm afraid I've been trying to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I've been trying to","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to express frustration about an ongoing, unsuccessful attempt.","example":"I'm afraid I've been trying to call room service for ten minutes with no answer.","plain":"I have been trying to call room service for ten minutes.","note":"Softens a complaint about lack of service.","audio":"audio/ceac4afc84ee004c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-unable-to","text":"I'm afraid I've been unable to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I've been unable to","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you have not succeeded in doing something.","example":"I'm afraid I've been unable to connect to the hotel Wi-Fi despite several attempts.","plain":"I could not connect to the hotel Wi-Fi.","note":"Softens the admission of failure.","audio":"audio/6502bf3b3d154154.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-unable-to-access","text":"I'm afraid I've been unable to access","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I've been unable to access","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you cannot gain entry or use something.","example":"I'm afraid I've been unable to access the hotel gym with my room key card.","plain":"I could not get into the hotel gym.","note":"Softens a complaint about restricted access.","audio":"audio/dbccb7ad76d65f2b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-unable-to-adjust","text":"I'm afraid I've been unable to adjust","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I've been unable to adjust","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you cannot change a setting or position.","example":"I'm afraid I've been unable to adjust the temperature in my room; it's too cold.","plain":"I could not change the room temperature.","note":"Softens a complaint about climate control issues.","audio":"audio/2c413facd3b8aaba.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-unable-to-book","text":"I'm afraid I've been unable to book","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I've been unable to book","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you could not reserve something.","example":"I'm afraid I've been unable to book a table at the restaurant you recommended; it's full.","plain":"I could not book a table at the restaurant.","note":"Softens the news of an unsuccessful booking attempt.","audio":"audio/a54715549943d3e3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-unable-to-charge","text":"I'm afraid I've been unable to charge","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I've been unable to charge","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you cannot power up an electronic device.","example":"I'm afraid I've been unable to charge my laptop as the power outlet isn't working.","plain":"I could not charge my laptop.","note":"Softens a complaint about a non-functional power outlet.","audio":"audio/43cf66a4bd71be4b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-unable-to-check-in","text":"I'm afraid I've been unable to check in","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I've been unable to check in","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you could not complete the check-in process.","example":"I'm afraid I've been unable to check in online for my flight; there's an error.","plain":"I could not check in online for my flight.","note":"Softens a complaint about check-in issues.","audio":"audio/28c459e19abeb171.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-unable-to-close","text":"I'm afraid I've been unable to close","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I've been unable to close","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you cannot shut something.","example":"I'm afraid I've been unable to close the window properly, and it's quite noisy outside.","plain":"I could not close the window.","note":"Softens a complaint about a faulty window.","audio":"audio/8f7b4495b4a8e1ec.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-unable-to-communicate","text":"I'm afraid I've been unable to communicate","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I've been unable to communicate","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you are having difficulty conveying your message.","example":"I'm afraid I've been unable to communicate effectively with the taxi driver due to the language barrier.","plain":"I could not communicate with the taxi driver.","note":"Softens a statement about language difficulties.","audio":"audio/fec9a4d750d282c4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-unable-to-confirm","text":"I'm afraid I've been unable to confirm","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I've been unable to confirm","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you could not verify something.","example":"I'm afraid I've been unable to confirm my flight details online; the website is down.","plain":"I could not confirm my flight details.","note":"Softens the news of a failed confirmation attempt.","audio":"audio/d72286fbc9c05a60.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-unable-to-connect","text":"I'm afraid I've been unable to connect","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I've been unable to connect","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you cannot establish a link, often electronically.","example":"I'm afraid I've been unable to connect to the internet since I arrived at the hotel.","plain":"I could not connect to the internet.","note":"Softens a complaint about connectivity issues.","audio":"audio/f929ede4e67820a6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-unable-to-find","text":"I'm afraid I've been unable to find","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I've been unable to find","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you have searched for something without success.","example":"I'm afraid I've been unable to find the departure gate for my flight; it's not listed.","plain":"I could not find my flight's departure gate.","note":"Softens a request for help after a search.","audio":"audio/391e2b847fa835e3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-unable-to-find-anyone","text":"I'm afraid I've been unable to find anyone","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I've been unable to find anyone","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you have searched for staff without success.","example":"I'm afraid I've been unable to find anyone at the reception desk for the last ten minutes.","plain":"I could not find anyone at reception.","note":"Softens a complaint about absent staff.","audio":"audio/a60ee602cc978a0a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-unable-to-get-through","text":"I'm afraid I've been unable to get through","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I've been unable to get through","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you could not connect by phone or other means.","example":"I'm afraid I've been unable to get through to the airline's customer service line all morning.","plain":"I could not reach the airline's customer service.","note":"Softens a complaint about difficulty contacting someone.","audio":"audio/406443fb95717927.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-unable-to-open","text":"I'm afraid I've been unable to open","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I've been unable to open","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you cannot unseal or unlock something.","example":"I'm afraid I've been unable to open the safe in my room; it seems to be jammed.","plain":"I could not open the safe.","note":"Softens a complaint about a jammed or locked item.","audio":"audio/be8ec8a5f16b3235.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-unable-to-retrieve","text":"I'm afraid I've been unable to retrieve","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I've been unable to retrieve","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you could not get something back.","example":"I'm afraid I've been unable to retrieve my luggage from the carousel; it's not there.","plain":"I could not get my luggage from the carousel.","note":"Softens a complaint about missing luggage.","audio":"audio/63ce3aef0f2ddfb9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-unable-to-understand","text":"I'm afraid I've been unable to understand","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I've been unable to understand","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you could not comprehend something.","example":"I'm afraid I've been unable to understand the instructions for the self-service kiosk.","plain":"I could not understand the kiosk instructions.","note":"Softens an admission of confusion.","audio":"audio/4ab04b2b63793291.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-unable-to-use","text":"I'm afraid I've been unable to use","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I've been unable to use","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that something is not functioning or available for your use.","example":"I'm afraid I've been unable to use the shower in my room; there's no hot water.","plain":"I could not use the shower because there's no hot water.","note":"Softens a complaint about a non-functional amenity.","audio":"audio/0f08a6d660c38708.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-been-waiting","text":"I'm afraid I've been waiting","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I've been waiting","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to express dissatisfaction about a long delay.","example":"I'm afraid I've been waiting for over an hour for my luggage to arrive.","plain":"I have waited for over an hour for my luggage.","note":"Softens a complaint about a delay.","audio":"audio/0a12f275d2fa05db.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-forgotten","text":"I'm afraid I've forgotten","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I've forgotten","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite and apologetic way to state that you cannot recall something.","example":"I'm afraid I've forgotten my room number; could you please look it up for me?","plain":"I forgot my room number.","note":"Softens the admission of forgetting.","audio":"audio/5b148c4a3fe5c0fe.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-left","text":"I'm afraid I've left","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I've left","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite and apologetic way to state that you have accidentally left something behind.","example":"I'm afraid I've left my passport in the taxi; do you have a lost and found?","plain":"I left my passport in the taxi.","note":"Softens the bad news of leaving something behind.","audio":"audio/681f7bde12a7a85c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-lost","text":"I'm afraid I've lost","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I've lost","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite and apologetic way to state that you cannot find something important.","example":"I'm afraid I've lost my boarding pass, and I can't find it anywhere.","plain":"I lost my boarding pass.","note":"\"I'm afraid\" softens the bad news. Use for lost items.","audio":"audio/1440b83cb7addf22.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-lost-my-way","text":"I'm afraid I've lost my way","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I've lost my way","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you are disoriented and do not know where you are going.","example":"I'm afraid I've lost my way to the museum; could you help me with directions?","plain":"I am lost and don't know how to get to the museum.","note":"More formal than \"I'm a bit lost\".","audio":"audio/7108673ea4a6ee86.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-made-a-mistake","text":"I'm afraid I've made a mistake","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I've made a mistake","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite and apologetic way to admit an error.","example":"I'm afraid I've made a mistake with my booking dates; can they be changed?","plain":"I made a mistake with my booking dates.","note":"Softens the admission of an error.","audio":"audio/4155865c01d3493d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-missed","text":"I'm afraid I've missed","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I've missed","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite and apologetic way to state that you failed to catch a transport or attend an event.","example":"I'm afraid I've missed my connecting flight due to the delay of the first leg.","plain":"I missed my connecting flight.","note":"Softens the bad news of missing something important.","audio":"audio/31175132b4fc6d0d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-ive-run-out-of","text":"I'm afraid I've run out of","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I've run out of","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you no longer have a supply of something.","example":"I'm afraid I've run out of local currency; is there an ATM nearby?","plain":"I have no more local currency.","note":"Softens the statement of lacking something.","audio":"audio/646d9347b1d8c42c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-that-wont-work","text":"I'm afraid that won't work","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid that won't work","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite but firm way to reject a suggestion or solution.","example":"I'm afraid that won't work for me, as I have another appointment at that time.","plain":"That suggestion is not acceptable for me.","note":"Softens a refusal. Use when a proposed solution is unsuitable.","audio":"audio/6d06c117c2b3441a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-thats-not-possible","text":"I'm afraid that's not possible","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid that's not possible","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"A polite but definitive way to state that something cannot be done.","example":"I'm afraid that's not possible with our current resource constraints.","plain":"That is not possible with our current resource constraints.","note":"A firm but polite refusal, often citing practical limitations.","audio":"audio/8d5fcdec62ff98f2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-thats-our-final-offer","text":"I'm afraid that's our final offer","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid that's our final offer","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"A polite but firm statement indicating that no further concessions will be made.","example":"I'm afraid that's our final offer on the pricing for this volume.","plain":"That is our final offer on the pricing for this volume.","note":"A strong statement used to signal the end of negotiation on a specific point.","audio":"audio/92dcf0680adac2d9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-theres-been-a-misunderstanding","text":"I'm afraid there's been a misunderstanding","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid there's been a misunderstanding","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Politely indicating that there is a mistake or incorrect information.","example":"I'm afraid there's been a misunderstanding regarding my booking details.","plain":"I think there's a mistake with my booking details.","note":"Used to correct an error or clarify a situation gently.","audio":"audio/160e1e114b3f3d65.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-we-cant-go-lower-than","text":"I'm afraid we can't go lower than","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid we can't go lower than","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"A polite but firm way to state a minimum acceptable price or condition.","example":"I'm afraid we can't go lower than this price point for the premium package.","plain":"We cannot accept a price lower than this for the premium package.","note":"Sets a clear boundary on price or other quantifiable terms.","audio":"audio/a7664dc51c88477e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-we-cant-make-an-exception","text":"I'm afraid we can't make an exception","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid we can't make an exception","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"A polite but firm refusal to deviate from standard rules or policies.","example":"I'm afraid we can't make an exception to our standard payment terms.","plain":"We cannot deviate from our standard payment terms.","note":"Used to uphold established rules, often to maintain fairness or consistency.","audio":"audio/6f70f47568379e4f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-we-have-to-insist-on","text":"I'm afraid we have to insist on","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid we have to insist on","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"A polite but firm way to state a non-negotiable requirement.","example":"I'm afraid we have to insist on the security audit before launch.","plain":"We must insist on the security audit before launch.","note":"Used to convey a firm demand while maintaining a polite tone.","audio":"audio/0af195e5d756d921.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-were-at-our-limit","text":"I'm afraid we're at our limit","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid we're at our limit","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"A polite but firm statement indicating that no further concessions can be made.","example":"I'm afraid we're at our limit regarding the budget for this project.","plain":"We cannot make any further concessions regarding the budget for this project.","note":"Signals a firm boundary, often used when a negotiation is nearing its end.","audio":"audio/bf5a2c82f63cad0f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-were-fully-booked","text":"I'm afraid we're fully booked","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid we're fully booked","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way for staff to inform a customer that there are no more available spaces.","example":"I'm afraid we're fully booked for tonight, but we have availability tomorrow.","plain":"We have no more rooms available tonight.","note":"Standard phrase used by hotels, restaurants, etc., to decline a booking.","audio":"audio/71ae0a2f6708c1b9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-were-unable-to","text":"I'm afraid we're unable to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid we're unable to","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"A polite and formal way to state an inability to fulfill a request or condition.","example":"I'm afraid we're unable to meet that specific delivery date.","plain":"We cannot meet that specific delivery date.","note":"Formal and polite, used to convey a firm refusal without being abrupt.","audio":"audio/63467df9e0029f5c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-always-looking-for-ways","text":"I'm always looking for ways","kind":"frame","form":"I'm always looking for ways","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Indicates a continuous effort to find methods for improvement or growth.","example":"I'm always looking for ways to enhance my contribution to the team's overall success.","plain":"I constantly seek methods to enhance my contribution to the team's overall success.","note":"Shows initiative and a proactive approach to self-improvement and team contribution.","audio":"audio/cb4eaeee3e8b19be.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-available-to-meet","text":"I'm available to meet","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm available to meet","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Stating one's readiness to attend a meeting or social gathering.","example":"I'm available to meet any time after 3 PM on Friday.","plain":"I can meet any time after 3 PM on Friday.","note":"A clear and direct way to communicate one's availability.","audio":"audio/ad6c0010247f0459.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-about","text":"I'm calling about","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling about","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I am making this phone call to discuss or inquire about a specific subject.","example":"Hello, I'm calling about the job application I submitted last week.","plain":"Hello, I'm calling regarding the job application I sent last week.","note":"A standard and direct way to state the purpose of your call at the beginning.","audio":"audio/21a5dd208dff2ec6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-in-response-to","text":"I'm calling in response to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling in response to","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Stating the reason for a call is to reply to a previous communication.","example":"I'm calling in response to your email about the project timeline.","plain":"I am calling because of your email about the project timeline.","note":"A formal and clear way to state the purpose of your call.","audio":"audio/50f0a1921b67eb30.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-regarding","text":"I'm calling regarding","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling regarding","level":"C1","situations":["travel","calls"],"meaning":"I am making this phone call in relation to a specific subject or issue.","example":"Good morning, I'm calling regarding the invoice for last month's services.","plain":"Good morning, I'm calling about the invoice for last month's services.","note":"A slightly more formal alternative to 'I'm calling about', suitable for professional contexts.","audio":"audio/fa816c78e733aac7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-confirm","text":"I'm calling to confirm","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to confirm","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I am making this call specifically to verify a detail or arrangement.","example":"Hi, I'm calling to confirm our appointment for tomorrow morning.","plain":"Hello, I'm calling to verify our appointment for tomorrow morning.","note":"A clear and concise way to state the purpose of a call, often for appointments or bookings.","audio":"audio/395daff194c01fc4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-confirm-receipt","text":"I'm calling to confirm receipt","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm calling to confirm receipt","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Stating the purpose of a call is to verify that something has been received.","example":"I'm calling to confirm receipt of the documents you sent yesterday.","plain":"I am calling to verify that I received the documents you sent yesterday.","note":"A formal and precise way to confirm delivery of items.","audio":"audio/fc08d68ea4c8e141.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-discuss","text":"I'm calling to discuss","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to discuss","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Stating the purpose of a call is to talk about a specific topic.","example":"I'm calling to discuss the upcoming changes to the company policy.","plain":"I am calling to talk about the upcoming changes to the company policy.","note":"A direct and professional way to state the call's objective.","audio":"audio/578122abb74bdfc5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-inquire-about","text":"I'm calling to inquire about","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to inquire about","level":"C1","situations":["travel","calls"],"meaning":"I am making this call to ask for information about a specific topic.","example":"Good afternoon, I'm calling to inquire about the status of my recent order.","plain":"Good afternoon, I'm calling to ask about the status of my recent order.","note":"A formal and polite way to state the purpose of a call when seeking information.","audio":"audio/66c1ff9091391c75.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-provide-an-update","text":"I'm calling to provide an update","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm calling to provide an update","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Stating the purpose of a call is to give current information.","example":"I'm calling to provide an update on the status of your application.","plain":"I am calling to give you current information on your application's status.","note":"A professional way to initiate a call with new information.","audio":"audio/98ef8c886b76ff16.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-schedule","text":"I'm calling to schedule","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to schedule","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Stating the purpose of a call is to arrange an appointment or event.","example":"I'm calling to schedule a meeting regarding the new client proposal.","plain":"I am calling to arrange a meeting about the new client proposal.","note":"A common and direct way to state the purpose of arranging something.","audio":"audio/e3be27371dbb03a7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-certain-we-can","text":"I'm certain we can","kind":"frame","form":"I'm certain we can","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To express absolute conviction in the team's ability to succeed.","example":"I'm certain we can overcome this technical challenge with a focused effort.","plain":"I absolutely believe we can overcome this technical challenge with a focused effort.","note":"Stronger than \"confident,\" conveys a high degree of assurance.","audio":"audio/91de23f73b7ae5e4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-committed-to-improving","text":"I'm committed to improving","kind":"frame","form":"I'm committed to improving","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Expresses a strong dedication to making something better.","example":"I'm committed to improving my time management skills based on your valuable feedback.","plain":"I am dedicated to making my time management skills better based on your valuable feedback.","note":"A strong statement of intent when responding to constructive criticism.","audio":"audio/73c936307d2b613a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-about","text":"I'm confident about","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident about","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express certainty or positive expectation regarding a situation or plan.","example":"I'm confident about the success of our new marketing campaign.","plain":"I believe our new marketing campaign will be successful.","note":"Conveys assurance and can help build trust in a proposal.","audio":"audio/fae04ef1bed962a8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-in-my","text":"I'm confident in my","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident in my","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express belief in one's own ability or judgment.","example":"I'm confident in my assessment of the market trends for next quarter.","plain":"I believe my assessment of the market trends for next quarter is accurate.","note":"Used to assert personal expertise or conviction.","audio":"audio/a1fa34d81a3f0bc8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-in-our","text":"I'm confident in our","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident in our","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express belief in the collective strength or capability of a group.","example":"I'm confident in our ability to adapt to these new market conditions.","plain":"I believe in our ability to adapt to these new market conditions.","note":"Builds team spirit and reinforces trust in collective competence.","audio":"audio/f9d593ccf3cf3442.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-in-our-ability","text":"I'm confident in our ability","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident in our ability","level":"C1","situations":["feedback","persuading"],"meaning":"Expresses strong belief in the collective capacity of a group to succeed.","example":"I'm confident in our ability to meet the challenging targets for this quarter.","plain":"I strongly believe in our collective ability to meet the challenging targets for this quarter.","note":"Used to inspire confidence and unity within a team, especially during challenges.","audio":"audio/3aac59d9ff725f13.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-in-the","text":"I'm confident in the","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident in the","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express belief in the quality or effectiveness of something.","example":"I'm confident in the reliability of our new security system.","plain":"I believe our new security system is reliable.","note":"Conveys trust in a product, system, or plan.","audio":"audio/c42ee694101142ba.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-in-the-fact-that","text":"I'm confident in the fact that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident in the fact that","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express certainty about a specific truth or reality.","example":"I'm confident in the fact that our team is fully capable of handling this.","plain":"I believe it is true that our team is fully capable of handling this.","note":"A slightly more formal way to express conviction about a factual statement.","audio":"audio/7742585c39b81580.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-in-the-vision-for","text":"I'm confident in the vision for","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident in the vision for","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express belief in a future plan or aspiration.","example":"I'm confident in the vision for expanding our services globally next year.","plain":"I believe in the plan for expanding our services globally next year.","note":"Conveys trust in a long-term strategy or goal.","audio":"audio/29a1848dd3ea32a3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-in-your","text":"I'm confident in your","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident in your","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express belief in the listener's ability or judgment.","example":"I'm confident in your ability to lead this project to a successful conclusion.","plain":"I believe in your ability to lead this project to a successful conclusion.","note":"A supportive phrase, often used by a manager or mentor.","audio":"audio/7705b6e64c636392.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-in-your-ability","text":"I'm confident in your ability","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident in your ability","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Expresses strong belief in someone's skills or capacity to succeed.","example":"I'm confident in your ability to lead this new initiative successfully.","plain":"I strongly believe you can lead this new initiative successfully.","note":"Provides reassurance and positive reinforcement, especially when assigning new tasks.","audio":"audio/96227cf57483a8fe.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-that-the","text":"I'm confident that the","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident that the","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express belief in the positive outcome or quality of something specific.","example":"I'm confident that the new project manager will excel in their role.","plain":"I believe the new project manager will excel in their role.","note":"Conveys trust in a person or a specific element of a plan.","audio":"audio/e96bc51cf286f0b5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-that-this","text":"I'm confident that this","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident that this","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express strong belief in a specific proposal or plan.","example":"I'm confident that this strategy will yield positive returns within the year.","plain":"I believe this strategy will yield positive returns within the year.","note":"Projects assurance in a particular course of action.","audio":"audio/30552ea7d048ada3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-that-this-approach","text":"I'm confident that this approach","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident that this approach","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express belief in the effectiveness of a specific method.","example":"I'm confident that this approach will yield the best results for our clients.","plain":"I believe this method will yield the best results for our clients.","note":"Conveys trust in a particular strategy or methodology.","audio":"audio/386ec06a93cb1e3d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-that-we","text":"I'm confident that we","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident that we","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express strong belief in the group's capacity to achieve something.","example":"I'm confident that we can find a way to make this project financially viable.","plain":"I believe we can find a way to make this project financially viable.","note":"Reassures and motivates, often used when presenting a challenging goal.","audio":"audio/fffb97704a5253ba.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-that-we-can-achieve","text":"I'm confident that we can achieve","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident that we can achieve","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express belief in the group's ability to reach a goal.","example":"I'm confident that we can achieve our sales targets with this new strategy.","plain":"I believe we can reach our sales targets with this new strategy.","note":"Projects optimism and encourages collective effort towards a specific goal.","audio":"audio/7ca53c02590ee406.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-that-well","text":"I'm confident that we'll","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident that we'll","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express belief in a future positive outcome for the group.","example":"I'm confident that we'll find a way to overcome these logistical challenges.","plain":"I believe we'll find a way to overcome these logistical challenges.","note":"Reassures and motivates, focusing on future success despite obstacles.","audio":"audio/98e66a64e38a6818.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-that-well-be","text":"I'm confident that we'll be","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident that we'll be","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express belief in a future state or condition for the group.","example":"I'm confident that we'll be able to adapt to any unforeseen challenges.","plain":"I believe we'll be able to adapt to any unforeseen challenges.","note":"Reassures about future resilience and adaptability.","audio":"audio/b8abd9380bb4e9e8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-that-well-be-able","text":"I'm confident that we'll be able","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident that we'll be able","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express belief in the group's future capability to perform an action.","example":"I'm confident that we'll be able to meet the increased demand effectively.","plain":"I believe we'll be able to meet the increased demand effectively.","note":"Reassures about future capacity and competence.","audio":"audio/328c13c6d6889e8f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-that-well-be-able-to","text":"I'm confident that we'll be able to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident that we'll be able to","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express strong belief in the group's future capability to achieve something.","example":"I'm confident that we'll be able to successfully launch the product on schedule.","plain":"I believe we'll be able to successfully launch the product on schedule.","note":"Reassures about future success and capability, often used for project timelines.","audio":"audio/0d941fe9caaf72e7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-that-well-be-successful","text":"I'm confident that we'll be successful","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident that we'll be successful","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express strong belief in the group's future success.","example":"I'm confident that we'll be successful in expanding into the new market.","plain":"I believe we'll succeed in expanding into the new market.","note":"Reassures about future success and capability, often used for ambitious goals.","audio":"audio/ab3275476fbe85b8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-that-you","text":"I'm confident that you","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident that you","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express belief in the listener's understanding or agreement.","example":"I'm confident that you understand the urgency of this matter.","plain":"I believe you understand the urgency of this matter.","note":"A polite way to check for comprehension or anticipate agreement.","audio":"audio/bd2a3d46191e24d7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-that-youll","text":"I'm confident that you'll","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident that you'll","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express belief in the listener's positive reaction or understanding.","example":"I'm confident that you'll see the value in this long-term investment strategy.","plain":"I believe you'll see the value in this long-term investment strategy.","note":"A polite way to anticipate agreement or positive reception from the audience.","audio":"audio/e7b19db6bb110938.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-that-youll-agree","text":"I'm confident that you'll agree","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident that you'll agree","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express belief in the listener's eventual agreement with a point.","example":"I'm confident that you'll agree this is the most logical path forward.","plain":"I believe you'll agree this is the most logical path forward.","note":"A polite and persuasive way to anticipate consensus.","audio":"audio/a5e636f39c8a1031.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-that-youll-appreciate","text":"I'm confident that you'll appreciate","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident that you'll appreciate","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express belief in the listener's positive evaluation of something.","example":"I'm confident that you'll appreciate the thoroughness of our market research.","plain":"I believe you'll value the thoroughness of our market research.","note":"A polite way to anticipate a positive reception or understanding of value.","audio":"audio/00f08a9895cc2999.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-that-youll-find","text":"I'm confident that you'll find","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident that you'll find","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express belief in the listener's discovery of a positive attribute.","example":"I'm confident that you'll find this new software incredibly user-friendly.","plain":"I believe you'll discover this new software is incredibly user-friendly.","note":"A polite way to anticipate a positive user experience or discovery.","audio":"audio/4bc8aa66058556ab.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-that-youll-see","text":"I'm confident that you'll see","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident that you'll see","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express belief in the listener's eventual recognition of a point.","example":"I'm confident that you'll see the long-term benefits of this investment.","plain":"I believe you'll recognize the long-term benefits of this investment.","note":"A polite and persuasive way to anticipate understanding and agreement.","audio":"audio/97faac67431a9569.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-convinced-we-can","text":"I'm convinced we can","kind":"frame","form":"I'm convinced we can","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express strong belief in the group's ability to succeed.","example":"I'm convinced we can find a solution that satisfies all stakeholders.","plain":"I strongly believe we can find a solution that satisfies all stakeholders.","note":"Projects confidence and encourages a positive, problem-solving mindset.","audio":"audio/5f8ecdc2d0960476.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-curious-about","text":"I'm curious about","kind":"frame","form":"I'm curious about","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Expressing an interest in learning more about a specific topic.","example":"I'm curious about the potential impact of this decision on our partners.","plain":"I want to know more about the potential impact of this decision on our partners.","note":"A polite and engaging way to ask for more information.","audio":"audio/036aeb7a2f992844.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-deeply-saddened","text":"I'm deeply saddened","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm deeply saddened","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses profound sorrow or unhappiness.","example":"I'm deeply saddened to learn of your grandmother's passing; she was a wonderful woman.","plain":"I'm profoundly unhappy to learn of your grandmother's passing; she was a wonderful woman.","note":"Used for serious, often tragic, news; conveys strong empathy.","audio":"audio/d31a44ac4276c1fc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-delighted-for-you","text":"I'm delighted for you","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm delighted for you","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses strong happiness and pleasure for someone else's good fortune.","example":"You got the promotion? That's fantastic! I'm delighted for you and your hard work.","plain":"You got the promotion? That's fantastic! I'm very happy for you and your hard work.","note":"A warm and sincere expression of joy for another person's success.","audio":"audio/44265037049b0288.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-delighted-to-announce","text":"I'm delighted to announce","kind":"frame","form":"I'm delighted to announce","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Introduces exciting or very positive news with enthusiasm.","example":"I'm delighted to announce the successful completion of the pilot program ahead of schedule.","plain":"I am very happy to announce the successful completion of the pilot program ahead of schedule.","note":"Used for significant positive announcements, conveying strong positive emotion.","audio":"audio/ebbe09553f76e7be.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-delighted-to-share","text":"I'm delighted to share","kind":"frame","form":"I'm delighted to share","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Introduces positive news or information with enthusiasm.","example":"I'm delighted to share that our team successfully secured the new contract.","plain":"Our team successfully secured the new contract.","note":"Expresses strong positive emotion when delivering good news.","audio":"audio/cc02c8ec05d113a8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-double-booked","text":"I'm double booked","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm double booked","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Explaining that one has two appointments or commitments scheduled for the same time.","example":"Unfortunately, I'm double booked for that time, so I'll have to miss the meeting.","plain":"Unfortunately, I have two things scheduled for that time, so I'll miss the meeting.","note":"A common, slightly informal way to explain a scheduling conflict.","audio":"audio/a353319e793190a0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-eager-to-hear","text":"I'm eager to hear","kind":"frame","form":"I'm eager to hear","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Expresses strong enthusiasm for listening to someone's thoughts or opinions.","example":"I'm eager to hear your perspective on the recent client negotiations.","plain":"I'm very enthusiastic about hearing your perspective on the recent client negotiations.","note":"Shows strong interest and openness to input, slightly more formal than \"keen\".","audio":"audio/06592e1950ce761a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-experiencing-issues-with","text":"I'm experiencing issues with","kind":"frame","form":"I'm experiencing issues with","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A formal way to state that you are encountering problems with something.","example":"I'm experiencing issues with the air conditioning unit in my room; it's not working.","plain":"The air conditioning in my room is not working.","note":"More formal than \"having trouble\". Suitable for complaints or technical problems.","audio":"audio/ee6c1f2cb8016f02.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-experiencing-some-interference","text":"I'm experiencing some interference","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm experiencing some interference","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Explaining that there's unwanted noise or disruption on the line.","example":"I'm experiencing some interference on my end; it's making it hard to focus.","plain":"There's some noise on my line, making it hard to focus.","note":"Formal and polite, used to describe technical audio issues.","audio":"audio/7076162c31a28dda.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-genuinely-surprised","text":"I'm genuinely surprised","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm genuinely surprised","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses sincere astonishment at something unexpected.","example":"I'm genuinely surprised by how quickly they managed to resolve the issue.","plain":"I'm truly astonished by how quickly they managed to resolve the issue.","note":"Emphasizes the sincerity of the surprise, often positive or neutral.","audio":"audio/0af60fb9914c57f3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-glad-to-know","text":"I'm glad to know","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm glad to know","level":"C1","situations":["feedback","reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses satisfaction or relief upon learning new information, often positive or reassuring.","example":"I'm glad to know that the project is back on track after all the delays.","plain":"I'm happy that the project is back on track after all the delays.","note":"Used to convey positive feelings about receiving information that resolves uncertainty.","audio":"audio/074fca741c563307.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-glad-we-could-make-it-work","text":"I'm glad we could make it work","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm glad we could make it work","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing satisfaction that a plan or meeting was successfully arranged despite difficulties.","example":"With our busy schedules, I'm glad we could make it work for dinner tonight.","plain":"Given our busy schedules, I'm happy we managed to arrange dinner tonight.","note":"Used after successfully coordinating plans, especially when there were challenges.","audio":"audio/77fd404d1204e336.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-glad-we-ran-into-each-other","text":"I'm glad we ran into each other","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm glad we ran into each other","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to express pleasure at an unplanned meeting with someone.","example":"I'm glad we ran into each other; I've been meaning to ask you something.","plain":"I'm glad we met unexpectedly; I've been meaning to ask you something.","note":"A friendly way to acknowledge an unplanned meeting and open conversation.","audio":"audio/de383549bdcf1722.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-glad-you-asked-that","text":"I'm glad you asked that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm glad you asked that","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To express appreciation for a question, often because it allows for clarification.","example":"I'm glad you asked that, as it allows me to clarify our position.","plain":"I appreciate that question, as it allows me to clarify our position.","note":"Shows enthusiasm for the question, often when it leads to an important point.","audio":"audio/cdfd16625afc1dc9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-grateful-for-your-honesty","text":"I'm grateful for your honesty","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm grateful for your honesty","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Expresses appreciation for direct and truthful feedback, even if it's difficult.","example":"I'm grateful for your honesty regarding the project's challenges and potential pitfalls.","plain":"I appreciate your honesty regarding the project's challenges and potential pitfalls.","note":"A sincere way to acknowledge difficult but necessary feedback. Builds trust.","audio":"audio/73d822bf07075757.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to-chip-in","text":"I'm happy to chip in","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm happy to chip in","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Offering to contribute money or effort to a shared expense or task.","example":"If you're buying the tickets, I'm happy to chip in for the cost.","plain":"I'll gladly contribute money for the tickets if you buy them.","note":"Common and friendly, often used for shared expenses or group efforts.","audio":"audio/245255bafe4f0dba.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to-contribute","text":"I'm happy to contribute","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm happy to contribute","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing willingness to provide something or help financially for a shared activity.","example":"I'm happy to contribute to the cost of the food for the picnic.","plain":"I am willing to pay for some of the food for the picnic.","note":"A polite way to offer to share expenses or provide items for a group event.","audio":"audio/d20492bcc7cc108d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to-go-with-the-flow","text":"I'm happy to go with the flow","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm happy to go with the flow","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing willingness to adapt to whatever plans others decide.","example":"I don't have a strong preference for dinner, I'm happy to go with the flow.","plain":"I don't mind what we do for dinner, I'll adapt to your choices.","note":"Shows flexibility and an easygoing attitude, useful when others are making decisions.","audio":"audio/16432ff5d55e0d9b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to-help-out","text":"I'm happy to help out","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm happy to help out","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing willingness to assist with tasks or preparations.","example":"If you need an extra pair of hands for setting up, I'm happy to help out.","plain":"If you need an extra pair of hands for setting up, I am willing to assist.","note":"A friendly and common offer of practical assistance.","audio":"audio/23e568d6a8835a83.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to-host-this-time","text":"I'm happy to host this time","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm happy to host this time","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Offering to be the host for a social gathering on a particular occasion.","example":"You hosted last time, so I'm happy to host this time.","plain":"You were the host last time, so I'd like to host now.","note":"A polite and reciprocal offer, often used among friends who take turns hosting.","audio":"audio/8154f744043b6662.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to-reschedule","text":"I'm happy to reschedule","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm happy to reschedule","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing willingness to change the time or date of a planned event.","example":"If that time doesn't work for you, I'm happy to reschedule.","plain":"If that time is not convenient for you, I am willing to change our meeting time.","note":"Shows flexibility and consideration for the other person's availability.","audio":"audio/073af87a092be206.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to-say","text":"I'm happy to say","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm happy to say","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to introduce positive or welcome news, expressing pleasure.","example":"I'm happy to say, we've successfully secured the funding for the next phase of the project.","plain":"We've successfully secured the funding for the next phase of the project.","note":"A polite way to deliver good news, expressing personal satisfaction.","audio":"audio/9703d28ea45e1930.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to-take","text":"I'm happy to take","kind":"frame","form":"I'm happy to take","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To volunteer to be responsible for a task or action.","example":"I'm happy to take the lead on researching potential vendors.","plain":"I will lead the research on potential vendors.","note":"A polite and proactive way to volunteer for a task.","audio":"audio/796c8ca61eca4c8f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-having-a-bit-of-trouble-with","text":"I'm having a bit of trouble with","kind":"frame","form":"I'm having a bit of trouble with","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Explaining that you are experiencing a minor problem or difficulty.","example":"I'm having a bit of trouble with the hotel safe; it won't open.","plain":"I'm having a small problem with the hotel safe; it won't open.","note":"A polite and understated way to report a problem.","audio":"audio/36980e71236daf0d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-having-difficulty-connecting","text":"I'm having difficulty connecting","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm having difficulty connecting","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Explaining that one is struggling to establish or maintain a call.","example":"I'm having difficulty connecting to the meeting; it keeps timing out.","plain":"I'm struggling to join the meeting because it keeps timing out.","note":"A general phrase for technical issues preventing a call from starting.","audio":"audio/43b298a476b265f7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-having-difficulty-with","text":"I'm having difficulty with","kind":"frame","form":"I'm having difficulty with","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Explaining that you are experiencing a problem or challenge with something.","example":"I'm having difficulty with the online check-in process.","plain":"I'm finding the online check-in process challenging.","note":"A polite and clear way to state a problem you are facing.","audio":"audio/b437b44140018890.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-having-difficulty-with-the-language","text":"I'm having difficulty with the language","kind":"frame","form":"I'm having difficulty with the language","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Explaining that you are struggling to communicate due to language barriers.","example":"Please bear with me, I'm having difficulty with the language here.","plain":"Please bear with me, I'm struggling with the language here.","note":"A polite way to explain communication challenges in a foreign country.","audio":"audio/ad5d9a2fe67bbceb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-having-network-issues","text":"I'm having network issues","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm having network issues","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Explaining that one's internet connection is problematic.","example":"I'm having network issues today, so please bear with me if I drop off.","plain":"My internet connection is bad today, so please be patient if I disconnect.","note":"A common and professional way to explain connectivity problems.","audio":"audio/c612b8a152a09684.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-having-second-thoughts","text":"I'm having second thoughts","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm having second thoughts","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Expressing that you are reconsidering a decision or plan.","example":"I'm having second thoughts about the early morning excursion.","plain":"I'm reconsidering the early morning excursion.","note":"Used when you are unsure about a previous decision.","audio":"audio/6c72c9f2e5879ea6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-having-second-thoughts-about","text":"I'm having second thoughts about","kind":"frame","form":"I'm having second thoughts about","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Expressing that you are reconsidering a decision or plan.","example":"I'm having second thoughts about renting a car, given the traffic.","plain":"I'm reconsidering renting a car, given the traffic.","note":"Used when you are unsure about a previous decision.","audio":"audio/dc6701cc9d18f21e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-having-trouble-connecting-to-the-wi-fi","text":"I'm having trouble connecting to the Wi-Fi","kind":"frame","form":"I'm having trouble connecting to the Wi-Fi","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Explaining that you are experiencing difficulty accessing the internet.","example":"Excuse me, I'm having trouble connecting to the Wi-Fi in my room.","plain":"Excuse me, I cannot connect to the Wi-Fi in my room.","note":"Common issue in hotels; this is a clear way to state the problem.","audio":"audio/d90c1df048900704.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-having-trouble-reaching","text":"I'm having trouble reaching","kind":"frame","form":"I'm having trouble reaching","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state that you are finding it difficult to contact someone by phone or other means.","example":"I'm having trouble reaching the hotel reception; the line is constantly busy.","plain":"I can't get through to the hotel reception.","note":"Common for phone calls or attempts to contact someone.","audio":"audio/cea0c9582319c796.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-here-for-a-conference","text":"I'm here for a conference","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm here for a conference","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of your visit, specifically for attending a professional meeting.","example":"I'm here for a conference at the convention centre, starting tomorrow morning.","plain":"I am attending a conference here.","note":"Direct and clear statement of purpose for business travel.","audio":"audio/d84aea37d666b8c9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-here-to-listen","text":"I'm here to listen","kind":"frame","form":"I'm here to listen","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Indicates readiness to hear and understand someone's thoughts or concerns.","example":"I'm here to listen to any concerns you might have about the new policy changes.","plain":"I am ready to hear any concerns you might have about the new policy changes.","note":"Creates an open and supportive environment for receiving feedback or concerns.","audio":"audio/5426a0f07f5a39ce.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-here-to-listen-to","text":"I'm here to listen to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm here to listen to","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Emphasizes one's availability and willingness to hear someone's thoughts or concerns.","example":"I'm here to listen to any suggestions you have for improving our team meetings.","plain":"I am available to hear any suggestions you have for improving our team meetings.","note":"Creates an open and receptive atmosphere for receiving feedback.","audio":"audio/ae71fc3cf58730a8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-here-to-make-the-case-for","text":"I'm here to make the case for","kind":"frame","form":"I'm here to make the case for","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To state the intention of arguing in favor of something.","example":"I'm here to make the case for investing more in employee training programs.","plain":"I'm here to argue for investing more in employee training programs.","note":"Clearly states the speaker's persuasive intent at the outset.","audio":"audio/1c5a85932dc70a03.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-here-to-support","text":"I'm here to support","kind":"frame","form":"I'm here to support","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Offers assistance and encouragement, emphasizing a supportive role.","example":"I'm here to support you through any challenges you encounter with the new system.","plain":"I am available to assist you through any challenges you encounter with the new system.","note":"Reassuring and collaborative, often used by managers or mentors.","audio":"audio/cb2e1c9978424f3d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-hoping-to-find-a-place-to","text":"I'm hoping to find a place to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm hoping to find a place to","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To express your desire to locate a suitable venue for a specific activity.","example":"I'm hoping to find a place to have a quiet dinner near the hotel tonight.","plain":"I want to find a quiet dinner place near the hotel.","note":"Expresses a wish or expectation, softer than \"I'm looking for\".","audio":"audio/c952b900364632cd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-hosting-a","text":"I'm hosting a","kind":"frame","form":"I'm hosting a","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Announcing that you are organizing and holding an event at your home or venue.","example":"I'm hosting a small gathering next weekend, just a few friends.","plain":"I am organizing a small gathering next weekend, just a few friends.","note":"Clear and direct way to inform someone about an event you are putting on.","audio":"audio/92de6a0bd262870a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-inclined-to-believe","text":"I'm inclined to believe","kind":"frame","form":"I'm inclined to believe","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To express a strong tendency towards a particular belief or conclusion.","example":"Given the data, I'm inclined to believe that the market will recover soon.","plain":"Given the data, I tend to believe that the market will recover soon.","note":"A formal way to express a reasoned opinion or prediction.","audio":"audio/84fd1ac67e315064.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-inclined-to-disagree","text":"I'm inclined to disagree","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm inclined to disagree","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that you lean towards a different opinion or conclusion.","example":"While I understand your reasoning, I'm inclined to disagree with your final assessment.","plain":"While I understand your reasoning, I tend to disagree with your final assessment.","note":"Suggests a thoughtful consideration before expressing a differing view. Diplomatic.","audio":"audio/de6995d089d4d823.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-inclined-to-think-that","text":"I'm inclined to think that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm inclined to think that","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to express a tendency of one's thought or a tentative opinion.","example":"I'm inclined to think that the root cause of the delay is a lack of clear communication.","plain":"I tend to think that the root cause of the delay is a lack of clear communication.","note":"A polite and slightly formal way to offer an opinion, suggesting it's a considered view.","audio":"audio/3a66e78ce299229c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-about-to","text":"I'm just about to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just about to","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","socialising"],"meaning":"Used to indicate that you are on the verge of doing something.","example":"I'm just about to send out the meeting agenda, so let me know if you have any last-minute points.","plain":"I'm sending out the meeting agenda soon, so let me know if you have any last-minute points.","note":"Useful for indicating immediate future actions, often in a helpful context.","audio":"audio/ca2bf53f63e13186.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-calling-about","text":"I'm just calling about","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just calling about","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Stating the purpose of your phone call directly and politely.","example":"Hello, I'm just calling about my hotel reservation for next week.","plain":"Hello, I'm calling regarding my hotel reservation for next week.","note":"Standard opening for a phone call to state the reason.","audio":"audio/f3e9a6ca33236013.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-calling-regarding","text":"I'm just calling regarding","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just calling regarding","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of your phone call in a concise and polite way.","example":"I'm just calling regarding my flight booking for next month, reference number XYZ.","plain":"I'm calling about my flight booking.","note":"Slightly more formal than \"calling about\". Good for business calls.","audio":"audio/34df1f435c42cecb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-checking-my-itinerary","text":"I'm just checking my itinerary","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm just checking my itinerary","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Explaining that you are reviewing your travel schedule.","example":"Please wait a moment; I'm just checking my itinerary for the next leg.","plain":"Please wait a moment; I'm just reviewing my travel schedule for the next leg.","note":"Used when you need a moment to consult your travel plans.","audio":"audio/42a0455697dc5aae.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-confirming","text":"I'm just confirming","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm just confirming","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Explaining that you are verifying details that you believe are correct.","example":"I'm just confirming our flight departure time for tomorrow morning.","plain":"I'm just verifying our flight departure time for tomorrow morning.","note":"Similar to \"making sure\" but often for details already known.","audio":"audio/f0580c680732fefa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-curious","text":"I'm just curious","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm just curious","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to soften a question, implying it's for general interest rather than a specific need.","example":"I'm just curious, how did you manage to finish that so quickly?","plain":"How did you manage to finish that so quickly?","note":"Similar to 'just out of curiosity', makes a question less intrusive.","audio":"audio/9face69055b1f94a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-following-up","text":"I'm just following up","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm just following up","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","calls"],"meaning":"I am contacting you again to check on the status of a previous matter or conversation.","example":"Hi, I'm just following up on the email I sent regarding the project proposal.","plain":"Hello, I'm contacting you again about the project proposal email I sent.","note":"A common and polite way to initiate a call or message about an ongoing issue.","audio":"audio/ce6626c1d8b44526.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-making-an-enquiry","text":"I'm just making an enquiry","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm just making an enquiry","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Explaining that you are simply asking for information.","example":"I'm just making an enquiry about the availability of rooms next month.","plain":"I'm just asking for information about the availability of rooms next month.","note":"A polite and formal way to ask for information without committing.","audio":"audio/ea69d5c180908ae6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-making-sure","text":"I'm just making sure","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just making sure","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","travel"],"meaning":"Used to explain that you are confirming something, often to avoid errors or misunderstandings.","example":"I'm just making sure I have the correct version of the document before I start editing.","plain":"I need to confirm I have the correct version of the document before I start editing.","note":"A polite way to double-check information, often in a professional context.","audio":"audio/47fb95ec29c22e9f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-passing-through","text":"I'm just passing through","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm just passing through","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","travel"],"meaning":"Used to explain that you are only in a place temporarily, not staying long.","example":"I can't stay long, I'm just passing through on my way to another meeting.","plain":"I can't stay long, I'm only here temporarily on my way to another meeting.","note":"Useful for setting expectations about your availability in a conversation.","audio":"audio/89e0523d4e6a723f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-so-glad-that","text":"I'm just so glad that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just so glad that","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses strong relief or happiness about a positive outcome or avoided negative situation.","example":"I'm just so glad that everyone arrived safely despite the bad weather.","plain":"I'm very relieved that everyone arrived safely despite the bad weather.","note":"A warm and sincere way to express strong positive feelings, often relief.","audio":"audio/5afccd8cefe1cbce.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to","text":"I'm just trying to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","storytelling"],"meaning":"Used to explain your intention or purpose, often to justify an action or question.","example":"I'm just trying to understand the rationale behind this sudden change in direction.","plain":"I'm trying to understand the rationale behind this sudden change in direction.","note":"A common softener, used to explain one's motives in a non-confrontational way.","audio":"audio/4fb7d419f1b66b0d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-clarify","text":"I'm just trying to clarify","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm just trying to clarify","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","travel"],"meaning":"Explaining that you are attempting to make something clearer or understand it better.","example":"I'm just trying to clarify the terms of the car rental agreement.","plain":"I'm just trying to understand the terms of the car rental agreement better.","note":"Used when seeking more precise information or understanding.","audio":"audio/bb6f6023551e42f4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-confirm","text":"I'm just trying to confirm","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm just trying to confirm","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Explaining that you are attempting to verify details.","example":"I'm just trying to confirm the opening hours of the museum.","plain":"I'm just attempting to verify the opening hours of the museum.","note":"Used when seeking to double-check information.","audio":"audio/800b73bf1f45f5e3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-find-out","text":"I'm just trying to find out","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to find out","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To explain that you are seeking specific information.","example":"I'm just trying to find out what time the last train departs for the city centre.","plain":"I want to know the last train departure time.","note":"Common for polite information gathering.","audio":"audio/5a83ed02bc1d3120.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-find-out-if","text":"I'm just trying to find out if","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to find out if","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Explaining that you are attempting to discover whether something is true or possible.","example":"I'm just trying to find out if there's a vegetarian option on the menu.","plain":"I'm just attempting to discover if there's a vegetarian option on the menu.","note":"Used when seeking specific information or possibilities.","audio":"audio/552c735d43244cc5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-get-everything-sorted","text":"I'm just trying to get everything sorted","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm just trying to get everything sorted","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Explaining that you are attempting to arrange or resolve all outstanding matters.","example":"I'm just trying to get everything sorted before we leave for the airport.","plain":"I'm just attempting to arrange all matters before we leave for the airport.","note":"Informal but common for managing multiple tasks or problems.","audio":"audio/626eef1125fa56b0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-get-some-clarity-on","text":"I'm just trying to get some clarity on","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to get some clarity on","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Explaining that you are attempting to gain a better understanding of something unclear.","example":"I'm just trying to get some clarity on the hotel's cancellation policy.","plain":"I'm just attempting to better understand the hotel's cancellation policy.","note":"Used when seeking to resolve ambiguity or confusion.","audio":"audio/4a00566ecab2bb0e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-get-some-information","text":"I'm just trying to get some information","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm just trying to get some information","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To explain that you are seeking general details or facts.","example":"I'm just trying to get some information about local bus routes to the main attractions.","plain":"I want some information about local bus routes.","note":"A polite way to initiate an information-seeking conversation.","audio":"audio/ddffc1005adcfb4c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-make-sense-of","text":"I'm just trying to make sense of","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to make sense of","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","travel"],"meaning":"Explaining that you are attempting to understand something confusing.","example":"I'm just trying to make sense of the complex public transport system.","plain":"I'm just trying to understand the complex public transport system.","note":"Used when grappling with complicated information or systems.","audio":"audio/d222e2ae5361994e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-make-sure-that","text":"I'm just trying to make sure that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to make sure that","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Explaining that you are attempting to verify something to prevent errors.","example":"I'm just trying to make sure that my passport is still valid.","plain":"I'm just attempting to verify that my passport is still valid.","note":"Used when double-checking an important detail.","audio":"audio/8390067e15501248.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-resolve","text":"I'm just trying to resolve","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm just trying to resolve","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Explaining that you are actively working to find a solution to an issue.","example":"I'm just trying to resolve the issue with the incorrect flight booking.","plain":"I'm just trying to fix the problem with the incorrect flight booking.","note":"More formal than \"sort out,\" used for more significant problems.","audio":"audio/2cff33091f06bf50.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-see-if","text":"I'm just trying to see if","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to see if","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Explaining that you are attempting to determine if something is possible or available.","example":"I'm just trying to see if I can book a later train.","plain":"I'm just attempting to determine if I can book a later train.","note":"Used when exploring options or possibilities.","audio":"audio/e4583e458d2729a8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-understand","text":"I'm just trying to understand","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to understand","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling","travel"],"meaning":"Expresses an attempt to comprehend a situation or someone's perspective.","example":"I'm just trying to understand why the decision was made so abruptly.","plain":"I want to understand why the decision was made so abruptly.","note":"A softener used when seeking clarification without being confrontational.","audio":"audio/0326b2ed2d77ae9a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-work-out","text":"I'm just trying to work out","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to work out","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","travel"],"meaning":"Explaining that you are attempting to calculate or understand something.","example":"I'm just trying to work out the total cost of the excursion.","plain":"I'm just attempting to calculate the total cost of the excursion.","note":"Used when trying to figure out a sum, plan, or solution.","audio":"audio/22d6c394e66d97a1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-verifying-if","text":"I'm just verifying if","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just verifying if","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state that you are confirming whether something is true or correct.","example":"I'm just verifying if my passport is still valid for international travel next year.","plain":"I'm confirming if my passport is valid.","note":"Slightly more formal than \"just checking\".","audio":"audio/0455e34b72f2a85a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-wondering","text":"I'm just wondering","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just wondering","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite and casual way to introduce a question or a thought you're considering.","example":"I'm just wondering if you've had a chance to look at those figures yet.","plain":"Have you had a chance to look at those figures yet?","note":"Softens a direct question, making it sound less demanding.","audio":"audio/caed0054b2a193df.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-keen-to-get-your","text":"I'm keen to get your","kind":"frame","form":"I'm keen to get your","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Expresses eagerness to receive someone's specific input or perspective.","example":"I'm keen to get your insights on the market research data we collected.","plain":"I am eager to receive your insights on the market research data we collected.","note":"Common in British English. Shows genuine interest in specific contributions.","audio":"audio/4efb28ad9ebf0634.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-keen-to-hear","text":"I'm keen to hear","kind":"frame","form":"I'm keen to hear","level":"C1","situations":["feedback","persuading","socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses eagerness to listen to someone's thoughts or updates.","example":"I'm keen to hear your thoughts on the proposed changes to the workflow.","plain":"I am eager to hear your thoughts on the proposed changes to the workflow.","note":"Common in British English. Shows genuine interest in others' opinions.","audio":"audio/6bbda2f27f5810d9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-keen-to-hear-more","text":"I'm keen to hear more","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm keen to hear more","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing strong interest in receiving additional information or details.","example":"Your trip sounds fascinating, I'm keen to hear more about it.","plain":"Your trip sounds interesting, I really want to hear more details.","note":"Shows genuine interest in a conversation topic, encouraging the other person to continue.","audio":"audio/640e3d7157ca765d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-keen-to-hear-your-thoughts","text":"I'm keen to hear your thoughts","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm keen to hear your thoughts","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Expressing eagerness to listen to someone's opinions or ideas.","example":"Before we decide, I'm keen to hear your thoughts on the new strategy.","plain":"Before we decide, I really want to hear your opinions on the new strategy.","note":"A polite and engaging way to invite contributions.","audio":"audio/b7c9a194392b2e8a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-keen-to-learn","text":"I'm keen to learn","kind":"frame","form":"I'm keen to learn","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Expresses strong eagerness and enthusiasm to acquire new knowledge or skills.","example":"I'm keen to learn more about how I can improve my presentation skills effectively.","plain":"I am eager to learn more about how I can improve my presentation skills effectively.","note":"Common in British English. Shows a proactive attitude towards personal development.","audio":"audio/9fd7da41e2f5119a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-looking-for-a-way","text":"I'm looking for a way","kind":"frame","form":"I'm looking for a way","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Expressing that you are trying to find a method or route to achieve something.","example":"I'm looking for a way to get to the airport without taking a taxi.","plain":"I want to find a method to get to the airport without a taxi.","note":"Useful when seeking advice on transport or methods.","audio":"audio/afb64cd0c12ddd4b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-looking-forward-to-hearing-from-you","text":"I'm looking forward to hearing from you","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm looking forward to hearing from you","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing anticipation for a response or communication from someone.","example":"Let me know your availability soon, I'm looking forward to hearing from you.","plain":"Please tell me your availability soon, I am eager to receive your reply.","note":"A polite closing remark in an email or conversation when awaiting a response.","audio":"audio/e77addbc8b688330.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-looking-forward-to-seeing-you","text":"I'm looking forward to seeing you","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm looking forward to seeing you","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing anticipation and pleasure about an upcoming meeting with someone.","example":"Our dinner plans sound great, I'm looking forward to seeing you on Friday.","plain":"Our dinner plans are good, I'm excited to meet you on Friday.","note":"A warm and friendly way to end a conversation about future plans.","audio":"audio/498a0105b03e0a8a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-looking-to-book","text":"I'm looking to book","kind":"frame","form":"I'm looking to book","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Stating your intention to make a reservation for something.","example":"I'm looking to book a guided tour of the city for tomorrow.","plain":"I want to book a guided tour of the city for tomorrow.","note":"A common and natural way to express your booking intentions.","audio":"audio/a682ed3651bbbc07.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-looking-to-reschedule","text":"I'm looking to reschedule","kind":"frame","form":"I'm looking to reschedule","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To express your desire to change the time or date of an appointment or booking.","example":"I'm looking to reschedule my flight to a later date if possible.","plain":"I want to change the date of my flight.","note":"Common for changing appointments, flights, or meetings.","audio":"audio/1b2c9d21d923fb18.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-losing-you-a-bit","text":"I'm losing you a bit","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm losing you a bit","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I'm starting to find it difficult to follow your explanation or the conversation.","example":"The technical details are getting complex; I'm losing you a bit.","plain":"The technical details are complex; I'm starting to not understand.","note":"A polite way to indicate you're struggling to keep up with the conversation's complexity.","audio":"audio/a7ec2228a7925117.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-comfortable-with","text":"I'm not comfortable with","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not comfortable with","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Expresses unease or disagreement with a particular proposal or situation.","example":"I'm not comfortable with the proposed payment terms; they're too aggressive.","plain":"I disagree with the proposed payment terms; they're too aggressive.","note":"A polite but firm way to express dissent or objection.","audio":"audio/e171eb577c948f12.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-convinced-by","text":"I'm not convinced by","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not convinced by","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing that you are not persuaded by an argument or evidence.","example":"I'm not convinced by the arguments for expanding into that volatile market.","plain":"I'm not persuaded by the arguments for expanding into that volatile market.","note":"Focuses on the lack of persuasive power in the other person's argument.","audio":"audio/6b85b916ba299531.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-convinced-that","text":"I'm not convinced that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not convinced that","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","small-talk","disagreeing","problem-solving"],"meaning":"Expressing skepticism or doubt about the truth or effectiveness of something.","example":"I'm not convinced that simply adding more staff will solve the underlying problem.","plain":"I doubt that simply adding more staff will solve the underlying problem.","note":"A clear but polite way to express a lack of belief in an idea or solution.","audio":"audio/9887188ebff5706b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-convinced-we-should","text":"I'm not convinced we should","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not convinced we should","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses a lack of conviction about a proposed action, implying hesitation or disagreement.","example":"I'm not convinced we should proceed with the merger without further due diligence.","plain":"I don't think we should proceed with the merger without further due diligence.","note":"Softens disagreement by focusing on a personal lack of conviction rather than a direct refusal.","audio":"audio/a8ffb182389c107b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-entirely-clear-on","text":"I'm not entirely clear on","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not entirely clear on","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving","calls"],"meaning":"Expressing partial confusion or a need for more information about something.","example":"I'm not entirely clear on the next steps for the implementation phase.","plain":"I don't fully understand the next steps for the implementation phase.","note":"A polite and professional way to admit a lack of full understanding.","audio":"audio/90e0604626c494d5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-entirely-happy-with","text":"I'm not entirely happy with","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not entirely happy with","level":"C1","situations":["travel","feedback","reacting"],"meaning":"Expressing dissatisfaction in a polite and understated way.","example":"I'm not entirely happy with the service we received at dinner last night.","plain":"I'm quite unhappy with the service we received at dinner last night.","note":"A common British English softener for complaints; sounds less direct.","audio":"audio/601ac23d2f5d254a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-entirely-sold-on","text":"I'm not entirely sold on","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not entirely sold on","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing","problem-solving"],"meaning":"Indicates that you are not fully persuaded by an idea or proposal.","example":"I'm not entirely sold on the idea of outsourcing our customer support.","plain":"I'm not completely convinced by the idea of outsourcing our customer support.","note":"A common idiom to express partial or complete disagreement in a softened way.","audio":"audio/4e9d6e21d7380780.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-feeling-up-to-it","text":"I'm not feeling up to it","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not feeling up to it","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Explaining that one lacks the energy or desire to do something, often due to tiredness or illness.","example":"I'm not feeling up to it tonight, so I think I'll stay home instead of going out.","plain":"I don't have the energy tonight, so I think I'll stay home instead of going out.","note":"A common, gentle way to decline due to personal well-being.","audio":"audio/a7072d20e1aefc14.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-going-to-pretend","text":"I'm not going to pretend","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not going to pretend","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Introduces an honest admission, often about a difficult truth or feeling.","example":"I'm not going to pretend that this wasn't a difficult period for the company.","plain":"This was a difficult period for the company.","note":"Used to be direct and honest about a situation.","audio":"audio/6cf670f350a60440.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-in-a-position-to","text":"I'm not in a position to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not in a position to","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation","socialising"],"meaning":"States an inability to do something due to lack of authority or resources.","example":"I'm not in a position to approve that level of discount right now.","plain":"I cannot approve that level of discount right now.","note":"A polite way to decline a request by citing limitations.","audio":"audio/9d020a0687997fc4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-quite-convinced","text":"I'm not quite convinced","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not quite convinced","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Expressing polite skepticism or a lack of full belief in an argument.","example":"I'm not quite convinced by the proposed timeline; it seems too ambitious.","plain":"I don't fully believe the proposed timeline; it seems too ambitious.","note":"A polite and diplomatic way to express doubt or disagreement.","audio":"audio/d7649cecee362de1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure","text":"I'm not sure","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt or hesitation about an idea or statement, often leading to a gentle disagreement.","example":"I'm not sure that's the best approach for our new marketing campaign.","plain":"I doubt that's the best approach for our new marketing campaign.","note":"A very common and polite way to express disagreement or reservation. Versatile.","audio":"audio/5d952f58be13515a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-how-to-feel","text":"I'm not sure how to feel","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure how to feel","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses confusion or mixed emotions in response to complex or unexpected news.","example":"After hearing both sides of the story, I'm not sure how to feel about the situation.","plain":"After hearing both sides of the story, I have mixed emotions about the situation.","note":"A common phrase to convey uncertainty or conflicting emotions.","audio":"audio/0875025a43bb15b1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-how-to-put-it","text":"I'm not sure how to put it","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure how to put it","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Expresses hesitation before articulating a difficult or sensitive point.","example":"I'm not sure how to put it, but I think we need to reconsider our approach.","plain":"I think we need to reconsider our approach, but it's hard to say.","note":"A softener used when expressing a potentially awkward or critical idea.","audio":"audio/8ff6e4ebcf5a9126.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-i-buy-that","text":"I'm not sure I buy that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I buy that","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses skepticism or disbelief about an argument or statement.","example":"I'm not sure I buy that explanation for the sudden drop in sales figures.","plain":"I don't really believe that explanation for the sudden drop in sales figures.","note":"A slightly informal but polite way to express doubt or skepticism.","audio":"audio/c613a30c01513cb8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-i-can-commit","text":"I'm not sure I can commit","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I can commit","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing uncertainty about being able to promise attendance or participation.","example":"I'm not sure I can commit to the weekly book club right now.","plain":"I'm uncertain if I can promise to attend the weekly book club right now.","note":"A gentle way to express hesitation without a firm refusal.","audio":"audio/b9a531c7e3f5c954.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-i-can-get-behind","text":"I'm not sure I can get behind","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure I can get behind","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses a lack of full support or endorsement for an idea or plan.","example":"I'm not sure I can get behind a strategy that doesn't prioritize employee development.","plain":"I don't think I can support a strategy that doesn't prioritize employee development.","note":"Indicates a personal inability to support something, softening the disagreement.","audio":"audio/e654266d149bdd54.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-i-can-make-it","text":"I'm not sure I can make it","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I can make it","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing uncertainty about being able to attend an event.","example":"Thanks for the invitation, but I'm not sure I can make it.","plain":"Thank you for inviting me, but I don't know if I can attend.","note":"A soft and polite way to indicate potential unavailability without a firm decline.","audio":"audio/de0644ac8c270d27.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-i-caught-that","text":"I'm not sure I caught that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I caught that","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Politely indicating that one did not hear or understand something.","example":"Sorry, I'm not sure I caught that last part; could you repeat it?","plain":"Sorry, I didn't hear that last part; could you repeat it?","note":"A polite way to ask for repetition, often implying a technical issue or distraction.","audio":"audio/ef2615332290b85f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-i-fully-grasp","text":"I'm not sure I fully grasp","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure I fully grasp","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses a lack of complete understanding, often as a precursor to raising a doubt or question.","example":"I'm not sure I fully grasp the implications of this change for our international partners.","plain":"I don't completely understand the implications of this change for our international partners.","note":"A polite way to ask for clarification or express a potential gap in understanding.","audio":"audio/abd254eb71763ab3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-i-like","text":"I'm not sure I like","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure I like","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to express mild disapproval or dislike.","example":"I'm not sure I like the new color scheme for the website; it feels a bit too bright.","plain":"I don't like the new color scheme for the website; it feels a bit too bright.","note":"A polite softener for expressing a negative opinion about something.","audio":"audio/2c03cc752e562050.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-i-understand","text":"I'm not sure I understand","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I understand","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","disagreeing"],"meaning":"Indicating a lack of comprehension, often used to prompt clarification or challenge a point.","example":"I'm not sure I understand why we're prioritizing this over other urgent tasks.","plain":"I don't understand why we're prioritizing this over other urgent tasks.","note":"A polite way to ask for more information, which can reveal flaws in an argument.","audio":"audio/4fa8000d02b252c5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-i-want-to","text":"I'm not sure I want to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure I want to","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to express reluctance or hesitation about doing something.","example":"I'm not sure I want to take on another big responsibility right now, to be honest.","plain":"I'm hesitant to take on another big responsibility right now.","note":"A polite way to express disinclination without being blunt.","audio":"audio/c24f67fa1c43f5bd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-id-accept-that","text":"I'm not sure I'd accept that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I'd accept that","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses a reluctance to agree to or receive something, often implying dissatisfaction.","example":"I'm not sure I'd accept that offer without negotiating the terms further.","plain":"I wouldn't accept that offer without negotiating the terms further.","note":"A polite way to indicate that a proposal is not satisfactory as is.","audio":"audio/23bd9359890ba2a2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-id-agree-entirely","text":"I'm not sure I'd agree entirely","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I'd agree entirely","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses partial disagreement, indicating that while some points are valid, others are not.","example":"I'm not sure I'd agree entirely with that assessment; there are nuances to consider.","plain":"I don't completely agree with that assessment; there are nuances to consider.","note":"A polite way to signal that your agreement is not absolute.","audio":"audio/e992dbfd4de95d49.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-id-allow-that","text":"I'm not sure I'd allow that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I'd allow that","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Indicates a reluctance to grant permission for something to happen.","example":"I'm not sure I'd allow that kind of expenditure without proper justification.","plain":"I wouldn't allow that kind of expenditure without proper justification.","note":"A polite way to refuse permission, often implying a need for more information or justification.","audio":"audio/fdc5fe03055a05e0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-id-approve-that","text":"I'm not sure I'd approve that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I'd approve that","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Indicates a reluctance to give formal consent or sanction to something.","example":"I'm not sure I'd approve that budget request without a more detailed breakdown.","plain":"I wouldn't approve that budget request without a more detailed breakdown.","note":"A polite way to withhold formal approval, often implying conditions for acceptance.","audio":"audio/fc9921a6be180e4a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-id-authorize-that","text":"I'm not sure I'd authorize that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I'd authorize that","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Indicates a reluctance to give formal power or permission for an action.","example":"I'm not sure I'd authorize that payment without verifying the invoice details.","plain":"I wouldn't give official permission for that payment without verifying the invoice details.","note":"A formal way to withhold authorization, often implying a need for verification.","audio":"audio/3472289175ab1fde.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-id-be-comfortable-with-that","text":"I'm not sure I'd be comfortable with that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I'd be comfortable with that","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses personal unease or reluctance regarding a proposal or situation.","example":"I'm not sure I'd be comfortable with that level of exposure to market fluctuations.","plain":"I wouldn't be comfortable with that level of exposure to market fluctuations.","note":"Softens disagreement by focusing on personal feelings of unease rather than direct opposition.","audio":"audio/e9b7f24933c36d95.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-id-be-happy-with-that","text":"I'm not sure I'd be happy with that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I'd be happy with that","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses personal dissatisfaction or discomfort with a proposed outcome or situation.","example":"I'm not sure I'd be happy with that outcome; it doesn't seem to address our core issues.","plain":"I wouldn't be happy with that outcome; it doesn't seem to address our core issues.","note":"Softens disagreement by focusing on personal feelings rather than a direct challenge.","audio":"audio/b6938927efd64759.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-id-be-keen-on-that","text":"I'm not sure I'd be keen on that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I'd be keen on that","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses a lack of enthusiasm or strong preference for a particular idea or action.","example":"I'm not sure I'd be keen on that idea; it seems to involve too much travel.","plain":"I wouldn't be enthusiastic about that idea; it seems to involve too much travel.","note":"A British English idiom to politely express a lack of interest or preference.","audio":"audio/8f81af237656b7b2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-id-be-okay-with-that","text":"I'm not sure I'd be okay with that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I'd be okay with that","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Indicates a lack of personal acceptance or approval for a situation or decision.","example":"I'm not sure I'd be okay with that change to our working hours without more notice.","plain":"I wouldn't be okay with that change to our working hours without more notice.","note":"A casual but clear way to express personal disapproval or non-acceptance.","audio":"audio/3ce7bb553f792521.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-id-consent-to-that","text":"I'm not sure I'd consent to that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I'd consent to that","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses a reluctance to agree or give permission, often implying personal discomfort or disagreement.","example":"I'm not sure I'd consent to that clause in the contract without further review.","plain":"I wouldn't agree to that clause in the contract without further review.","note":"A polite way to refuse agreement or permission, often in a formal context.","audio":"audio/71433c0d27063e09.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-id-go-that-far","text":"I'm not sure I'd go that far","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I'd go that far","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing","reacting"],"meaning":"Indicates that you agree with a point but not to the extreme or full extent suggested.","example":"I agree it's challenging, but I'm not sure I'd go that far as to call it impossible.","plain":"I agree it's challenging, but I wouldn't call it impossible.","note":"A common idiom to express partial disagreement, especially regarding the intensity of a statement.","audio":"audio/386f863b0e132345.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-id-put-it-that-way","text":"I'm not sure I'd put it that way","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I'd put it that way","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing","reacting"],"meaning":"Suggests that the phrasing or characterization of something is inaccurate or inappropriate.","example":"I'm not sure I'd put it that way; perhaps 'challenging' is a better description than 'disastrous'.","plain":"I wouldn't phrase it like that; 'challenging' is a better description than 'disastrous'.","note":"Politely corrects or reframes someone's choice of words or interpretation.","audio":"audio/e3941507a60b5b42.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-id-recommend-that","text":"I'm not sure I'd recommend that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I'd recommend that","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses a lack of endorsement or advice against a particular course of action.","example":"I'm not sure I'd recommend that approach, given the potential for negative public reaction.","plain":"I wouldn't recommend that approach, given the potential for negative public reaction.","note":"A polite way to advise against something, often based on potential negative consequences.","audio":"audio/530887108645bd75.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-id-sanction-that","text":"I'm not sure I'd sanction that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I'd sanction that","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses a reluctance to give official permission or approval to something.","example":"I'm not sure I'd sanction that decision without consulting the legal department first.","plain":"I wouldn't officially approve that decision without consulting the legal department first.","note":"A formal way to withhold official approval, often implying a need for due process.","audio":"audio/8c579425cb516018.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-id-say-that","text":"I'm not sure I'd say that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I'd say that","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Indicates that you would not express something in the same way, implying disagreement with the statement.","example":"I'm not sure I'd say that our competitors are completely irrelevant in this market.","plain":"I wouldn't say that our competitors are completely irrelevant in this market.","note":"A subtle way to express disagreement with a statement, often implying it's too strong or inaccurate.","audio":"audio/7332db86d44a4986.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-id-sign-off-on-that","text":"I'm not sure I'd sign off on that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I'd sign off on that","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Indicates a reluctance to give final approval or endorsement to a plan or document.","example":"I'm not sure I'd sign off on that report until all the data has been double-checked.","plain":"I wouldn't give final approval to that report until all the data has been double-checked.","note":"A common idiom for withholding final approval, often in a professional setting.","audio":"audio/b4f2b1ae809a6b08.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-id-support-that","text":"I'm not sure I'd support that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I'd support that","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Indicates a lack of backing or approval for a proposal or idea.","example":"I'm not sure I'd support that policy without more evidence of its effectiveness.","plain":"I wouldn't support that policy without more evidence of its effectiveness.","note":"A clear but polite way to state that you cannot endorse something.","audio":"audio/cf13086f51edbe48.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-id-use-that-word","text":"I'm not sure I'd use that word","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I'd use that word","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Politely objects to a specific term or descriptor used by someone else.","example":"I'm not sure I'd use that word to describe the situation; 'complex' seems more appropriate.","plain":"I wouldn't use that word to describe the situation; 'complex' seems more appropriate.","note":"A subtle way to correct or refine language, indicating a difference in perception.","audio":"audio/45a724eb83e7165e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-im-comfortable-with","text":"I'm not sure I'm comfortable with","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure I'm comfortable with","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing","reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses personal unease or reluctance regarding a proposal or situation.","example":"I'm not sure I'm comfortable with the level of risk involved in this new venture.","plain":"I'm uneasy about the level of risk involved in this new venture.","note":"Softens disagreement by focusing on personal feelings rather than a direct challenge.","audio":"audio/f6535bd6a44aa98a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-im-explaining-myself","text":"I'm not sure I'm explaining myself","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I'm explaining myself","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt about the clarity of one's own communication.","example":"I'm not sure I'm explaining myself well, but I hope you understand my concern.","plain":"I don't know if I'm being clear, but I hope you understand my concern.","note":"Used when you feel your message might not be coming across clearly.","audio":"audio/d0afb5f12acae141.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-ive-got","text":"I'm not sure I've got","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure I've got","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to indicate uncertainty about possessing something or understanding.","example":"I'm not sure I've got all the necessary documents for the application.","plain":"I'm uncertain if I have all the necessary documents for the application.","note":"A polite way to express doubt about having something.","audio":"audio/4b0561cea6d2a592.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-ive-got-it","text":"I'm not sure I've got it","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I've got it","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to indicate that you don't fully understand something.","example":"Could you explain that again? I'm not sure I've got it completely.","plain":"Could you explain that again? I don't understand it completely.","note":"Informal, used to politely ask for re-explanation.","audio":"audio/b407110b11033686.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-ive-got-the-energy","text":"I'm not sure I've got the energy","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure I've got the energy","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to express that you are tired or lack the enthusiasm for something.","example":"I'm not sure I've got the energy for another late-night brainstorming session tonight.","plain":"I might not have the energy for another late-night brainstorming session tonight.","note":"Informal, used to politely decline due to fatigue or lack of enthusiasm.","audio":"audio/f16a7b3450e33388.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-ive-got-the-patience","text":"I'm not sure I've got the patience","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure I've got the patience","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to express that you are feeling impatient or easily annoyed.","example":"I'm not sure I've got the patience for another round of revisions on this document.","plain":"I might not have the patience for another round of revisions on this document.","note":"Informal, used to politely express frustration or a limit to one's tolerance.","audio":"audio/7805002e48b55d46.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-ive-got-the-right-idea","text":"I'm not sure I've got the right idea","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I've got the right idea","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to express uncertainty about one's understanding or approach to something.","example":"I'm not sure I've got the right idea about how to tackle this complex problem.","plain":"I'm uncertain about how to tackle this complex problem.","note":"A polite way to express doubt and invite input or clarification.","audio":"audio/1ce3cd5f73c7e1a1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-ive-got-time","text":"I'm not sure I've got time","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure I've got time","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to express that you are busy or have limited availability.","example":"I'm not sure I've got time for a long discussion right now, but I can spare a few minutes.","plain":"I might not have time for a long discussion right now, but I can spare a few minutes.","note":"A polite way to manage expectations about your availability.","audio":"audio/775a12c0080b9511.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-ive-had","text":"I'm not sure I've had","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure I've had","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to indicate that you don't recall having a particular experience or item.","example":"I'm not sure I've had the chance to review those figures yet; I'll check my inbox.","plain":"I don't recall reviewing those figures yet; I'll check my inbox.","note":"A polite way to admit something hasn't been done or experienced.","audio":"audio/05874936688d7787.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-ive-heard","text":"I'm not sure I've heard","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure I've heard","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to indicate unfamiliarity with a piece of information or a person.","example":"I'm not sure I've heard about that new initiative; could you tell me more?","plain":"I haven't heard about that new initiative; could you tell me more?","note":"A polite way to admit lack of knowledge and invite further explanation.","audio":"audio/df02d60cbe2e0656.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-ive-met","text":"I'm not sure I've met","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure I've met","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to indicate that you don't recall meeting someone before.","example":"I'm not sure I've met you before; my name is Sarah, I work in marketing.","plain":"I don't recall meeting you before; my name is Sarah, I work in marketing.","note":"A polite way to initiate an introduction or re-introduction.","audio":"audio/e31b4aaf3fa9a0fb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-ive-seen","text":"I'm not sure I've seen","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure I've seen","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to indicate unfamiliarity with something visual or a document.","example":"I'm not sure I've seen the updated project timeline; is it available online?","plain":"I haven't seen the updated project timeline; is it available online?","note":"A polite way to admit lack of visual information and request it.","audio":"audio/0825aa3f1ce88af8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-if-you-know","text":"I'm not sure if you know","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure if you know","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Introduces information that the listener might not be aware of.","example":"I'm not sure if you know, but the office is closing early today.","plain":"Perhaps you don't know, but the office is closing early today.","note":"A polite way to share new information, especially if it's important.","audio":"audio/75e1ea5caf360a31.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-its-quite-that-simple","text":"I'm not sure it's quite that simple","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure it's quite that simple","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Suggests that a situation or solution is more complex than it appears, implying a need for more thought.","example":"I'm not sure it's quite that simple to just cut costs without affecting service quality.","plain":"I think cutting costs without affecting service quality is more complicated than it seems.","note":"A polite way to challenge an oversimplified view or solution.","audio":"audio/46fe044f328564f2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-that-holds","text":"I'm not sure that holds","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that holds","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the validity or applicability of an argument or statement.","example":"I'm not sure that holds true for all our international markets, given their unique regulations.","plain":"I doubt that is true for all our international markets, given their unique regulations.","note":"Questions the general truth or universal applicability of a statement.","audio":"audio/26f40d0d5a279886.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-entirely-accurate","text":"I'm not sure that's entirely accurate","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's entirely accurate","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Politely questioning the correctness or truth of a statement.","example":"I'm not sure that's entirely accurate; my data shows slightly different figures.","plain":"I doubt that's completely correct; my data shows slightly different figures.","note":"A diplomatic way to correct someone without directly saying they are wrong.","audio":"audio/d4f835df4fdb03e4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-entirely-correct","text":"I'm not sure that's entirely correct","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's entirely correct","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Politely questioning the accuracy of a statement, similar to 'entirely true'.","example":"I'm not sure that's entirely correct; the latest report shows a different trend.","plain":"I doubt that's completely correct; the latest report shows a different trend.","note":"A diplomatic way to correct someone, implying a minor inaccuracy rather than a major error.","audio":"audio/949da9d0207a4dda.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-entirely-feasible","text":"I'm not sure that's entirely feasible","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's entirely feasible","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing strong doubt about the practicality or possibility of an idea or plan.","example":"I'm not sure that's entirely feasible given the current market conditions.","plain":"I doubt that's completely possible given the current market conditions.","note":"A pragmatic way to express disagreement, emphasizing significant practical hurdles.","audio":"audio/3931468c89f3162e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-entirely-necessary","text":"I'm not sure that's entirely necessary","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's entirely necessary","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt about the essential nature or importance of an action or item.","example":"I'm not sure that's entirely necessary for achieving our core objectives.","plain":"I doubt that's completely needed for achieving our core objectives.","note":"A polite way to suggest something is superfluous or could be omitted.","audio":"audio/d6c0c5ade91b5919.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-entirely-true","text":"I'm not sure that's entirely true","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's entirely true","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Politely questioning the factual accuracy of a statement.","example":"I'm not sure that's entirely true; I recall a different outcome from that meeting.","plain":"I doubt that's completely true; I recall a different outcome from that meeting.","note":"A diplomatic way to correct someone without directly calling them a liar.","audio":"audio/b6ee014e7f168033.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-fair","text":"I'm not sure that's fair","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's fair","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing disagreement based on a sense of injustice or imbalance.","example":"I'm not sure that's fair to the team members who worked extra hours.","plain":"I don't think that's fair to the team members who worked extra hours.","note":"Appeals to a sense of equity. Can be used in both personal and professional contexts.","audio":"audio/7f51d97e3983ff3c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-feasible","text":"I'm not sure that's feasible","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's feasible","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt about the possibility or practicality of an idea or plan.","example":"I'm not sure that's feasible within the given timeframe and budget.","plain":"I doubt that's possible within the given timeframe and budget.","note":"A pragmatic way to express disagreement, focusing on practical limitations.","audio":"audio/678be0e84bea99c6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-practical","text":"I'm not sure that's practical","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's practical","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt about the feasibility or realism of a plan or idea.","example":"I'm not sure that's practical given our current resource constraints.","plain":"I doubt that's feasible given our current resource constraints.","note":"A pragmatic way to express disagreement, focusing on real-world limitations.","audio":"audio/9fc0fcc38c71441a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-best-course","text":"I'm not sure that's the best course","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the best course","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt about the optimal path or plan of action.","example":"I'm not sure that's the best course for our company in the current economic climate.","plain":"I doubt that's the best plan for our company in the current economic climate.","note":"Used to question strategic choices or overall direction.","audio":"audio/29499a6adfe58170.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-best-outcome","text":"I'm not sure that's the best outcome","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the best outcome","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt about whether a particular result is the most desirable or beneficial.","example":"I'm not sure that's the best outcome for all stakeholders involved.","plain":"I doubt that's the most desirable result for all stakeholders involved.","note":"Used to question the desirability of a result, often implying negative consequences.","audio":"audio/794f0fb5d0e1f8ea.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-best-solution","text":"I'm not sure that's the best solution","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the best solution","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt about the optimal answer or resolution to a problem.","example":"I'm not sure that's the best solution for our long-standing customer service issues.","plain":"I doubt that's the best answer for our long-standing customer service issues.","note":"A common and polite way to suggest an alternative without direct criticism.","audio":"audio/882b146af2ff86ba.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-best-use-of","text":"I'm not sure that's the best use of","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's the best use of","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt about the efficiency or effectiveness of resource allocation.","example":"I'm not sure that's the best use of our limited time and resources.","plain":"I doubt that's the most efficient use of our limited time and resources.","note":"A pragmatic way to express disagreement, focusing on resource optimization.","audio":"audio/4b1a06364c293eb0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-best-way","text":"I'm not sure that's the best way","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the best way","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt about the optimal method or approach to a task.","example":"I'm not sure that's the best way to handle client complaints; it might escalate things.","plain":"I doubt that's the best method to handle client complaints; it might escalate things.","note":"A common and polite way to suggest an alternative without direct criticism.","audio":"audio/c63c7c3b6594ce76.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-case","text":"I'm not sure that's the case","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the case","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt about the truth or reality of a situation or statement.","example":"I'm not sure that's the case; our internal data suggests otherwise.","plain":"I doubt that's true; our internal data suggests otherwise.","note":"A common and polite way to express disagreement with a factual claim.","audio":"audio/bee62225da2d612c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-main-issue","text":"I'm not sure that's the main issue","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the main issue","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Implying that the identified problem is not the most significant one, or there's a deeper cause.","example":"I'm not sure that's the main issue; I think the root cause lies elsewhere.","plain":"I doubt that's the primary problem; I think the root cause lies elsewhere.","note":"Suggests a more profound analysis is needed, subtly redirecting the discussion.","audio":"audio/13614adcdc41b5fd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-most-appropriate","text":"I'm not sure that's the most appropriate","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's the most appropriate","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt about the suitability or correctness of an action or response.","example":"I'm not sure that's the most appropriate response to the client's feedback.","plain":"I doubt that's the most suitable response to the client's feedback.","note":"A polite way to suggest a different, more fitting course of action.","audio":"audio/0a2457d6100ef682.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-most-appropriate-response","text":"I'm not sure that's the most appropriate response","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the most appropriate response","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt about the suitability or correctness of a reaction or reply.","example":"I'm not sure that's the most appropriate response to the recent market downturn.","plain":"I doubt that's the most suitable response to the recent market downturn.","note":"A polite way to suggest a different, more fitting reaction.","audio":"audio/b430a14f2e18ab64.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-most-effective","text":"I'm not sure that's the most effective","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's the most effective","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt about the efficiency or success of a method or strategy.","example":"I'm not sure that's the most effective way to communicate with our global teams.","plain":"I doubt that's the most successful way to communicate with our global teams.","note":"A pragmatic way to express disagreement, focusing on results and impact.","audio":"audio/327b5ff10d98e2bb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-most-effective-approach","text":"I'm not sure that's the most effective approach","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the most effective approach","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt about the efficiency or success of a method or strategy.","example":"I'm not sure that's the most effective approach for engaging with our new clients.","plain":"I doubt that's the most successful method for engaging with our new clients.","note":"A pragmatic way to express disagreement, focusing on results and impact.","audio":"audio/93d57f0fdf8e0843.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-most-effective-way","text":"I'm not sure that's the most effective way","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's the most effective way","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Questions the efficiency or success of a proposed method, suggesting alternatives might be better.","example":"I'm not sure that's the most effective way to reach our target audience; we need a broader campaign.","plain":"I don't think that's the best way to reach our target audience; we need a broader campaign.","note":"A polite way to challenge the efficacy of a plan or method.","audio":"audio/a2305ad0c7b26e95.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-most-efficient","text":"I'm not sure that's the most efficient","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's the most efficient","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt about the productivity or resourcefulness of a method or process.","example":"I'm not sure that's the most efficient way to manage our inventory.","plain":"I doubt that's the most productive way to manage our inventory.","note":"A pragmatic way to express disagreement, focusing on optimization.","audio":"audio/b8736d7b10313808.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-most-practical","text":"I'm not sure that's the most practical","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's the most practical","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt about the feasibility or realism of a plan or idea.","example":"I'm not sure that's the most practical solution for our small team.","plain":"I doubt that's the most feasible solution for our small team.","note":"A pragmatic way to express disagreement, focusing on real-world limitations.","audio":"audio/a1f730939c63fbfd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-most-viable-option","text":"I'm not sure that's the most viable option","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's the most viable option","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the practicality or feasibility of a choice, suggesting it might not work.","example":"I'm not sure that's the most viable option given our current financial constraints.","plain":"I don't think that's the most practical option given our current financial constraints.","note":"A polite way to question the feasibility or long-term success of a choice.","audio":"audio/036c23dae5864527.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-only-consideration","text":"I'm not sure that's the only consideration","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the only consideration","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Implying that other factors or aspects should also be taken into account.","example":"I'm not sure that's the only consideration; we also need to think about ethics.","plain":"I doubt that's the only thing to consider; we also need to think about ethics.","note":"Broadens the scope of discussion, challenging a narrow focus.","audio":"audio/77401d52fa1a89f3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-only-explanation","text":"I'm not sure that's the only explanation","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the only explanation","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Implying that there might be other reasons or causes for a situation.","example":"I'm not sure that's the only explanation for the recent drop in engagement.","plain":"I doubt that's the only reason for the recent drop in engagement.","note":"Suggests a more nuanced understanding is required, challenging a singular cause.","audio":"audio/b3f7d2b44a10ae2f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-only-factor","text":"I'm not sure that's the only factor","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the only factor","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Implying that an explanation is incomplete and other elements should be considered.","example":"I'm not sure that's the only factor contributing to the decline in sales.","plain":"I doubt that's the only reason for the decline in sales.","note":"Suggests a more complex reality, subtly challenging a simplistic explanation.","audio":"audio/e507f49a2cd4d04d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-only-option","text":"I'm not sure that's the only option","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the only option","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Implying that there are other possibilities that should be considered.","example":"I'm not sure that's the only option; we might have other avenues to explore.","plain":"I doubt that's the only choice; we might have other avenues to explore.","note":"Encourages further brainstorming and exploration of alternatives.","audio":"audio/632127619bd45d28.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-optimal-solution","text":"I'm not sure that's the optimal solution","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's the optimal solution","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Questions whether a proposed solution is the best possible one, implying there might be better options.","example":"I'm not sure that's the optimal solution; we might find a more efficient way to achieve this.","plain":"I don't think that's the best solution; we might find a more efficient way to achieve this.","note":"A polite way to challenge the effectiveness or superiority of a proposed solution.","audio":"audio/60614833268b7a7d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-right-approach","text":"I'm not sure that's the right approach","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the right approach","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt about the suitability or effectiveness of a method or strategy.","example":"I'm not sure that's the right approach for engaging with our key stakeholders.","plain":"I doubt that's the best method for engaging with our key stakeholders.","note":"A common and polite way to suggest an alternative without direct criticism.","audio":"audio/84c8824c27a7e5c8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-right-call","text":"I'm not sure that's the right call","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the right call","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt about the correctness or wisdom of a decision or judgment.","example":"I'm not sure that's the right call for our long-term brand image.","plain":"I doubt that's the correct decision for our long-term brand image.","note":"Informal but professional. Used to question a decision without being overly critical.","audio":"audio/77e78cfd8163d9ca.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-right-conclusion","text":"I'm not sure that's the right conclusion","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the right conclusion","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt about the correctness or validity of a final judgment or inference.","example":"I'm not sure that's the right conclusion based on all the available evidence.","plain":"I doubt that's the correct conclusion based on all the available evidence.","note":"Used to question the logical outcome of an argument or analysis.","audio":"audio/440b186f7cfa329e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-right-decision","text":"I'm not sure that's the right decision","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the right decision","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt about the correctness or wisdom of a choice made.","example":"I'm not sure that's the right decision for the long-term health of the company.","plain":"I doubt that's the correct decision for the long-term health of the company.","note":"A common and polite way to question a choice without being overly critical.","audio":"audio/f51bbdaa6644916c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-right-direction","text":"I'm not sure that's the right direction","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the right direction","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt about the overall course of action or strategy.","example":"I'm not sure that's the right direction for our company in the long run.","plain":"I doubt that's the correct path for our company in the long run.","note":"Used to question strategic choices or long-term plans.","audio":"audio/7b8472feb281bd71.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-right-fit","text":"I'm not sure that's the right fit","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's the right fit","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the suitability or appropriateness of something for a particular context.","example":"I'm not sure that's the right fit for our company culture, despite its technical merits.","plain":"I don't think that's suitable for our company culture, despite its technical merits.","note":"A polite way to express reservations about compatibility or appropriateness.","audio":"audio/a09f2f96b7088c0c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-right-priority","text":"I'm not sure that's the right priority","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the right priority","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt about the importance or urgency assigned to a task or goal.","example":"I'm not sure that's the right priority given our current strategic objectives.","plain":"I doubt that's the correct priority given our current strategic objectives.","note":"Used to question the allocation of focus or resources.","audio":"audio/b7d4dac6d3635734.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-right-track","text":"I'm not sure that's the right track","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's the right track","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the chosen direction or strategy, suggesting it might be incorrect.","example":"I'm not sure that's the right track for our long-term growth; we need to diversify.","plain":"I don't think that's the correct direction for our long-term growth; we need to diversify.","note":"A polite way to question the overall direction of a plan or discussion.","audio":"audio/b87470c981a65312.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-right-way-forward","text":"I'm not sure that's the right way forward","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the right way forward","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt about the best path or strategy for future actions.","example":"I'm not sure that's the right way forward for our team's development.","plain":"I doubt that's the best path for our team's development.","note":"Used to question strategic direction or future plans.","audio":"audio/fa3bbed011e2e5f8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-whole-picture","text":"I'm not sure that's the whole picture","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's the whole picture","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Suggests that important information or context is missing from the current discussion.","example":"I'm not sure that's the whole picture; we need to consider the long-term market trends too.","plain":"I think there's more to it; we need to consider the long-term market trends too.","note":"Implies that the current argument is incomplete, inviting further discussion.","audio":"audio/86281e77d394a1e5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-whole-story","text":"I'm not sure that's the whole story","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the whole story","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Implying that there are other facts or considerations that haven't been mentioned.","example":"I'm not sure that's the whole story; there might be other factors at play.","plain":"I think there's more to it; there might be other factors at play.","note":"Suggests a deeper understanding or additional information, subtly challenging completeness.","audio":"audio/f0282f73fc3040ee.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-we-can","text":"I'm not sure we can","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure we can","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expressing doubt about the feasibility or possibility of an action.","example":"I'm not sure we can meet that deadline without compromising quality.","plain":"I doubt we can meet that deadline without compromising quality.","note":"A polite way to express concern about capability or resources.","audio":"audio/309dcf472f393b07.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-were-on-the-same-page","text":"I'm not sure we're on the same page","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure we're on the same page","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Indicating that there might be a misunderstanding or disagreement in understanding.","example":"I'm not sure we're on the same page regarding the project's ultimate goal.","plain":"I think we might have different understandings of the project's ultimate goal.","note":"A common idiom for identifying a potential disagreement or miscommunication.","audio":"audio/b1136e703ca330f6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-were-seeing-eye-to-eye","text":"I'm not sure we're seeing eye to eye","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure we're seeing eye to eye","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Indicates a difference in opinion or perspective, suggesting a need for further discussion.","example":"I'm not sure we're seeing eye to eye on the priorities for this quarter.","plain":"I don't think we agree on the priorities for this quarter.","note":"A common idiom to politely point out a disagreement in views.","audio":"audio/ca2c64dedb01dc58.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-what-to-say","text":"I'm not sure what to say","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure what to say","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a feeling of being overwhelmed, surprised, or at a loss for words, often in response to unexpected news.","example":"When they told me I got the scholarship, I was so shocked, I'm not sure what to say.","plain":"When they told me I got the scholarship, I was so shocked, I didn't know how to react.","note":"Used to convey a strong emotional reaction that leaves one speechless.","audio":"audio/fa8903002576622f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-of-the-view-that","text":"I'm of the view that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm of the view that","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"States the speaker's opinion or belief in a formal and considered manner.","example":"I'm of the view that collaborative projects foster a more dynamic learning environment.","plain":"I believe that collaborative projects foster a more dynamic learning environment.","note":"A formal and slightly more British English way to express an opinion.","audio":"audio/2f84a68650c7d1ea.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-open-to","text":"I'm open to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm open to","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Indicates a willingness to consider or accept new ideas, suggestions, or changes.","example":"I'm open to discussing alternative solutions for the software integration.","plain":"I am willing to discuss alternative solutions for the software integration.","note":"Shows flexibility and a collaborative spirit, encouraging further proposals.","audio":"audio/98147917a2099ab5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-open-to-feedback","text":"I'm open to feedback","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm open to feedback","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Indicates a willingness to receive and consider comments or criticism.","example":"I'm open to feedback on my presentation style for future improvements.","plain":"I welcome comments on my presentation style for future improvements.","note":"A proactive statement showing a desire for self-improvement. Professional.","audio":"audio/d06d49ff09c59f00.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-open-to-hearing","text":"I'm open to hearing","kind":"frame","form":"I'm open to hearing","level":"C1","situations":["feedback","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To express willingness to listen to different ideas or opinions.","example":"I'm open to hearing any alternative solutions you might have for this problem.","plain":"I am willing to listen to any alternative solutions you might have for this problem.","note":"Encourages participation and diverse input.","audio":"audio/b0cf18515bebc14d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-open-to-suggestions-on","text":"I'm open to suggestions on","kind":"frame","form":"I'm open to suggestions on","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to indicate a willingness to receive and consider ideas or advice about a particular topic.","example":"I'm open to suggestions on how we can improve the efficiency of our weekly team meetings.","plain":"I'm willing to consider ideas on how we can improve the efficiency of our weekly team meetings.","note":"Encourages collaborative problem-solving and shows a receptive attitude. Positive.","audio":"audio/b461aa0bca55512a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-optimistic-about","text":"I'm optimistic about","kind":"frame","form":"I'm optimistic about","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Expresses a positive outlook or hope for a particular situation or future event.","example":"I'm optimistic about the potential for growth in this new market segment.","plain":"I have a positive outlook on the potential for growth in this new market segment.","note":"Conveys a positive and forward-looking attitude, often in strategic discussions.","audio":"audio/58fdd2c28c0e5654.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-optimistic-that-we-can","text":"I'm optimistic that we can","kind":"frame","form":"I'm optimistic that we can","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To express a positive outlook on the possibility of success.","example":"I'm optimistic that we can find a workable solution if we collaborate effectively.","plain":"I have a positive outlook that we can find a workable solution if we collaborate effectively.","note":"Used to convey hope and positive expectation.","audio":"audio/a8c9604460804252.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-particularly-impressed-with","text":"I'm particularly impressed with","kind":"frame","form":"I'm particularly impressed with","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Highlights a specific aspect of someone's performance that is especially admirable.","example":"I'm particularly impressed with how you managed to resolve that difficult client issue.","plain":"I am especially impressed with how you managed to resolve that difficult client issue.","note":"Adds emphasis to praise, making it very specific and impactful.","audio":"audio/2228c9032dadab51.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-pleased-to-hear","text":"I'm pleased to hear","kind":"frame","form":"I'm pleased to hear","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","feedback"],"meaning":"Used to express pleasure or satisfaction about something you have just learned.","example":"I'm pleased to hear that your project is progressing so well and on schedule.","plain":"Your project is progressing so well and on schedule, which makes me happy.","note":"A slightly more formal alternative to \"I'm glad to hear,\" expressing satisfaction.","audio":"audio/a6f71e30694bc16e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-pleased-to-say","text":"I'm pleased to say","kind":"frame","form":"I'm pleased to say","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling","feedback"],"meaning":"Introduces positive news or a positive statement.","example":"I'm pleased to say that the team exceeded all expectations this quarter.","plain":"I am happy to announce that the team exceeded all expectations this quarter.","note":"A formal and positive way to deliver good news or praise. Professional.","audio":"audio/04fab1a75029cb77.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-prepared-to","text":"I'm prepared to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm prepared to","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Indicates a willingness to do something, often a concession or a firm action.","example":"I'm prepared to offer a discount if you sign the contract by Friday.","plain":"I am willing to offer a discount if you sign the contract by Friday.","note":"Shows readiness to act, often used when making a conditional offer.","audio":"audio/03990eed67e26125.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-pretty-flexible","text":"I'm pretty flexible","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm pretty flexible","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Indicating that one's schedule is adaptable and open to various times or dates.","example":"I'm pretty flexible next week, so just let me know what day suits you best.","plain":"My schedule is open next week, so tell me what day is best.","note":"A common, friendly way to show willingness to accommodate others' schedules.","audio":"audio/e19f9b7e4e5d71e5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-annoyed-by","text":"I'm quite annoyed by","kind":"frame","form":"I'm quite annoyed by","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a moderate level of irritation or displeasure caused by something.","example":"I'm quite annoyed by the constant interruptions during my presentation.","plain":"I'm moderately irritated by the constant interruptions during my presentation.","note":"Specifies the cause of annoyance; direct but not overly aggressive.","audio":"audio/ee20191a70de6845.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-concerned","text":"I'm quite concerned","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm quite concerned","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a moderate level of worry or anxiety about a situation.","example":"I'm quite concerned about the recent drop in sales figures for this quarter.","plain":"I'm somewhat worried about the recent drop in sales figures for this quarter.","note":"A polite and professional way to express worry without being alarmist.","audio":"audio/42d717a241bbd9ef.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-content","text":"I'm quite content","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm quite content","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a state of moderate happiness and satisfaction.","example":"After a long day, I'm quite content just to relax at home with a book.","plain":"After a long day, I'm moderately happy just to relax at home with a book.","note":"Describes a peaceful and satisfied state of mind.","audio":"audio/71cc58fde632be2b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-content-with","text":"I'm quite content with","kind":"frame","form":"I'm quite content with","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a state of moderate happiness and satisfaction regarding something specific.","example":"I'm quite content with my current work-life balance, it feels just right.","plain":"I'm moderately happy with my current work-life balance, it feels just right.","note":"Describes a peaceful and satisfied state of mind concerning a particular aspect.","audio":"audio/167922e8ec84a7f6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-disappointed-by","text":"I'm quite disappointed by","kind":"frame","form":"I'm quite disappointed by","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a moderate level of unhappiness or dissatisfaction caused by something.","example":"I'm quite disappointed by the lack of communication from the project team.","plain":"I'm moderately unhappy with the lack of communication from the project team.","note":"Specifies the cause of disappointment; polite but direct.","audio":"audio/326684b54276d68c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-disappointed-with","text":"I'm quite disappointed with","kind":"frame","form":"I'm quite disappointed with","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Expressing a strong but polite feeling of let-down.","example":"I'm quite disappointed with the lack of communication about the delay.","plain":"I'm very disappointed about the lack of communication about the delay.","note":"A polite but clear way to express significant dissatisfaction.","audio":"audio/a6b7414bc090e7b3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-impressed","text":"I'm quite impressed","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm quite impressed","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses admiration or respect for something or someone.","example":"I'm quite impressed with how quickly you mastered the new software.","plain":"I'm rather admiring of how quickly you mastered the new software.","note":"A polite and common way to give a compliment or express admiration.","audio":"audio/6126da4e686ac0e8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-intrigued","text":"I'm quite intrigued","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm quite intrigued","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses curiosity and interest in something.","example":"I'm quite intrigued by your proposal for the new marketing campaign.","plain":"I'm moderately curious about your proposal for the new marketing campaign.","note":"Shows intellectual interest; polite and professional.","audio":"audio/a474ad0b5e4bee30.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-intrigued-by","text":"I'm quite intrigued by","kind":"frame","form":"I'm quite intrigued by","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses curiosity and interest in something specific.","example":"I'm quite intrigued by the new research findings on renewable energy.","plain":"I'm moderately curious about the new research findings on renewable energy.","note":"Shows intellectual interest in a particular subject or discovery.","audio":"audio/4844edc71a5f74d0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-looking-forward-to","text":"I'm quite looking forward to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm quite looking forward to","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses eager anticipation and positive expectation for a future event.","example":"I'm quite looking forward to the company retreat next month.","plain":"I'm really anticipating the company retreat next month.","note":"A common and polite way to express excitement about a future event.","audio":"audio/939b48ffd990cf92.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-optimistic","text":"I'm quite optimistic","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm quite optimistic","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a moderate belief that good things will happen.","example":"Despite the challenges, I'm quite optimistic about the future of our startup.","plain":"Despite the challenges, I'm moderately hopeful about the future of our startup.","note":"Indicates a positive outlook; polite and professional.","audio":"audio/c420a26471d1c405.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-optimistic-about","text":"I'm quite optimistic about","kind":"frame","form":"I'm quite optimistic about","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a moderate belief that good things will happen regarding something specific.","example":"I'm quite optimistic about the chances of our proposal being accepted.","plain":"I'm moderately hopeful about the chances of our proposal being accepted.","note":"Indicates a positive outlook for a particular situation or outcome.","audio":"audio/fdecefad642631ba.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-pleased","text":"I'm quite pleased","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm quite pleased","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses moderate satisfaction or happiness.","example":"I'm quite pleased with the outcome of the negotiations, all things considered.","plain":"I'm moderately satisfied with the outcome of the negotiations, all things considered.","note":"A polite and slightly formal way to express contentment.","audio":"audio/3e5abe348d2874ff.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-pleased-with","text":"I'm quite pleased with","kind":"frame","form":"I'm quite pleased with","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a moderate level of satisfaction or contentment regarding an outcome or situation.","example":"I'm quite pleased with how the team handled the unexpected challenges.","plain":"I'm rather satisfied with how the team handled the unexpected challenges.","note":"A polite and common way to express positive evaluation or contentment.","audio":"audio/d778b91bd4e5528b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-put-out","text":"I'm quite put out","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm quite put out","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses mild annoyance or inconvenience, often feeling slightly offended.","example":"I'm quite put out that they changed the meeting time without telling me.","plain":"I'm moderately annoyed that they changed the meeting time without telling me.","note":"Indicates irritation, often with a sense of being unfairly treated.","audio":"audio/1bc1e37e80729460.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-put-out-by","text":"I'm quite put out by","kind":"frame","form":"I'm quite put out by","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses mild annoyance or inconvenience, often feeling slightly offended by something.","example":"I'm quite put out by the lack of recognition for our team's efforts.","plain":"I'm moderately annoyed by the lack of recognition for our team's efforts.","note":"Specifies the cause of irritation, often implying unfairness.","audio":"audio/d000752baa6811b5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-put-out-by-the-fact-that","text":"I'm quite put out by the fact that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm quite put out by the fact that","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses mild annoyance or inconvenience, often feeling slightly offended by a specific fact.","example":"I'm quite put out by the fact that they didn't consult us before making the decision.","plain":"I'm moderately annoyed by the fact that they didn't consult us before making the decision.","note":"Specifies the cause of irritation, often implying a breach of expectation.","audio":"audio/b0a1348059ce875a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-relieved","text":"I'm quite relieved","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm quite relieved","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a moderate feeling of comfort and ease after worry.","example":"I'm quite relieved that the project deadline has been extended by a week.","plain":"I'm moderately comforted that the project deadline has been extended by a week.","note":"A common and natural expression of relief for less intense situations.","audio":"audio/ef01b7415391c10f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-relieved-that","text":"I'm quite relieved that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm quite relieved that","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses moderate comfort and ease because a specific worry has been removed.","example":"I'm quite relieved that the project budget was approved without any cuts.","plain":"I'm moderately comforted that the project budget was approved without any cuts.","note":"A common and natural expression of relief for a specific positive outcome.","audio":"audio/b07f88f04ca97eae.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-relieved-to-hear","text":"I'm quite relieved to hear","kind":"frame","form":"I'm quite relieved to hear","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses moderate comfort and ease upon receiving reassuring news.","example":"I'm quite relieved to hear that your surgery went well and you're recovering.","plain":"I'm moderately comforted to hear that your surgery went well and you're recovering.","note":"A common and natural expression of relief for less intense situations.","audio":"audio/aa77a03bc812b988.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-surprised-at","text":"I'm quite surprised at","kind":"frame","form":"I'm quite surprised at","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses moderate astonishment or unexpectedness regarding something specific.","example":"I'm quite surprised at the sudden increase in demand for our product.","plain":"I'm moderately astonished at the sudden increase in demand for our product.","note":"Similar to \"surprised by,\" but often used for a specific event or statistic.","audio":"audio/5bd33a213bf42155.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-surprised-by","text":"I'm quite surprised by","kind":"frame","form":"I'm quite surprised by","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a moderate level of astonishment or unexpectedness regarding something.","example":"I'm quite surprised by how quickly they adapted to the new software.","plain":"I'm rather astonished by how quickly they adapted to the new software.","note":"Used to convey a genuine, but not overwhelming, sense of surprise.","audio":"audio/95f8b1053414cf1d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-surprised-to-hear","text":"I'm quite surprised to hear","kind":"frame","form":"I'm quite surprised to hear","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses moderate astonishment upon receiving unexpected news.","example":"I'm quite surprised to hear that they're moving to another country next year.","plain":"I'm moderately astonished to hear that they're moving to another country next year.","note":"A common and polite way to react to unexpected verbal information.","audio":"audio/29cce1e5ff1e572e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-taken-by","text":"I'm quite taken by","kind":"frame","form":"I'm quite taken by","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a strong positive impression or attraction to something or someone.","example":"I'm quite taken by her innovative approach to problem-solving.","plain":"I have a strong positive impression of her innovative approach to problem-solving.","note":"Similar to \"quite taken with,\" often used for qualities or characteristics.","audio":"audio/f5270aebb640581c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-taken-by-surprise","text":"I'm quite taken by surprise","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm quite taken by surprise","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a moderate level of astonishment or unexpectedness.","example":"I'm quite taken by surprise by the sudden announcement of the merger.","plain":"I'm moderately astonished by the sudden announcement of the merger.","note":"Similar to \"quite surprised,\" emphasizing the unexpected nature of the event.","audio":"audio/455a9df4dccecb70.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-taken-with","text":"I'm quite taken with","kind":"frame","form":"I'm quite taken with","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a strong positive impression, admiration, or liking for something or someone.","example":"I'm quite taken with the new design of the office space; it's very modern.","plain":"I'm rather impressed by the new design of the office space; it's very modern.","note":"A slightly more formal way to express strong positive feelings or admiration.","audio":"audio/c35cd8a90759f0ab.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-taken-with-the-concept","text":"I'm quite taken with the concept","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm quite taken with the concept","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a strong positive impression or attraction to a general idea.","example":"I'm quite taken with the concept of flexible working hours for all employees.","plain":"I have a strong positive impression of the general idea of flexible working hours for all employees.","note":"Indicates a strong liking or interest in a broad idea or principle.","audio":"audio/dd50f096adc898a0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-taken-with-the-idea-of","text":"I'm quite taken with the idea of","kind":"frame","form":"I'm quite taken with the idea of","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a strong positive impression or attraction to a concept or plan.","example":"I'm quite taken with the idea of collaborating with that innovative startup.","plain":"I have a strong positive impression of the idea of collaborating with that innovative startup.","note":"Indicates a strong liking or interest in a particular concept or plan.","audio":"audio/c98fbc584ec87f2f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-taken-with-the-idea-of-doing","text":"I'm quite taken with the idea of doing","kind":"frame","form":"I'm quite taken with the idea of doing","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a strong positive impression or attraction to a specific action or activity.","example":"I'm quite taken with the idea of doing a sabbatical next year to travel.","plain":"I have a strong positive impression of the idea of doing a sabbatical next year to travel.","note":"Indicates a strong liking or interest in a particular activity or plan.","audio":"audio/9390483f9614dac2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-taken-with-the-notion","text":"I'm quite taken with the notion","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm quite taken with the notion","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a strong positive impression or attraction to a specific thought or belief.","example":"I'm quite taken with the notion that creativity can be taught and developed.","plain":"I have a strong positive impression of the thought that creativity can be taught and developed.","note":"Indicates a strong liking or interest in a particular belief or abstract idea.","audio":"audio/d6d79282b766ca9d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-touched-by","text":"I'm quite touched by","kind":"frame","form":"I'm quite touched by","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a feeling of emotional warmth, gratitude, or sympathy in response to someone's kindness or story.","example":"I'm quite touched by your thoughtful gesture of bringing me flowers.","plain":"I'm rather moved by your thoughtful gesture of bringing me flowers.","note":"Used to convey a sincere emotional reaction to kindness or a moving story.","audio":"audio/34516dc29359f167.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-upset","text":"I'm quite upset","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm quite upset","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a moderate level of distress or unhappiness.","example":"I'm quite upset about the way I was treated during the customer service call.","plain":"I'm moderately distressed about the way I was treated during the customer service call.","note":"Indicates emotional disturbance, but not necessarily extreme anger.","audio":"audio/47c18338ac9aefe6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-upset-about","text":"I'm quite upset about","kind":"frame","form":"I'm quite upset about","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a moderate level of distress or unhappiness regarding a specific issue.","example":"I'm quite upset about the recent changes to our company's benefits package.","plain":"I'm moderately distressed about the recent changes to our company's benefits package.","note":"Specifies the cause of upset; direct but not overly emotional.","audio":"audio/ad73b4e4f1cc554b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-rather-concerned","text":"I'm rather concerned","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm rather concerned","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a moderate level of worry or anxiety, often with a formal tone.","example":"I'm rather concerned about the potential impact of these new regulations.","plain":"I'm somewhat worried about the potential impact of these new regulations.","note":"Slightly more formal than \"quite concerned,\" suitable for serious topics.","audio":"audio/65b7436d6f9766f8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-rather-concerned-about-the-implications","text":"I'm rather concerned about the implications","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm rather concerned about the implications","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses moderate worry about the potential consequences of something.","example":"I'm rather concerned about the implications of this new policy on our long-term strategy.","plain":"I'm somewhat worried about the consequences of this new policy on our long-term strategy.","note":"A formal way to express worry about future effects, often in professional contexts.","audio":"audio/d2474d24c7a400ea.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-rather-disappointed","text":"I'm rather disappointed","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm rather disappointed","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a moderate level of unhappiness or dissatisfaction.","example":"I'm rather disappointed that the event was cancelled at the last minute.","plain":"I'm somewhat unhappy that the event was cancelled at the last minute.","note":"A polite and slightly formal way to express dissatisfaction.","audio":"audio/317a8e9164164fc1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-rather-pleased","text":"I'm rather pleased","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm rather pleased","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses moderate satisfaction or happiness, often with a hint of formality.","example":"I'm rather pleased with the progress we've made on the project this week.","plain":"I'm somewhat satisfied with the progress we've made on the project this week.","note":"Slightly more formal than \"quite pleased,\" often used in professional contexts.","audio":"audio/b2b922a2a1d15312.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-rather-pleased-with","text":"I'm rather pleased with","kind":"frame","form":"I'm rather pleased with","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses moderate satisfaction or happiness about something specific.","example":"I'm rather pleased with the progress we've made on the new software development.","plain":"I'm somewhat satisfied with the progress we've made on the new software development.","note":"Slightly more formal than \"quite pleased with,\" often in professional contexts.","audio":"audio/625dda5985b570f2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-really-keen-to","text":"I'm really keen to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm really keen to","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing strong enthusiasm or eagerness to do something.","example":"I'm really keen to try that new restaurant, so thanks for the invitation!","plain":"I'm very eager to try that new restaurant, so thanks for the invitation!","note":"Shows genuine enthusiasm; common in both British and American English.","audio":"audio/81f19791800eda61.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-really-sorry-but","text":"I'm really sorry but","kind":"frame","form":"I'm really sorry but","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing sincere regret before delivering a refusal or bad news.","example":"I'm really sorry but I won't be able to join you for dinner.","plain":"I apologize, but I won't be able to join you for dinner.","note":"A strong apology, indicating genuine regret for the refusal.","audio":"audio/7f4d2552dc987450.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-relieved-to-say","text":"I'm relieved to say","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm relieved to say","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Introduces a piece of good news or a positive outcome that alleviates previous worry.","example":"I'm relieved to say that the test results came back negative.","plain":"I'm happy to say that the test results came back negative.","note":"Used to share reassuring news, often after a period of anxiety.","audio":"audio/d88ae0fd666f51e3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-returning-your-call","text":"I'm returning your call","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm returning your call","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I am calling you back in response to a previous call you made to me.","example":"Hi John, I'm returning your call about the project update.","plain":"Hi John, I'm calling you back about the project update.","note":"A standard opening phrase when you are calling someone back who previously called you.","audio":"audio/25a6143cdfbec008.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-running-a-bit-late","text":"I'm running a bit late","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm running a bit late","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To inform someone that you will arrive after the scheduled time.","example":"I'm running a bit late for my reservation due to unexpected traffic.","plain":"I will be late for my reservation.","note":"Common informal apology for tardiness. \"A bit\" softens it.","audio":"audio/0e9efb48d6093359.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-so-glad-we-could","text":"I'm so glad we could","kind":"frame","form":"I'm so glad we could","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing happiness or relief that a shared activity or meeting was possible.","example":"I'm so glad we could find a time to get together; it's been too long.","plain":"I'm very happy we found a time to meet; it's been too long.","note":"A warm and appreciative phrase, often used at the beginning or end of a social gathering.","audio":"audio/a2fb933135f40ac2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-so-pleased-to-hear","text":"I'm so pleased to hear","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm so pleased to hear","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses genuine happiness and satisfaction upon receiving good news or information.","example":"I'm so pleased to hear that your project was approved; you've put in a lot of effort.","plain":"I'm very happy that your project was approved; you've put in a lot of effort.","note":"A warm and sincere way to react positively to someone's good news or success.","audio":"audio/3c2c2839fc98208e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-so-sorry-to-bother-you","text":"I'm so sorry to bother you","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm so sorry to bother you","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses polite regret before interrupting or making a request.","example":"I'm so sorry to bother you, but could you spare a moment to look at this?","plain":"I regret interrupting you, but could you spare a moment to look at this?","note":"A common and polite softener when initiating an interaction or request.","audio":"audio/f6a75bbf791d88a4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-so-sorry-to-have-to","text":"I'm so sorry to have to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm so sorry to have to","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses regret before performing an unpleasant or difficult action.","example":"I'm so sorry to have to ask you to work late again, but it's urgent.","plain":"I regret that I must ask you to work late again, but it's urgent.","note":"A polite way to introduce an unwelcome request or task.","audio":"audio/31c8858e62345e57.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-so-sorry-to-hear-about","text":"I'm so sorry to hear about","kind":"frame","form":"I'm so sorry to hear about","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses sympathy or regret regarding a specific negative event or situation.","example":"I'm so sorry to hear about your recent health issues; I hope you recover soon.","plain":"I regret hearing about your recent health issues; I hope you recover soon.","note":"A common and empathetic way to respond to bad news about someone's situation.","audio":"audio/3b095d9bffc8eb01.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-sorry-to-hear-about-that","text":"I'm sorry to hear about that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm sorry to hear about that","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses sympathy or regret upon receiving bad news.","example":"Oh, I'm sorry to hear about that; your flight was cancelled, which is really inconvenient.","plain":"Oh, I regret hearing that; your flight was cancelled, which is really inconvenient.","note":"A common, polite way to express sympathy for minor inconveniences or bad news.","audio":"audio/051f3b44ed319535.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-struggling-to-understand","text":"I'm struggling to understand","kind":"frame","form":"I'm struggling to understand","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Expressing significant difficulty in comprehending something.","example":"I'm struggling to understand the rationale behind this sudden change.","plain":"I'm having a lot of difficulty understanding why this change happened so suddenly.","note":"A more emphatic way to express confusion than \"I'm not clear.\"","audio":"audio/2ae4ebae86ef84f1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-struggling-with","text":"I'm struggling with","kind":"frame","form":"I'm struggling with","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To express that you are finding something very challenging or hard to manage.","example":"I'm struggling with the online check-in system; it keeps showing an error.","plain":"I'm finding the online check-in system very difficult.","note":"Implies a greater degree of difficulty than \"having trouble with\".","audio":"audio/b2c59349ed5de776.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-sure-you-understand","text":"I'm sure you understand","kind":"frame","form":"I'm sure you understand","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Assumes the listener comprehends a situation, often used before stating a difficult truth.","example":"I'm sure you understand that budget constraints mean we can't pursue all ideas.","plain":"I believe you understand that budget constraints mean we can't pursue all ideas.","note":"Can soften difficult news by implying shared understanding, but can also sound slightly condescending if overused.","audio":"audio/dee0abf859fdadfc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-sure-youll-agree","text":"I'm sure you'll agree","kind":"frame","form":"I'm sure you'll agree","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Used to express an opinion that one expects others to share, seeking consensus.","example":"I'm sure you'll agree that clear communication is vital for team success.","plain":"I believe you will agree that clear communication is vital for team success.","note":"A persuasive phrase, often used to build agreement around a point of feedback.","audio":"audio/d2827a6e113a6808.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-swamped-with-work","text":"I'm swamped with work","kind":"collocation","form":"I'm swamped with work","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing that one has an overwhelming amount of work to do.","example":"I'd love to join, but I'm swamped with work this week.","plain":"I want to come, but I have too much work to do this week.","note":"A common and understandable reason for declining social invitations.","audio":"audio/c3703016414c40f4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-taking-notes","text":"I'm taking notes","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm taking notes","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Indicates active listening and a commitment to remembering the discussion points.","example":"I'm taking notes to ensure I remember all your valuable feedback on my performance.","plain":"I am writing things down to ensure I remember all your valuable feedback on my performance.","note":"Reassures the speaker that their feedback is being seriously considered.","audio":"audio/b9a74b51b1433009.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-tied-up-with","text":"I'm tied up with","kind":"collocation","form":"I'm tied up with","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Explaining that you are busy or committed to another activity.","example":"I'm tied up with work commitments all next week, unfortunately.","plain":"I am busy with work commitments all next week, unfortunately.","note":"A common and natural way to explain unavailability due to other obligations.","audio":"audio/c8bed72b0d74ab47.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-truly-amazed","text":"I'm truly amazed","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm truly amazed","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses sincere wonder and astonishment.","example":"I'm truly amazed by your dedication to learning a new language so quickly.","plain":"I'm sincerely astonished by your dedication to learning a new language so quickly.","note":"Conveys strong positive surprise and admiration.","audio":"audio/e20e24d3af409664.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-truly-amazed-by","text":"I'm truly amazed by","kind":"frame","form":"I'm truly amazed by","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses sincere wonder and astonishment caused by something specific.","example":"I'm truly amazed by the resilience of the human spirit in challenging times.","plain":"I'm sincerely astonished by the resilience of the human spirit in challenging times.","note":"Conveys strong positive surprise and admiration for a specific quality or action.","audio":"audio/a3cf2a574bc8c91b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-truly-grateful","text":"I'm truly grateful","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm truly grateful","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses sincere appreciation and thankfulness.","example":"For all your help with the move, I'm truly grateful; I couldn't have done it alone.","plain":"For all your help with the move, I'm sincerely thankful; I couldn't have done it alone.","note":"A more formal and emphatic way to express thanks than \"thank you.\"","audio":"audio/d6b1a0e2f449c5e7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-truly-grateful-for","text":"I'm truly grateful for","kind":"frame","form":"I'm truly grateful for","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses sincere appreciation and thankfulness for something specific.","example":"I'm truly grateful for your unwavering support throughout this challenging period.","plain":"I'm sincerely thankful for your unwavering support throughout this challenging period.","note":"A more formal and emphatic way to express thanks for a specific act.","audio":"audio/a04ce60d160e96e2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-truly-sorry-for-any-trouble","text":"I'm truly sorry for any trouble","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm truly sorry for any trouble","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses sincere regret for causing difficulty or problems.","example":"I'm truly sorry for any trouble my late submission may have caused the team.","plain":"I sincerely regret any difficulty my late submission may have caused the team.","note":"A polite and formal apology for potential or actual problems.","audio":"audio/ee1542b95cba20a1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-truly-sorry-for-the-inconvenience","text":"I'm truly sorry for the inconvenience","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm truly sorry for the inconvenience","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses sincere regret for causing trouble or difficulty.","example":"I'm truly sorry for the inconvenience caused by the system outage yesterday.","plain":"I sincerely regret the trouble caused by the system outage yesterday.","note":"A formal and polite apology for disruption, often in professional settings.","audio":"audio/1cda5dba6503ae6a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-truly-sorry-for-your-loss","text":"I'm truly sorry for your loss","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm truly sorry for your loss","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses deep sympathy and condolences to someone who has experienced a death.","example":"Please accept my deepest sympathies; I'm truly sorry for your loss.","plain":"Please accept my deepest sympathies; I deeply regret your bereavement.","note":"A standard and respectful phrase for expressing condolences.","audio":"audio/3567b1696fbfe60d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-truly-sorry-to-hear","text":"I'm truly sorry to hear","kind":"frame","form":"I'm truly sorry to hear","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses deep sympathy or regret upon receiving unfortunate news.","example":"I'm truly sorry to hear that your grandmother is unwell; please send her my best.","plain":"I deeply regret hearing that your grandmother is unwell; please send her my best.","note":"More emphatic than \"I'm sorry to hear,\" conveying deeper concern.","audio":"audio/1025b54c91a0b193.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-truly-sorry-to-inform-you","text":"I'm truly sorry to inform you","kind":"frame","form":"I'm truly sorry to inform you","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses deep regret before delivering formal bad news.","example":"I'm truly sorry to inform you that your application was unsuccessful this time.","plain":"I deeply regret to inform you that your application was unsuccessful this time.","note":"A formal and polite way to deliver negative news, often in writing or official settings.","audio":"audio/ea66d08bc9348982.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-truly-sorry-to-say","text":"I'm truly sorry to say","kind":"frame","form":"I'm truly sorry to say","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses deep regret before delivering bad news or an unpleasant statement.","example":"I'm truly sorry to say that we won't be able to approve your request this time.","plain":"I deeply regret to say that we won't be able to approve your request this time.","note":"A polite but firm way to deliver negative information.","audio":"audio/265f1012f1ecd2db.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-trying-to-figure-out","text":"I'm trying to figure out","kind":"frame","form":"I'm trying to figure out","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Explaining that you are attempting to understand or solve something.","example":"I'm trying to figure out the best route to the museum on foot.","plain":"I'm attempting to find the best route to the museum on foot.","note":"Common when you are actively working to understand a problem.","audio":"audio/05e8b4f6013dde48.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-trying-to-get-a-refund","text":"I'm trying to get a refund","kind":"frame","form":"I'm trying to get a refund","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state your intention to recover money paid for a service or product.","example":"I'm trying to get a refund for the cancelled tour, as I paid upfront.","plain":"I want to get my money back for the cancelled tour.","note":"Direct and clear statement of purpose when seeking a refund.","audio":"audio/deccaf94c3bb202a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-trying-to-grasp","text":"I'm trying to grasp","kind":"frame","form":"I'm trying to grasp","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Expressing that one is making an effort to understand something complex.","example":"I'm trying to grasp the implications of these new regulations.","plain":"I'm trying to understand the implications of these new regulations.","note":"Shows active engagement in understanding a difficult topic.","audio":"audio/62621099730a5d5b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-trying-to-locate","text":"I'm trying to locate","kind":"frame","form":"I'm trying to locate","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To politely state that you are attempting to find the exact position of something.","example":"I'm trying to locate the baggage claim area for flight BA249.","plain":"I'm looking for the baggage claim area.","note":"More formal and precise than \"I'm looking for\".","audio":"audio/1643a2f26b47fc3d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-trying-to-orient-myself","text":"I'm trying to orient myself","kind":"frame","form":"I'm trying to orient myself","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To attempt to understand your position relative to your surroundings.","example":"I'm trying to orient myself with this map; the streets here are quite confusing.","plain":"I'm trying to understand where I am using this map.","note":"More formal than \"get my bearings\". Useful when navigating.","audio":"audio/4dc82094c22e776a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-trying-to-understand-why","text":"I'm trying to understand why","kind":"frame","form":"I'm trying to understand why","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to express a desire to comprehend the reasons or causes behind a situation or decision.","example":"I'm trying to understand why the previous team decided to use this outdated system.","plain":"I want to understand the reasons why the previous team decided to use this outdated system.","note":"Shows a desire for clarity and root cause analysis. Non-confrontational.","audio":"audio/889174680aab1b3b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-unable-to","text":"I'm unable to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm unable to","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite and slightly formal way to state an inability to do something.","example":"I'm unable to provide a definitive answer on that matter until I consult with legal.","plain":"I cannot provide a definitive answer on that matter until I consult with legal.","note":"More formal than \"I can't,\" used to politely decline or state a limitation.","audio":"audio/2e1415e3e13eaea1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-unavailable-right-now","text":"I'm unavailable right now","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm unavailable right now","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I am currently not able to take your call or speak with you.","example":"I'm unavailable right now, but please leave a message after the tone.","plain":"I cannot speak at this moment, but please leave a message.","note":"A standard phrase used in voicemail greetings or automated responses.","audio":"audio/293e6fef1d24272d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-very-impressed-with","text":"I'm very impressed with","kind":"frame","form":"I'm very impressed with","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Expresses strong admiration for a specific achievement or quality.","example":"I'm very impressed with your ability to adapt to the sudden changes in the project scope.","plain":"Your ability to adapt to the sudden changes in the project scope is very impressive.","note":"Stronger than 'I'm impressed by,' highlighting exceptional performance.","audio":"audio/22ac6b32fa934d50.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-willing-to-go-as-far-as","text":"I'm willing to go as far as","kind":"frame","form":"I'm willing to go as far as","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"States the maximum concession or offer one is personally prepared to make.","example":"I'm willing to go as far as a 5% reduction, but that's my limit.","plain":"I am prepared to offer up to a 5% reduction, but that's my limit.","note":"Sets a personal boundary, indicating a firm but not entirely inflexible position.","audio":"audio/3d0399314d156eab.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-with-you-so-far","text":"I'm with you so far","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm with you so far","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I understand what you've said up to this point in your explanation.","example":"Yes, I'm with you so far; please continue with the next step.","plain":"Yes, I understand what you've said; please explain the next step.","note":"A useful phrase to indicate comprehension and encourage the speaker to proceed.","audio":"audio/1a334f00a565d98f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ive-been-meaning-to-ask","text":"I've been meaning to ask","kind":"frame","form":"I've been meaning to ask","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Introducing a question or topic that one has intended to bring up for some time.","example":"I've been meaning to ask you about your new job; how's it going?","plain":"I wanted to ask you about your new job; how's it going?","note":"A natural way to transition to a new topic or ask a specific question.","audio":"audio/5becf47b4f35a639.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.identify-bottlenecks","text":"identify bottlenecks","kind":"collocation","form":"identify bottlenecks","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To pinpoint points in a process where work slows down or stops, causing delays.","example":"Our analysis helped us identify bottlenecks in the production line that were causing delays.","plain":"Our analysis helped us pinpoint points in the production line where work slowed down, causing delays.","note":"Crucial for process improvement and efficiency. Technical and professional.","audio":"audio/7dab3d661e70e928.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.if-i-may","text":"if I may","kind":"chunk","form":"If I may","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A polite way to ask for permission to speak or interrupt.","example":"If I may, I think we're overlooking a crucial aspect of the budget.","plain":"May I speak? I think we're overlooking a crucial aspect of the budget.","note":"Very polite, often used to soften an interruption or strong opinion.","audio":"audio/ab8f0550061b905e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.if-i-may-add","text":"if I may add","kind":"chunk","form":"If I may add","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to contribute an additional point or piece of information to a discussion.","example":"If I may add, the market research also indicates a strong demand for this feature.","plain":"The market research also indicates a strong demand for this feature.","note":"Formal and polite, used to contribute to a conversation without interrupting rudely.","audio":"audio/0d3c04c3b850bf3a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.if-i-may-be-so-bold","text":"if I may be so bold","kind":"chunk","form":"If I may be so bold","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A very polite and formal way to introduce a potentially daring or presumptuous suggestion or question.","example":"If I may be so bold, perhaps we should consider a complete overhaul of the current system.","plain":"Perhaps we should consider a complete overhaul of the current system.","note":"Very formal, used when offering a strong opinion or suggestion that might challenge the status quo.","audio":"audio/5a823f768e0c0120.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.if-i-may-interject","text":"if I may interject","kind":"chunk","form":"If I may interject","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","negotiation","small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to interrupt someone to add a point or ask a question.","example":"If I may interject, I have a quick question about the budget allocation.","plain":"I have a quick question about the budget allocation.","note":"Formal and polite, used to politely break into a conversation.","audio":"audio/e96e0c659492c70a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.if-i-may-just-comment","text":"If I may just comment","kind":"chunk","form":"If I may just comment","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"A polite way to ask for permission to speak or add a remark.","example":"If I may just comment, I think we should consider the long-term implications.","plain":"May I add a comment? I think we should consider the long-term implications.","note":"A formal and deferential way to interject.","audio":"audio/36ff41d8f7f08745.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.if-i-recall-correctly","text":"if I recall correctly","kind":"chunk","form":"if I recall correctly","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","storytelling"],"meaning":"Used to introduce a piece of information that the speaker believes to be true, but isn't entirely certain of.","example":"If I recall correctly, the deadline for that report was actually next Friday, not this Friday.","plain":"The deadline for that report was actually next Friday, not this Friday.","note":"A polite way to offer information while acknowledging a slight possibility of error.","audio":"audio/9b8f6b19fd73651a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.if-i-understand-correctly","text":"If I understand correctly","kind":"frame","form":"If I understand correctly","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","small-talk","disagreeing","interviews","presenting","calls"],"meaning":"Seeks clarification before potentially disagreeing, ensuring the point of contention is clear.","example":"If I understand correctly, we're proposing to cut the training budget entirely?","plain":"Am I right that we're proposing to cut the training budget entirely?","note":"A useful phrase to confirm understanding, often preceding a polite challenge or question.","audio":"audio/3b61db9fb1c72a60.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.if-im-being-honest","text":"if I'm being honest","kind":"frame","form":"If I'm being honest","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","small-talk","socialising"],"meaning":"A slightly more emphatic way to introduce a sincere or candid opinion, often a mild criticism.","example":"If I'm being honest, I found the presentation a bit confusing.","plain":"I found the presentation a bit confusing.","note":"Adds a touch more emphasis than 'to be honest'; can soften a critical remark.","audio":"audio/d1330ec3a9565bfd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.if-im-not-mistaken","text":"if I'm not mistaken","kind":"chunk","form":"if I'm not mistaken","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","travel"],"meaning":"Used to introduce a piece of information that the speaker believes to be true, but isn't entirely certain of.","example":"If I'm not mistaken, you used to work for a competitor a few years back.","plain":"You used to work for a competitor a few years back.","note":"A polite way to offer information while acknowledging a slight possibility of error.","audio":"audio/6a8ca7c9b10d9e45.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.if-that-makes-sense","text":"If that makes sense","kind":"chunk","form":"if that makes sense","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Asks for confirmation that the listener understands or agrees with a point.","example":"We need to prioritize efficiency over speed, if that makes sense.","plain":"We need to prioritize efficiency over speed, do you understand?","note":"A common softener to check for listener comprehension or agreement.","audio":"audio/3b13c550e150dcb2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.if-you-catch-my-drift","text":"if you catch my drift","kind":"chunk","form":"if you catch my drift","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to check if the listener understands an indirect suggestion or implication.","example":"We need to be more proactive with our clients, if you catch my drift, before they look elsewhere.","plain":"We need to be more proactive with our clients before they look elsewhere.","note":"Informal, implies a subtle or indirect message, often used to avoid being too direct.","audio":"audio/cf57eaa414d1f764.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.if-you-dont-mind-me-asking","text":"if you don't mind me asking","kind":"frame","form":"If you don't mind me asking","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to introduce a potentially personal or sensitive question, seeking permission first.","example":"If you don't mind me asking, how long have you been working in this department?","plain":"How long have you been working in this department?","note":"A polite softener for questions; shows respect for the other person's privacy.","audio":"audio/9964eab057deb488.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.if-you-will","text":"if you will","kind":"chunk","form":"if you will","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","presenting"],"meaning":"To soften a statement or suggest an analogy, inviting the listener to accept the phrasing.","example":"We need to create a new framework, a blueprint, if you will, for future development.","plain":"We need to create a new framework, a blueprint, for future development.","note":"Adds a touch of politeness or self-correction; often used with metaphors.","audio":"audio/59fdea4e3de8c9aa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.if-youll-excuse-me","text":"if you'll excuse me","kind":"chunk","form":"If you'll excuse me","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to ask for permission to leave or to briefly step away from a conversation.","example":"If you'll excuse me, I need to make a quick phone call before the meeting starts.","plain":"I need to make a quick phone call before the meeting starts.","note":"A polite and common way to temporarily or permanently exit a conversation.","audio":"audio/8728bdfeaf907a75.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.implement-a-strategy","text":"implement a strategy","kind":"collocation","form":"implement a strategy","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To put a carefully planned course of action into effect.","example":"Once we finalize the plan, we'll need to implement a strategy for its rollout across all departments.","plain":"Once we finalize the plan, we'll need to put a course of action into effect for its rollout across all departments.","note":"Refers to the execution phase of planning. Formal and business-oriented.","audio":"audio/fea67181212a50a9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.in-broad-terms","text":"In broad terms","kind":"chunk","form":"In broad terms","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Describes something generally, without going into specific details or fine points.","example":"In broad terms, the project aims to enhance community engagement through digital platforms.","plain":"Generally, the project aims to enhance community engagement through digital platforms.","note":"Useful for providing an overview before delving into specifics.","audio":"audio/60a7094f082920c0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.in-contrast-to","text":"In contrast to","kind":"chunk","form":"In contrast to","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a point that is significantly different from what was just mentioned.","example":"In contrast to earlier theories, this new model emphasizes dynamic interactions.","plain":"This new model emphasizes dynamic interactions, unlike earlier theories.","note":"A formal way to highlight a difference or opposition.","audio":"audio/a19fd7bc2d0eef46.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.in-future-perhaps","text":"in future perhaps","kind":"frame","form":"In future perhaps","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Suggests a different approach for subsequent occasions, softening the advice.","example":"In future perhaps, we could involve the marketing team earlier in the design process.","plain":"Next time, we could involve the marketing team earlier in the design process.","note":"A polite way to suggest an improvement for the next time, avoiding direct criticism of past actions.","audio":"audio/cc2288effe09b1e4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.in-hindsight","text":"In hindsight","kind":"chunk","form":"In hindsight","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling","feedback"],"meaning":"Refers to understanding an event or situation after it has happened.","example":"In hindsight, I should have taken a different route to avoid traffic.","plain":"Looking back, I should have taken a different route to avoid traffic.","note":"Often used to express regret or a clearer understanding of past decisions.","audio":"audio/d301f7e9186815c3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.in-light-of-this","text":"In light of this","kind":"chunk","form":"In light of this","level":"C1","situations":["academic","presenting"],"meaning":"Considering the information or situation just mentioned.","example":"Our sales figures are down this quarter. In light of this, we must revise our strategy.","plain":"Our sales figures are down this quarter, so we must revise our strategy.","note":"Formal and logical connector, indicating a consequence or response.","audio":"audio/feb69cb85c0ed437.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.in-line-with","text":"In line with","kind":"chunk","form":"in line with","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Indicates that something is consistent with or in agreement with something else.","example":"Our findings are in line with previous studies on the impact of climate change.","plain":"Our findings are consistent with previous studies on the impact of climate change.","note":"A formal way to express agreement or consistency.","audio":"audio/a4773efe3b61f995.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.in-our-mutual-interest","text":"in our mutual interest","kind":"collocation","form":"in our mutual interest","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"Something that benefits all parties involved.","example":"It's in our mutual interest to collaborate on this project for a successful outcome.","plain":"It benefits all of us to collaborate on this project for a successful outcome.","note":"Used to appeal to shared benefits and encourage cooperation.","audio":"audio/b822218cfc39a527.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.in-simple-terms","text":"in simple terms","kind":"chunk","form":"in simple terms","level":"C1","situations":["presenting","explaining"],"meaning":"To explain something using easy-to-understand language, avoiding complexity.","example":"The algorithm optimizes data flow; in simple terms, it makes our internet faster.","plain":"The algorithm optimizes data flow; it makes our internet faster.","note":"Similar to \"to put it simply,\" but often used when contrasting with technical language.","audio":"audio/cb3e52bc722c5b03.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.in-some-respects","text":"In some respects","kind":"chunk","form":"In some respects","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Specifies that a statement is true or relevant only when considering certain aspects or features.","example":"In some respects, the two models are comparable, though their underlying assumptions differ significantly.","plain":"The two models are comparable in certain ways, though their underlying assumptions differ significantly.","note":"Similar to \"to some extent\" but focuses on specific aspects rather than degree.","audio":"audio/fd94152df60a4008.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.in-the-end","text":"In the end","kind":"chunk","form":"In the end","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Indicates the final outcome or conclusion of a series of events.","example":"In the end, we decided to postpone the launch until next quarter.","plain":"Finally, we decided to postpone the launch until next quarter.","note":"A common discourse marker for concluding a narrative.","audio":"audio/aaeaa7743cd0cf09.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.in-the-grand-scheme-of-things","text":"in the grand scheme of things","kind":"chunk","form":"in the grand scheme of things","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to put a particular issue into a broader perspective, often suggesting it's not as important as it seems.","example":"While this delay is frustrating, in the grand scheme of things, it won't derail the entire project.","plain":"This delay is frustrating, but it won't derail the entire project.","note":"Helps to manage expectations and reduce anxiety by providing a wider context.","audio":"audio/ee670b5083951a9a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.in-the-spirit-of-cooperation","text":"In the spirit of cooperation","kind":"chunk","form":"In the spirit of cooperation","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To suggest that actions should be taken with a willingness to work together and compromise.","example":"In the spirit of cooperation, we should find a solution that suits both teams.","plain":"Because we want to cooperate, we should find a solution that suits both teams.","note":"A diplomatic phrase used to encourage compromise and teamwork. Formal and conciliatory.","audio":"audio/1dc0aa7dc08095b1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.in-xs-view","text":"In X's view","kind":"chunk","form":"In the committee's view","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Presents an opinion or perspective held by a specific person or group.","example":"In the committee's view, the proposed changes are necessary for long-term sustainability.","plain":"The committee believes the proposed changes are necessary for long-term sustainability.","note":"Used to attribute an opinion, often in a formal context.","audio":"audio/07308b0ff8315fcc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.is-it-fair-to-say","text":"Is it fair to say","kind":"frame","form":"Is it fair to say","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Asks for confirmation of an interpretation, often to gently challenge an underlying assumption.","example":"Is it fair to say that we're prioritizing speed over thoroughness in this process?","plain":"Are we prioritizing speed over thoroughness in this process?","note":"A rhetorical question that prompts reflection on a potentially problematic aspect.","audio":"audio/624754091625252a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.is-that-clear-enough","text":"Is that clear enough","kind":"chunk","form":"Is that clear enough","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To ask if the explanation provided is sufficiently detailed and unambiguous.","example":"I've outlined the steps for the project. Is that clear enough, or should I add more detail?","plain":"I've outlined the steps for the project. Is that sufficiently clear, or should I add more detail?","note":"Direct and practical, often used after giving instructions or a summary.","audio":"audio/3d282de2aa730593.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.is-that-making-sense-so-far","text":"Is that making sense so far","kind":"chunk","form":"Is that making sense so far","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To check for understanding at an intermediate point in a longer explanation.","example":"I've covered the first two steps. Is that making sense so far, before we move on?","plain":"I've covered the first two steps. Do you understand what I've said up to now, before we move on?","note":"Good for breaking down complex information and ensuring continuous comprehension.","audio":"audio/365661c89b8886fd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.is-there-a-shuttle-service","text":"Is there a shuttle service","kind":"chunk","form":"Is there a shuttle service","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Asking if transport is provided, usually to/from an airport or attraction.","example":"We need to get to the airport; is there a shuttle service available?","plain":"We need to get to the airport; is transport provided?","note":"Useful for hotels or resorts that might offer complimentary transport.","audio":"audio/9dbe385551adf7de.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.is-there-any-chance","text":"Is there any chance","kind":"frame","form":"Is there any chance","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite and hopeful way to ask if something is possible, often when it seems unlikely.","example":"Is there any chance we could get a later check-out time tomorrow morning?","plain":"Can we get a later check-out time?","note":"Use when making a request that might be outside standard procedure.","audio":"audio/48773960dda56729.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-all-boiled-down-to","text":"It all boiled down to","kind":"frame","form":"It all boiled down to","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Explains the fundamental reason or core issue of a complex situation.","example":"It all boiled down to a misunderstanding about who was responsible for the task.","plain":"The main reason was a misunderstanding about who was responsible for the task.","note":"Useful for simplifying a complex problem to its essence.","audio":"audio/de55e54e0de39382.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-all-came-about","text":"It all came about","kind":"chunk","form":"It all came about","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Explains how a situation or event originated or developed.","example":"It all came about because we decided to take a spontaneous road trip.","plain":"The situation happened because we decided to take a spontaneous road trip.","note":"Useful for explaining the origins of a complex situation.","audio":"audio/f97f6fdd862a2a26.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-all-started-when","text":"It all started when","kind":"frame","form":"It all started when","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"A common way to introduce the beginning of an anecdote or story.","example":"It all started when I decided to take that last-minute trip.","plain":"The story began when I decided to take that last-minute trip.","note":"A classic opening for a personal narrative, setting the scene.","audio":"audio/5ee1a39e47a5c98e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-keeps-cutting-out","text":"it keeps cutting out","kind":"chunk","form":"It keeps cutting out","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"The audio or video connection is repeatedly failing and restoring, causing interruptions.","example":"I can't understand you because it keeps cutting out every few words.","plain":"I can't understand you; the connection is repeatedly failing.","note":"A common way to describe intermittent connection problems affecting audio or video.","audio":"audio/cac70fc401b32cfa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-makes-me-wonder","text":"It makes me wonder","kind":"frame","form":"It makes me wonder","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses a thought or question that arises from the discussion, often implying a doubt.","example":"Given the recent market changes, it makes me wonder if our current strategy is still optimal.","plain":"Given market changes, I question if our current strategy is still optimal.","note":"A reflective way to introduce a doubt or a need for further consideration.","audio":"audio/8a82914a947250ec.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-might-be-more-effective","text":"It might be more effective","kind":"frame","form":"It might be more effective","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Suggests an alternative action or method that could yield better results.","example":"It might be more effective to communicate this change directly to the affected employees.","plain":"Communicating this change directly to the affected employees might work better.","note":"A gentle way to propose an improvement or a different strategy.","audio":"audio/f864ba01c555cc5e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-raises-a-question","text":"It raises a question","kind":"collocation","form":"It raises a question","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Indicates that a point or statement prompts a query or doubt.","example":"Your analysis is thorough, but it raises a question about the long-term sustainability of this model.","plain":"Your analysis is thorough, but I have a question about the long-term sustainability of this model.","note":"A polite way to introduce a query that might challenge an assumption or conclusion.","audio":"audio/471c7ace48f24e27.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-remains-to-be-seen","text":"It remains to be seen","kind":"frame","form":"It remains to be seen","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Indicates that the outcome or truth of something is not yet known and will become clear in the future.","example":"It remains to be seen whether these reforms will genuinely improve educational outcomes.","plain":"We don't yet know if these reforms will genuinely improve educational outcomes.","note":"Used to express uncertainty about future developments.","audio":"audio/a40b749cfff97a94.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-seems-to-me","text":"It seems to me","kind":"frame","form":"It seems to me","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","disagreeing","feedback","persuading"],"meaning":"Presents a personal opinion or observation in a softened, less assertive way.","example":"It seems to me that we haven't fully explored all the potential risks involved.","plain":"I think we haven't fully explored all the potential risks involved.","note":"A common softener, making a statement sound like a personal impression rather than a definitive fact.","audio":"audio/36b404b1aba20952.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-sounds-a-bit-muffled","text":"it sounds a bit muffled","kind":"chunk","form":"It sounds a bit muffled","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Your voice is unclear and indistinct, as if something is covering the microphone.","example":"Could you adjust your microphone? It sounds a bit muffled.","plain":"Please fix your microphone; your voice is unclear.","note":"A polite way to suggest someone's audio quality isn't optimal, often due to mic placement.","audio":"audio/beaeddd2b8f570bb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-sounds-wonderful-but","text":"It sounds wonderful but","kind":"frame","form":"It sounds wonderful but","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Complimenting the invitation or idea before explaining why you cannot accept.","example":"It sounds wonderful but I'm afraid I have a prior engagement.","plain":"That sounds wonderful but I'm afraid I have a prior engagement.","note":"A polite softener, showing appreciation for the offer even if you can't accept.","audio":"audio/e731c6b7cb0863fb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-strikes-me-that","text":"It strikes me that","kind":"frame","form":"It strikes me that","level":"C1","situations":["academic","feedback"],"meaning":"Introduces a thought or realization that has just occurred to the speaker.","example":"It strikes me that we haven't fully considered the long-term societal impact of this technology.","plain":"I just realized that we haven't fully considered the long-term societal impact of this technology.","note":"Used to present a sudden insight or observation.","audio":"audio/0c4333e9c10482df.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-turned-out-that","text":"It turned out that","kind":"frame","form":"It turned out that","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Reveals the eventual outcome or truth of a situation.","example":"We thought he was busy, but it turned out that he was just on vacation.","plain":"We thought he was busy, but he was actually just on vacation.","note":"Similar to 'as it turned out', but often used for a more definitive revelation.","audio":"audio/a27f301f321fd6d4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-turned-out-to-be","text":"It turned out to be","kind":"frame","form":"It turned out to be","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Reveals the unexpected or final nature of something after a period of uncertainty.","example":"It turned out to be a much simpler solution than we had initially thought.","plain":"The solution was much simpler than we had initially thought.","note":"Used to disclose the actual outcome or nature of something.","audio":"audio/5249691b7d8b1d64.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-a-case-of","text":"It was a case of","kind":"frame","form":"It was a case of","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Describes a situation as an example of a particular type.","example":"It was a case of being in the wrong place at the wrong time, really.","plain":"I was in the wrong place at the wrong time.","note":"Often used to explain unfortunate or coincidental events.","audio":"audio/b65d8c84f3d1343c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-building-up-to","text":"It was building up to","kind":"frame","form":"It was building up to","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Indicates that events were gradually leading to a significant climax or outcome.","example":"It was building up to a major confrontation between the two departments.","plain":"Events were leading to a major confrontation between the two departments.","note":"Suggests a gradual increase in intensity or importance.","audio":"audio/0282d2983c40ff5d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-good-catching-up","text":"It was good catching up","kind":"chunk","form":"It was good catching up","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"A friendly way to express enjoyment of the conversation, especially with someone known.","example":"Thanks for your time, Sarah; it was good catching up with you.","plain":"Thanks for your time, Sarah; I enjoyed talking with you.","note":"Informal and friendly, suitable for colleagues or acquaintances.","audio":"audio/077ef295bac14b7d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-lovely-catching-up","text":"It was lovely catching up","kind":"chunk","form":"It was lovely catching up","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite and warm way to express enjoyment after a conversation or meeting.","example":"Thanks for the coffee, it was lovely catching up with you.","plain":"Thank you for coffee, I really enjoyed talking and getting updated with you.","note":"A common closing remark after a social interaction, especially with friends.","audio":"audio/36719692b1d12d82.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-nice-talking-to-you","text":"it was nice talking to you","kind":"chunk","form":"It was nice talking to you","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A common and polite phrase used to end a conversation, expressing enjoyment of the chat.","example":"Right, I should let you get back to it. It was nice talking to you!","plain":"Right, I should let you get back to it. I enjoyed talking to you!","note":"A standard and friendly way to conclude a conversation gracefully.","audio":"audio/4c23584e0be9a1e2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-only-a-matter-of-time","text":"It was only a matter of time","kind":"chunk","form":"It was only a matter of time","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Suggests an event was inevitable or highly likely to occur.","example":"It was only a matter of time before someone noticed the error in the report.","plain":"Someone was going to notice the error in the report eventually.","note":"Implies a sense of inevitability or impending consequence.","audio":"audio/60f4ad5ded90a713.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-touch-and-go","text":"It was touch and go","kind":"chunk","form":"It was touch and go","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Describes a situation with an uncertain outcome, often critical.","example":"For a while, it was touch and go whether the project would succeed.","plain":"For a while, the project's success was very uncertain.","note":"Conveys a sense of suspense and precariousness.","audio":"audio/d783c1c17c0c6147.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-would-seem-that","text":"It would seem that","kind":"frame","form":"It would seem that","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing","academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a cautious observation or conclusion, suggesting it's likely but not definitive.","example":"It would seem that our initial hypothesis requires further refinement based on these new data points.","plain":"Our initial hypothesis probably needs more refinement based on these new data points.","note":"A polite, academic way to present a conclusion without stating it as absolute fact.","audio":"audio/35c9f4d29803cdc4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-bit-frustrating","text":"It's a bit frustrating","kind":"collocation","form":"It's a bit frustrating","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Describes a situation that causes mild annoyance or irritation due to obstacles or lack of progress.","example":"It's a bit frustrating when you put in so much effort and don't see immediate results.","plain":"It's slightly annoying when you put in so much effort and don't see immediate results.","note":"A common and understated way to express mild irritation or impatience.","audio":"audio/ab720c8862f406fb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-common-misconception-that","text":"It's a common misconception that","kind":"frame","form":"It's a common misconception that","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Introduces a widely held but incorrect belief.","example":"It's a common misconception that all successful people work 24/7.","plain":"Many people wrongly believe that all successful people work 24/7.","note":"Used to correct a popular misunderstanding.","audio":"audio/9d44a3db756e7f91.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-fine-line-between","text":"It's a fine line between","kind":"frame","form":"It's a fine line between","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","storytelling"],"meaning":"Describes a subtle distinction between two things that are easily confused.","example":"It's a fine line between being confident and being arrogant.","plain":"There's a subtle difference between being confident and being arrogant.","note":"Highlights a delicate balance or distinction.","audio":"audio/ae58b50fd0ab26da.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-real-pity","text":"It's a real pity","kind":"collocation","form":"It's a real pity","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses genuine regret or sadness about an unfortunate situation or missed opportunity.","example":"It's a real pity that they had to cancel the annual conference this year.","plain":"It's truly regrettable that they had to cancel the annual conference this year.","note":"A common phrase to express sincere regret or disappointment.","audio":"audio/4be1293e42d369b1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-shame-i-cant","text":"It's a shame I can't","kind":"frame","form":"It's a shame I can't","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing regret or disappointment about being unable to do something.","example":"It's a shame I can't join you for the concert, as I already bought tickets for another show.","plain":"I'm disappointed I can't join you for the concert, as I already bought tickets for another show.","note":"Conveys regret without over-apologizing, often used when declining.","audio":"audio/c43f47f931e6c1f5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-tricky-situation","text":"It's a tricky situation","kind":"collocation","form":"It's a tricky situation","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling","reacting"],"meaning":"Describes a complex or difficult circumstance that requires careful handling or thought.","example":"It's a tricky situation, and I'm not sure what the best course of action is.","plain":"It's a difficult situation, and I'm not sure what the best course of action is.","note":"A common way to acknowledge complexity and express a need for careful consideration.","audio":"audio/d4461d7d50e84b56.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-also-worth-considering","text":"It's also worth considering","kind":"frame","form":"It's also worth considering","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To suggest an additional point or factor that should be taken into account.","example":"It's also worth considering the potential impact on our international partners.","plain":"We should also think about the potential impact on our international partners.","note":"Used to introduce a secondary but important point for discussion.","audio":"audio/90b3e1f4e124ae73.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-been-a-productive-call","text":"it's been a productive call","kind":"collocation","form":"It's been a productive call","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"The phone call or video conference has achieved its goals and yielded good results.","example":"Thank you for your input; it's been a productive call, and we've made good decisions.","plain":"Thanks for your input; the call was fruitful, and we made good decisions.","note":"A positive closing remark, indicating that the time spent on the call was worthwhile.","audio":"audio/23b151cc5986cad5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-been-hectic","text":"it's been hectic","kind":"chunk","form":"It's been hectic","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to describe a period of intense activity, busyness, or stress.","example":"How's work? It's been hectic lately with all the new projects.","plain":"How's work? It's been very busy lately with all the new projects.","note":"A common way to explain a lack of free time or energy.","audio":"audio/a853ef32cb1c9cc2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-been-too-long","text":"It's been too long","kind":"chunk","form":"It's been too long","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing that a significant amount of time has passed since the last meeting.","example":"Oh, Sarah! It's been too long, how have you been?","plain":"Sarah! We haven't seen each other in a very long time, how are you?","note":"A warm, friendly greeting used when reuniting with someone after a long absence.","audio":"audio/eae3b9055e8ad3ae.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-crucial-to","text":"It's crucial to","kind":"frame","form":"It's crucial to","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To emphasize that something is extremely important or essential to do.","example":"It's crucial to maintain open communication channels throughout this transition period.","plain":"It's extremely important to maintain open communication channels throughout this transition period.","note":"A strong way to highlight the importance of an action. Formal and emphatic.","audio":"audio/86f7a8b7b93e148e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-crucial-to-recognise","text":"It's crucial to recognise","kind":"frame","form":"It's crucial to recognise","level":"C1","situations":["academic","presenting"],"meaning":"Points out that a particular fact or aspect is extremely important to acknowledge.","example":"It's crucial to recognise the limitations of this methodology in diverse populations.","plain":"It's very important to acknowledge the limitations of this methodology.","note":"Stronger than \"important to note\"; implies a fundamental understanding is needed.","audio":"audio/6a36b7cda48dff40.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-crucial-to-understand","text":"It's crucial to understand","kind":"frame","form":"It's crucial to understand","level":"C1","situations":["presenting","persuading"],"meaning":"To emphasise that a particular piece of information is extremely important.","example":"It's crucial to understand the customer's needs before developing a solution.","plain":"It's very important to understand the customer's needs before developing a solution.","note":"Strong emphasis on the necessity of comprehension.","audio":"audio/037c9867a330d36d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-essential-to-consider","text":"It's essential to consider","kind":"frame","form":"It's essential to consider","level":"C1","situations":["presenting","problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to stress the critical importance of taking a particular factor into account.","example":"It's essential to consider the long-term implications of this decision, not just the immediate benefits.","plain":"It's critically important to take into account the long-term implications of this decision.","note":"A strong way to emphasize the necessity of thorough evaluation. Formal and impactful.","audio":"audio/d6bfaade1bbd1a72.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-fair-to-assume","text":"It's fair to assume","kind":"frame","form":"It's fair to assume","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Introduces a reasonable inference or conclusion based on available information.","example":"It's fair to assume that everyone has read the meeting minutes by now.","plain":"We can reasonably assume that everyone has read the meeting minutes by now.","note":"Used to present a logical and likely conclusion.","audio":"audio/9d0c33e8baaf7220.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-good-to-hear-from-you","text":"It's good to hear from you","kind":"chunk","form":"It's good to hear from you","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing pleasure at receiving communication from someone, often after a period of silence.","example":"Oh, hi Sarah! It's good to hear from you; I was just thinking about you.","plain":"Oh, hi Sarah! I'm happy you contacted me; I was just thinking about you.","note":"A standard, friendly greeting when someone calls, texts, or emails after a gap.","audio":"audio/d894f87a26405f83.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-great-to-finally","text":"It's great to finally","kind":"frame","form":"It's great to finally","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing satisfaction or relief that something long-awaited or desired is now happening.","example":"It's great to finally meet you in person after all our emails.","plain":"I'm very happy to meet you in person after all our emails.","note":"Used when an anticipated event or meeting is taking place.","audio":"audio/488069a52178ba77.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-hard-to-say","text":"It's hard to say","kind":"chunk","form":"It's hard to say","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing uncertainty or difficulty in giving a definitive answer or opinion.","example":"\"Will they come?\" \"It's hard to say, they're usually quite busy.\"","plain":"\"Will they come?\" \"I'm not sure, they're usually quite busy.\"","note":"A common way to acknowledge uncertainty without committing to an answer.","audio":"audio/d6fba581973d4fcf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-imperative-that","text":"It's imperative that","kind":"frame","form":"It's imperative that","level":"C1","situations":["presenting","persuading"],"meaning":"To state that something is absolutely necessary or urgent and must be done.","example":"It's imperative that we secure additional funding before the end of the fiscal year.","plain":"It's absolutely necessary that we secure additional funding before the end of the fiscal year.","note":"A very strong way to express necessity, often in formal or serious contexts. Authoritative.","audio":"audio/0af2de23c1b77321.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-acknowledge","text":"It's important to acknowledge","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to acknowledge","level":"C1","situations":["academic","presenting","persuading"],"meaning":"Highlights the necessity of recognizing or admitting a particular fact or perspective.","example":"It's important to acknowledge the limitations of our methodology when interpreting these results.","plain":"We must recognize the limitations of our methodology when interpreting these results.","note":"Used to ensure a balanced and fair assessment, often before presenting a counter-point.","audio":"audio/9a4b599cf3d10744.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-acknowledge-the-inherent-biases","text":"It's important to acknowledge the inherent biases","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to acknowledge the inherent biases","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Stresses the need to recognize the natural or built-in prejudices or leanings within a system or study.","example":"It's important to acknowledge the inherent biases in self-reported survey data.","plain":"We must recognize the natural biases in self-reported survey data.","note":"Essential for critical evaluation and transparent research.","audio":"audio/66e290c30468d063.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-acknowledge-the-inherent-subjectivity","text":"It's important to acknowledge the inherent subjectivity","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to acknowledge the inherent subjectivity","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Stresses the need to recognize that something is naturally influenced by personal perspectives.","example":"It's important to acknowledge the inherent subjectivity in qualitative data interpretation.","plain":"We must recognize the natural influence of personal perspectives in qualitative data.","note":"Used in fields where personal interpretation is a key factor.","audio":"audio/3d638444d94e3406.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-acknowledge-the-inherent-trade-offs","text":"It's important to acknowledge the inherent trade-offs","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to acknowledge the inherent trade-offs","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Stresses the need to recognize that achieving one thing often means sacrificing another.","example":"It's important to acknowledge the inherent trade-offs between speed and accuracy in data processing.","plain":"We must recognize the natural compromises between speed and accuracy in data processing.","note":"Used in decision-making contexts where choices involve costs.","audio":"audio/3a1b237a1a9fb9fe.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-acknowledge-the-interplay-between","text":"It's important to acknowledge the interplay between","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to acknowledge the interplay between","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Stresses the need to recognize how two or more things influence each other.","example":"It's important to acknowledge the interplay between economic factors and social outcomes.","plain":"We must recognize how economic factors and social outcomes influence each other.","note":"Used for complex systems where multiple factors interact.","audio":"audio/8e824e82d7230883.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-acknowledge-the-limitations","text":"It's important to acknowledge the limitations","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to acknowledge the limitations","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Stresses the need to recognize the boundaries or weaknesses of a study, theory, or approach.","example":"It's important to acknowledge the limitations of our sample size when interpreting these results.","plain":"We must recognize the weaknesses of our sample size when interpreting these results.","note":"Essential for academic honesty and responsible reporting.","audio":"audio/0e6f046cf72bcf11.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-acknowledge-the-nuances","text":"It's important to acknowledge the nuances","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to acknowledge the nuances","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Stresses the need to recognize the subtle differences and complexities within a topic.","example":"It's important to acknowledge the nuances of cultural interpretation in cross-cultural studies.","plain":"We must recognize the subtle differences in cultural interpretation.","note":"Used to encourage a deeper, more sophisticated understanding.","audio":"audio/09cb8d3d2ad6edca.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-acknowledge-the-subjective-nature-of","text":"It's important to acknowledge the subjective nature of","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to acknowledge the subjective nature of","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Stresses the need to recognize that something is based on personal feelings or opinions rather than facts.","example":"It's important to acknowledge the subjective nature of aesthetic judgments in art criticism.","plain":"We must recognize that aesthetic judgments in art criticism are subjective.","note":"Used in fields where personal interpretation plays a significant role.","audio":"audio/54a3419af976fd12.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-avoid-ad-hominem-attacks","text":"It's important to avoid ad hominem attacks","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to avoid ad hominem attacks","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Warns against attacking the person making an argument rather than the argument itself.","example":"It's important to avoid ad hominem attacks in academic debates to maintain intellectual integrity.","plain":"We must not attack the person, but the argument, in academic debates.","note":"Essential for respectful and productive academic discourse.","audio":"audio/95ec64827bfde6c7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-avoid-anecdotal-evidence","text":"It's important to avoid anecdotal evidence","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to avoid anecdotal evidence","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Warns against relying on personal stories or isolated examples rather than systematic data.","example":"It's important to avoid anecdotal evidence when making broad scientific claims.","plain":"We must not rely on personal stories when making broad scientific claims.","note":"Emphasizes scientific rigor over informal observations.","audio":"audio/c3b0b9583efed232.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-avoid-circular-reasoning","text":"It's important to avoid circular reasoning","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to avoid circular reasoning","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Warns against arguments where the conclusion is assumed in the premise, offering no real proof.","example":"It's important to avoid circular reasoning when constructing a logical argument.","plain":"We must avoid arguments where the conclusion is already assumed.","note":"Essential for sound logical argumentation.","audio":"audio/cb9d2b47aba9c905.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-avoid-confirmation-bias","text":"It's important to avoid confirmation bias","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to avoid confirmation bias","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Warns against the tendency to seek out or interpret information that confirms one's existing beliefs.","example":"It's important to avoid confirmation bias when analyzing data to ensure objectivity.","plain":"We must avoid confirmation bias when analyzing data to be objective.","note":"Crucial for scientific integrity and critical thinking.","audio":"audio/10cb6b54db113024.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-avoid-conflating","text":"It's important to avoid conflating","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to avoid conflating","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Warns against mistakenly treating two distinct things as if they were the same.","example":"It's important to avoid conflating correlation with causation when interpreting these results.","plain":"We must not confuse correlation with causation when interpreting these results.","note":"Formal and precise, used to prevent logical errors.","audio":"audio/2bf272f72ee9e6c2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-avoid-generalisations","text":"It's important to avoid generalisations","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to avoid generalisations","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Warns against making broad statements that may not apply to all cases or individuals.","example":"It's important to avoid generalisations about entire populations based on limited data.","plain":"We must not make broad statements about populations from limited data.","note":"Encourages precision and careful interpretation of data.","audio":"audio/992b1dadfc401e9b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-avoid-hasty-conclusions","text":"It's important to avoid hasty conclusions","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to avoid hasty conclusions","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Warns against making quick judgments without sufficient evidence or careful consideration.","example":"It's important to avoid hasty conclusions based on preliminary data alone.","plain":"We must not make quick judgments based only on preliminary data.","note":"Encourages patience and thoroughness in analysis.","audio":"audio/bce49abbbd6c2fb4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-avoid-making-assumptions","text":"It's important to avoid making assumptions","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to avoid making assumptions","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Warns against taking things for granted without sufficient evidence or justification.","example":"It's important to avoid making assumptions about causality based solely on correlation.","plain":"We must not assume causality based only on correlation.","note":"Encourages rigorous analysis and evidence-based reasoning.","audio":"audio/3e4f150cd1862956.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-avoid-overgeneralizing-from","text":"It's important to avoid overgeneralizing from","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to avoid overgeneralizing from","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Warns against drawing broad conclusions from specific or limited examples.","example":"It's important to avoid overgeneralizing from case studies to entire populations.","plain":"We must not draw broad conclusions from case studies to entire populations.","note":"Encourages careful interpretation of research scope.","audio":"audio/22b880b288b94734.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-avoid-oversimplification","text":"It's important to avoid oversimplification","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to avoid oversimplification","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Warns against reducing complex issues to overly simple terms, which can distort understanding.","example":"It's important to avoid oversimplification when discussing the causes of social inequality.","plain":"We must not make the causes of social inequality too simple.","note":"Encourages nuanced and thorough analysis.","audio":"audio/6a00c6a7f2a1f729.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-avoid-reductionism","text":"It's important to avoid reductionism","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to avoid reductionism","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Warns against oversimplifying complex phenomena by explaining them in terms of their basic parts.","example":"It's important to avoid reductionism when studying complex social systems.","plain":"We must not oversimplify when studying complex social systems.","note":"Encourages holistic and multi-faceted analysis.","audio":"audio/99c5f73ff0c74371.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-avoid-sweeping-generalisations","text":"It's important to avoid sweeping generalisations","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to avoid sweeping generalisations","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Stresses the need to refrain from making broad statements that might not be entirely accurate.","example":"It's important to avoid sweeping generalisations about entire cultural groups in our research.","plain":"We must not make broad statements about entire cultural groups in our research.","note":"Encourages precision and sensitivity in academic discourse.","audio":"audio/8b40de350763f053.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-bear-in-mind","text":"It's important to bear in mind","kind":"chunk","form":"It's important to bear in mind","level":"C1","situations":["academic","persuading"],"meaning":"Highlights the necessity of remembering a particular fact or consideration.","example":"It's important to bear in mind the ethical implications of data privacy.","plain":"We must remember the ethical implications of data privacy.","note":"A formal way to remind someone of an important contextual detail.","audio":"audio/f026b2bdd20a7dca.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-consider-alternative-explanations","text":"It's important to consider alternative explanations","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to consider alternative explanations","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Emphasizes the need to think about other possible reasons or interpretations for a phenomenon.","example":"It's important to consider alternative explanations for the observed correlation, not just causation.","plain":"We must think about other reasons for the correlation, not just causation.","note":"Encourages critical thinking and avoids premature conclusions.","audio":"audio/1b254681a83f7040.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-consider-the-broader-context","text":"It's important to consider the broader context","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to consider the broader context","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Stresses the need to understand something within its wider environment or circumstances.","example":"It's important to consider the broader context of historical events when analyzing political decisions.","plain":"We must understand historical events within their wider context.","note":"Encourages a holistic and comprehensive understanding.","audio":"audio/f82db89a6e17e700.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-consider-the-broader-implications","text":"It's important to consider the broader implications","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to consider the broader implications","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Stresses the need to think about the wider, more general consequences or effects of something.","example":"It's important to consider the broader implications of this technological shift for society.","plain":"We must think about the wider consequences of this technological shift for society.","note":"Encourages a comprehensive and far-reaching perspective.","audio":"audio/97748d78a2b2c339.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-consider-the-counter-arguments","text":"It's important to consider the counter-arguments","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to consider the counter-arguments","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Stresses the need to think about opposing viewpoints or objections to an argument.","example":"It's important to consider the counter-arguments before presenting a definitive conclusion.","plain":"We must think about opposing arguments before presenting a conclusion.","note":"Encourages balanced and thorough academic debate.","audio":"audio/4130c947c23cb59d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-consider-the-ethical-implications","text":"It's important to consider the ethical implications","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to consider the ethical implications","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Stresses the need to think about the moral consequences or considerations of an action or research.","example":"It's important to consider the ethical implications of using AI in decision-making processes.","plain":"We must think about the moral consequences of using AI in decision-making.","note":"Crucial in academic and professional contexts, especially in research.","audio":"audio/026c92259f4ff14f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-consider-the-implications","text":"It's important to consider the implications","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to consider the implications","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Stresses the need to think about the potential consequences or effects of something.","example":"It's important to consider the implications of these policy changes for future generations.","plain":"We must think about the consequences of these policy changes.","note":"Encourages foresight and a broader perspective.","audio":"audio/4fde9cc60a56bd48.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-consider-the-long-term-effects","text":"It's important to consider the long-term effects","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to consider the long-term effects","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Stresses the need to think about the future consequences or impacts of something over an extended period.","example":"It's important to consider the long-term effects of technological advancements on society.","plain":"We must think about the future impacts of technological advancements on society.","note":"Encourages foresight and strategic thinking.","audio":"audio/7d462b9df354a5a5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-consider-the-unintended-consequences","text":"It's important to consider the unintended consequences","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to consider the unintended consequences","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Stresses the need to think about unforeseen or unexpected negative outcomes of an action.","example":"It's important to consider the unintended consequences of new regulations on small businesses.","plain":"We must think about the unexpected negative outcomes of new regulations.","note":"Encourages comprehensive risk assessment and foresight.","audio":"audio/5187424820fba4ff.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-contextualise-these-findings","text":"It's important to contextualise these findings","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to contextualise these findings","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Stresses the need to understand research results within their specific background or circumstances.","example":"It's important to contextualise these findings within the broader socio-economic landscape.","plain":"We must understand these findings within their broader socio-economic background.","note":"Academic, used to ensure accurate interpretation of data.","audio":"audio/659e303be9818b3c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-draw-a-distinction","text":"It's important to draw a distinction","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to draw a distinction","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Emphasizes the need to clearly separate two similar but different concepts or ideas.","example":"It's important to draw a distinction between correlation and causation in this analysis.","plain":"We must clearly separate correlation from causation in this analysis.","note":"Academic and precise, used to avoid confusion.","audio":"audio/de3e7bdb084f283c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-highlight","text":"It's important to highlight","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to highlight","level":"C1","situations":["academic","presenting","persuading"],"meaning":"Emphasizes the significance of a particular aspect or finding.","example":"It's important to highlight the role of community leaders in facilitating this change.","plain":"We must emphasize the role of community leaders in facilitating this change.","note":"Used to draw strong attention to a key element.","audio":"audio/0779713ae8958da9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-highlight-that","text":"It's important to highlight that","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to highlight that","level":"C1","situations":["persuading","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To emphasize a particular point or fact.","example":"It's important to highlight that this solution has been successful in similar projects.","plain":"We must emphasize that this solution has been successful in similar projects.","note":"Used to draw strong attention to a specific detail.","audio":"audio/2145d33f15619325.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-maintain-a-critical-perspective","text":"It's important to maintain a critical perspective","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to maintain a critical perspective","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Emphasizes the need to evaluate information carefully and not accept it at face value.","example":"It's important to maintain a critical perspective when reviewing historical accounts.","plain":"We must keep a critical view when reviewing historical accounts.","note":"Encourages analytical thinking and academic rigor.","audio":"audio/4f9f23f4021b035a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-recognise","text":"It's important to recognise","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to recognise","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To highlight a crucial fact or aspect that needs to be acknowledged for a full understanding.","example":"It's important to recognise that this project requires significant upfront investment.","plain":"We must acknowledge that this project requires significant upfront investment.","note":"Used to draw attention to a key point that might be overlooked. Emphatic and formal.","audio":"audio/587cad84b11ea95b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-remain-cognisant-of","text":"It's important to remain cognisant of","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to remain cognisant of","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Stresses the need to stay aware and mindful of a particular fact or situation.","example":"It's important to remain cognisant of the cultural sensitivities when conducting international research.","plain":"We must stay aware of cultural sensitivities when conducting international research.","note":"Formal, emphasizes continuous awareness.","audio":"audio/6fa6de5c7d51e792.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-remain-objective","text":"It's important to remain objective","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to remain objective","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Stresses the need to stay neutral and unbiased, focusing on facts rather than personal feelings.","example":"It's important to remain objective when evaluating conflicting research findings.","plain":"We must stay neutral when evaluating conflicting research findings.","note":"Fundamental principle in academic and scientific work.","audio":"audio/d818f372d560d549.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-remain-vigilant-against","text":"It's important to remain vigilant against","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to remain vigilant against","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Stresses the need to stay watchful and alert to prevent something undesirable from happening.","example":"It's important to remain vigilant against potential biases in data collection and analysis.","plain":"We must stay watchful against potential biases in data collection.","note":"Emphasizes caution and proactive measures.","audio":"audio/ce95a67a88d35d7f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-scrutinise-the-data","text":"It's important to scrutinise the data","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to scrutinise the data","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Stresses the need to examine information very carefully and critically for accuracy.","example":"It's important to scrutinise the data for any inconsistencies before drawing conclusions.","plain":"We must examine the data very carefully for inconsistencies before concluding.","note":"Emphasizes thoroughness and critical evaluation in research.","audio":"audio/7dc296e52b8f02a0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-stress","text":"It's important to stress","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to stress","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To emphasize the significance of a particular point or idea.","example":"It's important to stress the urgency of addressing these security vulnerabilities.","plain":"We need to emphasize the urgency of addressing these security vulnerabilities.","note":"Used to highlight a critical aspect that requires immediate attention.","audio":"audio/357eb1fbe73cf0e4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-stress-that","text":"It's important to stress that","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to stress that","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Emphasizes the crucial nature of a particular point or condition.","example":"It's important to stress that these findings are specific to the cultural context studied.","plain":"We must emphasize that these findings are specific to the cultural context studied.","note":"Used to highlight a critical aspect that should not be overlooked.","audio":"audio/f48453283c9dbebc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-in-our-best-interest-to","text":"It's in our best interest to","kind":"frame","form":"It's in our best interest to","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To argue that a proposed action will benefit the group most.","example":"It's in our best interest to prioritize customer satisfaction above all else.","plain":"Prioritizing customer satisfaction will benefit us most.","note":"Appeals to self-interest, framing the proposal as advantageous for the audience.","audio":"audio/0446d9d49ef46aaa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-more-a-question-of","text":"it's more a question of","kind":"frame","form":"It's more a question of","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Used to reframe an issue, emphasizing a different aspect or underlying cause.","example":"It's more a question of resource allocation than a lack of effort from the team.","plain":"The issue is more about resource allocation than a lack of effort from the team.","note":"Useful for nuanced explanations, shifting focus to a different aspect of a problem.","audio":"audio/3b90ed924bbaf868.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-my-contention-that","text":"It's my contention that","kind":"frame","form":"It's my contention that","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a formal and strong claim or argument that the speaker intends to defend.","example":"It's my contention that the existing literature has overlooked this critical variable.","plain":"I argue that the existing literature has overlooked this critical variable.","note":"A very formal and assertive way to state a strong argument, often in academic papers.","audio":"audio/835c2a2884b22f00.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-no-wonder-that","text":"It's no wonder that","kind":"frame","form":"It's no wonder that","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Indicates that something is not surprising, given the circumstances.","example":"He worked tirelessly, so it's no wonder that he got the promotion.","plain":"He worked tirelessly, so it's not surprising that he got the promotion.","note":"Used to express that an outcome is a logical consequence.","audio":"audio/bc78a76c5e9ac27f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-not-always-the-case-that","text":"It's not always the case that","kind":"frame","form":"It's not always the case that","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To state that a particular situation or outcome is not universally true.","example":"It's not always the case that higher investment leads to higher returns.","plain":"Higher investment does not always lead to higher returns.","note":"Used to challenge a general assumption or provide nuance.","audio":"audio/068ce99890a4636d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-not-uncommon-for","text":"It's not uncommon for","kind":"frame","form":"It's not uncommon for","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Indicates that a particular situation or occurrence is fairly frequent or expected.","example":"It's not uncommon for initial research findings to be refined by subsequent studies.","plain":"It's fairly common for initial research findings to be refined by subsequent studies.","note":"A polite way to state that something happens regularly without being absolute.","audio":"audio/4954cf00dcef43f7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-not-what-i-booked","text":"It's not what I booked","kind":"chunk","form":"It's not what I booked","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Stating that what you received is different from what you reserved.","example":"This room has twin beds; it's not what I booked.","plain":"This room has twin beds; I did not book this.","note":"A direct but factual statement of discrepancy, often used in hotels.","audio":"audio/6166fa3f2cb8919a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-often-argued-that","text":"It's often argued that","kind":"frame","form":"It's often argued that","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a common viewpoint or argument, often one that the speaker may then challenge or elaborate on.","example":"It's often argued that economic growth inevitably leads to increased social inequality.","plain":"Many people argue that economic growth leads to increased social inequality.","note":"Useful for presenting existing debates or common beliefs.","audio":"audio/8e2430e8265832e5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-plausible-that","text":"It's plausible that","kind":"frame","form":"It's plausible that","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Suggests that a particular idea or outcome is believable or likely, but not certain.","example":"It's plausible that these findings could inform future policy decisions in public health.","plain":"These findings could possibly inform future policy decisions in public health.","note":"Used for presenting a hypothesis or a potential consequence.","audio":"audio/55bc9021fc88220a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-safe-to-assume-that","text":"It's safe to assume that","kind":"frame","form":"It's safe to assume that","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Introduces a reasonable inference or conclusion based on available information.","example":"It's safe to assume that everyone received the meeting agenda by now.","plain":"We can reasonably assume that everyone received the meeting agenda by now.","note":"Used to present a logical and likely conclusion.","audio":"audio/d4ed34dfe0006f29.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-safe-to-say","text":"It's safe to say","kind":"frame","form":"It's safe to say","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","storytelling"],"meaning":"Introduces a statement that is likely true and won't be contradicted.","example":"Given the results, it's safe to say the experiment was a success.","plain":"Given the results, we can confidently say the experiment was a success.","note":"Used to express a conclusion that is widely accepted or evident.","audio":"audio/f293c6a36af1408e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-understood-that","text":"It's understood that","kind":"frame","form":"It's understood that","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"States something that is implicitly agreed upon or generally known.","example":"It's understood that everyone will contribute equally to the group project.","plain":"Everyone implicitly agrees to contribute equally to the group project.","note":"Indicates a shared, unspoken agreement or expectation.","audio":"audio/9be2ea82a16b06e1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-worth-bearing-in-mind","text":"It's worth bearing in mind","kind":"chunk","form":"It's worth bearing in mind","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","storytelling","academic","presenting","explaining"],"meaning":"Suggests that a particular fact or consideration should be remembered or kept in mind.","example":"It's worth bearing in mind that these statistics represent averages, not individual experiences.","plain":"We should remember that these statistics represent averages, not individual experiences.","note":"A polite way to remind someone of an important contextual detail.","audio":"audio/82b1aa2c73288a21.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-worth-considering-the-implications","text":"It's worth considering the implications","kind":"collocation","form":"It's worth considering the implications","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Suggests that the potential consequences or effects of something should be examined.","example":"It's worth considering the implications of these technological advancements for future employment.","plain":"We should consider the implications of these technological advancements for future employment.","note":"Used to prompt thought about future effects or consequences.","audio":"audio/6089e7923123e608.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-worth-expanding-on","text":"It's worth expanding on","kind":"chunk","form":"It's worth expanding on","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Suggests that a topic or idea deserves more detailed discussion or explanation.","example":"It's worth expanding on the methodological limitations of the study before drawing conclusions.","plain":"We should discuss the methodological limitations of the study in more detail before drawing conclusions.","note":"A polite way to suggest further discussion or elaboration.","audio":"audio/ffc058f7d1323f8d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-worth-exploring","text":"It's worth exploring","kind":"frame","form":"It's worth exploring","level":"C1","situations":["academic","persuading"],"meaning":"To suggest that an idea or option deserves further investigation.","example":"It's worth exploring whether a flexible work schedule could boost team morale.","plain":"We should investigate whether a flexible work schedule could boost team morale.","note":"Encourages open-mindedness and a willingness to look into new possibilities.","audio":"audio/3c7eeaf74084919a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-worth-exploring-whether","text":"It's worth exploring whether","kind":"frame","form":"It's worth exploring whether","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Suggests that it would be beneficial to investigate a particular possibility or question.","example":"It's worth exploring whether these findings are generalizable to other cultural contexts.","plain":"We should investigate if these findings apply to other cultural contexts.","note":"Used to propose further research or discussion.","audio":"audio/57395ffeca52d993.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-worth-mentioning","text":"It's worth mentioning","kind":"frame","form":"It's worth mentioning","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Highlights a detail that is important enough to be included.","example":"It's worth mentioning that the team worked overtime to meet the deadline.","plain":"It's important to mention that the team worked overtime to meet the deadline.","note":"Draws attention to a relevant piece of information.","audio":"audio/686b2a16b6c73af7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-worth-pointing-out","text":"It's worth pointing out","kind":"chunk","form":"It's worth pointing out","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Highlights a specific detail or fact that is important for the discussion.","example":"It's worth pointing out that the control group received no intervention at all.","plain":"We should point out that the control group received no intervention at all.","note":"Used to draw attention to a particular piece of information.","audio":"audio/945c618ea9f62bb0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-worth-reiterating","text":"It's worth reiterating","kind":"frame","form":"It's worth reiterating","level":"C1","situations":["presenting","persuading"],"meaning":"To state again something that has already been said, for emphasis.","example":"It's worth reiterating that our budget for this phase is fixed.","plain":"It's important to say again that our budget for this phase is fixed.","note":"Used to reinforce a key message or remind the audience.","audio":"audio/1aec6fa7d180f086.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-worth-reiterating-that","text":"It's worth reiterating that","kind":"frame","form":"It's worth reiterating that","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Emphasizes a point already made, suggesting its importance for understanding.","example":"It's worth reiterating that our findings are preliminary and require further validation.","plain":"I want to repeat that our findings are preliminary and need more validation.","note":"Used to bring attention back to a crucial point, often after discussing details.","audio":"audio/765586849f944658.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.just-checking-in","text":"just checking in","kind":"chunk","form":"Just checking in","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to explain why you are contacting someone, usually to see how they are or if they need anything.","example":"Hi, I was just checking in to see how you're getting on with the report.","plain":"Hi, I wanted to see how you're getting on with the report.","note":"A casual and considerate way to initiate contact or follow up.","audio":"audio/bd672b1fafe7e174.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.just-for-the-record","text":"Just for the record","kind":"chunk","form":"Just for the record","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","small-talk"],"meaning":"To state something officially or to ensure it is noted.","example":"Just for the record, I did raise this concern in our previous meeting.","plain":"I want it noted that I raised this concern in our previous meeting.","note":"Used to ensure a point is officially acknowledged or remembered.","audio":"audio/aa8f2721f9954ab9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.just-my-two-cents","text":"Just my two cents","kind":"chunk","form":"just my two cents","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Offers an opinion humbly, implying it's a small contribution to the discussion.","example":"We could try a different marketing approach, just my two cents.","plain":"We could try a different marketing approach, that's my opinion.","note":"Informal, used to offer an opinion without being overly assertive.","audio":"audio/25948e6024122763.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.just-out-of-curiosity","text":"just out of curiosity","kind":"chunk","form":"Just out of curiosity","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to introduce a question, implying it's for general interest rather than a specific need.","example":"Just out of curiosity, how did you get into this line of work?","plain":"How did you get into this line of work?","note":"Makes a question sound less intrusive and more conversational.","audio":"audio/e0d897260d60723e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.just-so-you-know","text":"Just so you know","kind":"chunk","form":"Just so you know","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Provides information to the listener, often as a heads-up or clarification.","example":"Just so you know, the meeting room has been changed to Conference Room B.","plain":"The meeting room has been changed to Conference Room B.","note":"Informal, used to give a quick piece of information.","audio":"audio/c37f1096d528a243.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.just-to-confirm","text":"just to confirm","kind":"chunk","form":"Just to confirm","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","feedback","explaining","calls"],"meaning":"I want to verify a piece of information to ensure it is correct.","example":"Just to confirm, the meeting is indeed at 3 PM, not 2 PM, right?","plain":"I want to verify, the meeting is at 3 PM, not 2 PM, correct?","note":"A common phrase used to double-check details, often at the end of a discussion.","audio":"audio/1f82b188063fed36.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.just-to-give-you","text":"just to give you","kind":"frame","form":"Just to give you","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Introduces feedback or information, often softening it.","example":"Just to give you some context, the client was expecting a slightly different approach.","plain":"The client was expecting a slightly different approach.","note":"A common softener, makes the feedback sound less like a direct criticism.","audio":"audio/bc15a3250a7d6784.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.just-to-manage","text":"just to manage","kind":"frame","form":"Just to manage","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Used to set expectations or clarify boundaries.","example":"Just to manage expectations, the project deadline is quite tight this quarter.","plain":"The project deadline is quite tight this quarter, so I want to set expectations.","note":"Professional, used to proactively address potential issues or misunderstandings.","audio":"audio/e3b9e54f8c800515.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.just-to-play-devils-advocate","text":"just to play devil's advocate","kind":"frame","form":"Just to play devil's advocate","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To argue against a popular idea to test its validity.","example":"Just to play devil's advocate, what if the market doesn't respond as we expect?","plain":"To challenge the idea, what if the market doesn't respond as we expect?","note":"Used to introduce a counter-argument or challenge a consensus.","audio":"audio/788399263fb164f3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.just-to-recap","text":"just to recap","kind":"chunk","form":"Just to recap","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","small-talk"],"meaning":"To briefly summarize the main points discussed.","example":"Just to recap, we've agreed on the budget and the project lead.","plain":"To summarize, we've agreed on the budget and the project lead.","note":"Common way to summarize before moving on or concluding.","audio":"audio/1a647af4c4448b50.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.just-to-reiterate","text":"just to reiterate","kind":"chunk","form":"Just to reiterate","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","storytelling","presenting","feedback"],"meaning":"Used to repeat a point for emphasis or clarity.","example":"Just to reiterate, the deadline for submissions is Friday at 5 PM.","plain":"I want to repeat that the deadline for submissions is Friday at 5 PM.","note":"Professional, used to ensure important information is understood.","audio":"audio/6fac16c0195073fc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.just-wanted-to-flag","text":"just wanted to flag","kind":"chunk","form":"just wanted to flag","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A polite way to bring a potential issue or point to someone's attention.","example":"I just wanted to flag a small inconsistency in the data from the last report.","plain":"I wanted to mention a small inconsistency in the data from the last report.","note":"A common softener for introducing minor issues or concerns. Informal but professional.","audio":"audio/baf87d4195d63033.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.keep-tabs-on","text":"keep tabs on","kind":"collocation","form":"keep tabs on","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To monitor or observe someone or something closely and regularly.","example":"I'll keep tabs on the project budget to ensure we don't exceed our allocated funds.","plain":"I'll monitor the project budget closely to ensure we don't exceed our allocated funds.","note":"An informal way to say 'monitor'. Implies ongoing vigilance. Useful for tracking progress.","audio":"audio/db8fbfb39400ddf8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.key-takeaway","text":"key takeaway","kind":"collocation","form":"key takeaway","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"The most important piece of information or conclusion.","example":"The key takeaway from this meeting is our new marketing strategy.","plain":"The most important conclusion from this meeting is our new marketing strategy.","note":"Refers to the main point to remember.","audio":"audio/49e08e8b8e0e56dd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.knock-on-wood","text":"knock on wood","kind":"chunk","form":"knock on wood","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to ward off bad luck after mentioning something good or positive.","example":"My car hasn't broken down in years, knock on wood!","plain":"My car hasn't broken down in years, I hope it continues that way!","note":"Common in American English; equivalent to 'touch wood' in British English.","audio":"audio/3737c110c30bb528.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lay-the-groundwork","text":"lay the groundwork","kind":"collocation","form":"lay the groundwork","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To do the preparatory work for something.","example":"We need to lay the groundwork for the new system before we can begin implementation.","plain":"We need to do the preparatory work for the new system before we can begin implementation.","note":"Implies careful preparation for future actions.","audio":"audio/c28446022bf5dd91.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.let-me-be-clear","text":"Let me be clear","kind":"chunk","form":"Let me be clear","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Introduces a statement intended to remove any ambiguity or misunderstanding.","example":"Let me be clear, this policy applies to everyone, without exception.","plain":"This policy applies to everyone, without exception.","note":"A direct way to ensure a statement is understood precisely.","audio":"audio/1a94f04ff22f12aa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.let-me-check-my-understanding","text":"let me check my understanding","kind":"frame","form":"Let me check my understanding","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I want to confirm if I have correctly understood what you have said.","example":"Before we proceed, let me check my understanding of the next steps.","plain":"Before we continue, I want to confirm I understand the next steps.","note":"A professional way to ensure accuracy and avoid miscommunication.","audio":"audio/eb270058488df39a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.let-me-elaborate","text":"Let me elaborate","kind":"chunk","form":"Let me elaborate","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To provide more details or further explanation about something you have just mentioned.","example":"Let me elaborate on why this new software will streamline our workflow significantly.","plain":"I will explain in more detail why this new software will streamline our workflow significantly.","note":"Used when you want to expand on a point you've made. Professional and informative.","audio":"audio/8f3a7a31e777680b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.let-me-get-this-straight","text":"let me get this straight","kind":"frame","form":"Let me get this straight","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I want to confirm my understanding of the situation or details to ensure accuracy.","example":"So, let me get this straight: we're postponing the meeting until next week?","plain":"So, I want to confirm: the meeting is postponed until next week?","note":"Used to summarise or rephrase information to ensure correct understanding, often before agreeing.","audio":"audio/fa12db5410ade48a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.let-me-know-if-you-need","text":"Let me know if you need","kind":"frame","form":"let me know if you need","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Offering help or assistance, inviting the other person to ask if necessary.","example":"If you're staying overnight, let me know if you need an extra blanket.","plain":"If you're staying overnight, tell me if you need an extra blanket.","note":"A polite and helpful offer, showing consideration for a guest.","audio":"audio/fee95d4e92c04b27.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.let-me-lay-out","text":"Let me lay out","kind":"frame","form":"Let me lay out","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To present a plan, argument, or situation clearly and systematically for consideration.","example":"Let me lay out our proposed strategy for increasing market share next quarter.","plain":"I will present our strategy for increasing market share next quarter.","note":"Used when you want to explain something in a structured, detailed way. Professional and direct.","audio":"audio/f0f7be0643655bb9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.let-me-put-it-another-way","text":"let me put it another way","kind":"frame","form":"Let me put it another way","level":"C1","situations":["presenting","explaining","calls"],"meaning":"I will explain something again using different words to make it clearer.","example":"Perhaps that wasn't clear; let me put it another way to ensure understanding.","plain":"That might not have been clear; I'll explain it differently so you understand.","note":"Used when you suspect your initial explanation wasn't fully grasped.","audio":"audio/68a3029afcdd1426.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.let-me-reassure-you","text":"Let me reassure you","kind":"frame","form":"Let me reassure you","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To offer comfort and confidence to someone who is worried or doubtful about something.","example":"Let me reassure you that all necessary safety protocols will be strictly followed.","plain":"I want to assure you that all necessary safety protocols will be strictly followed.","note":"Used to calm fears or doubts, often by providing specific information. Direct and comforting.","audio":"audio/bf92e46e755f3b46.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.let-me-restart-the-call","text":"Let me restart the call","kind":"chunk","form":"Let me restart the call","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Suggesting to end the current call and initiate a new one to fix issues.","example":"The audio is really bad; let me restart the call and see if that helps.","plain":"The audio is terrible; I'll end and begin the call again to see if it improves.","note":"A common solution for persistent connection problems.","audio":"audio/834b557ed249e2c5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.let-me-try-again","text":"Let me try again","kind":"chunk","form":"Let me try again","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Stating an intention to re-explain or re-attempt something.","example":"That didn't come out right; let me try again to articulate my thoughts.","plain":"That wasn't clear; I'll try to explain my thoughts differently.","note":"A simple and direct way to indicate a second attempt at communication.","audio":"audio/f71d886fb5844265.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.let-me-try-reconnecting","text":"let me try reconnecting","kind":"frame","form":"Let me try reconnecting","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I will attempt to leave and rejoin the call to fix a technical issue.","example":"The sound is terrible, so let me try reconnecting to see if that helps.","plain":"The sound is bad, I'll leave and rejoin the call to fix it.","note":"A proactive statement when you're experiencing technical difficulties and want to fix them.","audio":"audio/b7e6f67dfa1fdf43.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-aim-for","text":"Let's aim for","kind":"frame","form":"Let's aim for","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To suggest a target or goal to work towards.","example":"Let's aim for a 10% increase in sales by the end of the quarter.","plain":"Let's target a 10% increase in sales by the end of the quarter.","note":"Used to propose a goal or objective.","audio":"audio/897b4d4b23e97c89.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-aim-for-next-week","text":"Let's aim for next week","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's aim for next week","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Suggesting a general timeframe for when to meet or complete something.","example":"We need to discuss the project. Let's aim for next week to meet.","plain":"We should try to meet next week to discuss the project.","note":"A common way to propose a general time without committing to a specific day yet.","audio":"audio/f94c66175da8ac6c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-be-honest","text":"let's be honest","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's be honest","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Used to introduce a frank or realistic assessment of a situation.","example":"Let's be honest, the current strategy isn't delivering the results we hoped for.","plain":"Realistically, the current strategy isn't delivering the results we hoped for.","note":"Informal but common, encourages a direct and truthful discussion.","audio":"audio/b40f8d3cc9003296.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-catch-up-over-coffee","text":"Let's catch up over coffee","kind":"collocation","form":"Let's catch up over coffee","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Suggesting a casual meeting to talk and update each other while having coffee.","example":"It's been a while, let's catch up over coffee next week.","plain":"We haven't met in a while, let's meet for coffee next week to talk.","note":"A very common and informal way to suggest a casual social meeting.","audio":"audio/cd642e6c7a6f0f27.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-clarify-the-next-steps","text":"Let's clarify the next steps","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's clarify the next steps","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To ensure everyone understands what actions need to be taken after the meeting.","example":"Before we finish, let's clarify the next steps and assign responsibilities.","plain":"Before we finish, let's make sure everyone understands the next actions.","note":"Essential for effective meeting outcomes.","audio":"audio/b09cb404f2ed7e73.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-clarify-the-terms","text":"let's clarify the terms","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's clarify the terms","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Suggests making the conditions or details of an agreement more precise.","example":"Before we proceed, let's clarify the terms of the service agreement.","plain":"Before we proceed, let's make the conditions of the service agreement more precise.","note":"Ensures mutual understanding and prevents future disputes.","audio":"audio/bcf37c090374c23f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-consider-for-a-moment","text":"Let's consider for a moment","kind":"frame","form":"Let's consider for a moment","level":"C1","situations":["academic","presenting","persuading"],"meaning":"Invites the audience to pause and think about a specific idea or scenario.","example":"Let's consider for a moment the ethical implications of using AI in medical diagnostics.","plain":"Let's think about the ethical implications of using AI in medical diagnostics.","note":"A polite way to introduce a point for reflection or deeper thought.","audio":"audio/41d4b284062c3458.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-consider-the-implications-of","text":"Let's consider the implications of","kind":"frame","form":"Let's consider the implications of","level":"C1","situations":["presenting","persuading"],"meaning":"To invite reflection on the potential consequences of a decision.","example":"Let's consider the implications of delaying this project any further.","plain":"We should think about what will happen if we delay this project any further.","note":"Encourages foresight and a thorough evaluation of potential outcomes.","audio":"audio/3dd35cc7d375454d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-consider-this","text":"let's consider this","kind":"frame","form":"Let's consider this","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Suggests taking a moment to think about a particular aspect or proposal.","example":"Let's consider this from a long-term strategic perspective.","plain":"Let's think about this from a long-term strategic perspective.","note":"Encourages careful thought and a broader view of the situation.","audio":"audio/e927e404e730dadd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-coordinate-our-schedules","text":"Let's coordinate our schedules","kind":"collocation","form":"let's coordinate our schedules","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Suggesting that people compare their calendars to find a mutually convenient time.","example":"It's hard to find a time for everyone, so let's coordinate our schedules soon.","plain":"It's hard to find a time for everyone, so let's compare our calendars soon.","note":"A slightly more formal or business-like way to suggest finding a meeting time.","audio":"audio/f078c54b88d3de5a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-dive-in","text":"Let's dive in","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's dive in","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To begin the meeting or a specific topic immediately.","example":"Alright team, we have a lot to cover today, so let's dive in.","plain":"We have a lot to cover, so let's start now.","note":"Informal but common way to start a meeting or a new topic.","audio":"audio/33769cfadd8cc234.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-get-straight-to","text":"let's get straight to","kind":"frame","form":"Let's get straight to","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","feedback"],"meaning":"Suggests addressing the main topic directly without delay.","example":"We have a lot to cover, so let's get straight to the agenda items.","plain":"We have a lot to cover, so let's address the agenda items directly.","note":"Direct and efficient, used to focus the conversation.","audio":"audio/e10c1a0cf06a0e51.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-get-this-straight","text":"let's get this straight","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's get this straight","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Used to ensure that everyone understands the facts or agreement clearly.","example":"Let's get this straight: you're proposing a three-month extension?","plain":"Let's confirm: you're proposing a three-month extension?","note":"A direct way to seek clarification and avoid misunderstandings.","audio":"audio/87e4c6587de67191.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-hear-from","text":"Let's hear from","kind":"frame","form":"Let's hear from","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To invite a specific person or group to speak.","example":"Now, let's hear from Maria about the progress on the marketing campaign.","plain":"Now, Maria, please tell us about the progress on the marketing campaign.","note":"Used by the meeting chair to direct the discussion.","audio":"audio/fc8eda9865c22561.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-kick-off","text":"Let's kick off","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's kick off","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To start the meeting or discussion.","example":"Right, everyone's here, so let's kick off this morning's session.","plain":"Right, everyone's here, so let's start this morning's session.","note":"Informal but common way to begin a meeting. Used by the chair.","audio":"audio/364f8e3ad86e2b40.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-look-at","text":"Let's look at","kind":"frame","form":"Let's look at","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To direct attention to a specific aspect or detail.","example":"Let's look at the data from last quarter to inform our strategy moving forward.","plain":"We should examine the data from last quarter to inform our strategy moving forward.","note":"A direct yet collaborative way to guide the discussion to specific evidence or information.","audio":"audio/cd473d2801285bee.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-move-forward-with","text":"let's move forward with","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's move forward with","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Suggests proceeding with a particular plan or aspect of the agreement.","example":"Given our agreement, let's move forward with the initial phase of the project.","plain":"Given our agreement, let's proceed with the initial phase of the project.","note":"Indicates a decision has been made and it's time for action.","audio":"audio/49cc5c8e140dc024.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-not-lose-sight-of","text":"Let's not lose sight of","kind":"frame","form":"Let's not lose sight of","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","persuading","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To remind the group of an important overarching goal or principle.","example":"While discussing details, let's not lose sight of our main objective: customer satisfaction.","plain":"While discussing details, let's remember our main objective: customer satisfaction.","note":"Used to keep the discussion focused on the bigger picture.","audio":"audio/7772ff57a9eef1e0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-open-it-up-to","text":"Let's open it up to","kind":"frame","form":"Let's open it up to","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To invite general discussion or questions from the group.","example":"We've covered the main points, so let's open it up to questions now.","plain":"We've covered the main points, so let's take questions now.","note":"Used to transition to a Q&A or general discussion segment.","audio":"audio/662b345d27e6f8d3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-pencil-something-in","text":"Let's pencil something in","kind":"collocation","form":"let's pencil something in","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Suggesting a tentative plan or appointment that can be confirmed later.","example":"We don't have to commit now, but let's pencil something in for next month.","plain":"We don't have to commit now, but let's make a tentative plan for next month.","note":"Implies flexibility and that the plan isn't set in stone yet.","audio":"audio/2eaf5067356ddf10.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-pivot-to","text":"let's pivot to","kind":"frame","form":"Let's pivot to","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Suggests changing the focus of the discussion to a new topic.","example":"We've covered the project status; now, let's pivot to the upcoming client presentation.","plain":"We've covered the project status; now, let's change the focus to the upcoming client presentation.","note":"Professional and dynamic, used to shift the conversation.","audio":"audio/c63a0360008d68aa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-put-a-pin-in","text":"let's put a pin in","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's put a pin in","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To temporarily set aside a topic to discuss it later.","example":"That's an important point, but let's put a pin in it for now and revisit it next week.","plain":"That's an important point, but let's temporarily set it aside for now and revisit it next week.","note":"Informal but common in professional settings, used to manage discussion flow.","audio":"audio/ece5ae16865eb1f2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-put-it-this-way","text":"Let's put it this way","kind":"frame","form":"Let's put it this way","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To explain something in a different, often more direct or illustrative, manner.","example":"Let's put it this way: if we don't innovate, we'll fall behind.","plain":"If we don't innovate, we'll fall behind.","note":"Used to offer an alternative explanation or a more impactful statement.","audio":"audio/34469e6ccd9fd854.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-recap","text":"Let's recap","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's recap","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To summarize the main points or decisions made.","example":"Before we finish, let's recap the key decisions and action items.","plain":"Before we finish, let's summarize the key decisions and action items.","note":"A common way to conclude a section or the entire meeting.","audio":"audio/8618ea83f0ab609b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-revisit-this","text":"let's revisit this","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's revisit this","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Suggests postponing discussion of a particular topic to a later time or stage.","example":"Let's revisit this pricing discussion once we've finalized the scope.","plain":"Let's discuss this pricing again once we've finalized the scope.","note":"Useful for managing the flow of a negotiation, focusing on one issue at a time.","audio":"audio/9fe386978b55013a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-schedule-a-follow-up","text":"Let's schedule a follow-up","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's schedule a follow-up","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","calls"],"meaning":"Suggesting to arrange another meeting or call to continue a topic.","example":"We couldn't cover everything, so let's schedule a follow-up for next week.","plain":"We didn't finish everything, so let's arrange another meeting for next week.","note":"Used when more discussion is needed on a topic.","audio":"audio/c0a54aabbcbb8d30.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-set-a-date","text":"Let's set a date","kind":"collocation","form":"Let's set a date","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Suggesting to choose a specific day and time for a future meeting or event.","example":"We should definitely get together soon. Let's set a date next week.","plain":"We should meet soon. Let's choose a specific day and time next week.","note":"A common phrase for moving from a general intention to concrete planning.","audio":"audio/43a7dd9bddb99bb0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-table-that","text":"let's table that","kind":"chunk","form":"let's table that","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To postpone a discussion until a later time.","example":"That's a complex issue, so let's table that for a dedicated session.","plain":"That's a complex issue, so let's postpone that for a dedicated session.","note":"American English meaning: to postpone. Be aware of British English difference.","audio":"audio/0625acf727e1b130.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-take-a-closer-look-at","text":"Let's take a closer look at","kind":"frame","form":"Let's take a closer look at","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To invite the audience to examine a specific detail or aspect in more depth.","example":"Now, let's take a closer look at the customer feedback we received.","plain":"Now, let's examine the customer feedback we received in more detail.","note":"Engages the audience and signals a deeper dive into a topic.","audio":"audio/6e3d3b25f18e16ce.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-take-a-step-back","text":"let's take a step back","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's take a step back","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Suggests pausing to review the overall situation or strategy from a broader perspective.","example":"Let's take a step back and look at the bigger picture before we decide.","plain":"Let's pause and look at the bigger picture before we decide.","note":"Useful for de-escalating tension or gaining perspective in a complex negotiation.","audio":"audio/ff481121d5bb4ad1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-take-an-example","text":"let's take an example","kind":"frame","form":"Let's take an example","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To propose providing a specific instance to clarify a general statement or concept.","example":"The new policy has broad implications. Let's take an example: how it affects remote workers.","plain":"The new policy has broad implications. Consider how it affects remote workers.","note":"A direct way to introduce an illustrative example.","audio":"audio/593954486a2617f2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-think-about","text":"Let's think about","kind":"frame","form":"Let's think about","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To invite others to consider a particular aspect or idea.","example":"Let's think about the potential impact on our existing customer base.","plain":"We should consider the potential impact on our existing customer base.","note":"A collaborative way to introduce a new point for consideration.","audio":"audio/af19a1d6e1ce83f4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-touch-base","text":"let's touch base","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's touch base","level":"C1","situations":["feedback","socialising"],"meaning":"To briefly communicate or meet to discuss something.","example":"Let's touch base next week to see how the new process is working out.","plain":"Let's briefly communicate next week to see how the new process is working out.","note":"Informal but widely used in professional contexts for quick check-ins.","audio":"audio/7112341a6e56e340.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-try-calling-back","text":"let's try calling back","kind":"frame","form":"Let's try calling back","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"We should end this call and initiate a new one to attempt to fix connection issues.","example":"The connection is too bad; let's try calling back in a couple of minutes.","plain":"The connection is poor; we should end this call and start a new one soon.","note":"A suggestion to restart the call when technical problems are persistent and severe.","audio":"audio/83878b0be113cce1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-try-for","text":"Let's try for","kind":"frame","form":"let's try for","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Suggesting a specific time or date for a plan, often when other options are difficult.","example":"I'm quite busy, but let's try for next Tuesday evening if that works for you.","plain":"I'm quite busy, but can we aim for next Tuesday evening?","note":"Used when proposing a specific time, implying flexibility might be limited.","audio":"audio/bcb4272b9e3e0550.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-turn-our-attention-to","text":"Let's turn our attention to","kind":"frame","form":"Let's turn our attention to","level":"C1","situations":["academic","presenting"],"meaning":"Signals a shift in focus to a new topic or aspect of the discussion.","example":"Let's turn our attention to the practical implications of these theoretical findings.","plain":"Now, let's focus on the practical implications of these theoretical findings.","note":"A common and polite way to transition between topics in a presentation or discussion.","audio":"audio/008f28e17fd7d676.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.little-did-i-know","text":"Little did I know","kind":"chunk","form":"Little did I know","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Creates suspense by indicating the speaker was unaware of future events.","example":"Little did I know that decision would completely change my career path.","plain":"I didn't know that decision would completely change my career path.","note":"Adds dramatic effect, often used before a turning point in a story.","audio":"audio/d0e2bb7092807b1c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.live-up-to","text":"live up to","kind":"collocation","form":"live up to","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To meet an expected standard or promise.","example":"We need to ensure our new product can live up to the high expectations of our customers.","plain":"We need to ensure our new product can meet the high expectations of our customers.","note":"Often used with expectations, promises, or reputation.","audio":"audio/524eb0c54e430b3c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lodge-a-complaint","text":"lodge a complaint","kind":"collocation","form":"lodge a complaint","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To formally register a grievance or dissatisfaction.","example":"I had to lodge a complaint about the poor condition of the rental car.","plain":"I had to formally complain about the poor condition of the rental car.","note":"Formal way to make a complaint, often in writing.","audio":"audio/b113cdfda38902a8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.looking-back-now","text":"Looking back now","kind":"chunk","form":"Looking back now","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Reflects on past events from the present perspective.","example":"Looking back now, that experience taught me a lot about resilience.","plain":"Reflecting on it now, that experience taught me a lot about resilience.","note":"A common way to introduce reflection or a lesson learned from the past.","audio":"audio/f94b1e1577be0865.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.loop-someone-in","text":"loop someone in","kind":"chunk","form":"loop me in","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To include someone in a discussion or communication.","example":"Please loop me in on any developments regarding the new software rollout.","plain":"Please include me in any developments regarding the new software rollout.","note":"Informal but very common in business.","audio":"audio/170b98016d05e35d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lose-sight-of","text":"lose sight of","kind":"collocation","form":"lose sight of","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To forget or no longer be aware of an important fact, goal, or principle.","example":"Let's not lose sight of our main objective while we're dealing with these minor issues.","plain":"Let's not forget our main objective while we're dealing with these minor issues.","note":"A reminder to stay focused on the bigger picture or core purpose. Common in strategy discussions.","audio":"audio/b5cbb807e106a5af.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.make-a-point-of","text":"Make a point of","kind":"collocation","form":"make a point of","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling","travel"],"meaning":"To make a special effort to do something.","example":"I always make a point of arriving early for appointments.","plain":"I always make sure to arrive early for appointments.","note":"Emphasizes intentionality and consistent effort.","audio":"audio/4ab93c70d41e7ee3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.make-headway","text":"make headway","kind":"collocation","form":"make headway","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To make progress, especially when it is difficult or slow.","example":"We're starting to make headway on resolving the technical issues, but it's slow going.","plain":"We're starting to make progress on resolving the technical issues, but it's slow going.","note":"Implies progress against resistance or difficulty. Often used in ongoing problem-solving.","audio":"audio/1dc96fb04b87f371.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.may-i-jump-in-here","text":"may I jump in here","kind":"chunk","form":"May I jump in here","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I would like to politely interrupt the current speaker to add a point or ask a question.","example":"May I jump in here for a moment? I have a quick question about that.","plain":"Can I interrupt briefly? I have a quick question.","note":"A polite way to interject into a conversation, often used in group discussions.","audio":"audio/42bf72e62158930f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.maybe-another-time","text":"Maybe another time","kind":"chunk","form":"maybe another time","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Suggesting the possibility of meeting or doing the activity at a later date.","example":"I can't make it this weekend, but maybe another time soon?","plain":"I can't make it this weekend, but perhaps we can meet another time soon?","note":"Softens a refusal and keeps the door open for future plans.","audio":"audio/3aae26b041e56eca.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.mend-fences","text":"mend fences","kind":"chunk","form":"mend fences","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To repair a damaged relationship or disagreement.","example":"We need to mend fences with the marketing team after that heated discussion.","plain":"We need to repair our relationship with the marketing team after that heated discussion.","note":"Informal, implies reconciliation after a dispute.","audio":"audio/0512dc66a56f446d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.might-be-worth-considering","text":"might be worth considering","kind":"chunk","form":"might be worth considering","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Suggests an idea or alternative in a gentle, non-confrontational way.","example":"It might be worth considering a different approach for the next phase of the project.","plain":"Perhaps we should consider a different approach for the next phase of the project.","note":"A soft suggestion, inviting discussion rather than dictating. Very common in feedback.","audio":"audio/7be6886e0a1974ff.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.minutes-of-the-meeting","text":"minutes of the meeting","kind":"collocation","form":"minutes of the meeting","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"The official written record of what was discussed and decided.","example":"Please circulate the minutes of the meeting by tomorrow morning.","plain":"Please circulate the official written record of what was discussed by tomorrow morning.","note":"Formal term for the official record of a meeting.","audio":"audio/596e6424036e9958.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.miss-a-connection","text":"miss a connection","kind":"collocation","form":"miss our connection","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To fail to make a connecting flight, train, or bus.","example":"Due to the delay, we might miss our connection in Frankfurt.","plain":"Due to the delay, we might not make our next flight in Frankfurt.","note":"A common concern when travelling with multiple legs.","audio":"audio/8211f7a696004ec3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.miss-a-deadline","text":"miss a deadline","kind":"collocation","form":"miss a deadline","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To fail to complete a task or project by the specified due date.","example":"We cannot afford to miss a deadline on this critical project, as it has severe consequences.","plain":"We cannot afford to fail to complete this critical project by the due date, as it has severe consequences.","note":"Highlights a negative outcome in project management. Emphasizes urgency.","audio":"audio/63365ff4705d4e98.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.mitigate-risks","text":"mitigate risks","kind":"collocation","form":"mitigate risks","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To reduce the severity, seriousness, or likelihood of potential dangers or problems.","example":"We need to implement new protocols to mitigate risks associated with data breaches.","plain":"We need to implement new protocols to reduce the severity of potential dangers associated with data breaches.","note":"A key concept in project management and strategic planning. Formal and precise.","audio":"audio/2d328c684c595be7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.mitigate-the-risks","text":"mitigate the risks","kind":"collocation","form":"mitigate the risks","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","persuading"],"meaning":"To reduce the severity or likelihood of potential dangers or problems.","example":"Our plan includes several strategies to mitigate the risks associated with this expansion.","plain":"Our plan includes several strategies to reduce the dangers associated with this expansion.","note":"A professional phrase used in project management and strategic planning.","audio":"audio/0e33dd2f2ec88c50.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.monitor-progress","text":"monitor progress","kind":"collocation","form":"monitor progress","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To regularly check the development or improvement of a task or project.","example":"We'll need to monitor progress closely to ensure we meet all the key milestones.","plain":"We'll need to regularly check the development closely to ensure we meet all the key milestones.","note":"Essential for project management and accountability. Professional and neutral.","audio":"audio/9b3472bf06e03138.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.move-things-along","text":"move things along","kind":"chunk","form":"move things along","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To encourage progress or speed up a discussion.","example":"We're running out of time, so let's try to move things along.","plain":"We're running out of time, so let's try to speed up.","note":"Used by the chair or someone trying to manage time.","audio":"audio/b483eff374874ab8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.moving-on-to","text":"Moving on to","kind":"chunk","form":"Moving on to","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","academic"],"meaning":"Indicates a transition to the next topic or section of a discussion or presentation.","example":"Moving on to the next slide, we can see the demographic breakdown of our survey participants.","plain":"Next, we can see the demographic breakdown of our survey participants on the next slide.","note":"A very common and natural discourse marker for transitions.","audio":"audio/42271cc75ca291e1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.much-to-my-dismay","text":"Much to my dismay","kind":"chunk","form":"Much to my dismay","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Expresses the speaker's disappointment or distress about an event.","example":"Much to my dismay, the restaurant was closed when we got there.","plain":"I was very disappointed that the restaurant was closed when we got there.","note":"Conveys a strong negative emotional reaction; slightly formal.","audio":"audio/c051c75e794db822.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-apologies-im-already-booked","text":"My apologies, I'm already booked","kind":"chunk","form":"My apologies, I'm already booked","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A formal apology followed by a reason for being unable to attend.","example":"My apologies, I'm already booked for that evening.","plain":"I apologize, I already have an appointment for that evening.","note":"Polite and clear, suitable for slightly more formal contexts.","audio":"audio/5408fa89c7a029b7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-connection-is-unstable","text":"my connection is unstable","kind":"collocation","form":"my connection is unstable","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"My internet or phone signal is unreliable, causing interruptions or poor quality during the call.","example":"Apologies if I drop off, my connection is unstable today due to the bad weather.","plain":"Sorry if I get disconnected, my internet is not reliable today because of the weather.","note":"A common and polite way to pre-emptively explain potential technical issues on your end.","audio":"audio/b67e3e2073985e3b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-initial-reaction-is","text":"My initial reaction is","kind":"frame","form":"My initial reaction is","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To share one's first thoughts or feelings about something.","example":"My initial reaction is that the proposal is too ambitious for our current resources.","plain":"My first thought is that the proposal is too ambitious for our current resources.","note":"Useful for giving immediate, unfiltered feedback.","audio":"audio/0ebd6d883ef42d2a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-initial-thought-was","text":"My initial thought was","kind":"frame","form":"My initial thought was","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Introduces a first impression or idea, often contrasted with a later one.","example":"My initial thought was to decline the offer, but I've reconsidered.","plain":"I first thought to decline the offer, but I've reconsidered.","note":"Used to present a preliminary idea or reaction.","audio":"audio/c7724351667db109.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-internet-is-acting-up","text":"My internet is acting up","kind":"chunk","form":"My internet is acting up","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Informally stating that one's internet connection is not working correctly.","example":"My internet is acting up, so I might need to rejoin the call.","plain":"My internet is not working well, so I might need to rejoin.","note":"More informal than \"network issues,\" but still acceptable in many professional settings.","audio":"audio/bcdea54c377e7089.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-main-concern-is","text":"My main concern is","kind":"frame","form":"My main concern is","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving","calls"],"meaning":"Introducing the primary worry or issue one has.","example":"My main concern is how this will affect our existing customers.","plain":"My biggest worry is how this will affect our existing customers.","note":"A direct way to state your primary reservation or worry.","audio":"audio/a9a046c366f53019.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-main-concern-revolves-around","text":"My main concern revolves around","kind":"frame","form":"My main concern revolves around","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Identifies the central issue or problem that is causing hesitation or disagreement.","example":"My main concern revolves around the potential impact on our existing client relationships.","plain":"My biggest worry is the potential impact on our existing client relationships.","note":"Clearly states the core issue without being overly confrontational.","audio":"audio/1a615afebda38f30.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-main-reservation-is","text":"My main reservation is","kind":"frame","form":"My main reservation is","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation","disagreeing"],"meaning":"Highlights the primary reason for hesitation or disagreement, focusing on a specific point.","example":"My main reservation is the lack of clear metrics to measure success for this initiative.","plain":"My biggest concern is the lack of clear metrics to measure success for this initiative.","note":"Clearly states the core issue without being overly confrontational.","audio":"audio/940e83619ec521e7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-perspective-is-that","text":"My perspective is that","kind":"frame","form":"My perspective is that","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To introduce your personal viewpoint or opinion on a matter.","example":"My perspective is that we should prioritize customer satisfaction above all else.","plain":"I think we should prioritize customer satisfaction above all else.","note":"A diplomatic way to share an opinion, acknowledging it's one viewpoint.","audio":"audio/0a05871ec6420fed.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-sense-is-that","text":"My sense is that","kind":"frame","form":"My sense is that","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"Expresses an intuitive feeling or a strong impression about a situation.","example":"My sense is that the team is ready for this challenge, despite the initial hesitation.","plain":"I feel that the team is ready for this challenge, despite the initial hesitation.","note":"A slightly more tentative way to offer an opinion than \"I think,\" suggesting intuition.","audio":"audio/2f504b0d21aca98b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-sincere-apologies","text":"my sincere apologies","kind":"collocation","form":"my sincere apologies","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a genuine and heartfelt apology for a mistake or an unfortunate event.","example":"Please accept my sincere apologies for the misunderstanding earlier.","plain":"Please accept my true apologies for the misunderstanding earlier.","note":"A more formal and emphatic way to apologize than 'I'm sorry'.","audio":"audio/d929fb71772b1dee.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-video-is-lagging","text":"my video is lagging","kind":"collocation","form":"my video is lagging","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"My video feed is not smooth and appears to be behind the audio or real-time actions.","example":"I'm sorry if I look strange, my video is lagging quite a bit today.","plain":"My video is delayed, so I might appear odd on screen.","note":"Indicates a delay in the visual component of a video call, often due to bandwidth issues.","audio":"audio/ed2b8bf8f9c1d8e7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.no-offense","text":"no offense","kind":"chunk","form":"No offense","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to soften a potentially critical or controversial statement, ensuring it's not taken personally.","example":"No offense, but I think your idea might be a bit too ambitious for our current resources.","plain":"I think your idea might be a bit too ambitious for our current resources.","note":"Informal; often used before a mild criticism or a direct statement.","audio":"audio/b0ffebf5825b03c1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.on-a-related-note","text":"On a related note","kind":"chunk","form":"On a related note","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Introduces a new point that is connected to the previous topic.","example":"We're discussing project timelines. On a related note, the client requested an update.","plain":"We're discussing project timelines, and the client requested an update.","note":"Used to smoothly transition to a connected but distinct point.","audio":"audio/4d57ea4d410d0085.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.on-second-thought","text":"on second thought","kind":"chunk","form":"on second thought","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","travel"],"meaning":"Used to indicate that you have changed your mind or reconsidered a previous statement or decision.","example":"I'll have the chicken salad. On second thought, maybe I'll go for the pasta instead.","plain":"I'll have the chicken salad. Maybe I'll go for the pasta instead.","note":"Common in informal settings when making a quick change of mind.","audio":"audio/d1f44da22ea34eaa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.on-that-note","text":"on that note","kind":"chunk","form":"On that note","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","small-talk","presenting","feedback"],"meaning":"Used to transition to a related topic or action based on the previous point.","example":"We've discussed the budget; on that note, let's talk about resource allocation.","plain":"We've discussed the budget; now let's talk about resource allocation.","note":"Smoothly transitions the conversation, linking it to the previous point.","audio":"audio/1d62186c7aeda11d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.on-the-flip-side","text":"on the flip side","kind":"chunk","form":"on the flip side","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to introduce a contrasting or opposite aspect of a situation or argument.","example":"The new office is closer to home. On the flip side, the rent is significantly higher.","plain":"The new office is closer to home, but the rent is significantly higher.","note":"Informal way to present a counterpoint or a different perspective.","audio":"audio/99e0c60551a94bbe.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.on-the-subject-of","text":"on the subject of","kind":"frame","form":"On the subject of","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to introduce a new point that is related to what was just discussed.","example":"We were talking about travel. On the subject of holidays, where are you going this year?","plain":"We were talking about travel. Where are you going on holiday this year?","note":"A slightly more formal way to transition topics than 'speaking of which'.","audio":"audio/8fd86b4197a1b5c9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.one-area-for","text":"one area for","kind":"frame","form":"One area for","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Introduces a specific aspect where improvement is needed, often used with \"improvement\".","example":"One area for development might be strengthening your presentation delivery skills.","plain":"An aspect where you could improve might be strengthening your presentation delivery skills.","note":"A very common and diplomatic way to introduce constructive criticism.","audio":"audio/a877b29148919cb4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.one-could-argue-that","text":"One could argue that","kind":"frame","form":"One could argue that","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Introduces an alternative viewpoint or counter-argument in an impersonal and objective way.","example":"One could argue that focusing solely on short-term gains might harm long-term growth.","plain":"Some might say that focusing solely on short-term gains might harm long-term growth.","note":"Presents a different perspective without directly stating it as your own, making it less confrontational.","audio":"audio/755fba7488c977a3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.one-could-reasonably-assume","text":"One could reasonably assume","kind":"frame","form":"One could reasonably assume","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Suggests that a particular conclusion or premise is logical and likely to be true.","example":"One could reasonably assume that increased funding would lead to improved research facilities.","plain":"It's logical to assume that increased funding would lead to improved research facilities.","note":"Used to introduce a logical inference or a widely accepted premise.","audio":"audio/261de1a47d972041.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.one-might-argue","text":"One might argue","kind":"frame","form":"One might argue","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Presents a potential argument or perspective in a polite, non-confrontational way.","example":"One might argue that the current framework overlooks crucial socio-economic factors.","plain":"Someone could argue that the current framework overlooks crucial socio-economic factors.","note":"A formal and academic way to introduce a point for discussion.","audio":"audio/4df9fc92b260252b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.one-might-say-that","text":"One might say that","kind":"frame","form":"One might say that","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To introduce a statement as a general observation or a possible interpretation.","example":"One might say that the market is ripe for disruption in this sector.","plain":"It could be said that the market is ready for disruption in this sector.","note":"A cautious and impersonal way to present an idea.","audio":"audio/bf9e9b7bfd3aa65b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.one-thing-to-consider-is","text":"One thing to consider is","kind":"frame","form":"One thing to consider is","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to introduce an important factor or aspect that should be taken into account.","example":"One thing to consider is how these changes will affect our international partners.","plain":"We should consider how these changes will affect our international partners.","note":"A polite way to bring up a new point or a reminder of an overlooked detail.","audio":"audio/5994005546a558a9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.open-for-discussion","text":"open for discussion","kind":"chunk","form":"open for discussion","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"Available for people to talk about and give opinions on.","example":"This topic is now open for discussion, please share your thoughts.","plain":"This topic is now available for people to talk about, please share your thoughts.","note":"Used by the chair to invite input.","audio":"audio/b8ade985f8cdea2a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.open-the-floor","text":"open the floor","kind":"chunk","form":"open the floor","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To invite others to speak or ask questions.","example":"I'd like to open the floor for any questions or comments on the presentation.","plain":"I'd like to invite questions or comments on the presentation.","note":"Formal phrase used by a chair to invite participation.","audio":"audio/89460f9c8dbc746f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.our-collective-goal","text":"Our collective goal","kind":"collocation","form":"our collective goal","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To refer to an objective that everyone involved is working towards together.","example":"Achieving customer satisfaction remains our collective goal across all departments.","plain":"Achieving customer satisfaction remains the goal for everyone in all departments.","note":"Emphasises shared purpose and encourages teamwork. Inclusive and unifying.","audio":"audio/401f953db8372ae2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.our-objective-is-to","text":"Our objective is to","kind":"frame","form":"Our objective is to","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To state the specific goal or target.","example":"Our objective is to increase market share by 15% within the next year.","plain":"Our specific goal is to increase market share by 15% within the next year.","note":"Formal, used to define a clear target.","audio":"audio/d7d323b7d827f329.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.our-time-is-drawing-to-a-close","text":"Our time is drawing to a close","kind":"chunk","form":"Our time is drawing to a close","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Informing participants that the scheduled duration of the call is nearly over.","example":"Our time is drawing to a close, so let's quickly summarize our action points.","plain":"Our time is nearly finished, so let's quickly summarize our action points.","note":"A formal way to signal the need to conclude a meeting.","audio":"audio/a1945c2f648c9cc4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.overcome-an-obstacle","text":"overcome an obstacle","kind":"collocation","form":"overcome an obstacle","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To successfully deal with or surmount a barrier or difficulty.","example":"The team worked hard to overcome an obstacle with the software's compatibility issues.","plain":"The team worked hard to successfully deal with a barrier with the software's compatibility issues.","note":"Emphasizes success in dealing with difficulties. Positive and action-oriented.","audio":"audio/0af0186d00498c2b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.parking-lot","text":"parking lot","kind":"chunk","form":"parking lot","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A list of topics to be discussed later, not in the current meeting.","example":"Let's put that idea in the parking lot for a future discussion.","plain":"Let's save that idea for a future discussion.","note":"Common business jargon for deferring topics.","audio":"audio/b0b633016880bd57.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.perhaps-a-different-approach","text":"Perhaps a different approach","kind":"frame","form":"Perhaps a different approach","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Suggests an alternative method or strategy in a gentle, exploratory manner.","example":"Perhaps a different approach to stakeholder engagement would yield better results.","plain":"Maybe a different approach to stakeholder engagement would yield better results.","note":"Opens the door to new ideas without dismissing the current one outright.","audio":"audio/026cdcf012907601.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.perhaps-i-didnt-explain-myself","text":"Perhaps I didn't explain myself","kind":"chunk","form":"Perhaps I didn't explain myself","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"A polite way to acknowledge that one's previous statement might have been unclear.","example":"Perhaps I didn't explain myself clearly; let me try another approach.","plain":"Maybe I wasn't clear; I'll try to explain it differently.","note":"A diplomatic way to take responsibility for a communication breakdown.","audio":"audio/2cb6dffc0d30453f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.perhaps-we-could-consider","text":"Perhaps we could consider","kind":"frame","form":"Perhaps we could consider","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing","presenting","feedback"],"meaning":"To suggest an idea or alternative in a polite and tentative manner.","example":"Perhaps we could consider integrating this feature into the next update.","plain":"Maybe we should consider integrating this feature into the next update.","note":"A diplomatic way to propose a new idea or course of action.","audio":"audio/3b5031ad09c9d82e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.please-call-me-back","text":"please call me back","kind":"chunk","form":"Please call me back","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I am requesting that you return my phone call at your convenience.","example":"I'm unavailable right now, so please call me back when you have a moment.","plain":"I'm busy now, so please return my call when you are free.","note":"A common request left on voicemails or in messages when you can't speak immediately.","audio":"audio/f41a3d62c429d9e5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.point-of-clarification","text":"point of clarification","kind":"collocation","form":"point of clarification","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A request for more specific information.","example":"I have a point of clarification regarding the project scope.","plain":"I have a question asking for more specific information regarding the project scope.","note":"Formal way to ask for clarification.","audio":"audio/0c660d1457eb0aad.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.pre-empt-any-objections","text":"pre-empt any objections","kind":"collocation","form":"pre-empt any objections","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To deal with potential criticisms or disagreements before they are raised.","example":"I've prepared a detailed FAQ to pre-empt any objections from the board members.","plain":"I've prepared a detailed FAQ to deal with potential criticisms from the board members.","note":"Used when you anticipate resistance and want to address it proactively.","audio":"audio/b92694be5a88f190.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.provide-clarity","text":"provide clarity","kind":"collocation","form":"provide clarity","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To make something easier to understand or less confusing.","example":"Could you provide clarity on the new reporting requirements for the quarterly review?","plain":"Could you make the new reporting requirements for the quarterly review easier to understand?","note":"Essential for effective communication and avoiding misunderstandings. Professional.","audio":"audio/04b113791e66532f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.push-back-on","text":"push back on","kind":"chunk","form":"push back on","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","negotiation"],"meaning":"To resist or object to a proposal or decision.","example":"I might have to push back on that deadline; it seems unrealistic.","plain":"I might have to object to that deadline; it seems unrealistic.","note":"Informal but common for expressing resistance.","audio":"audio/4b3654542099a7ec.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.put-a-pin-in-it","text":"put a pin in it","kind":"chunk","form":"put a pin in it","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To temporarily set aside a topic for later discussion.","example":"That's an interesting point, but let's put a pin in it for now and return later.","plain":"That's an interesting point, but let's save it for now and return later.","note":"Informal, but common in business to defer a topic.","audio":"audio/422faf8571784ad4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.put-a-question-to","text":"put a question to","kind":"collocation","form":"put a question to","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To ask someone a question directly.","example":"I'd like to put a question to the finance team regarding these figures.","plain":"I want to ask the finance team a question about these figures.","note":"Slightly more formal than \"ask a question\".","audio":"audio/62076983ceaaa2f7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.put-a-strain-on","text":"put a strain on","kind":"collocation","form":"put a strain on","level":"C1","situations":["travel","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To create pressure or tension on something or someone.","example":"The increased workload is starting to put a strain on the team's morale.","plain":"The increased workload is starting to create pressure on the team's morale.","note":"Used to describe negative impact or stress.","audio":"audio/a296a9eb286d1b01.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.put-forward-an-argument","text":"put forward an argument","kind":"collocation","form":"put forward an argument","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To present a reason or set of reasons to persuade others.","example":"I'd like to put forward an argument for adopting a more flexible work schedule.","plain":"I'd like to present a reason for adopting a more flexible work schedule.","note":"A formal way to introduce a point you wish to debate or advocate for.","audio":"audio/79b8dc1f2bfdbfe9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.put-it-another-way","text":"put it another way","kind":"frame","form":"put it another way","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To rephrase something for clarity or emphasis.","example":"To put it another way, our current approach isn't sustainable long-term.","plain":"To rephrase, our current approach isn't sustainable long-term.","note":"Useful for clarifying a point or offering a different perspective.","audio":"audio/1e8883e99fb52e96.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.put-on-the-table","text":"put on the table","kind":"collocation","form":"put on the table","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To formally propose an offer or a condition for discussion during a negotiation.","example":"We're ready to put on the table a revised proposal for the project scope.","plain":"We're ready to propose a revised plan for the project scope.","note":"Common in formal and informal negotiations, indicating a willingness to discuss a specific point.","audio":"audio/76184cda5d100a41.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.put-our-best-foot-forward","text":"put our best foot forward","kind":"chunk","form":"put our best foot forward","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To make the best possible effort or impression.","example":"We need to put our best foot forward when presenting this proposal to the potential investors.","plain":"We need to make the best possible effort when presenting this proposal to the potential investors.","note":"Implies presenting oneself or one's work in the most favorable light.","audio":"audio/c42151b548c7279d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.reach-a-destination","text":"reach a destination","kind":"collocation","form":"reach our destination","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To arrive at the place you are travelling to.","example":"After a long journey, we were relieved to finally reach our destination.","plain":"After a long journey, we were relieved to finally arrive at our intended place.","note":"A slightly more formal way to say \"arrive\".","audio":"audio/e92b363954b541ac.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.really-stood-out","text":"really stood out","kind":"chunk","form":"really stood out","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To be particularly noticeable and impressive among others.","example":"Your proactive approach to problem-solving really stood out during the project's critical phase.","plain":"Your proactive approach to problem-solving was very noticeable and impressive during the project's critical phase.","note":"Informal but positive, highlights a specific positive action or quality.","audio":"audio/79e6920a2467856d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.request-a-refund","text":"request a refund","kind":"collocation","form":"request a refund","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To formally ask for your money back for a product or service.","example":"Since the tour was cancelled, I decided to request a refund.","plain":"Since the tour was cancelled, I decided to ask for my money back.","note":"Standard phrase for asking for money back.","audio":"audio/4d52d88fb06a84d7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.resolve-an-issue","text":"resolve an issue","kind":"collocation","form":"resolve the issue","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To find a solution to a problem or difficulty.","example":"We are working hard to resolve the issue with your lost luggage.","plain":"We are working hard to find a solution for your lost luggage problem.","note":"Formal and common in customer service for fixing problems.","audio":"audio/1e289cfbb4506361.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.rest-assured-that","text":"Rest assured that","kind":"frame","form":"Rest assured that","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To strongly guarantee that something is true or will happen, removing any doubt.","example":"Rest assured that your feedback will be taken into serious consideration.","plain":"Be certain that your feedback will be taken into serious consideration.","note":"A formal and strong way to give a guarantee or promise. Very reassuring.","audio":"audio/631d156f0450305d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.run-into-a-snag","text":"run into a snag","kind":"collocation","form":"run into a snag","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To encounter an unexpected problem or difficulty.","example":"We've run into a snag with the budget approval process, which might delay things.","plain":"We've encountered an unexpected problem with the budget approval process, which might delay things.","note":"Slightly informal, refers to a minor but disruptive issue.","audio":"audio/24929c28614b8c6c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.run-over-time","text":"run over time","kind":"collocation","form":"run over time","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To exceed the scheduled duration of the meeting.","example":"We're starting to run over time, so let's try to keep our comments concise.","plain":"We are exceeding the scheduled duration, so let's be concise.","note":"Often used as a gentle warning to speed up.","audio":"audio/a207dde0988f05b5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.same-old-same-old","text":"same old same old","kind":"chunk","form":"Same old same old","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"An informal response indicating that things are continuing as usual, without much change.","example":"How's life? Oh, you know, same old same old. Busy as ever.","plain":"How's life? Oh, you know, things are continuing as usual. Busy as ever.","note":"Very informal; implies a routine or predictable situation.","audio":"audio/362c3b6db21bc31b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.see-you-around","text":"see you around","kind":"chunk","form":"See you around","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"An informal farewell, implying you expect to see the person again soon, but not at a specific time.","example":"I'm heading out. See you around the office!","plain":"I'm heading out. I'll see you again soon in the office!","note":"Common among colleagues or neighbors; implies regular, informal encounters.","audio":"audio/c4c45f72eb859592.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.seek-assistance","text":"seek assistance","kind":"collocation","form":"seek assistance","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To ask for help or support.","example":"If you have any issues, please don't hesitate to seek assistance from staff.","plain":"If you have any issues, please don't hesitate to ask for help from staff.","note":"Formal way to ask for help, often seen in official notices.","audio":"audio/7712d6d26ab19e64.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.seek-input","text":"seek input","kind":"collocation","form":"seek input","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To ask for opinions, advice, or information from others.","example":"We plan to seek input from all stakeholders before finalizing the project scope.","plain":"We plan to ask for opinions from all stakeholders before finalizing the project scope.","note":"Emphasizes inclusivity and collaborative decision-making. Formal and professional.","audio":"audio/d164c3aa3db23f8a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.serve-our-best-interests","text":"Serve our best interests","kind":"collocation","form":"serve our best interests","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To explain that a particular action or decision will be most advantageous for everyone involved.","example":"Implementing these changes will ultimately serve our best interests in the long run.","plain":"Implementing these changes will ultimately be most advantageous for us in the long run.","note":"Used to persuade by highlighting mutual benefit. Professional and strategic.","audio":"audio/be7cbd57f779c48a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.set-a-high-bar","text":"set a high bar","kind":"collocation","form":"set a high bar","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To establish a very high standard of quality or performance.","example":"Your meticulous attention to detail on this project has really set a high bar for future work.","plain":"Your meticulous attention to detail on this project has established a very high standard for future work.","note":"Implies excellence and serves as an example for others. Can be used for individuals or teams.","audio":"audio/e86091817b54e46d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.set-the-stage","text":"set the stage","kind":"collocation","form":"set the stage","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To provide necessary background information or context before discussing the main topic.","example":"Before diving into the data, let me set the stage with a brief overview of our project goals.","plain":"Before the data, I'll give a brief overview of our project goals.","note":"Used to introduce context; sounds professional and engaging.","audio":"audio/3c1b8b2eb903fae6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.shall-we-move-on-to","text":"Shall we move on to","kind":"frame","form":"Shall we move on to","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To politely suggest transitioning to the next topic on the agenda.","example":"We've discussed this thoroughly, so shall we move on to the next agenda item?","plain":"We've discussed this thoroughly, so let's move to the next agenda item.","note":"A polite way to steer the meeting to the next point.","audio":"audio/6ad158d2c131f0e4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.share-concerns","text":"share concerns","kind":"collocation","form":"share concerns","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To communicate worries or anxieties to others.","example":"I wanted to share concerns about the feasibility of meeting the tight deadline with current resources.","plain":"I wanted to communicate worries about the feasibility of meeting the tight deadline with current resources.","note":"A diplomatic way to express worries in a group setting. Professional and collaborative.","audio":"audio/8400f888fa58914c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.shared-understanding","text":"Shared understanding","kind":"collocation","form":"shared understanding","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To refer to a mutual agreement or common comprehension of a topic or situation.","example":"It's crucial that we establish a shared understanding of the project's scope early on.","plain":"It's crucial that we all agree on the project's scope early on.","note":"Emphasises the importance of clear communication and mutual comprehension. Professional.","audio":"audio/a6de2b1d523fae47.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.she-emphasized-that","text":"She emphasized that","kind":"frame","form":"She emphasized that","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Reports someone giving particular importance or attention to a fact or idea.","example":"She emphasized that teamwork was crucial for the project's success.","plain":"She highlighted that teamwork was crucial for the project's success.","note":"Formal, used to convey a strong recommendation or warning.","audio":"audio/8a198d4d93783ff8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.she-made-it-clear-that","text":"She made it clear that","kind":"frame","form":"She made it clear that","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Reports that someone communicated something unambiguously.","example":"She made it clear that she expected the report by Friday.","plain":"She stated explicitly that she expected the report by Friday.","note":"Emphasizes the directness and certainty of the communication.","audio":"audio/7845a91f97da2402.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.she-pointed-out-that","text":"She pointed out that","kind":"frame","form":"She pointed out that","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Reports someone drawing attention to a specific fact or detail.","example":"She pointed out that we had overlooked a crucial clause in the contract.","plain":"She noted that we had overlooked a crucial clause in the contract.","note":"Neutral and factual, used for conveying information shared.","audio":"audio/d553134bb5ad4858.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.she-went-on-about","text":"She went on about","kind":"chunk","form":"She went on about","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Describes someone talking at length, often excessively or annoyingly, about a topic.","example":"She went on about her holiday for ages, showing us all her photos.","plain":"She talked for a long time about her holiday, showing us all her photos.","note":"Informal, can imply slight annoyance or boredom from the listener.","audio":"audio/820af1d9ed8e8e1d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.shed-some-light-on","text":"shed some light on","kind":"collocation","form":"shed some light on","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To provide information that makes something clearer.","example":"Can anyone shed some light on why the project was delayed?","plain":"Can anyone explain why the project was delayed?","note":"Asks for an explanation or clarification.","audio":"audio/b6ee8a1d5ad01362.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.small-wonder-that","text":"Small wonder that","kind":"frame","form":"Small wonder that","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Expresses that a particular outcome or situation is not surprising.","example":"With such a tight deadline, small wonder that the team felt stressed.","plain":"With such a tight deadline, it's not surprising that the team felt stressed.","note":"More concise and slightly more emphatic than \"it's no wonder.\"","audio":"audio/f7976307458c3ced.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.something-to-consider","text":"something to consider","kind":"chunk","form":"something to consider","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"An idea or point that should be thought about carefully.","example":"When planning the next steps, the budget constraints are definitely something to consider.","plain":"When planning the next steps, the budget constraints are definitely an idea to think about carefully.","note":"Presents feedback as a suggestion rather than a direct instruction or criticism.","audio":"audio/c6fb15be33f441d8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.sorry-i-missed-your-call","text":"sorry I missed your call","kind":"chunk","form":"Sorry I missed your call","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"An apology for not being available to answer your phone call when you rang.","example":"Sorry I missed your call earlier; I was in a meeting.","plain":"My apologies for not answering your call earlier; I was in a meeting.","note":"A polite apology when you call someone back after they tried to reach you.","audio":"audio/cb2e0aa82aba9ec5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.sorry-to-interrupt","text":"sorry to interrupt","kind":"chunk","form":"Sorry to interrupt","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"An apology for breaking into someone else's speech, used before making your own point.","example":"Sorry to interrupt, but I have a conflicting appointment at that time.","plain":"My apologies for interrupting, but I have another appointment then.","note":"A standard polite apology when you need to interject, showing respect for the other speaker.","audio":"audio/e4a509e9217e8f1d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.sounds-like-a-plan","text":"Sounds like a plan","kind":"chunk","form":"Sounds like a plan","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"An informal way to express agreement with a proposed plan or suggestion.","example":"\"So, we'll meet at the cafe at two?\" \"Yep, sounds like a plan!\"","plain":"\"So, we'll meet at the cafe at two?\" \"Yep, that's a good plan!\"","note":"Casual and friendly, often used to finalize informal arrangements.","audio":"audio/b4efac2a612d22b9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.stay-on-top-of","text":"stay on top of","kind":"collocation","form":"stay on top of","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To manage a situation or workload effectively and keep it under control.","example":"It's challenging to stay on top of all the incoming requests during peak season.","plain":"It's challenging to manage all the incoming requests effectively during peak season.","note":"Emphasizes proactive management and control. Common in busy work environments.","audio":"audio/0d3e3702b6b0f1a8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.step-up-to-the-plate","text":"step up to the plate","kind":"chunk","form":"step up to the plate","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To take responsibility and deal with a difficult situation.","example":"We need someone to step up to the plate and lead this recovery effort.","plain":"We need someone to take responsibility and lead this recovery effort.","note":"Informal, implies taking initiative in a challenging situation.","audio":"audio/49a881c3b569fb33.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.stick-to-the-agenda","text":"stick to the agenda","kind":"collocation","form":"stick to the agenda","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To remain focused on the planned topics for the meeting.","example":"I know there are other issues, but for now, let's try to stick to the agenda.","plain":"Let's try to remain focused on the planned topics for this meeting.","note":"A polite way to redirect a discussion that is going off-topic.","audio":"audio/e550ff4d20494c1d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.streamline-processes","text":"streamline processes","kind":"collocation","form":"streamline processes","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To make methods or procedures more efficient and effective, often by simplifying them.","example":"We need to streamline processes in the onboarding department to reduce administrative burden.","plain":"We need to make methods in the onboarding department more efficient to reduce administrative burden.","note":"Focuses on improving efficiency and reducing waste. Common in operations management.","audio":"audio/f246588c48f973e4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.suffice-it-to-say","text":"Suffice it to say","kind":"chunk","form":"Suffice it to say","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling","presenting"],"meaning":"It is enough to say, implying that more could be said but isn't necessary.","example":"The negotiations were challenging; suffice it to say, we reached a compromise.","plain":"The negotiations were challenging, but we reached a compromise.","note":"A formal way to indicate that a brief summary is sufficient without details.","audio":"audio/b0761a4f7da642f9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.suggests-a-correlation","text":"Suggests a correlation","kind":"collocation","form":"suggests a correlation","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Indicates that data shows a relationship between two things, without implying causation.","example":"The data suggests a correlation between increased screen time and reduced attention spans.","plain":"The data shows a relationship between increased screen time and reduced attention spans.","note":"Crucial in academic writing to distinguish correlation from causation.","audio":"audio/2e621a12b9bb8107.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.sum-up","text":"sum up","kind":"collocation","form":"sum up","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To provide a brief summary of the main points.","example":"To sum up, we need to finalize the proposal by Friday.","plain":"To summarize, we need to finalize the proposal by Friday.","note":"Standard phrase for summarizing.","audio":"audio/7f06b7e20e899a4c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.table-a-discussion","text":"table a discussion","kind":"collocation","form":"table a discussion","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To postpone a discussion until a later time.","example":"Given the time, I suggest we table a discussion until our next meeting.","plain":"Given the time, I suggest we postpone this discussion until our next meeting.","note":"In American English, this means to postpone. In British English, it means to propose for discussion.","audio":"audio/3b4c66e2234a99ba.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.take-a-collaborative-approach","text":"take a collaborative approach","kind":"collocation","form":"take a collaborative approach","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To work together with others to achieve a common goal.","example":"We should take a collaborative approach to solving this complex inter-departmental issue.","plain":"We should work together to solve this complex inter-departmental issue.","note":"Emphasizes teamwork and shared effort.","audio":"audio/8e8e4f7e09de78af.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.take-a-moment","text":"take a moment","kind":"chunk","form":"take a moment","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To pause briefly to consider something.","example":"Let's take a moment to review the key takeaways from our discussion.","plain":"Let's pause briefly to review the key takeaways from our discussion.","note":"Suggests a short pause for reflection or review.","audio":"audio/3a763f7f6bc3cdb4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.take-a-note-of","text":"take a note of","kind":"collocation","form":"take a note of","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To write down something important to remember or act upon.","example":"Could someone please take a note of that action item for the minutes?","plain":"Could someone please write down that action item for the minutes?","note":"Used for recording specific details or tasks.","audio":"audio/460406eac8020ce5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.take-a-toll-on","text":"take a toll on","kind":"collocation","form":"take a toll on","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To have a serious negative effect on something or someone.","example":"The constant project delays are starting to take a toll on our reputation.","plain":"The constant project delays are starting to have a serious negative effect on our reputation.","note":"Stronger than \"put a strain on,\" implies significant damage or exhaustion.","audio":"audio/00760c7a1112fa00.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.take-a-turn-for-the-worse","text":"Take a turn for the worse","kind":"collocation","form":"took a turn for the worse","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"To become worse or more negative.","example":"After the initial success, the situation quickly took a turn for the worse.","plain":"After the initial success, the situation quickly became worse.","note":"Describes a deterioration in circumstances or health.","audio":"audio/d13c9bcf56b2fd36.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.take-a-vote","text":"take a vote","kind":"collocation","form":"take a vote","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To formally ask for a decision by counting opinions.","example":"If there are no further objections, let's take a vote on the proposal.","plain":"If there are no further objections, let's formally decide on the proposal.","note":"Standard procedure for making a collective decision.","audio":"audio/ff3261a4f6ba2971.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.take-responsibility","text":"take responsibility","kind":"collocation","form":"take responsibility","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To accept accountability for one's actions or for a particular situation.","example":"I will take responsibility for the oversight in the report and ensure it's corrected.","plain":"I will accept accountability for the oversight in the report and ensure it's corrected.","note":"A crucial aspect of professional conduct, especially when problems arise. Positive trait.","audio":"audio/c5f7462d67d010f1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.take-something-offline","text":"take something offline","kind":"chunk","form":"take that offline","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To discuss a specific topic outside of the current meeting.","example":"That's a detailed point, perhaps we should take that offline with the relevant team.","plain":"That's a detailed point, perhaps we should discuss that separately with the relevant team.","note":"Professional way to defer a discussion that is too specific or lengthy for the current meeting.","audio":"audio/8151ea9684e2f2ce.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.talk-through","text":"talk through","kind":"collocation","form":"talk through","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To discuss something in detail, often to resolve issues or understand it better.","example":"Let's talk through the proposed changes to the project scope during our next meeting.","plain":"Let's discuss the proposed changes to the project scope in detail during our next meeting.","note":"Implies a comprehensive discussion to ensure understanding.","audio":"audio/03ef941e3ab7c2ac.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.tell-me-all-about-it","text":"Tell me all about it","kind":"chunk","form":"tell me all about it","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Inviting someone to share the full details of a story or experience.","example":"I heard you got a new job; tell me all about it!","plain":"I heard you got a new job; please share all the details!","note":"Enthusiastic and encouraging, shows genuine interest.","audio":"audio/41ace37d26cf59fa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.tend-to-suggest","text":"Tend to suggest","kind":"collocation","form":"tend to suggest","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Describes data or evidence that points towards a conclusion without proving it definitively.","example":"The preliminary results tend to suggest a positive correlation between the variables.","plain":"The preliminary results indicate a positive correlation between the variables, but it's not certain.","note":"A common way to hedge claims in research, implying a likelihood rather than certainty.","audio":"audio/daa01e945e3aa617.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thank-you-for-pointing-that-out","text":"thank you for pointing that out","kind":"chunk","form":"Thank you for pointing that out","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Expresses gratitude for someone drawing attention to an important detail or error.","example":"Thank you for pointing that out; I hadn't noticed that error in the calculations.","plain":"I appreciate you mentioning that; I hadn't noticed that error in the calculations.","note":"A polite and common way to acknowledge helpful corrective feedback.","audio":"audio/30ccc22d3dec79ae.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thanks-for-calling","text":"thanks for calling","kind":"chunk","form":"Thanks for calling","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"An expression of gratitude to the person who initiated the phone call.","example":"It was good to catch up. Thanks for calling!","plain":"It was good to talk. I appreciate you calling!","note":"A standard, polite closing phrase when ending a call, especially if you were called.","audio":"audio/01eef218671b632a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thanks-for-having-me-over","text":"Thanks for having me over","kind":"chunk","form":"Thanks for having me over","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite expression of gratitude to a host for inviting one to their home.","example":"The dinner was delicious, thanks for having me over.","plain":"The dinner was delicious, thank you for inviting me to your home.","note":"Standard polite phrase used when leaving someone's home after a visit.","audio":"audio/35ab658da7a93077.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thanks-so-much-for","text":"Thanks so much for","kind":"frame","form":"Thanks so much for","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing strong gratitude, often used before declining an invitation.","example":"Thanks so much for thinking of me, but I'm already booked.","plain":"I really appreciate you thinking of me, but I'm already booked.","note":"Softens the refusal by emphasizing appreciation for the invitation.","audio":"audio/d9a3263790e40ee7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.that-being-said","text":"That being said","kind":"chunk","form":"That being said","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation","small-talk","disagreeing","storytelling","persuading","explaining"],"meaning":"Introduces a contrasting point or qualification after acknowledging a previous statement.","example":"The proposal has some strong points; that being said, I have a few reservations about its feasibility.","plain":"The proposal has strong points, but I have reservations about its feasibility.","note":"A useful discourse marker to pivot to a different idea without directly contradicting.","audio":"audio/6f78de7e491cfa07.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.that-must-be-tough","text":"That must be tough","kind":"chunk","form":"That must be tough","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses sympathy and understanding for a difficult situation someone is facing.","example":"Losing your pet after so many years, that must be tough to go through.","plain":"Losing your pet after so many years, that must be difficult to go through.","note":"A compassionate way to acknowledge someone's hardship without being intrusive.","audio":"audio/3abb253f3791fcb4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.that-must-have-been","text":"That must have been","kind":"frame","form":"That must have been","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Expresses a sympathetic or empathetic guess about someone's past experience.","example":"Losing your passport abroad, that must have been a nightmare.","plain":"Losing your passport abroad, that was probably a nightmare.","note":"Used to show understanding of the difficulty or intensity of an experience.","audio":"audio/fe0a968399628ad1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.that-reminds-me","text":"that reminds me","kind":"chunk","form":"That reminds me","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to introduce a new topic or thought that has been triggered by the current conversation.","example":"You mentioned the new software. That reminds me, I need to update my system.","plain":"You mentioned the new software. I need to update my system.","note":"A natural way to shift topics smoothly based on a previous comment.","audio":"audio/dca784473ada8fff.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.that-works-perfectly","text":"That works perfectly","kind":"chunk","form":"That works perfectly","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Confirming that a proposed time, date, or plan is entirely suitable and convenient.","example":"\"How about meeting at 7 PM?\" \"Yes, that works perfectly for me.\"","plain":"\"How about meeting at 7 PM?\" \"Yes, that time is completely suitable for me.\"","note":"A strong and clear affirmation of suitability.","audio":"audio/b4a2595b62d57c86.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-deal","text":"that's a deal","kind":"chunk","form":"That's a deal","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"A concise way to express agreement and finalize a negotiation.","example":"You agree to the new terms? Excellent, that's a deal!","plain":"You agree to the new terms? Excellent, we have an agreement!","note":"Informal and direct, used to confirm an agreement has been reached.","audio":"audio/3e8f4c537d00028b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-fair-assessment","text":"that's a fair assessment","kind":"chunk","form":"That's a fair assessment","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation","storytelling","reacting"],"meaning":"Acknowledges that an evaluation or judgment is accurate and reasonable.","example":"That's a fair assessment of the risks involved; we should address them.","plain":"Your evaluation of the risks involved is accurate; we should address them.","note":"Shows agreement and validates the other party's analysis.","audio":"audio/a489d180db1af73b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-fair-compromise","text":"that's a fair compromise","kind":"chunk","form":"That's a fair compromise","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Acknowledges that a proposed solution or agreement is reasonable and balanced.","example":"That's a fair compromise on the payment schedule; we can work with that.","plain":"That is a reasonable compromise on the payment schedule; we can work with that.","note":"Expresses acceptance of a middle ground, signaling progress.","audio":"audio/0a368462313b6cfa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-reasonable-request","text":"that's a reasonable request","kind":"chunk","form":"That's a reasonable request","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Acknowledges that a demand or suggestion is fair and justifiable.","example":"That's a reasonable request for additional reporting, we can implement that.","plain":"Your request for additional reporting is fair, we can implement that.","note":"A diplomatic way to accept a point, showing willingness to cooperate.","audio":"audio/5dff2d057c9011d0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-relief","text":"That's a relief","kind":"chunk","form":"That's a relief","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Expresses satisfaction or comfort that a difficult situation has ended well.","example":"So the package finally arrived? That's a relief, I was starting to worry.","plain":"So the package finally arrived? I'm glad, I was starting to worry.","note":"A common expression of comfort after a period of concern.","audio":"audio/c1ff4ae0fa612551.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-tough-one","text":"that's a tough one","kind":"chunk","form":"That's a tough one","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Acknowledges that a particular issue or request is difficult to resolve or agree upon.","example":"That's a tough one, as it impacts our core operational efficiency.","plain":"That is a difficult issue, as it impacts our core operational efficiency.","note":"Informal but professional, used to express empathy for a difficult situation.","audio":"audio/37c6579671dfcea7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-valid-point","text":"That's a valid point","kind":"chunk","form":"That's a valid point","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","disagreeing","academic"],"meaning":"Acknowledges that another person's argument or observation is reasonable and well-founded.","example":"That's a valid point regarding the potential for bias in the data collection process.","plain":"That's a good point regarding the potential for bias in the data collection process.","note":"A common and polite way to show agreement or acknowledge the strength of an argument.","audio":"audio/6df07a1a67e541d9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-an-interesting-point","text":"That's an interesting point","kind":"chunk","form":"That's an interesting point","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","presenting"],"meaning":"To acknowledge a contribution as thought-provoking or relevant.","example":"That's an interesting point about market saturation; we should consider it.","plain":"That is a thought-provoking point about market saturation; we should consider it.","note":"A polite way to acknowledge a comment without necessarily agreeing.","audio":"audio/4f9479c29d9617c7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-not-ideal","text":"that's not ideal","kind":"chunk","form":"That's not ideal","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"A polite way to express dissatisfaction or indicate that something is less than perfect.","example":"That's not ideal for our current resource allocation, but we can discuss it.","plain":"That is not perfect for our current resource allocation, but we can discuss it.","note":"Softens a negative reaction, leaving room for further discussion.","audio":"audio/27d590495df36a4e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-outside-the-scope-of","text":"That's outside the scope of","kind":"frame","form":"that's outside the scope of","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To indicate that a question or topic is not relevant to the current discussion.","example":"While important, that's outside the scope of today's presentation on marketing strategy.","plain":"While important, that is not relevant to today's presentation on marketing strategy.","note":"A polite way to defer or redirect a question that is off-topic.","audio":"audio/3a97615eece8f1d3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-quite-a-story","text":"That's quite a story","kind":"chunk","form":"That's quite a story","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Expresses surprise or admiration for an interesting or unusual anecdote.","example":"Wow, that's quite a story; I can't believe you managed to fix it yourself.","plain":"Wow, that's an interesting story; I can't believe you managed to fix it yourself.","note":"A polite and engaging way to acknowledge someone's narrative.","audio":"audio/0c82e7ce62c31d97.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-really-kind-of-you","text":"That's really kind of you","kind":"chunk","form":"That's really kind of you","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses sincere gratitude and appreciation for someone's thoughtful gesture or offer.","example":"That's really kind of you to offer to help me move house this weekend.","plain":"It's very thoughtful of you to offer to help me move house this weekend.","note":"A warm and polite way to thank someone for their generosity or helpfulness.","audio":"audio/c5b7097729c5bfbe.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-really-tough","text":"That's really tough","kind":"chunk","form":"That's really tough","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Acknowledges that someone's situation or experience is difficult and challenging.","example":"Losing your job and then having car trouble, that's really tough, I hope things improve soon.","plain":"Losing your job and then having car trouble, that's very difficult, I hope things improve soon.","note":"Informal and empathetic. Shows understanding of another's hardship.","audio":"audio/335f4ad3f036d155.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-very-kind-of-you","text":"That's very kind of you","kind":"chunk","form":"That's very kind of you","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing appreciation for someone's thoughtfulness or generosity.","example":"That's very kind of you to invite me, but I'm not feeling well.","plain":"It's thoughtful of you to invite me, but I'm not feeling well.","note":"A polite way to acknowledge an invitation, often used before declining.","audio":"audio/2e4eb95f36cf4351.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-audio-is-choppy","text":"the audio is choppy","kind":"collocation","form":"the audio is choppy","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"The sound quality is inconsistent, with parts of the speech cutting in and out rapidly.","example":"It's hard to follow; the audio is choppy, making your words sound broken.","plain":"Your sound quality is bad; your words are broken and hard to understand.","note":"Describes fragmented sound, often due to a weak or intermittent internet connection.","audio":"audio/8d3926fc60a3e9f9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-audio-quality-is-poor","text":"The audio quality is poor","kind":"chunk","form":"The audio quality is poor","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Describing the sound on the call as low fidelity or unclear.","example":"I'm afraid the audio quality is poor, making it hard to catch every word.","plain":"The sound is bad, so it's hard to hear every word.","note":"A general statement about bad sound, often used in formal contexts.","audio":"audio/3e5ea2f17b081949.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-catch-was","text":"The catch was","kind":"frame","form":"The catch was","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Introduces a hidden problem, difficulty, or disadvantage in a situation.","example":"The offer seemed great, but the catch was, it only lasted for a day.","plain":"The offer seemed great, but the problem was, it only lasted for a day.","note":"Used to reveal a complication or a 'downside' in a story.","audio":"audio/4a8c6a21c5be80b3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-central-message-is","text":"The central message is","kind":"frame","form":"The central message is","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To state the most important idea or conclusion.","example":"After reviewing the data, the central message is that we need to diversify.","plain":"After reviewing the data, the most important idea is that we need to diversify.","note":"A clear way to articulate the core takeaway from a discussion.","audio":"audio/6a8a3338de285bdd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-challenge-here-is-to","text":"The challenge here is to","kind":"frame","form":"The challenge here is to","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to define the core difficulty or objective that needs to be addressed.","example":"The challenge here is to balance innovation with the need for stability in our product development.","plain":"The main difficulty here is to balance innovation with the need for stability in our product development.","note":"Clearly articulates the central problem or dilemma. Useful for framing discussions.","audio":"audio/42551b9bdcbe613f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-core-idea-is","text":"the core idea is","kind":"frame","form":"the core idea is","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"Introduces the central and most important concept of a discussion.","example":"The core idea is that we need to innovate constantly to stay competitive in this market.","plain":"The most important concept is that we need to innovate constantly to stay competitive in this market.","note":"Direct and emphasizes the central message.","audio":"audio/ccac821c0e5f09c2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-core-message-is","text":"The core message is","kind":"frame","form":"The core message is","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To state the central idea or most important information.","example":"The core message is that we must adapt quickly to changing market demands.","plain":"The central idea is that we must adapt quickly to changing market demands.","note":"Used to convey the essential point.","audio":"audio/38ca56f38e6eb008.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-core-of-the-issue","text":"The core of the issue","kind":"chunk","form":"The core of the issue","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To identify the fundamental problem or central point of a discussion or situation.","example":"The core of the issue is not a lack of effort, but rather insufficient resources.","plain":"The main problem is not a lack of effort, but rather insufficient resources.","note":"Used to get to the most important part of a problem or argument. Clear and analytical.","audio":"audio/2acaef6727888d19.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-critical-factor-is","text":"The critical factor is","kind":"frame","form":"The critical factor is","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To identify the most important element influencing a situation or outcome.","example":"The critical factor is securing additional funding to complete the research phase.","plain":"The most important element is securing additional funding to complete the research phase.","note":"Used to pinpoint a decisive element.","audio":"audio/e5554793ea9cc101.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-crucial-point-was","text":"The crucial point was","kind":"chunk","form":"The crucial point was","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Highlights the most important or decisive aspect of a situation.","example":"The crucial point was that we needed to secure external investment quickly.","plain":"The most important thing was that we needed to secure external investment quickly.","note":"Emphasizes the key factor that determined the outcome.","audio":"audio/83731fd8b9700964.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-evidence-suggests-that","text":"The evidence suggests that","kind":"frame","form":"The evidence suggests that","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a conclusion or interpretation that is supported by available data or facts.","example":"The evidence suggests that early intervention programs have a lasting positive impact.","plain":"The data indicates that early intervention programs have a lasting positive impact.","note":"A formal and common way to present findings in academic contexts.","audio":"audio/631839eb533ffd80.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-flip-side-is","text":"the flip side is","kind":"collocation","form":"The flip side is","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Introduces the opposite or less positive aspect of a situation or idea.","example":"The flip side is that it might alienate some of our existing users who prefer simplicity.","plain":"The negative aspect is that it might alienate some of our existing users who prefer simplicity.","note":"A common, slightly informal way to introduce a counter-argument or drawback.","audio":"audio/c3daeebb8c7b40ed.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-general-idea-is","text":"the general idea is","kind":"frame","form":"The general idea is","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To provide the main concept or overall purpose without getting into specific details.","example":"We're developing a new app. The general idea is to connect local businesses with customers.","plain":"We're developing a new app. The main concept is to connect local businesses with customers.","note":"Useful for giving a high-level overview before diving into specifics.","audio":"audio/a421f797694e6f4f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-gist-of-it-is","text":"The gist of it is","kind":"frame","form":"the gist of it is","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To introduce the main idea or essence of something, without all the details.","example":"The report is quite dense, but the gist of it is that our market share is growing.","plain":"The report is quite dense, but the main idea is that our market share is growing.","note":"Useful for providing a quick summary of complex information.","audio":"audio/4bcf3bc95736ac44.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-image-is-very-jumpy","text":"The image is very jumpy","kind":"chunk","form":"The image is very jumpy","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Describing a video feed that is not smooth and appears to skip frames.","example":"The image is very jumpy on your video feed, so I can't see you clearly.","plain":"Your video is skipping frames, so I can't see you clearly.","note":"Used to describe poor video quality, often due to low bandwidth.","audio":"audio/048b48a346f723fe.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-irony-was","text":"The irony was","kind":"chunk","form":"The irony was","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Introduces a situation where the outcome is contrary to what was expected.","example":"The irony was, I had spent hours preparing, only for the meeting to be cancelled.","plain":"It was ironic that I had spent hours preparing, only for the meeting to be cancelled.","note":"Used to highlight a surprising or contradictory turn of events.","audio":"audio/4ed966beb66ea22e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-key-is-to","text":"The key is to","kind":"frame","form":"The key is to","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To identify the most important factor or method for success.","example":"The key is to communicate proactively with all stakeholders to manage expectations.","plain":"The most important factor is to communicate proactively with all stakeholders to manage expectations.","note":"Used to highlight the critical element for achieving a goal.","audio":"audio/eb65f8167e29ab28.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-key-message-here-is","text":"The key message here is","kind":"frame","form":"The key message here is","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To state the most important idea or information to be conveyed.","example":"The key message here is that innovation drives sustainable growth.","plain":"The most important idea here is that innovation drives sustainable growth.","note":"Used to distil the core essence of the presentation.","audio":"audio/08ad153d9b7f3cbd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-lesson-i-learned-was","text":"The lesson I learned was","kind":"frame","form":"The lesson I learned was","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"States the personal insight or knowledge gained from an experience.","example":"The lesson I learned was to always trust my instincts in difficult situations.","plain":"I learned to always trust my instincts in difficult situations.","note":"A personal and reflective way to conclude an anecdote.","audio":"audio/dbe60b70b861e82b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-line-is-cutting-out","text":"the line is cutting out","kind":"chunk","form":"the line is cutting out","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"The phone connection is intermittently failing, causing parts of the conversation to be lost.","example":"I think we have a bad connection; the line is cutting out every few seconds.","plain":"Our connection is poor; I keep losing parts of what you're saying.","note":"Refers specifically to the phone connection itself, often used for mobile or landline calls.","audio":"audio/6edcafef0e7e2b70.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-main-advantage-is-that","text":"The main advantage is that","kind":"frame","form":"The main advantage is that","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To highlight the primary benefit of a proposal.","example":"The main advantage is that this new system will significantly reduce our operational costs.","plain":"The biggest benefit is that this new system will significantly reduce our operational costs.","note":"Directly points out the most compelling positive aspect of an idea.","audio":"audio/51b5c230a0f42b28.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-main-drawback","text":"The main drawback","kind":"collocation","form":"the main drawback","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To identify the primary disadvantage or negative feature of something.","example":"The main drawback of this solution is its high implementation cost.","plain":"The primary disadvantage of this solution is its high implementation cost.","note":"Used to pinpoint the most significant negative aspect. Direct and evaluative.","audio":"audio/c28c87a30f18e953.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-main-hurdle-we-face-is","text":"The main hurdle we face is","kind":"frame","form":"The main hurdle we face is","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to identify the biggest obstacle or challenge that needs to be overcome.","example":"The main hurdle we face is getting all departments to adopt the new system consistently.","plain":"The biggest obstacle we face is getting all departments to adopt the new system consistently.","note":"Clearly defines the primary difficulty, setting the stage for problem-solving.","audio":"audio/f8f0ebe8b64d67d6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-main-objective-is","text":"The main objective is","kind":"frame","form":"The main objective is","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To state the primary goal or aim of the discussion or project.","example":"The main objective is to enhance efficiency across all departments.","plain":"The primary goal is to enhance efficiency across all departments.","note":"Clearly defines the central purpose of an initiative.","audio":"audio/44bd0ccf58f34200.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-main-takeaway-is","text":"the main takeaway is","kind":"frame","form":"The main takeaway is","level":"C1","situations":["presenting","explaining"],"meaning":"To state the most important piece of information or conclusion to remember from a discussion.","example":"We covered a lot, but the main takeaway is that we need to focus on customer retention.","plain":"We covered a lot, but the most important thing to remember is that we need to focus on customer retention.","note":"Highlights the key message or learning point.","audio":"audio/e492695529d89428.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-main-thrust-of","text":"The main thrust of","kind":"collocation","form":"The main thrust of","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To refer to the primary purpose, intention, or direction of something.","example":"The main thrust of our marketing campaign is to reach a younger demographic.","plain":"The primary purpose of our marketing campaign is to reach a younger demographic.","note":"Describes the core idea or driving force behind an initiative.","audio":"audio/354daa38b3eb4871.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-only-thing-is","text":"The only thing is","kind":"frame","form":"The only thing is","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Introduces a single, often significant, point of disagreement or difficulty.","example":"The plan sounds good; the only thing is, we don't have the budget for it right now.","plain":"The plan sounds good, but we don't have the budget for it right now.","note":"A casual but effective way to introduce a problem or objection.","audio":"audio/1cdca92ff6f89e69.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-overriding-concern","text":"The overriding concern","kind":"collocation","form":"The overriding concern","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To identify the most important or dominant worry or consideration.","example":"The overriding concern for the board is the company's financial stability.","plain":"The most important concern for the board is the company's financial stability.","note":"Refers to the primary issue that takes precedence over others.","audio":"audio/a2ec0726ba35bcb9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-picture-is-pixelated","text":"the picture is pixelated","kind":"collocation","form":"the picture is pixelated","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Your video image appears blocky and low-resolution due to a poor connection.","example":"Your video is very blurry; the picture is pixelated on my screen.","plain":"Your video is blurry and blocky on my screen.","note":"Describes poor video quality where individual pixels are visible, common with low bandwidth.","audio":"audio/6130f80872325e92.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-plot-thickened","text":"The plot thickened","kind":"chunk","form":"The plot thickened","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Indicates that a situation became more complicated or mysterious.","example":"The plot thickened when we discovered a hidden message in the old letter.","plain":"The situation became more complicated when we discovered a hidden message.","note":"Informal, often used humorously or dramatically in storytelling.","audio":"audio/bdc53a4c241a7ff5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-potential-downside","text":"The potential downside","kind":"collocation","form":"the potential downside","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To refer to a possible negative aspect or disadvantage of a plan, decision, or situation.","example":"We need to carefully evaluate the potential downside of expanding into a new market.","plain":"We need to carefully evaluate the possible negative aspect of expanding into a new market.","note":"Used when discussing risks or disadvantages. Analytical and cautious.","audio":"audio/3117d07c971f568c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-primary-obstacle-is","text":"The primary obstacle is","kind":"frame","form":"The primary obstacle is","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To identify the most significant barrier to progress.","example":"The primary obstacle is the lack of skilled personnel for this specialized task.","plain":"The most significant barrier is the lack of skilled personnel for this specialized task.","note":"Used to highlight the biggest challenge.","audio":"audio/49ed304454d6a894.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-primary-reason-is","text":"The primary reason is","kind":"collocation","form":"The primary reason is","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To state the main or most important cause or justification for something.","example":"The primary reason is that this approach offers the best return on investment.","plain":"The main reason is that this approach offers the best return on investment.","note":"Used to present the most compelling justification for a decision or proposal. Direct and logical.","audio":"audio/0314c0ba90531306.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-punchline-was","text":"The punchline was","kind":"chunk","form":"The punchline was","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Introduces the surprising or humorous conclusion of a story or joke.","example":"The punchline was, he'd been talking to the wrong person the whole time!","plain":"The funny part was, he'd been talking to the wrong person the whole time!","note":"Specifically for the climax of a humorous anecdote.","audio":"audio/91647703c75b4f92.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-room-isnt-up-to-standard","text":"The room isn't up to standard","kind":"chunk","form":"The room isn't up to standard","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Stating that the quality of a room is not good enough.","example":"The air conditioning is broken, so the room isn't up to standard.","plain":"The air conditioning is broken, so the room is not good enough.","note":"A common phrase for hotel complaints, implying a breach of expected quality.","audio":"audio/15d909d13bded0a7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-sticking-point-for-me","text":"The sticking point for me","kind":"frame","form":"The sticking point for me","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Highlights the specific issue that prevents full agreement or progress.","example":"The sticking point for me is the lack of flexibility in the proposed schedule.","plain":"The main problem for me is the lack of flexibility in the proposed schedule.","note":"A clear and direct way to identify the obstacle to agreement.","audio":"audio/726cb40e4804931f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-tension-was-palpable","text":"The tension was palpable","kind":"collocation","form":"The tension was palpable","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Describes a situation where stress or anxiety is very noticeable.","example":"The tension was palpable as we waited for the exam results to be announced.","plain":"We could feel the tension as we waited for the exam results.","note":"A vivid description, often used for high-stakes situations.","audio":"audio/7d8409818ca58e30.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-thing-was","text":"The thing was","kind":"chunk","form":"The thing was","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Introduces an important detail or explanation that clarifies a situation.","example":"The thing was, I hadn't checked my email all weekend, so I missed the update.","plain":"I hadn't checked my email all weekend, so I missed the update.","note":"A common discourse marker to add crucial information or context.","audio":"audio/2385ab927a15cf83.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-upshot-was","text":"The upshot was","kind":"frame","form":"The upshot was","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Introduces the final result or outcome of a series of events.","example":"We debated for hours, and the upshot was, we decided to postpone.","plain":"We debated for hours, and the result was, we decided to postpone.","note":"A slightly more formal way to state the conclusion or consequence.","audio":"audio/8edc8c2a6b792eaf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-video-feed-is-choppy","text":"The video feed is choppy","kind":"chunk","form":"The video feed is choppy","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Describing a video stream that is not smooth and appears to stutter.","example":"The video feed is choppy, so it's hard to follow your presentation slides.","plain":"Your video is not smooth, making it hard to follow your presentation.","note":"Similar to \"jumpy,\" but often implies more frequent, smaller interruptions.","audio":"audio/844815a502c08b0e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-way-i-look-at-it","text":"the way I look at it","kind":"frame","form":"The way I look at it","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To introduce one's personal opinion or interpretation of a situation.","example":"The way I look at it, this challenge is an opportunity for innovation.","plain":"In my opinion, this challenge is an opportunity for innovation.","note":"Similar to \"the way I see it,\" but perhaps slightly more reflective.","audio":"audio/6accf2939dbb1a70.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-way-i-see-it-is","text":"The way I see it is","kind":"frame","form":"The way I see it is","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Used to introduce your personal opinion or perspective on a matter.","example":"The way I see it is, clear communication is paramount for project success.","plain":"In my opinion, clear communication is paramount for project success.","note":"Sounds thoughtful and assertive.","audio":"audio/ea4b7313719c2ec0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.there-seems-to-be-a-problem-with","text":"There seems to be a problem with","kind":"frame","form":"There seems to be a problem with","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Politely indicating that an issue exists, without directly blaming.","example":"There seems to be a problem with the hot water in our room.","plain":"The hot water in our room isn't working.","note":"A diplomatic way to introduce a complaint or issue.","audio":"audio/e1bdef0c41a11bf8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.theres-a-delay","text":"there's a delay","kind":"collocation","form":"there's a delay","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"There is a noticeable pause or lag between when one person speaks and the other hears it.","example":"Sorry, there's a delay on the line, so I might accidentally talk over you.","plain":"The sound is lagging, so I might interrupt you by mistake.","note":"Explains why conversation flow might be awkward, often due to international calls or poor internet.","audio":"audio/fb05bad10d5f0f02.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.theres-a-lot-of-static","text":"There's a lot of static","kind":"chunk","form":"There's a lot of static","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Describing a crackling or hissing sound on the call.","example":"I'm sorry, but there's a lot of static on your line, making it difficult to understand.","plain":"Your line has a lot of crackling noise, so it's hard to understand.","note":"Common phrase for poor audio quality, usually due to a weak signal.","audio":"audio/e55d66b63a6d7010.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.theres-a-slight-wrinkle","text":"There's a slight wrinkle","kind":"chunk","form":"There's a slight wrinkle","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Indicates a minor problem or complication that needs to be addressed.","example":"Your plan is solid, but there's a slight wrinkle regarding the availability of key personnel.","plain":"Your plan is solid, but there's a small problem regarding the availability of key personnel.","note":"A casual and understated way to introduce a problem or objection.","audio":"audio/27a32563e2408e29.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.things-are-looking-up","text":"things are looking up","kind":"chunk","form":"Things are looking up","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to express optimism that a situation is improving or will improve.","example":"After a tough quarter, things are looking up for the company.","plain":"After a tough quarter, the company's situation is improving.","note":"A positive and hopeful phrase, good for sharing good news or optimism.","audio":"audio/c2bbdaaad0847672.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.things-took-an-unexpected-turn","text":"Things took an unexpected turn","kind":"chunk","form":"Things took an unexpected turn","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Describes a sudden and surprising change in a situation.","example":"Things took an unexpected turn when the client suddenly changed their requirements.","plain":"The situation changed suddenly when the client changed their requirements.","note":"Useful for marking a surprising development in a narrative.","audio":"audio/e68096140bd80bfe.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.think-of-it-like-this","text":"think of it like this","kind":"frame","form":"Think of it like this","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To introduce an analogy or a simpler way of conceptualizing a complex idea.","example":"The network acts as a central hub. Think of it like this: it's the brain of the whole system.","plain":"The network acts as a central hub; it's the brain of the whole system.","note":"Excellent for simplifying complex ideas through comparison.","audio":"audio/02e05cb8b1c10cd1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-brings-me-to-my-next-point","text":"This brings me to my next point","kind":"frame","form":"This brings me to my next point","level":"C1","situations":["academic","presenting"],"meaning":"A transition phrase used to introduce the subsequent topic or argument.","example":"Given these challenges, this brings me to my next point: the need for adaptive strategies.","plain":"Given these challenges, my next point is the need for adaptive strategies.","note":"A clear and direct way to move the discussion forward.","audio":"audio/d97796321007538c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-brings-us-nicely-to","text":"This brings us nicely to","kind":"frame","form":"this brings us nicely to","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To smoothly transition to a related topic that naturally follows.","example":"Our discussion on market trends, this brings us nicely to our new product strategy.","plain":"Our discussion on market trends leads us smoothly to our new product strategy.","note":"A common and elegant way to link ideas in a presentation.","audio":"audio/0d39dc3d186025dd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-brings-us-to","text":"This brings us to","kind":"frame","form":"This brings us to","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Connects a previous point or discussion to the next logical topic or stage.","example":"This brings us to the practical implications of these theoretical findings for policy makers.","plain":"Now, let's discuss the practical implications of these theoretical findings for policy makers.","note":"A smooth transition phrase, often used in presentations.","audio":"audio/8736467459645787.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-a-chance-to","text":"This is a chance to","kind":"frame","form":"This is a chance to","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To present a situation as an opportunity for improvement or success.","example":"This is a chance to learn from our mistakes and implement better practices.","plain":"We can learn from our mistakes and implement better practices.","note":"Frames a challenge as an opportunity for growth, encouraging a positive outlook.","audio":"audio/86cdaf0624884ca9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-crucial-because","text":"This is crucial because","kind":"frame","form":"This is crucial because","level":"C1","situations":["presenting","persuading"],"meaning":"To explain the fundamental importance of a point or action.","example":"This is crucial because it directly impacts our ability to meet regulatory requirements.","plain":"This is very important because it directly impacts our ability to meet regulatory requirements.","note":"Used to stress the significance of a particular aspect, often followed by a reason.","audio":"audio/62b5636ab6e15777.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-not-necessarily-the-case","text":"This is not necessarily the case","kind":"frame","form":"This is not necessarily the case","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Challenges an assumption by stating that a situation might not always be as it seems.","example":"While increased funding often helps, this is not necessarily the case for all research areas.","plain":"While increased funding often helps, it's not always true for all research areas.","note":"A polite way to express disagreement or introduce a nuance.","audio":"audio/33406ca8abc78965.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-not-to-diminish-the-role-of","text":"This is not to diminish the role of","kind":"frame","form":"This is not to diminish the role of","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Clarifies that a statement is not intended to lessen the importance of something else.","example":"This is not to diminish the role of individual effort, but systemic factors are crucial.","plain":"I don't mean to say individual effort isn't important, but systemic factors are crucial.","note":"Diplomatic, used to qualify a statement and avoid misinterpretation.","audio":"audio/118597d396d78b7c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-not-to-say","text":"This is not to say","kind":"frame","form":"This is not to say","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Clarifies a previous statement by indicating what it does not imply or mean.","example":"This is not to say that the previous research was flawed, but rather that our understanding has evolved.","plain":"I'm not saying the previous research was flawed; our understanding has simply evolved.","note":"Used to prevent misinterpretation or to refine a point.","audio":"audio/376d0edc604690fb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-not-to-suggest-that","text":"This is not to suggest that","kind":"frame","form":"This is not to suggest that","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Clarifies a previous statement by indicating what it does not imply or mean.","example":"This is not to suggest that the previous research was flawed, but rather that our understanding has evolved.","plain":"I'm not suggesting the previous research was flawed; our understanding has simply evolved.","note":"Used to prevent misinterpretation or to refine a point.","audio":"audio/68b2059afaf68ff9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-paramount","text":"This is paramount","kind":"collocation","form":"this is paramount","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To state that something is of utmost importance or significance.","example":"Ensuring data security, this is paramount for our clients' trust.","plain":"Ensuring data security is extremely important for our clients' trust.","note":"A formal and strong way to express extreme importance.","audio":"audio/e79e95403f003e30.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-particularly-relevant-when","text":"This is particularly relevant when","kind":"frame","form":"This is particularly relevant when","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Emphasizes the special applicability of a point in a specific situation or scenario.","example":"This is particularly relevant when designing educational programs for diverse learners.","plain":"This is especially important when designing educational programs for diverse learners.","note":"Used to highlight the specific context where a point holds strong significance.","audio":"audio/4ca16fe1fa73162c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-particularly-true","text":"This is particularly true","kind":"frame","form":"This is particularly true","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Emphasizes that a general statement applies with special force or relevance in a specific context.","example":"This is particularly true when discussing the ethical implications of AI development.","plain":"This is especially true when discussing the ethical implications of AI development.","note":"Used to highlight a specific application or context where a point is highly relevant.","audio":"audio/ec619174d1f43d32.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-why-i-believe","text":"This is why I believe","kind":"frame","form":"This is why I believe","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To introduce a conclusion or opinion, linking it to previous points.","example":"This is why I believe we should proceed with the proposed budget adjustments.","plain":"Based on what I've said, I think we should proceed with the proposed budget adjustments.","note":"Connects reasoning to a personal conviction, making the argument clearer.","audio":"audio/f6381fbfce1c5a80.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-leads-me-to","text":"This leads me to","kind":"frame","form":"This leads me to","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Connects a previous point or observation to the next logical step or conclusion.","example":"This leads me to the next section, which discusses the practical applications of our findings.","plain":"This connects to the next section, which discusses the practical applications of our findings.","note":"A useful discourse marker for smooth transitions in a presentation or argument.","audio":"audio/854b49f04e628110.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-one-time","text":"This one time","kind":"chunk","form":"This one time","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"An informal way to introduce a specific past event or anecdote.","example":"This one time, I accidentally locked my keys inside my apartment.","plain":"Once, I accidentally locked my keys inside my apartment.","note":"Very common in casual conversation, often followed by a personal story.","audio":"audio/78a392f521b37494.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-raises-the-question-of","text":"This raises the question of","kind":"frame","form":"This raises the question of","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Indicates that a previous point naturally leads to a particular inquiry or issue.","example":"This raises the question of how we can effectively measure long-term societal impact.","plain":"This makes us ask how we can effectively measure long-term societal impact.","note":"A common way to transition to a new area of inquiry or problem.","audio":"audio/bc84383f15a9a541.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-suggests-that","text":"This suggests that","kind":"frame","form":"This suggests that","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a conclusion or interpretation drawn from previous information or data.","example":"The data shows a decline in engagement, and this suggests that our outreach strategy needs revision.","plain":"The data shows a decline in engagement, so our outreach strategy needs revision.","note":"Used to present an inference or a logical next step in reasoning.","audio":"audio/4d4b40ea2e71583c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-underscores-the-need-for","text":"This underscores the need for","kind":"frame","form":"this underscores the need for","level":"C1","situations":["academic","presenting"],"meaning":"To show that something makes a particular need or requirement very clear.","example":"The recent security breach, this underscores the need for stronger protocols.","plain":"The recent security breach makes the need for stronger protocols very clear.","note":"Emphasises a consequence or a lesson learned.","audio":"audio/28470ffe83d60323.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-will-lead-to","text":"This will lead to","kind":"frame","form":"This will lead to","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To explain the direct consequence or outcome of an action.","example":"This will lead to increased efficiency and reduced operational costs in the long run.","plain":"This will result in increased efficiency and reduced operational costs in the long run.","note":"Clearly outlines the expected results, often positive, of a proposed change.","audio":"audio/84bd0e673f04a283.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-will-result-in","text":"This will result in","kind":"frame","form":"This will result in","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To explain the direct outcome or consequence of an action.","example":"This will result in a significant reduction in our operating expenses.","plain":"This will cause a significant reduction in our operating expenses.","note":"A formal way to state the expected effects of a decision or action.","audio":"audio/49656fe51150a843.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-a-certain-degree","text":"To a certain degree","kind":"chunk","form":"to a certain degree","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Indicates that something is true or applicable only partially, not completely.","example":"The model is accurate to a certain degree, but it struggles with extreme outliers.","plain":"The model is somewhat accurate, but it struggles with extreme outliers.","note":"Similar to \"to some extent,\" emphasizing the limited scope of applicability.","audio":"audio/db9b8ec1bb6e0175.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-address-a-concern","text":"to address a concern","kind":"collocation","form":"address a concern","level":"C1","situations":["interviews","travel"],"meaning":"To deal with or respond to a worry or issue.","example":"I'd like to address a concern regarding the project timeline if I may.","plain":"I'd like to deal with an issue regarding the project timeline if I may.","note":"Professional way to bring up an issue.","audio":"audio/6a1fed5909e16ffc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-address-your-concern","text":"To address your concern","kind":"chunk","form":"To address your concern","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To respond directly to a worry or issue raised by someone.","example":"To address your concern about data privacy, we have implemented robust encryption.","plain":"To respond to your concern about data privacy, we have implemented robust encryption.","note":"Shows that you are listening and taking the audience's points seriously.","audio":"audio/bfd69b9e3f351447.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-address-your-point","text":"To address your point","kind":"chunk","form":"To address your point","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","interviews","presenting","persuading"],"meaning":"Indicates that you are about to respond directly to a specific comment or question.","example":"To address your point about resources, we've secured additional funding for this phase.","plain":"Regarding your point about resources, we've secured additional funding for this phase.","note":"A polite and direct way to respond to a specific query or objection.","audio":"audio/7a0670606c1a4dfc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-address-your-question","text":"To address your question","kind":"chunk","form":"To address your question","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To respond directly to a question that has been asked.","example":"To address your question about funding, we are actively seeking new investors.","plain":"Regarding your question about funding, we are actively seeking new investors.","note":"A polite and direct way to begin answering a question.","audio":"audio/7c6415a35591e624.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-articulate-a-vision","text":"to articulate a vision","kind":"collocation","form":"to articulate a vision","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To express a clear and coherent idea of what one wants to achieve in the future.","example":"A key leadership skill is the ability to articulate a vision that inspires the team.","plain":"A key leadership skill is the ability to express a clear future goal that inspires the team.","note":"Used for leaders or those in strategic roles.","audio":"audio/b09ef0aac5b2aaa0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-articulate-my-thoughts","text":"to articulate my thoughts","kind":"collocation","form":"articulate my thoughts","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To express one's ideas or opinions clearly and effectively.","example":"Please allow me a moment to articulate my thoughts on that complex question.","plain":"Please allow me a moment to express my thoughts clearly on that complex question.","note":"Buys time and signals a thoughtful response.","audio":"audio/cfb94ebf032d8c16.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-critical-thinker","text":"to be a critical thinker","kind":"collocation","form":"a critical thinker","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Someone who analyzes information objectively and makes reasoned judgments.","example":"As a critical thinker, I always evaluate situations from multiple angles before deciding.","plain":"As someone who analyzes information objectively, I always evaluate situations from multiple angles before deciding.","note":"Important for strategic roles.","audio":"audio/070b3202d54588d8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-double-edged-sword","text":"to be a double-edged sword","kind":"chunk","form":"a double-edged sword","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Something that has both positive and negative consequences or aspects.","example":"Outsourcing can be a double-edged sword; it saves costs but can impact quality control.","plain":"Outsourcing has both positive and negative consequences; it saves costs but can impact quality control.","note":"Highlights the inherent risks or trade-offs in a situation. Useful for nuanced discussions.","audio":"audio/3078c11230fa51d2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-analyst","text":"to be a good analyst","kind":"collocation","form":"to be a good analyst","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be skilled at examining information in detail to understand it or explain it.","example":"My background has trained me to be a good analyst of market trends.","plain":"My background has trained me to examine market trends in detail.","note":"Highlights analytical and interpretive skills.","audio":"audio/7fd7212935c9e1a0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-coach","text":"to be a good coach","kind":"collocation","form":"to be a good coach","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be skilled at guiding and training others to improve their performance.","example":"As a manager, I strive to be a good coach, empowering my team to succeed.","plain":"As a manager, I strive to guide and train my team to improve their performance.","note":"Emphasizes developing others' abilities.","audio":"audio/c008fb9e037ea4b7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-crisis-manager","text":"to be a good crisis manager","kind":"collocation","form":"to be a good crisis manager","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be skilled at handling difficult or dangerous situations effectively.","example":"In unexpected situations, I've learned to be a good crisis manager, staying calm.","plain":"In unexpected situations, I've learned to handle difficult situations effectively.","note":"Highlights composure and effectiveness under pressure.","audio":"audio/937bebf7499d3467.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-cultural-architect","text":"to be a good cultural architect","kind":"collocation","form":"a good cultural architect","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To actively design and build the desired values and environment of a company.","example":"In this role, you'll have the opportunity to be a good cultural architect for the team.","plain":"In this role, you'll have the opportunity to design and build the desired values for the team.","note":"Suggests a proactive role in shaping culture.","audio":"audio/0e29b96bede4716f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-cultural-champion","text":"to be a good cultural champion","kind":"collocation","form":"a good cultural champion","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be a strong and enthusiastic supporter of a company's culture, often leading by example.","example":"We need someone who can be a good cultural champion, inspiring others with our values.","plain":"We need someone who can be a strong supporter of our culture, inspiring others with our values.","note":"Implies leadership in cultural matters.","audio":"audio/e9fff47a34bdd86a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-cultural-contributor","text":"to be a good cultural contributor","kind":"collocation","form":"a good cultural contributor","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To actively participate in and positively influence the values and environment of a company.","example":"I aim to be a good cultural contributor, fostering a positive and inclusive work environment.","plain":"I aim to actively and positively influence the work environment and values.","note":"Emphasizes active participation in shaping culture.","audio":"audio/77c3a73bbc0bda89.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-cultural-match","text":"to be a good cultural match","kind":"collocation","form":"a good cultural match","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To align well with the values, norms, and environment of a company or team.","example":"Finding a good cultural match is as important as finding the right skills for us.","plain":"Finding someone who aligns with our culture is as important as finding the right skills for us.","note":"Another variation for cultural alignment.","audio":"audio/096697649ee53b65.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-decision-maker","text":"to be a good decision-maker","kind":"collocation","form":"to be a good decision-maker","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be skilled at making sound and timely choices.","example":"In high-pressure situations, I've learned to be a good decision-maker under uncertainty.","plain":"In high-pressure situations, I've learned to make sound and timely choices.","note":"Emphasizes judgment and ability to act.","audio":"audio/501b43d3f9d14115.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-delegator","text":"to be a good delegator","kind":"collocation","form":"to be a good delegator","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be skilled at assigning tasks and responsibilities to others effectively.","example":"Learning to be a good delegator was crucial for managing larger projects.","plain":"Learning to assign tasks and responsibilities effectively was crucial.","note":"Highlights efficiency and trust in team members.","audio":"audio/c8e6d55c34dc1ba2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-executor","text":"to be a good executor","kind":"collocation","form":"to be a good executor","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be skilled at carrying out plans or orders effectively.","example":"It's one thing to plan, but you also need to be a good executor to see results.","plain":"It's one thing to plan, but you also need to carry out plans effectively.","note":"Emphasizes practical implementation and delivery.","audio":"audio/8e2c4408af2eebff.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-fit","text":"to be a good fit","kind":"chunk","form":"a good fit","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be suitable or well-matched for a particular role, team, or culture.","example":"I believe my skills and values make me a good fit for your organization.","plain":"I believe my skills and values make me suitable for your organization.","note":"Very common in interviews. Can be used by either party.","audio":"audio/aeb82aa792b072ba.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-fit-for","text":"to be a good fit for","kind":"frame","form":"a good fit for","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be suitable or well-matched for a particular role, team, or culture.","example":"I believe my experience makes me a good fit for this challenging leadership role.","plain":"I believe my experience makes me suitable for this challenging leadership role.","note":"Common way to express suitability for a role.","audio":"audio/a4c7797f47efae50.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-fit-overall","text":"to be a good fit overall","kind":"collocation","form":"a good fit overall","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be suitable in general, considering all aspects.","example":"I believe I'm a good fit overall for both the role and the company culture.","plain":"I believe I'm suitable in general for both the role and the company culture.","note":"A comprehensive way to express suitability.","audio":"audio/3886bb6b2b33a5a9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-leader","text":"to be a good leader","kind":"collocation","form":"to be a good leader","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be skilled at guiding and directing a group of people.","example":"My goal is to be a good leader, inspiring trust and fostering collaboration.","plain":"My goal is to guide and direct a group of people effectively.","note":"Focuses on inspirational and directional qualities.","audio":"audio/d06ab950e60e7d37.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-manager","text":"to be a good manager","kind":"collocation","form":"to be a good manager","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be skilled at organizing and controlling people or resources.","example":"I aspire to be a good manager, empowering my team to achieve their best.","plain":"I aspire to organize and control people and resources effectively.","note":"General term for leadership and organizational skills.","audio":"audio/7dacba8dbe8a0b0f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-mediator","text":"to be a good mediator","kind":"collocation","form":"to be a good mediator","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be skilled at resolving disagreements between people or groups.","example":"In my previous role, I had to be a good mediator to resolve conflicts between departments.","plain":"In my previous role, I had to resolve conflicts between departments.","note":"Highlights conflict resolution skills.","audio":"audio/e7a10ed4f501604f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-motivator","text":"to be a good motivator","kind":"collocation","form":"to be a good motivator","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be skilled at inspiring and encouraging others to achieve goals.","example":"A key aspect of leadership is to be a good motivator for your team.","plain":"A key aspect of leadership is to inspire and encourage your team.","note":"Emphasizes inspiring enthusiasm and drive.","audio":"audio/fb693a554a1d3c68.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-negotiator","text":"to be a good negotiator","kind":"collocation","form":"to be a good negotiator","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be skilled at discussing and reaching agreements, especially in business.","example":"My experience in sales taught me how to be a good negotiator for complex deals.","plain":"My experience in sales taught me how to discuss and reach agreements for complex deals.","note":"Emphasizes persuasive and agreement-making skills.","audio":"audio/0f8c31f2803e6ae4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-planner","text":"to be a good planner","kind":"collocation","form":"to be a good planner","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be skilled at organizing and preparing for future events or projects.","example":"I always strive to be a good planner, ensuring all project steps are considered.","plain":"I always strive to organize and prepare for future projects.","note":"Highlights organizational and foresight skills.","audio":"audio/e709fe8bf2a03bac.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-presenter","text":"to be a good presenter","kind":"collocation","form":"to be a good presenter","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be skilled at delivering information or ideas to an audience.","example":"I often have to be a good presenter when explaining new strategies to stakeholders.","plain":"I often have to deliver information effectively when explaining new strategies.","note":"Emphasizes public speaking and explanation skills.","audio":"audio/8bd50c466da3ae25.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-problem-anticipator","text":"to be a good problem anticipator","kind":"collocation","form":"to be a good problem anticipator","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be skilled at foreseeing potential issues before they arise.","example":"My experience allows me to be a good problem anticipator, preventing many issues.","plain":"My experience allows me to foresee potential issues, preventing many problems.","note":"Emphasizes proactive thinking and foresight.","audio":"audio/3c0db785869a4967.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-problem-identifier","text":"to be a good problem identifier","kind":"collocation","form":"to be a good problem identifier","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be skilled at recognizing and pinpointing issues or challenges.","example":"Before solving, you need to be a good problem identifier to understand the root cause.","plain":"Before solving, you need to recognize and pinpoint issues to understand the root cause.","note":"Emphasizes diagnostic skills.","audio":"audio/9961510f7d9268fe.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-project-manager","text":"to be a good project manager","kind":"collocation","form":"to be a good project manager","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be skilled at planning, executing, and closing projects successfully.","example":"My experience has prepared me to be a good project manager for complex initiatives.","plain":"My experience has prepared me to plan, execute, and close projects successfully.","note":"Highlights specific project management skills.","audio":"audio/358167b0cc247990.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-resource-allocator","text":"to be a good resource allocator","kind":"collocation","form":"to be a good resource allocator","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be skilled at distributing assets and personnel efficiently.","example":"In my previous role, I had to be a good resource allocator for various projects.","plain":"In my previous role, I had to distribute assets and personnel efficiently.","note":"Highlights strategic planning and efficiency.","audio":"audio/4d9cd5eefb873d83.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-risk-assessor","text":"to be a good risk assessor","kind":"collocation","form":"to be a good risk assessor","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be skilled at identifying and evaluating potential dangers or uncertainties.","example":"It's crucial to be a good risk assessor in any new project development.","plain":"It's crucial to identify and evaluate potential dangers in any new project.","note":"Highlights foresight and analytical skills in risk management.","audio":"audio/0b5c67dadd0130e1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-solution-provider","text":"to be a good solution provider","kind":"collocation","form":"to be a good solution provider","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be skilled at offering effective answers or remedies to problems.","example":"Clients rely on us to be a good solution provider for their complex technical needs.","plain":"Clients rely on us to offer effective answers for their complex technical needs.","note":"Highlights practical problem-solving and service orientation.","audio":"audio/d862f4fde54c5be2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-student","text":"to be a good student","kind":"collocation","form":"to be a good student","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be skilled at acquiring knowledge and understanding new concepts.","example":"Even after years in the field, I strive to be a good student of emerging trends.","plain":"Even after years in the field, I strive to acquire knowledge of emerging trends.","note":"Implies a continuous desire for learning and improvement.","audio":"audio/f4e6dca417e0d9a4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-team-builder","text":"to be a good team builder","kind":"collocation","form":"to be a good team builder","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be skilled at creating and developing effective working groups.","example":"I believe it's essential to be a good team builder to achieve complex project goals.","plain":"I believe it's essential to create and develop effective working groups.","note":"Highlights skills in fostering group cohesion and effectiveness.","audio":"audio/c8280891e1d0033c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-time-manager","text":"to be a good time manager","kind":"collocation","form":"to be a good time manager","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be skilled at organizing and prioritizing tasks to use time effectively.","example":"I always strive to be a good time manager, balancing multiple deadlines efficiently.","plain":"I always strive to organize and prioritize tasks to use time effectively.","note":"Emphasizes efficiency and organizational skills.","audio":"audio/9615a5614f080b1c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-writer","text":"to be a good writer","kind":"collocation","form":"to be a good writer","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be skilled at expressing ideas clearly and effectively in written form.","example":"In my role, it's important to be a good writer for reports and proposals.","plain":"In my role, it's important to express ideas clearly in written form.","note":"Highlights written communication skills.","audio":"audio/5c4571489e46438a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-problem-solver","text":"to be a problem solver","kind":"collocation","form":"a problem solver","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Someone who is skilled at finding solutions to difficulties.","example":"I see myself as a problem solver, always looking for efficient ways to overcome obstacles.","plain":"I see myself as someone skilled at finding solutions, always looking for efficient ways to overcome obstacles.","note":"Essential skill in many roles.","audio":"audio/e6d13e40e569e85c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-quick-study","text":"to be a quick study","kind":"chunk","form":"a quick study","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Someone who can learn new things quickly and easily.","example":"I consider myself a quick study, able to adapt to new technologies rapidly.","plain":"I consider myself someone who learns new things quickly, able to adapt to new technologies rapidly.","note":"Positive self-description for an interviewee.","audio":"audio/d14cf1b9a4db0b02.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-self-starter","text":"to be a self-starter","kind":"chunk","form":"a self-starter","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Someone who is motivated and able to work independently without much supervision.","example":"I'm a self-starter who thrives in environments where initiative is encouraged.","plain":"I'm someone who is motivated and works independently, thriving where initiative is encouraged.","note":"Highly valued trait for employees.","audio":"audio/7c6bd35c284e0fe7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-sounding-board","text":"to be a sounding board","kind":"collocation","form":"a sounding board","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Someone who listens to others' ideas or problems and offers feedback.","example":"I often act as a sounding board for my colleagues when they're brainstorming new ideas.","plain":"I often listen to my colleagues' new ideas and offer feedback.","note":"Shows supportive and collaborative qualities.","audio":"audio/b7f278733a952b9f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-sticking-point","text":"to be a sticking point","kind":"chunk","form":"a sticking point","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation","problem-solving"],"meaning":"A point of disagreement or difficulty that prevents progress in a discussion or negotiation.","example":"The budget allocation remains a sticking point in our negotiations with the vendor.","plain":"The budget allocation remains a point of difficulty in our negotiations with the vendor.","note":"Highlights a specific issue that is causing a deadlock or preventing resolution.","audio":"audio/895ed7485d0a75ae.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-strategic-thinker","text":"to be a strategic thinker","kind":"collocation","form":"a strategic thinker","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Someone who can plan and make decisions with a long-term vision and goals in mind.","example":"I consider myself a strategic thinker, always aligning my work with broader company objectives.","plain":"I consider myself someone who plans with a long-term vision, always aligning my work with broader company objectives.","note":"Valued in leadership roles.","audio":"audio/5280a8597523fa64.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-strong-candidate","text":"to be a strong candidate","kind":"collocation","form":"a strong candidate","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be a highly suitable and competitive applicant for a position.","example":"We believe you are a strong candidate for this role, given your impressive background.","plain":"We believe you are a highly suitable applicant for this role, given your impressive background.","note":"Used by interviewers.","audio":"audio/0dbd77b432f1bf90.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-strong-communicator","text":"to be a strong communicator","kind":"collocation","form":"a strong communicator","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Someone who can express ideas clearly and effectively, both verbally and in writing.","example":"I pride myself on being a strong communicator, able to convey complex ideas simply.","plain":"I pride myself on expressing ideas clearly, able to convey complex ideas simply.","note":"Universal professional skill.","audio":"audio/e241822097e4176e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-team-player","text":"to be a team player","kind":"chunk","form":"a team player","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Someone who works well with others and contributes to group success.","example":"I always strive to be a team player, supporting my colleagues to achieve shared goals.","plain":"I always strive to work well with others, supporting my colleagues to achieve shared goals.","note":"Classic interview phrase.","audio":"audio/0c693c549426e914.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-tough-nut-to-crack","text":"to be a tough nut to crack","kind":"chunk","form":"a tough nut to crack","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To be a difficult problem to solve or a challenging person to deal with.","example":"Getting approval for this innovative project is proving to be a tough nut to crack.","plain":"Getting approval for this innovative project is proving to be a difficult problem to solve.","note":"An informal idiom for something very challenging. Can apply to tasks or individuals.","audio":"audio/1b9fa51b4b45905e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-zero-sum-game","text":"to be a zero-sum game","kind":"chunk","form":"to be a zero-sum game","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To be a situation where one person's gain is exactly equivalent to another's loss.","example":"Negotiations don't have to be a zero-sum game; we can both achieve our objectives.","plain":"Negotiations don't have to be a situation where one person's gain is another's loss; we can both achieve our objectives.","note":"Formal. Describes a competitive situation with fixed resources.","audio":"audio/02df9cfa4a203a7f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-absolutely-clear","text":"to be absolutely clear","kind":"chunk","form":"To be absolutely clear","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To emphasize that a statement is unambiguous and leaves no room for misinterpretation.","example":"To be absolutely clear, we will not tolerate any delays on this critical project.","plain":"We will not tolerate any delays on this critical project.","note":"Stronger than \"to be clear,\" used for emphasis and to remove all doubt.","audio":"audio/aa7743b87642b6ec.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-at-loggerheads","text":"to be at loggerheads","kind":"chunk","form":"to be at loggerheads","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To be in strong disagreement or dispute with someone.","example":"We seem to be at loggerheads over the intellectual property rights; we need a mediator.","plain":"We seem to be in strong disagreement over the intellectual property rights; we need a mediator.","note":"Formal. Describes a situation of deep disagreement.","audio":"audio/0e079d3beb8f600b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-candid","text":"to be candid","kind":"chunk","form":"To be candid","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To speak openly and honestly, often about something sensitive.","example":"To be candid, I found the previous work environment lacked opportunities for innovation.","plain":"Honestly, I found the previous work environment lacked opportunities for innovation.","note":"Slightly more formal than \"to be honest.\"","audio":"audio/1b3de0b8e4095052.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-fair-to","text":"to be fair to","kind":"frame","form":"To be fair to","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to acknowledge someone's perspective or circumstances, often to defend or explain their actions.","example":"To be fair to John, he was under immense pressure to deliver the results quickly.","plain":"John was under immense pressure to deliver the results quickly.","note":"A diplomatic way to offer a mitigating explanation for someone's behavior.","audio":"audio/710f1627e1bc6238.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-firm","text":"to be firm","kind":"chunk","form":"to be firm","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To be resolute and unyielding in one's position.","example":"We have to be firm on the quality standards; there's no room for compromise there.","plain":"We have to be resolute on the quality standards; there's no room for compromise there.","note":"Direct and assertive. Used to state a non-negotiable aspect.","audio":"audio/d8ced9bda0a253aa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-flexible","text":"to be flexible","kind":"chunk","form":"to be flexible","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To be willing to change or adapt to new circumstances.","example":"We need to be flexible on the payment schedule to accommodate their cash flow.","plain":"We need to be willing to adapt on the payment schedule to accommodate their cash flow.","note":"Positive. Encourages adaptability in negotiations.","audio":"audio/414aa25885a51726.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-frank-with-you","text":"to be frank with you","kind":"chunk","form":"to be frank with you","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to introduce a direct and honest opinion, often one that might be critical or sensitive.","example":"To be frank with you, I think we need to reconsider our approach to this problem.","plain":"I think we need to reconsider our approach to this problem.","note":"Emphasizes honesty, can sound quite direct, use with caution in sensitive situations.","audio":"audio/bdbf9df8fb730990.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-in-the-loop","text":"to be in the loop","kind":"chunk","form":"in the loop","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be kept informed about developments or decisions.","example":"I always ensure my team members are in the loop regarding project updates.","plain":"I always ensure my team members are informed about project updates.","note":"Important for team communication and transparency.","audio":"audio/36444c460a26856c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-keen-to","text":"to be keen to","kind":"chunk","form":"keen to","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be very eager or enthusiastic to do something.","example":"I'm very keen to learn more about the innovative projects your team is working on.","plain":"I'm very eager to learn more about the innovative projects your team is working on.","note":"More common in British English. Shows enthusiasm.","audio":"audio/5983341d469ec753.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-mindful-of","text":"to be mindful of","kind":"collocation","form":"be mindful of","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation","interviews"],"meaning":"To be aware of and consider something carefully.","example":"We need to be mindful of the budget constraints when planning this project.","plain":"We need to consider the budget constraints carefully when planning this project.","note":"Shows careful consideration and responsibility.","audio":"audio/e629c1ef8f85b0c0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-on-board-with","text":"to be on board with","kind":"chunk","form":"on board with","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To agree with or support a plan, idea, or decision.","example":"I want to ensure everyone is on board with the new strategic direction.","plain":"I want to ensure everyone agrees with the new strategic direction.","note":"Common in team discussions.","audio":"audio/716ff7370857748d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-on-the-fence","text":"to be on the fence","kind":"chunk","form":"on the fence","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be undecided or neutral about something.","example":"I'm still a bit on the fence about the best approach for this particular problem.","plain":"I'm still a bit undecided about the best approach for this particular problem.","note":"Shows careful consideration, not indecisiveness.","audio":"audio/755b93d15a0ee315.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-on-the-lookout-for","text":"to be on the lookout for","kind":"chunk","form":"on the lookout for","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be actively searching or watching for something.","example":"We're always on the lookout for talented individuals to join our growing team.","plain":"We're always actively searching for talented individuals to join our growing team.","note":"Used by interviewers.","audio":"audio/4955441d562cd23a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-on-the-right-track","text":"to be on the right track","kind":"chunk","form":"on the right track","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be proceeding in a way that is likely to lead to success.","example":"I believe our current strategy is definitely on the right track for achieving our goals.","plain":"I believe our current strategy is definitely likely to achieve our goals.","note":"Positive affirmation.","audio":"audio/f5fe59e871807e45.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-on-the-same-page","text":"to be on the same page","kind":"chunk","form":"on the same page","level":"C1","situations":["interviews","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To have a shared understanding or agreement about something.","example":"I want to ensure we're on the same page regarding the project's key objectives.","plain":"I want to ensure we agree on the project's key objectives.","note":"Important for collaboration and clarity.","audio":"audio/0fda9c7f4a2878e1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-on-track","text":"to be on track","kind":"chunk","form":"on track","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be making progress as expected towards a goal.","example":"We are currently on track to meet all our project deadlines.","plain":"We are currently making expected progress to meet all our project deadlines.","note":"Positive and reassuring, often used in project updates.","audio":"audio/9fed79c568ade417.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-open-to","text":"to be open to","kind":"collocation","form":"open to","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be willing to consider or accept new ideas, suggestions, or changes.","example":"I'm always open to feedback and new approaches to problem-solving.","plain":"I'm always willing to accept feedback and new approaches to problem-solving.","note":"Shows flexibility and a growth mindset.","audio":"audio/6198ca72bba42c5f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-out-of-the-loop","text":"to be out of the loop","kind":"chunk","form":"out of the loop","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To not be kept informed about developments or decisions.","example":"I was out of the loop on that decision, so I'll need to catch up.","plain":"I wasn't informed about that decision, so I'll need to catch up.","note":"Often used to explain a lack of knowledge. Can be negative.","audio":"audio/97da60a81a2f646f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-perfectly-candid","text":"To be perfectly candid","kind":"chunk","form":"To be perfectly candid","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation","storytelling","presenting"],"meaning":"To speak very openly and sincerely, without holding back.","example":"To be perfectly candid, we faced significant internal resistance.","plain":"Honestly, we faced significant internal resistance.","note":"A more formal and strong way to express complete honesty.","audio":"audio/cdd69a91d21e52fd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-perfectly-candid-i-feel","text":"To be perfectly candid, I feel","kind":"frame","form":"To be perfectly candid, I feel","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To introduce a very open and sincere personal feeling.","example":"To be perfectly candid, I feel quite concerned about the upcoming changes.","plain":"Honestly, I feel quite concerned about the upcoming changes.","note":"Used to express deep personal feelings openly and directly.","audio":"audio/0165ee6168a3bc04.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-perfectly-clear-about-this","text":"To be perfectly clear about this","kind":"chunk","form":"To be perfectly clear about this","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To ensure there is absolutely no misunderstanding regarding a specific point.","example":"To be perfectly clear about this, the new policy takes effect next Monday.","plain":"The new policy takes effect next Monday.","note":"A strong way to emphasize clarity on a particular issue.","audio":"audio/7f93df68e67445c7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-perfectly-clear-this-means","text":"To be perfectly clear, this means","kind":"frame","form":"To be perfectly clear, this means","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To explicitly state the implication or consequence of a previous statement.","example":"To be perfectly clear, this means we will have to adjust our production schedule.","plain":"This means we will have to adjust our production schedule.","note":"Used to spell out the exact consequences of a decision or situation.","audio":"audio/68293c7015d74917.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-perfectly-frank","text":"to be perfectly frank","kind":"chunk","form":"To be perfectly frank","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","negotiation","small-talk","storytelling","feedback","persuading","explaining"],"meaning":"To introduce a very honest and direct statement, often one that might be sensitive or critical.","example":"To be perfectly frank, I have serious reservations about the feasibility of this timeline.","plain":"I have serious reservations about the feasibility of this timeline.","note":"Similar to \"to be perfectly honest,\" signaling directness and sincerity.","audio":"audio/29b2952702fabc8b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-perfectly-frank-with-you","text":"To be perfectly frank with you","kind":"chunk","form":"To be perfectly frank with you","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To speak openly and directly to the listener, even if it's a difficult truth.","example":"To be perfectly frank with you, the results were quite disappointing.","plain":"Frankly, the results were quite disappointing.","note":"Similar to 'to be perfectly honest with you', often used for difficult news.","audio":"audio/b3c7f6f2c7c2980f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-perfectly-frank-i-believe","text":"To be perfectly frank, I believe","kind":"frame","form":"To be perfectly frank, I believe","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To introduce a direct and sincere personal belief.","example":"To be perfectly frank, I believe this project has been mismanaged from the start.","plain":"Frankly, I believe this project has been mismanaged from the start.","note":"A strong way to state a personal conviction, often a difficult one.","audio":"audio/2617bcfcffc2fe2e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-perfectly-honest-i-think","text":"To be perfectly honest, I think","kind":"frame","form":"To be perfectly honest, I think","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To introduce a frank and sincere personal opinion.","example":"To be perfectly honest, I think we need to re-evaluate our entire strategy.","plain":"Honestly, I think we need to re-evaluate our entire strategy.","note":"A common softener for expressing a potentially critical personal view.","audio":"audio/8d0b91bf12dc8d5a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-perfectly-transparent","text":"To be perfectly transparent","kind":"chunk","form":"To be perfectly transparent","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To be completely open and clear, revealing all relevant information.","example":"To be perfectly transparent, all project costs are available for review.","plain":"All project costs are available for review.","note":"Emphasizes openness and full disclosure; common in business.","audio":"audio/759f878b934e2955.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-perfectly-transparent-the-reason-is","text":"To be perfectly transparent, the reason is","kind":"frame","form":"To be perfectly transparent, the reason is","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To openly explain the cause or justification for something.","example":"To be perfectly transparent, the reason is a sudden increase in raw material costs.","plain":"The reason is a sudden increase in raw material costs.","note":"Used to provide a clear and open explanation for a situation.","audio":"audio/e5425f611e06e7b2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-perfectly-upfront","text":"To be perfectly upfront","kind":"chunk","form":"To be perfectly upfront","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To be completely direct and transparent about a situation.","example":"To be perfectly upfront, we are currently experiencing some financial challenges.","plain":"Honestly, we are currently experiencing some financial challenges.","note":"Used to introduce a direct and open statement, often about difficulties.","audio":"audio/b95346a4914f8668.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-perfectly-upfront-we-need","text":"To be perfectly upfront, we need","kind":"frame","form":"To be perfectly upfront, we need","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To state a direct and transparent need or requirement.","example":"To be perfectly upfront, we need additional resources to meet this deadline.","plain":"Honestly, we need additional resources to meet this deadline.","note":"Used to state a requirement directly and without hesitation.","audio":"audio/402f21e64b204aef.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-transparent-about","text":"to be transparent about","kind":"collocation","form":"be transparent about","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be open and honest, not hiding anything.","example":"We aim to be transparent about our company's challenges as well as our successes.","plain":"We aim to be open about our company's challenges as well as our successes.","note":"Shows integrity and builds trust.","audio":"audio/a905ec76dea9ff98.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-up-to-speed-on","text":"to be up to speed on","kind":"collocation","form":"up to speed on","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To have all the latest information about something.","example":"I'm confident I can get up to speed on the project quickly.","plain":"I'm confident I can learn all the latest information about the project quickly.","note":"Shows readiness to learn and adapt.","audio":"audio/05557ace8f153f41.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-upfront","text":"to be upfront","kind":"chunk","form":"to be upfront","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To be direct and honest about one's intentions or limitations.","example":"I want to be upfront about our budget constraints before we discuss further options.","plain":"I want to be direct about our budget constraints before we discuss further options.","note":"Encourages transparency. Used to state facts clearly from the start.","audio":"audio/0941658416b190fb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-upfront-about","text":"to be upfront about","kind":"collocation","form":"be upfront about","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation","interviews"],"meaning":"To be honest and direct about something, even if it's difficult.","example":"I like to be upfront about potential challenges early in a project's lifecycle.","plain":"I like to be honest about potential challenges early in a project's lifecycle.","note":"Shows transparency and integrity. Positive trait in a professional.","audio":"audio/1f8ea955922a9daf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-break-it-down-further","text":"To break it down further","kind":"chunk","form":"to break it down further","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To divide a complex topic into even smaller, more manageable parts for explanation.","example":"We've discussed the overall strategy. Now, to break it down further, let's look at each department's role.","plain":"We've discussed the overall strategy. Now, to divide it into smaller parts, let's look at each department's role.","note":"Implies a systematic approach to explaining complexity.","audio":"audio/98da8cd6be6b17ff.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-break-the-deadlock","text":"to break the deadlock","kind":"collocation","form":"to break the deadlock","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To find a way to resolve a situation where no progress can be made.","example":"We need a new approach to break the deadlock on the pricing structure.","plain":"We need a new approach to resolve the situation where no progress can be made on the pricing structure.","note":"Used when seeking a solution to an impasse.","audio":"audio/8c6d0934f2c913c9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-bridge-the-gap","text":"to bridge the gap","kind":"collocation","form":"bridge the gap","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation","interviews","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To connect two different things or overcome a difference.","example":"My role was to bridge the gap between technical teams and business stakeholders.","plain":"My role was to connect technical teams and business stakeholders.","note":"Shows ability to facilitate communication or understanding.","audio":"audio/6764b5ca6f95fd72.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-bring-a-fresh-perspective","text":"to bring a fresh perspective","kind":"collocation","form":"to bring a fresh perspective","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To introduce new ideas or a different way of looking at things.","example":"I hope to bring a fresh perspective to your team's current challenges.","plain":"I hope to introduce new ideas to your team's current challenges.","note":"Positive phrase, often used by new hires or candidates.","audio":"audio/5fcfe726fc0333c4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-bring-clarity-to","text":"to bring clarity to","kind":"collocation","form":"to bring clarity to","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To make something easier to understand or less confusing.","example":"My role often involves analyzing complex data to bring clarity to strategic decisions.","plain":"My role often involves analyzing complex data to make strategic decisions easier to understand.","note":"Professional, emphasizes analytical and communication skills.","audio":"audio/188a404a4d8b73e1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-bring-to-light","text":"to bring to light","kind":"collocation","form":"bring to light","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To reveal or uncover something previously unknown or hidden.","example":"My analysis helped bring to light several inefficiencies in the old process.","plain":"My analysis helped reveal several inefficiencies in the old process.","note":"Positive, shows problem-solving skills.","audio":"audio/7e08352b201cb392.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-bring-to-the-table","text":"to bring to the table","kind":"chunk","form":"bring to the table","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To contribute specific skills, experience, or qualities to a discussion or project.","example":"I believe my analytical skills are what I can bring to the table for this position.","plain":"I believe my analytical skills are what I can contribute to this position.","note":"Common in interviews when discussing contributions. Positive and direct.","audio":"audio/4464c8be16e69221.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-bring-us-back-to","text":"To bring us back to","kind":"frame","form":"To bring us back to","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Redirects the discussion to a previously mentioned main point or topic.","example":"To bring us back to the initial question, how do these findings impact urban planning?","plain":"Let's return to the initial question: how do these findings impact urban planning?","note":"Useful for refocusing a discussion that has strayed.","audio":"audio/9554020d6ca55c06.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-buy-time","text":"to buy time","kind":"collocation","form":"to buy time","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To delay something in order to gain more time to prepare or decide.","example":"We need to buy time to properly evaluate their revised offer before responding.","plain":"We need to delay our response to properly evaluate their revised offer.","note":"Strategic. Used when more time is needed for consideration.","audio":"audio/333adb006ef445f2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-circle-back","text":"to circle back","kind":"chunk","form":"To circle back","level":"C1","situations":["feedback","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To return to a previous topic or discussion point.","example":"To circle back to your earlier point about the timeline, I've made some adjustments.","plain":"To return to your earlier point about the timeline, I've made some adjustments.","note":"Professional and common, used to revisit a topic.","audio":"audio/cdd649a0c2d77773.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-circle-back-to","text":"to circle back to","kind":"chunk","form":"To circle back to","level":"C1","situations":["interviews","presenting","calls"],"meaning":"To return to a previous topic or point that was discussed earlier in the conversation.","example":"To circle back to our earlier discussion, have you had a chance to review the report?","plain":"Returning to our earlier discussion, have you reviewed the report yet?","note":"A useful discourse marker for revisiting a topic, ensuring all points are covered.","audio":"audio/d7f65c58809eb9e9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-clarify-any-confusion","text":"to clarify any confusion","kind":"collocation","form":"to clarify any confusion","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain something in a way that resolves misunderstandings or lack of clarity.","example":"I'd like to take a moment to clarify any confusion regarding the new project roles.","plain":"I'd like to take a moment to resolve any misunderstandings regarding the new project roles.","note":"Used when you anticipate or have observed misunderstandings.","audio":"audio/10b902f040651c43.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-clarify-further","text":"to clarify further","kind":"collocation","form":"to clarify further","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To provide additional explanation or details to make something even clearer.","example":"If you have more questions, I'd be happy to clarify further after the presentation.","plain":"If you have more questions, I'd be happy to explain more after the presentation.","note":"Used to offer more detailed explanations if needed.","audio":"audio/7bd9bd474ef30a5a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-clarify-my-position","text":"to clarify my position","kind":"chunk","form":"to clarify my position","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain one's stance or opinion more clearly.","example":"Just to clarify my position, I support the proposal but have reservations about the timeline.","plain":"I support the proposal but have reservations about the timeline, and I want to make that clear.","note":"Used when there might be ambiguity about one's viewpoint.","audio":"audio/eecffc3101909a24.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-clarify-the-details","text":"to clarify the details","kind":"collocation","form":"to clarify the details","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain the specific points or fine print of a plan or agreement.","example":"We've agreed on the main points, but now we need to clarify the details of the contract.","plain":"We've agreed on the main points, but now we need to explain the specific points of the contract.","note":"Used when moving from general agreement to specific implementation.","audio":"audio/096bd269538c0010.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-clarify-the-process","text":"to clarify the process","kind":"collocation","form":"to clarify the process","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain the steps or procedures involved in a particular task or system.","example":"Many people are unsure, so I'd like to clarify the process for submitting expense reports.","plain":"Many people are unsure, so I'd like to explain the steps for submitting expense reports.","note":"Specific to explaining procedures or sequences of actions.","audio":"audio/353b16259a235d5d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-clarify-the-scope","text":"to clarify the scope","kind":"collocation","form":"to clarify the scope","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain the boundaries, extent, or range of a project, task, or discussion.","example":"Before we start, let's take a moment to clarify the scope of this new initiative.","plain":"Before we start, let's explain the boundaries of this new initiative.","note":"Specific to defining the limits or extent of something.","audio":"audio/17f961969615a3bc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-clarify-the-situation","text":"to clarify the situation","kind":"collocation","form":"to clarify the situation","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain the circumstances or facts of a situation to make it easier to understand.","example":"There's been some confusion, so let me take a moment to clarify the situation.","plain":"There's been some confusion, so let me explain the circumstances.","note":"Used when a situation is unclear or has been misunderstood.","audio":"audio/d5317a6e3f1a7052.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-clarify-things","text":"to clarify things","kind":"collocation","form":"to clarify things","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To make a situation or explanation easier to understand by providing more information.","example":"There seems to be some confusion, so let me try to clarify things for everyone.","plain":"There seems to be some confusion, so let me try to make the situation clearer for everyone.","note":"A general phrase for making a situation or explanation less ambiguous.","audio":"audio/3960e0e8bfb01dfc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-clear-the-air","text":"to clear the air","kind":"chunk","form":"to clear the air","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To resolve misunderstandings or disagreements, often through open discussion, to improve a situation.","example":"Let's have a quick meeting to clear the air about the project roles and responsibilities.","plain":"Let's have a quick meeting to resolve any misunderstandings about the project roles and responsibilities.","note":"Focuses on interpersonal dynamics and communication. Implies a desire for harmony.","audio":"audio/64424e71ea82d76f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-come-to-a-consensus","text":"to come to a consensus","kind":"chunk","form":"to come to a consensus","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To reach a general agreement among all members of a group.","example":"Our goal is to come to a consensus on the project scope by the end of this meeting.","plain":"Our goal is to reach a general agreement on the project scope by the end of this meeting.","note":"Formal and collaborative. Used when seeking broad agreement.","audio":"audio/03e59049303eca6a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-conclude-our-discussion","text":"To conclude our discussion","kind":"chunk","form":"To conclude our discussion","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Introducing the final remarks or summary of a conversation.","example":"To conclude our discussion, I'll send out the meeting minutes by tomorrow.","plain":"To end our discussion, I'll send out the meeting minutes by tomorrow.","note":"A formal way to transition to the end of a conversation.","audio":"audio/3d3b3dc17b7a2bb8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-conclude-then","text":"To conclude, then","kind":"chunk","form":"To conclude, then","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Signals the end of a discussion or presentation and introduces the final summary or statement.","example":"To conclude, then, the evidence strongly supports the need for immediate policy intervention.","plain":"In conclusion, the evidence strongly supports the need for immediate policy intervention.","note":"A formal way to signal the end of a section or presentation.","audio":"audio/61d0b2026211abbb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-consider-all-options","text":"to consider all options","kind":"collocation","form":"to consider all options","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To evaluate every available choice or alternative.","example":"We need to consider all options before making a final decision on the project scope.","plain":"We need to evaluate every available choice before making a final decision on the project scope.","note":"Thorough and strategic. Used when a comprehensive review is needed.","audio":"audio/e8ae7c7cc6df2da9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-cultivate-relationships","text":"to cultivate relationships","kind":"collocation","form":"to cultivate relationships","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To develop and maintain good connections with people.","example":"I believe it's crucial to cultivate relationships with clients for long-term success.","plain":"I believe it's crucial to develop good connections with clients for long-term success.","note":"Emphasizes ongoing effort in building professional networks.","audio":"audio/6fa428413978bd78.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-cut-to-the-chase","text":"to cut to the chase","kind":"chunk","form":"to cut to the chase","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to indicate that you are going to get straight to the main point without unnecessary details.","example":"To cut to the chase, we need to secure additional funding to complete the project on time.","plain":"We need to secure additional funding to complete the project on time.","note":"Informal, used when you want to get to the point quickly, often in a slightly impatient tone.","audio":"audio/9b2e28e29134a09e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-delve-a-bit-deeper","text":"To delve a bit deeper","kind":"chunk","form":"to delve a bit deeper","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explore a topic in more detail or with greater analytical depth.","example":"We've discussed the surface issues. Now, to delve a bit deeper, let's consider the root causes.","plain":"We've discussed the surface issues. Now, to explore in more detail, let's consider the root causes.","note":"Suggests a more thorough and analytical examination of a subject.","audio":"audio/e9346904055587c4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-delve-deeper-into","text":"To delve deeper into","kind":"chunk","form":"To delve deeper into","level":"C1","situations":["academic","interviews","explaining"],"meaning":"Indicates an intention to explore a topic in more detail and with greater analytical depth.","example":"To delve deeper into the data, we employed a qualitative content analysis approach.","plain":"To explore the data in more detail, we employed a qualitative content analysis approach.","note":"Suggests a thorough and in-depth examination.","audio":"audio/b0a02d66b3732c3e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-demystify-the-process","text":"to demystify the process","kind":"chunk","form":"to demystify the process","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To make a complicated or confusing process easier to understand.","example":"Let me take a moment to demystify the process of applying for the new grant.","plain":"Let me take a moment to make the process of applying for the new grant easier to understand.","note":"Implies the process is currently seen as mysterious or difficult.","audio":"audio/5fc5e84fb073f1a4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-differentiate-between","text":"To differentiate between","kind":"collocation","form":"to differentiate between","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To explain or identify the distinct characteristics that separate two or more things.","example":"It's crucial to differentiate between correlation and causation in statistical analysis.","plain":"It's crucial to distinguish between correlation and causation in statistical analysis.","note":"Essential for precise academic discussion and avoiding confusion.","audio":"audio/1db16f013e41ed6f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-digress-for-a-moment","text":"To digress for a moment","kind":"chunk","form":"To digress for a moment","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To temporarily move away from the main topic to discuss something related.","example":"To digress for a moment, I'd like to mention a similar challenge we faced last year.","plain":"I'd like to briefly mention a similar challenge we faced last year.","note":"A polite way to signal a temporary shift in topic; professional.","audio":"audio/c9b459762eefdd69.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-draw-a-line-in-the-sand","text":"to draw a line in the sand","kind":"chunk","form":"to draw a line in the sand","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To set a limit beyond which one will not go, or a point at which one will stop.","example":"We need to draw a line in the sand regarding the budget; we cannot exceed this amount.","plain":"We need to set a limit regarding the budget; we cannot exceed this amount.","note":"Emphatic. Used to establish a clear, firm boundary.","audio":"audio/ada012663e3b3dbb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-draw-a-parallel","text":"To draw a parallel","kind":"collocation","form":"To draw a parallel","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To point out a similarity or connection between two different things or situations.","example":"Our current market challenge is similar to last year's. To draw a parallel, we need to adapt quickly again.","plain":"Our current market challenge is similar to last year's. To show the similarity, we need to adapt quickly again.","note":"Used to highlight similarities for better understanding or to make a point.","audio":"audio/736b42876792480e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-drive-a-hard-bargain","text":"to drive a hard bargain","kind":"collocation","form":"to drive a hard bargain","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To negotiate very assertively and refuse to make concessions easily.","example":"They are known to drive a hard bargain, so we need to be well-prepared for this negotiation.","plain":"They are known to negotiate very assertively, so we need to be well-prepared for this negotiation.","note":"Describes a negotiating style. Can be admiring or slightly critical.","audio":"audio/7029fd63d5f67d89.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-drive-results","text":"to drive results","kind":"collocation","form":"drive results","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be effective in achieving specific outcomes or goals.","example":"My focus has always been to drive results and exceed expectations in every role.","plain":"My focus has always been to achieve specific outcomes and exceed expectations in every role.","note":"Highly valued in performance-oriented environments.","audio":"audio/5dc753aa960e16ad.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-elaborate-a-little","text":"To elaborate a little","kind":"chunk","form":"To elaborate a little","level":"C1","situations":["explaining","calls"],"meaning":"Introducing a more detailed explanation of a point.","example":"To elaborate a little, our team has identified several potential risks.","plain":"To give more details, our team has identified several potential risks.","note":"Used when you want to add more information to what you've just said.","audio":"audio/08b1146cac506f0b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-elaborate-on","text":"to elaborate on","kind":"collocation","form":"elaborate on","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To provide more details or information about something previously mentioned.","example":"Could you elaborate on the challenges you faced in that particular project?","plain":"Could you give more details about the challenges you faced in that particular project?","note":"A polite and professional way to ask for more information.","audio":"audio/4351238ab9e1344c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-ensure-clarity","text":"To ensure clarity","kind":"frame","form":"To ensure clarity","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of asking a question or rephrasing something.","example":"To ensure clarity, could you confirm the deadline for the report?","plain":"Could you confirm the deadline for the report so it's clear?","note":"Used to justify a request for clarification.","audio":"audio/ce21769625c1bf17.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-ensure-seamless-transition","text":"to ensure seamless transition","kind":"collocation","form":"to ensure seamless transition","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To make certain that a change or move happens smoothly without problems.","example":"My priority was to ensure seamless transition for new employees joining the department.","plain":"My priority was to make certain that new employees joined the department smoothly.","note":"Professional, often used in project management or HR contexts.","audio":"audio/f333041f133c6cb1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-expand-on-that","text":"To expand on that","kind":"chunk","form":"To expand on that","level":"C1","situations":["presenting","explaining"],"meaning":"To provide more information or detail about a specific point previously mentioned.","example":"I briefly touched on the budget. To expand on that, we have allocated funds for training.","plain":"I briefly touched on the budget. To give more information about that, we have allocated funds for training.","note":"Signals that the speaker will now go into more depth on a particular topic.","audio":"audio/34cc1d7dc2949c28.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-expand-on-this","text":"To expand on this","kind":"chunk","form":"To expand on this","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To elaborate further on a particular idea or statement.","example":"I'd like to expand on this point about market saturation and its implications.","plain":"I'd like to elaborate on this point about market saturation and its implications.","note":"Used to develop an idea more fully; sounds analytical.","audio":"audio/16e182c9467d5127.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-explore-in-depth","text":"To explore in depth","kind":"chunk","form":"To explore in depth","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To examine a topic very thoroughly and comprehensively.","example":"We will now take some time to explore in depth the potential risks involved.","plain":"We will now take some time to examine the potential risks very thoroughly.","note":"Suggests a detailed and comprehensive analysis; sounds academic or professional.","audio":"audio/5e71597dc5543de1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-factor-that-in","text":"to factor that in","kind":"chunk","form":"to factor that in","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To include a particular fact or element when making a calculation or decision.","example":"We need to factor that in when calculating the total project cost.","plain":"We need to include that when calculating the total project cost.","note":"Used to ensure all relevant information is considered.","audio":"audio/a8299b9be071c5cf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-follow-up-on","text":"to follow up on","kind":"collocation","form":"to follow up on","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To take further action regarding something previously discussed or initiated.","example":"I'd like to follow up on the points we discussed regarding team integration.","plain":"I'd like to take further action regarding the points we discussed about team integration.","note":"Common in professional settings for continuing a conversation or task.","audio":"audio/4e77015efdbac2fc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-follow-up-on-that","text":"To follow up on that","kind":"chunk","form":"To follow up on that","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To continue discussing a previous point or to add more information to it.","example":"You mentioned the market trends. To follow up on that, we've seen a shift in consumer preferences.","plain":"You mentioned market trends; we've seen a shift in consumer preferences.","note":"Used to build on a previous statement or question; sounds collaborative.","audio":"audio/c47f54c01e6bc9a2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-foster-a-culture-of","text":"to foster a culture of","kind":"collocation","form":"foster a culture of","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To encourage and develop a particular set of values, beliefs, or behaviors within a group.","example":"I always strive to foster a culture of open communication within my teams.","plain":"I always strive to encourage open communication within my teams.","note":"Positive and professional. Often used by managers or team leads.","audio":"audio/b863dd08b64a5f07.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-foster-a-sense-of","text":"to foster a sense of","kind":"collocation","form":"to foster a sense of","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To help develop a particular feeling or atmosphere.","example":"My leadership style aims to foster a sense of shared purpose among team members.","plain":"My leadership style aims to help develop a feeling of shared purpose.","note":"Used by leaders to describe their impact on team morale.","audio":"audio/3a2fbd51b7045475.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-foster-an-environment","text":"to foster an environment","kind":"collocation","form":"to foster an environment","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To help create and encourage a particular atmosphere or setting.","example":"We strive to foster an environment where creativity and open communication thrive.","plain":"We strive to help create an atmosphere where creativity and open communication thrive.","note":"Often used by management to describe workplace culture.","audio":"audio/75bb0312aef423dc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-foster-collaboration","text":"to foster collaboration","kind":"collocation","form":"foster collaboration","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To encourage people to work together effectively.","example":"I always strive to foster collaboration among team members on complex projects.","plain":"I always strive to encourage team members to work together effectively on complex projects.","note":"Positive leadership trait.","audio":"audio/b20772a83f5d9c6d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-foster-growth","text":"to foster growth","kind":"collocation","form":"to foster growth","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To encourage and help the development or progress of something.","example":"We aim to foster growth within our team members through mentorship programs.","plain":"We aim to encourage the development of our team members through mentorship programs.","note":"Positive and forward-looking, often used by management.","audio":"audio/71971a0ab26c8a81.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-frame-the-discussion","text":"To frame the discussion","kind":"chunk","form":"To frame the discussion","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To set the context, boundaries, or perspective for a conversation or debate.","example":"To frame the discussion, let's consider the historical evolution of environmental policy.","plain":"To set the context for the discussion, let's consider the historical evolution of environmental policy.","note":"Used to establish the parameters or lens through which a topic will be viewed.","audio":"audio/48d1ff30f865f9aa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-gain-a-deeper-understanding","text":"to gain a deeper understanding","kind":"collocation","form":"to gain a deeper understanding","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To acquire a more thorough and profound comprehension of something.","example":"I'm hoping to gain a deeper understanding of your company's market position.","plain":"I'm hoping to acquire a more thorough understanding of your company's market position.","note":"Shows intellectual curiosity and desire for comprehensive knowledge.","audio":"audio/90139a1721f587dc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-gain-exposure-to","text":"to gain exposure to","kind":"collocation","form":"to gain exposure to","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To get experience with or knowledge of something new.","example":"I'm looking to gain exposure to international markets in my next role.","plain":"I'm looking to get experience with international markets in my next role.","note":"Common for career development, especially when seeking new areas of expertise.","audio":"audio/7cb25a158756dac8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-gain-insight-into","text":"to gain insight into","kind":"collocation","form":"gain insight into","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To acquire a deep understanding of something.","example":"I'm hoping to gain insight into the company's long-term strategic vision.","plain":"I'm hoping to deeply understand the company's long-term strategic vision.","note":"Shows intellectual curiosity and strategic thinking.","audio":"audio/ea5ed2ceab85707d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-gain-traction","text":"to gain traction","kind":"collocation","form":"to gain traction","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To start to become popular, accepted, or successful.","example":"Our new marketing strategy is starting to gain traction with target customers.","plain":"Our new marketing strategy is starting to become successful with target customers.","note":"Business-oriented, implies progress and increasing acceptance.","audio":"audio/4c41b2b4d3add615.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-gain-valuable-experience","text":"to gain valuable experience","kind":"collocation","form":"to gain valuable experience","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To acquire useful and important knowledge or skills through practice.","example":"In my last role, I was able to gain valuable experience in international project management.","plain":"In my last role, I was able to acquire useful knowledge in international project management.","note":"Common for candidates describing their career progression.","audio":"audio/e4684f70c32c2534.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-a-better-handle-on","text":"to get a better handle on","kind":"collocation","form":"get a better handle on","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To understand or manage a situation or problem more effectively.","example":"I'm hoping to get a better handle on the team's current priorities during this discussion.","plain":"I'm hoping to understand the team's current priorities more effectively during this discussion.","note":"Shows a desire for deeper understanding. Informal but professional.","audio":"audio/db7b55a67030d797.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-a-better-sense","text":"to get a better sense","kind":"frame","form":"to get a better sense","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To try to understand something more clearly or thoroughly.","example":"I'd like to get a better sense of the project's overall objectives before we proceed.","plain":"I'd like to understand the project's overall objectives more clearly before we proceed.","note":"Professional and inquisitive, used when seeking deeper understanding.","audio":"audio/6aa63adec52e6465.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-a-clearer-picture","text":"to get a clearer picture","kind":"collocation","form":"get a clearer picture","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To gain a more complete and accurate understanding of a situation.","example":"Could you help me get a clearer picture of the team's current challenges?","plain":"Could you help me understand the team's current challenges more completely?","note":"Shows a desire for detailed understanding.","audio":"audio/afceacf34fad833f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-a-feel-for","text":"to get a feel for","kind":"frame","form":"to get a feel for","level":"C1","situations":["interviews","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To develop an intuitive understanding or impression of something.","example":"I'm hoping to get a feel for the company culture during my visit.","plain":"I'm hoping to understand the company culture intuitively during my visit.","note":"Similar to \"get a sense for,\" often used for atmosphere or culture.","audio":"audio/d9226b1b554c9bd5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-a-grasp-of","text":"to get a grasp of","kind":"collocation","form":"to get a grasp of","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To understand something, especially a complex idea or situation.","example":"I'm trying to get a grasp of the company's long-term strategic goals.","plain":"I'm trying to understand the company's long-term strategic goals.","note":"Implies a process of understanding, not instant comprehension.","audio":"audio/ba300e4be9cb3bd8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-a-handle-on","text":"to get a handle on","kind":"collocation","form":"to get a handle on","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling","interviews","explaining","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To begin to understand or deal with a complex or difficult situation.","example":"It's a lot of information, but I'm starting to get a handle on the new project requirements.","plain":"It's a lot of information, but I'm starting to understand the new project requirements.","note":"Informal. Implies gaining control or understanding of something challenging.","audio":"audio/3a05383024ae8e32.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-a-sense-for","text":"to get a sense for","kind":"frame","form":"to get a sense for","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To develop an intuitive understanding or feeling about something.","example":"I'm trying to get a sense for the company's long-term growth potential.","plain":"I'm trying to understand the company's long-term growth potential intuitively.","note":"Similar to \"get a sense of,\" but often implies a more subjective understanding.","audio":"audio/28fc5a9c0d911c88.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-a-sense-of","text":"to get a sense of","kind":"frame","form":"to get a sense of","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation","interviews","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To gain a general understanding or impression of something.","example":"I'm trying to get a sense of the team's dynamic and working style.","plain":"I'm trying to understand the team's dynamic and working style.","note":"Common when an interviewee is trying to understand company culture.","audio":"audio/aae261451a86972d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-a-sense-of-how","text":"to get a sense of how","kind":"frame","form":"to get a sense of how","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To gain an understanding of the manner or way in which something happens.","example":"I'm trying to get a sense of how decisions are typically made within the team.","plain":"I'm trying to understand how decisions are typically made within the team.","note":"Useful for understanding processes and dynamics.","audio":"audio/158d85abd81602b9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-a-sense-of-what","text":"to get a sense of what","kind":"frame","form":"to get a sense of what","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To gain an understanding of the nature or identity of something.","example":"I'm trying to get a sense of what the biggest challenges are for the team right now.","plain":"I'm trying to understand what the biggest challenges are for the team right now.","note":"Useful for probing into specific issues.","audio":"audio/08a067ae1b8b7530.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-back-to-my-point","text":"To get back to my point","kind":"chunk","form":"To get back to my point","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To return to the main topic after a digression.","example":"I apologize for the tangent, but to get back to my point, the deadline is non-negotiable.","plain":"I apologize for the tangent, but the deadline is non-negotiable.","note":"Used to refocus the discussion after a brief deviation.","audio":"audio/ebac350258ddf3d2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-buy-in","text":"to get buy-in","kind":"collocation","form":"to get buy-in","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To obtain agreement and support from others for a plan or decision.","example":"We need to get buy-in from all stakeholders before implementing these changes.","plain":"We need to obtain agreement and support from all stakeholders before implementing these changes.","note":"Common in business. Used when seeking broad acceptance.","audio":"audio/295252e25cfd72c3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-straight-to-the-point","text":"To get straight to the point","kind":"chunk","form":"to get straight to the point","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To begin discussing the main topic immediately, without preamble.","example":"I don't want to waste your time, so to get straight to the point, we need more funding.","plain":"I don't want to waste your time, so directly, we need more funding.","note":"Indicates efficiency and a desire to be direct.","audio":"audio/d013b0ea6df5c72e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-the-ball-rolling","text":"to get the ball rolling","kind":"chunk","form":"get the ball rolling","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation","interviews","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To start a process or activity.","example":"I'm ready to get the ball rolling on this project as soon as possible.","plain":"I'm ready to start this project as soon as possible.","note":"Enthusiastic and proactive.","audio":"audio/bcff5be4f26e55eb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-the-gist-of-it","text":"to get the gist of it","kind":"collocation","form":"to get the gist of it","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To understand the main point or general meaning of something, even if not all the details.","example":"I haven't read the full report, but I think I've managed to get the gist of it.","plain":"I haven't read the full report, but I think I've managed to understand the main point.","note":"Informal. Used when you have a general understanding without all the specifics.","audio":"audio/1ef38f9cea45f8df.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-things-back-on-track","text":"to get things back on track","kind":"chunk","form":"to get things back on track","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To return to a normal or desired course of action or progress after a deviation or problem.","example":"We need a clear action plan to get things back on track after the unexpected delay.","plain":"We need a clear action plan to return to our normal progress after the unexpected delay.","note":"Commonly used when a project or process has veered off course and needs correction.","audio":"audio/b70693ce621fdb67.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-to-the-bottom-of","text":"to get to the bottom of","kind":"chunk","form":"to get to the bottom of","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To investigate thoroughly in order to discover the true cause or explanation of something.","example":"We need to get to the bottom of why these errors are occurring before we can fix them permanently.","plain":"We need to find the true cause of why these errors are occurring before we can fix them permanently.","note":"Implies a determined effort to uncover hidden facts or root causes. Common in problem-solving.","audio":"audio/e7e8077f87edcf43.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-to-the-crux-of-it","text":"To get to the crux of it","kind":"chunk","form":"to get to the crux of it","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To identify and explain the most critical or central point of an issue or argument.","example":"There are many minor issues, but to get to the crux of it, our main problem is communication.","plain":"There are many minor issues, but to get to the most critical point, our main problem is communication.","note":"Used to cut through less important details and focus on the core problem or idea.","audio":"audio/013b8babe2243f06.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-to-the-heart-of","text":"to get to the heart of","kind":"chunk","form":"to get to the heart of","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation","explaining"],"meaning":"To identify the most important or central part of an issue or topic.","example":"Let's try to get to the heart of why the project is experiencing these delays.","plain":"Let's try to identify the most important reason why the project is experiencing these delays.","note":"Used when trying to understand the core problem or truth.","audio":"audio/e931019af26f23ed.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-a-little","text":"to give a little","kind":"chunk","form":"to give a little","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To make a small concession or compromise.","example":"Both sides need to give a little to reach a mutually beneficial deal.","plain":"Both sides need to make a small concession to reach a mutually beneficial deal.","note":"Informal and collaborative. Encourages flexibility from both parties.","audio":"audio/b4aaab4006717c24.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-credit-where-its-due","text":"to give credit where it's due","kind":"chunk","form":"give credit where it's due","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To acknowledge someone's contribution or achievement.","example":"To give credit where it's due, the success of that project was a team effort.","plain":"The success of that project was a team effort, and everyone deserves recognition.","note":"Shows humility and appreciation for others.","audio":"audio/60cdfb37347368d9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-me-pause","text":"to give me pause","kind":"collocation","form":"give me pause","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To make one hesitate or think carefully before acting or speaking.","example":"That question does give me pause; I want to ensure I provide a comprehensive answer.","plain":"That question does make me hesitate; I want to ensure I provide a comprehensive answer.","note":"Buys time to think. Shows thoughtfulness.","audio":"audio/aeed8973f58aa1bf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-some-thought-to","text":"to give some thought to","kind":"collocation","form":"give some thought to","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation","interviews"],"meaning":"To consider something carefully or reflect on it.","example":"That's an excellent question; I'll need to give some thought to the best way to answer it.","plain":"That's an excellent question; I'll need to consider the best way to answer it.","note":"Buys time to think. Sounds thoughtful and engaged.","audio":"audio/827e12c039e2d866.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-the-green-light","text":"to give the green light","kind":"chunk","form":"to give the green light","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To give permission to go ahead with a project or plan.","example":"We're waiting for management to give the green light on the budget increase.","plain":"We're waiting for management to give permission on the budget increase.","note":"Informal. Used when granting approval to start.","audio":"audio/71d67559cdebbfbb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-you-a-better-understanding","text":"to give you a better understanding","kind":"frame","form":"to give you a better understanding","level":"C1","situations":["presenting","explaining"],"meaning":"To provide information or explanation that improves someone's comprehension of a topic.","example":"I'll share some case studies to give you a better understanding of our client's needs.","plain":"I'll share some case studies to help you understand our client's needs more effectively.","note":"A common phrase for offering insights that deepen comprehension.","audio":"audio/5957e2dff7fde755.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-you-a-clearer-idea","text":"to give you a clearer idea","kind":"frame","form":"to give you a clearer idea","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To provide additional information or explanation to improve understanding of a concept.","example":"Let me show you the prototype to give you a clearer idea of the final product.","plain":"Let me show you the prototype to help you understand the final product better.","note":"Useful for enhancing understanding, often with a visual or tangible aid.","audio":"audio/8aa50962c35225d2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-you-a-clearer-perspective","text":"to give you a clearer perspective","kind":"frame","form":"to give you a clearer perspective","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To provide information that helps someone understand a situation from a broader or different viewpoint.","example":"Let's look at the global market trends to give you a clearer perspective on our local challenges.","plain":"Let's look at the global market trends to help you understand our local challenges better.","note":"Useful for broadening understanding by adding context or a different viewpoint.","audio":"audio/ef1eb6959d0950dc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-you-a-concrete-example","text":"to give you a concrete example","kind":"frame","form":"to give you a concrete example","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To provide a clear, tangible instance that illustrates a general concept or rule.","example":"The theory sounds abstract, so let me try to give you a concrete example from our daily work.","plain":"The theory sounds abstract, so let me try to provide a clear example from our daily work.","note":"Emphasizes that the example will be practical and easy to grasp.","audio":"audio/4424ffd560b4cdad.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-you-a-framework","text":"to give you a framework","kind":"chunk","form":"to give you a framework","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To provide a basic structure or set of ideas for understanding a complex topic.","example":"To give you a framework, we'll discuss the project in terms of scope, resources, and timeline.","plain":"We'll discuss the project in terms of scope, resources, and timeline, to give you a basic structure.","note":"Often used in academic or professional settings to organize information.","audio":"audio/c5b5eef581da449b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-you-a-rough-idea","text":"to give you a rough idea","kind":"frame","form":"to give you a rough idea","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To provide an approximate understanding or estimate of something, not exact details.","example":"I can't give you exact numbers, but to give you a rough idea, we're looking at a 10% increase.","plain":"I can't give you exact numbers, but we're looking at approximately a 10% increase.","note":"Useful for providing estimates or general impressions when exact figures aren't available.","audio":"audio/bf0e2fc0fa23b574.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-you-a-sense","text":"to give you a sense","kind":"chunk","form":"to give you a sense","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To provide a general understanding or impression of something, rather than precise details.","example":"I can't share exact figures, but to give you a sense, our growth has been exponential.","plain":"I can't share exact figures, but our growth has been exponential.","note":"Useful for conveying an overall impression without revealing specific data.","audio":"audio/140f992344ab9b52.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-you-a-sense-of","text":"To give you a sense of","kind":"frame","form":"To give you a sense of","level":"C1","situations":["interviews","presenting","explaining"],"meaning":"To provide an impression or general understanding of something, rather than precise details.","example":"The project is massive. To give you a sense of its scale, it involves five different countries.","plain":"The project is massive. To give you an impression of its scale, it involves five different countries.","note":"Useful for conveying scope, atmosphere, or general characteristics.","audio":"audio/d779d754ca0abac4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-you-a-sense-of-proportion","text":"to give you a sense of proportion","kind":"chunk","form":"to give you a sense of proportion","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To help someone understand the relative importance or size of something.","example":"To give you a sense of proportion, this budget cut represents only 2% of our total operating costs.","plain":"This budget cut represents only 2% of our total operating costs, to help you understand its relative size.","note":"Used to prevent overreaction or to provide a balanced view.","audio":"audio/5f6070f093fa180e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-you-a-sense-of-scale","text":"to give you a sense of scale","kind":"frame","form":"to give you a sense of scale","level":"C1","situations":["presenting","explaining"],"meaning":"To provide information that helps understand the size, extent, or importance of something.","example":"The project involves millions of data points; to give you a sense of scale, it's unprecedented.","plain":"The project involves millions of data points; it's unprecedented in its size.","note":"Useful for conveying the magnitude of something.","audio":"audio/aac635fc930d7be6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-you-an-analogy","text":"To give you an analogy","kind":"collocation","form":"To give you an analogy","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To provide a comparison to something familiar to help explain a new or complex idea.","example":"Think of the network as a highway system. To give you an analogy, data are the cars moving along it.","plain":"Think of the network as a highway system. To give you a comparison, data are the cars moving along it.","note":"A very effective technique for explaining abstract concepts.","audio":"audio/513a35376a166a1a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-you-some","text":"to give you some","kind":"frame","form":"To give you some","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Introduces information or context, often before giving feedback.","example":"To give you some background, the client has expressed concerns about the budget.","plain":"The client has expressed concerns about the budget.","note":"Useful for providing context before delivering potentially sensitive information.","audio":"audio/ac735288e9996340.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-you-some-background","text":"to give you some background","kind":"frame","form":"to give you some background","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling","explaining"],"meaning":"To provide preliminary information or context necessary for understanding a situation.","example":"Before I discuss the proposal, I need to give you some background on our previous attempts.","plain":"Before I discuss the proposal, I need to tell you about our previous attempts.","note":"Used to set the stage before diving into the main topic.","audio":"audio/9617d71a6f9dc53c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-you-the-background","text":"to give you the background","kind":"frame","form":"to give you the background","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To provide the historical or contextual information needed to understand a current situation.","example":"Before we discuss the new proposal, I need to give you the background on why we're considering it.","plain":"Before we discuss the new proposal, I need to tell you why we're considering it.","note":"Used to provide context before presenting new information.","audio":"audio/4d7cd159a6c844c7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-you-the-big-picture","text":"to give you the big picture","kind":"chunk","form":"to give you the big picture","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To provide a broad, overall understanding of a situation or topic, without focusing on details.","example":"Before we get into the specifics, I want to give you the big picture of our strategic goals.","plain":"Before we get into the specifics, I want to give you a broad understanding of our strategic goals.","note":"Used to provide context and overall direction.","audio":"audio/a3e4422c2594b2ff.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-you-the-essence","text":"To give you the essence","kind":"collocation","form":"to give you the essence","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To provide the most fundamental and important qualities or characteristics of something.","example":"The document is long, but to give you the essence, it's about improving customer satisfaction.","plain":"The document is long, but to give you the most important part, it's about improving customer satisfaction.","note":"Focuses on the core meaning or spirit of something.","audio":"audio/5ecb4d7c0fbdc537.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-you-the-full-picture","text":"to give you the full picture","kind":"chunk","form":"to give you the full picture","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling","explaining"],"meaning":"To provide all the relevant information about a situation, not just a part of it.","example":"I need to give you the full picture of the project's challenges before you make a decision.","plain":"I need to give you all the relevant information about the project's challenges before you make a decision.","note":"Implies that previous information might have been incomplete.","audio":"audio/dd9e796e5de190a5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-you-the-gist","text":"to give you the gist","kind":"frame","form":"to give you the gist","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To provide the main point or general meaning of something without going into full detail.","example":"I won't go into all the details, but to give you the gist, the meeting went well.","plain":"I won't go into all the details, but the main point is, the meeting went well.","note":"Useful for offering a quick summary when time is limited or full detail isn't needed.","audio":"audio/d8e5f3d40f4b0d8a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-you-the-key-points","text":"to give you the key points","kind":"frame","form":"to give you the key points","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To provide the most important ideas or pieces of information from a discussion or document.","example":"I'll quickly go over the report to give you the key points before the meeting starts.","plain":"I'll quickly go over the report to tell you the most important ideas before the meeting starts.","note":"Used for summarizing the most crucial information.","audio":"audio/dc35c8107e2728c8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-help-bridge-the-gap","text":"to help bridge the gap","kind":"chunk","form":"to help bridge the gap","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To connect two different ideas or understandings, making them compatible.","example":"This training module is designed to help bridge the gap between theory and practical application.","plain":"This training module is designed to connect theory and practical application.","note":"Useful when explaining how different concepts relate or how to apply knowledge.","audio":"audio/a2bbca10691a8c6a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-help-clarify","text":"to help clarify","kind":"collocation","form":"to help clarify","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To assist in making something easier to understand or less confusing.","example":"I've prepared some visuals to help clarify the complex data analysis.","plain":"I've prepared some visuals to make the complex data analysis easier to understand.","note":"A polite way to introduce an aid or additional explanation.","audio":"audio/b990fa3321480391.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-help-clarify-things","text":"To help clarify things","kind":"chunk","form":"to help clarify things","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To assist in making a situation or explanation easier to understand.","example":"I've drawn a diagram to help clarify things regarding the new workflow.","plain":"I've drawn a diagram to make the new workflow easier to understand.","note":"A polite and helpful way to introduce an aid to understanding.","audio":"audio/66f006ab5ebce89b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-help-you-conceptualize","text":"to help you conceptualize","kind":"frame","form":"to help you conceptualize","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To assist in forming a mental idea or understanding of something abstract.","example":"To help you conceptualize the scale of the problem, consider the global impact of plastic waste.","plain":"Consider the global impact of plastic waste to help you form a mental idea of the problem's scale.","note":"Used for abstract or large-scale ideas.","audio":"audio/e7f974dbf2b3c933.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-help-you-connect-the-dots","text":"to help you connect the dots","kind":"chunk","form":"to help you connect the dots","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To assist someone in seeing the relationships between different pieces of information.","example":"I'll provide some examples to help you connect the dots between these seemingly unrelated events.","plain":"I'll provide some examples to help you see the relationships between these seemingly unrelated events.","note":"Implies that the listener needs to understand how various elements fit together.","audio":"audio/dda9972ad5bd6723.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-help-you-follow","text":"to help you follow","kind":"frame","form":"to help you follow","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"Indicates an effort to make the explanation easy to understand and keep up with.","example":"I'll use a diagram to help you follow the flow of data through the system.","plain":"I'll use a diagram to make it easier for you to understand the flow of data through the system.","note":"Polite and considerate, often used when explaining a sequence or process.","audio":"audio/1ba753b3b32838a7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-help-you-grasp-the-concept","text":"to help you grasp the concept","kind":"frame","form":"to help you grasp the concept","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"Offers assistance in understanding a difficult or abstract idea.","example":"To help you grasp the concept, let's look at a practical application in our daily operations.","plain":"Let's look at a practical application in our daily operations to understand the concept.","note":"Polite and supportive, used when the topic is challenging.","audio":"audio/b9061cd9c6b468d3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-help-you-grasp-the-magnitude","text":"to help you grasp the magnitude","kind":"frame","form":"to help you grasp the magnitude","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To assist in understanding the large size or extent of something.","example":"To help you grasp the magnitude of the task, we're talking about migrating millions of data records.","plain":"We're talking about migrating millions of data records, to help you understand the large size of the task.","note":"Used for emphasizing the scale or importance of something.","audio":"audio/07b1a19336bf077b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-help-you-grasp-the-significance","text":"to help you grasp the significance","kind":"frame","form":"to help you grasp the significance","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To assist in understanding the importance or meaning of something.","example":"To help you grasp the significance of this discovery, it could revolutionize medical treatment.","plain":"This discovery could revolutionize medical treatment, to help you understand its importance.","note":"Used when emphasizing the importance or impact of a topic.","audio":"audio/ec18959b680da9c5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-help-you-see","text":"to help you see","kind":"frame","form":"to help you see","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"Guides the listener towards a particular understanding or perspective.","example":"To help you see the potential, imagine this technology integrated into every household device.","plain":"Imagine this technology integrated into every household device, so you can understand its potential.","note":"Encourages a specific viewpoint or realization.","audio":"audio/b4a83499bc3cfb51.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-help-you-see-the-bigger-picture","text":"to help you see the bigger picture","kind":"frame","form":"to help you see the bigger picture","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To assist in understanding the overall context or broader implications of a situation.","example":"Let's step back for a moment to help you see the bigger picture of our long-term strategy.","plain":"Let's step back for a moment to help you understand the overall context of our long-term strategy.","note":"Encourages a broader perspective beyond immediate details.","audio":"audio/ef41fe7088a9e8cb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-help-you-see-the-connection","text":"to help you see the connection","kind":"frame","form":"to help you see the connection","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To assist someone in understanding how two or more things are related.","example":"Let me provide an example to help you see the connection between these two seemingly separate issues.","plain":"Let me provide an example to help you understand how these two seemingly separate issues are related.","note":"Useful for explaining relationships or causality.","audio":"audio/1be55ba739a8c809.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-help-you-understand","text":"to help you understand","kind":"frame","form":"to help you understand","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To offer assistance in comprehending a complex topic or situation.","example":"I'll draw a diagram to help you understand the intricate network architecture.","plain":"I'll draw a diagram so you can understand the intricate network architecture.","note":"A direct and helpful way to introduce an explanation or aid.","audio":"audio/21ffc46301d2b3bf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-help-you-understand-the-implications","text":"to help you understand the implications","kind":"frame","form":"to help you understand the implications","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To assist in comprehending the possible future effects or consequences of something.","example":"We need to discuss this further to help you understand the implications of delaying the project.","plain":"We need to discuss this further to help you comprehend the possible future effects of delaying the project.","note":"Focuses on consequences and future impact.","audio":"audio/c60ec022229d8558.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-help-you-visualize","text":"to help you visualize","kind":"frame","form":"to help you visualize","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"Encourages the listener to form a mental image to understand an idea better.","example":"To help you visualize the new office layout, here's a 3D rendering.","plain":"Here's a 3D rendering to help you imagine the new office layout.","note":"Often used with visual aids or descriptive language.","audio":"audio/3c92f4ad3d8d7631.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-help-you-visualize-it","text":"to help you visualize it","kind":"frame","form":"to help you visualize it","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"Encourages the listener to form a mental image of an abstract concept.","example":"To help you visualize it, imagine a network of interconnected sensors across the city.","plain":"Imagine a network of interconnected sensors across the city, to help you form a mental image.","note":"Similar to \"to help you visualize,\" but more direct with \"it.\"","audio":"audio/7241b905227afd22.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-help-you-visualize-the-data","text":"to help you visualize the data","kind":"frame","form":"to help you visualize the data","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To assist in forming a mental image or understanding of numerical information.","example":"We've created a dashboard to help you visualize the data trends more effectively.","plain":"We've created a dashboard to help you understand the data trends more effectively.","note":"Specific to data or statistics, often implies visual aids.","audio":"audio/071ccd19c13a9f9f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-highlight-a-point","text":"To highlight a point","kind":"collocation","form":"To highlight a point","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To draw attention to a specific piece of information or idea.","example":"To highlight a point, our research shows a clear correlation between these factors.","plain":"To draw attention to a point, our research shows a clear correlation between these factors.","note":"A common way to introduce an important observation.","audio":"audio/97af80f7f6e1d7b2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-highlight-the-key-points","text":"To highlight the key points","kind":"collocation","form":"to highlight the key points","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To emphasize the most important pieces of information or arguments.","example":"I've prepared a summary to highlight the key points from our discussion.","plain":"I've prepared a summary to emphasize the most important points from our discussion.","note":"Common in presentations or summaries to draw attention to critical information.","audio":"audio/aa1baacaf205c578.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-hold-all-the-cards","text":"to hold all the cards","kind":"chunk","form":"to hold all the cards","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To be in a very strong position, having all the advantages.","example":"They seem to hold all the cards in this negotiation, which makes it tough for us.","plain":"They seem to have all the advantages in this negotiation, which makes it tough for us.","note":"Informal. Describes a dominant negotiating position.","audio":"audio/a7719ec5ffe1d2de.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-hold-firm","text":"to hold firm","kind":"collocation","form":"to hold firm","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To maintain one's position or demands without yielding during a negotiation.","example":"We need to hold firm on the delivery date; any delay would impact our launch schedule.","plain":"We need to maintain our position on the delivery date; any delay would impact our launch schedule.","note":"Indicates resolve. Used when refusing to concede on a specific point.","audio":"audio/13b9ae19476cfdbb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-hold-out-for","text":"to hold out for","kind":"collocation","form":"to hold out for","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To insist on getting a particular thing, especially after a long negotiation.","example":"We're going to hold out for a better price, as we know they need this deal.","plain":"We're going to insist on getting a better price, as we know they need this deal.","note":"Shows determination. Used when refusing to settle for less than desired.","audio":"audio/f23b1703e1c1cc72.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-illustrate-the-point","text":"to illustrate the point","kind":"collocation","form":"to illustrate the point","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To provide an example or story that helps to make an idea or statement clearer.","example":"Let me tell you about a client success story to illustrate the point about our new strategy.","plain":"Let me tell you about a client success story to make our new strategy clearer.","note":"Often precedes an example or anecdote.","audio":"audio/e66a40e01f0bc5fd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-iron-out-the-details","text":"to iron out the details","kind":"collocation","form":"iron out the details","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation","interviews","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To resolve small problems or disagreements in a plan or agreement.","example":"We still need to iron out the details of the project timeline next week.","plain":"We still need to resolve the small problems of the project timeline next week.","note":"Common in project management.","audio":"audio/bd73e9f4d5296d9b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-iron-out-the-kinks","text":"to iron out the kinks","kind":"chunk","form":"to iron out the kinks","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To resolve minor problems or difficulties in a plan or system to make it run smoothly.","example":"Before launching, we need to iron out the kinks in the new workflow to ensure efficiency.","plain":"Before launching, we need to resolve the minor problems in the new workflow to ensure efficiency.","note":"Suggests a process of refinement and troubleshooting. Often used for new systems or plans.","audio":"audio/01e16f2efdd33364.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-kick-things-off","text":"To kick things off","kind":"chunk","form":"To kick things off","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","negotiation","presenting"],"meaning":"To begin the presentation or discussion.","example":"To kick things off, I'd like to share some recent market insights.","plain":"To start, I want to share some recent market insights.","note":"A common, informal way to start a presentation or meeting.","audio":"audio/f12471bcb570d244.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-lose-ground","text":"to lose ground","kind":"collocation","form":"to lose ground","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To lose an advantage or position previously held.","example":"We can't afford to lose ground on our market share by delaying this launch.","plain":"We can't afford to lose our advantage on our market share by delaying this launch.","note":"Negative. Used when one's position weakens.","audio":"audio/9599747a3291b54b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-a-case-for","text":"to make a case for","kind":"collocation","form":"to make a case for","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To argue in favor of something or explain why it should be done.","example":"I'd like to make a case for extending the deadline, given the unforeseen challenges.","plain":"I'd like to argue for extending the deadline, given the unforeseen challenges.","note":"Used when presenting a persuasive argument.","audio":"audio/f26d58f90788fb99.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-a-long-story-short","text":"To make a long story short","kind":"chunk","form":"to make a long story short","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","storytelling","explaining"],"meaning":"To summarize a lengthy explanation or narrative concisely.","example":"I had a lot of trouble with the flight, but to make a long story short, I arrived safely.","plain":"I had a lot of trouble with the flight, but in summary, I arrived safely.","note":"Used to get to the main point quickly after a potentially long preamble.","audio":"audio/52c9a06998857ddb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-a-strong-case","text":"to make a strong case","kind":"collocation","form":"to make a strong case","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To present a convincing argument in favor of something.","example":"You'll need to make a strong case for that budget increase to get it approved.","plain":"You'll need to present a convincing argument for that budget increase to get it approved.","note":"Used when a persuasive argument is required.","audio":"audio/853eddd437c54780.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-an-offer","text":"to make an offer","kind":"collocation","form":"to make an offer","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To propose a price or terms for something.","example":"We'd like to make an offer for the property, subject to a final inspection.","plain":"We'd like to propose a price for the property, subject to a final inspection.","note":"Standard phrase for proposing terms, often financial.","audio":"audio/d4bfdbcd9c16139f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-it-clear","text":"to make it clear","kind":"collocation","form":"to make it clear","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To ensure that a statement or explanation is easily understood and unambiguous.","example":"I want to make it clear that this is a recommendation, not a mandatory directive.","plain":"I want to ensure that this is a recommendation, not a mandatory directive.","note":"Used to remove any potential misunderstanding or ambiguity.","audio":"audio/1adbe4480757c804.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-it-crystal-clear","text":"to make it crystal clear","kind":"collocation","form":"to make it crystal clear","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain something with extreme clarity, leaving absolutely no room for doubt or misunderstanding.","example":"I want to make it crystal clear that this project is our top priority for the quarter.","plain":"I want to ensure there is no doubt that this project is our top priority for the quarter.","note":"Stronger than \"to make it clear,\" emphasizing absolute clarity.","audio":"audio/125321f822232ecc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-it-digestible","text":"to make it digestible","kind":"collocation","form":"to make it digestible","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To present complex information in a simplified, easy-to-process format.","example":"The report is very dense, so I've created an executive summary to make it digestible.","plain":"The report is very dense, so I've created an executive summary to make it easy to process.","note":"Implies breaking down complex information into manageable parts.","audio":"audio/91c988f304912d19.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-it-easier-to-follow","text":"to make it easier to follow","kind":"frame","form":"to make it easier to follow","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain something in a way that is easy to understand and keep up with.","example":"I've prepared a handout to make it easier to follow the complex financial projections.","plain":"I've prepared a handout so the complex financial projections are easier to understand.","note":"Focuses on the clarity and progression of the explanation.","audio":"audio/711eb7bdb9a62a1d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-it-easier-to-grasp","text":"to make it easier to grasp","kind":"frame","form":"to make it easier to grasp","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain something in a way that makes it less difficult to understand.","example":"We'll use visual aids to make it easier to grasp the complex data analysis techniques.","plain":"We'll use visual aids so the complex data analysis techniques are easier to understand.","note":"Focuses on simplifying complex information.","audio":"audio/dc36093a8447fe85.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-it-easier-to-understand","text":"to make it easier to understand","kind":"collocation","form":"to make it easier to understand","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain something in a simpler way so that the listener can grasp it more readily.","example":"I'll use a visual aid to make it easier to understand the complex data analysis.","plain":"I'll use a visual aid to help you understand the complex data analysis more easily.","note":"A general phrase indicating an effort to simplify an explanation.","audio":"audio/e3378755a36521a5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-it-more-accessible","text":"To make it more accessible","kind":"chunk","form":"to make it more accessible","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To present information in a way that is easier for a wider audience to understand or use.","example":"We need to rewrite the user manual to make it more accessible for new employees.","plain":"We need to rewrite the user manual to make it easier for new employees to understand.","note":"Focuses on the ease of understanding and use for the target audience.","audio":"audio/813c53b5d016c4df.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-it-more-concrete","text":"to make it more concrete","kind":"frame","form":"to make it more concrete","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain an abstract idea using specific examples or details.","example":"To make it more concrete, imagine a scenario where all our data is instantly accessible.","plain":"Imagine a scenario where all our data is instantly accessible, to make the idea less abstract.","note":"Useful for moving from theoretical to practical understanding.","audio":"audio/4930a37d35b59334.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-it-more-relatable","text":"to make it more relatable","kind":"frame","form":"to make it more relatable","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain something in a way that connects to the listener's own experiences or understanding.","example":"I'll use an everyday example to make it more relatable for everyone in the room.","plain":"I'll use an everyday example so everyone in the room can connect it to their own experiences.","note":"Aims to make complex ideas personally relevant.","audio":"audio/7f2b6c64a98161a3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-it-more-tangible","text":"to make it more tangible","kind":"frame","form":"to make it more tangible","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain an abstract concept by relating it to something that can be touched or felt.","example":"To make it more tangible, think of our customer loyalty program as a points system for frequent flyers.","plain":"Think of our customer loyalty program as a points system for frequent flyers, to make it less abstract.","note":"Similar to \"make it more concrete,\" but emphasizes physical or real-world connection.","audio":"audio/c21cdae8c0972c9d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-it-plain","text":"to make it plain","kind":"collocation","form":"to make it plain","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain something in a very straightforward and unambiguous manner.","example":"I want to make it plain that this is a non-negotiable requirement for all team members.","plain":"I want to state clearly that this is a non-negotiable requirement for all team members.","note":"Emphasizes directness and lack of ambiguity, similar to \"to put it plainly.\"","audio":"audio/99745ccb1c830cef.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-it-understandable","text":"to make it understandable","kind":"collocation","form":"to make it understandable","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain something in a way that is easy for the listener to comprehend.","example":"I'll use a simple analogy to make it understandable for those unfamiliar with the concept.","plain":"I'll use a simple analogy so those unfamiliar with the concept can understand it.","note":"A general phrase for ensuring clarity and accessibility of an explanation.","audio":"audio/0705246106dc7826.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-sense-of","text":"to make sense of","kind":"collocation","form":"to make sense of","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling","explaining"],"meaning":"To understand something that is confusing or difficult to comprehend.","example":"It took me a while to make sense of the new tax regulations, but I finally got it.","plain":"It took me a while to understand the new tax regulations, but I finally got it.","note":"Refers to the act of understanding something complex.","audio":"audio/f5ca4851d80516c6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-sure-were-on-the-same-page","text":"to make sure we're on the same page","kind":"collocation","form":"to make sure we're on the same page","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To confirm that everyone involved has the same understanding of a situation or plan.","example":"Before we proceed, I want to make sure we're on the same page regarding the project scope.","plain":"Before we proceed, I want to confirm that we all understand the project scope in the same way.","note":"Essential for ensuring alignment and avoiding misunderstandings in collaborative work.","audio":"audio/9339d8687ea4d517.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-things-clearer","text":"to make things clearer","kind":"collocation","form":"to make things clearer","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain a situation or concept in a way that is easier to understand.","example":"Let me use an analogy to make things clearer about how this complex system operates.","plain":"Let me use an analogy to explain how this complex system operates more easily.","note":"A general phrase for improving the clarity of an explanation.","audio":"audio/cd44d82b431b06de.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-mitigate-risks","text":"to mitigate risks","kind":"collocation","form":"mitigate risks","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To reduce the severity or likelihood of potential problems or dangers.","example":"A key part of my role was to identify and mitigate risks associated with new product launches.","plain":"A key part of my role was to identify and reduce the severity of potential problems with new product launches.","note":"Important for project management and strategic planning.","audio":"audio/f55b6fd2d5f70bca.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-my-utter-astonishment","text":"To my utter astonishment","kind":"chunk","form":"To my utter astonishment","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Expresses extreme surprise or amazement.","example":"To my utter astonishment, she remembered my name after all these years.","plain":"I was extremely astonished when she remembered my name after all these years.","note":"A more emphatic version of 'to my surprise', conveying stronger emotion.","audio":"audio/57ef65991ba4bc43.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-narrow-the-differences","text":"to narrow the differences","kind":"collocation","form":"to narrow the differences","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To reduce the disagreements or discrepancies between parties.","example":"Let's try to narrow the differences on these minor points to move forward.","plain":"Let's try to reduce the disagreements on these minor points to move forward.","note":"Collaborative. Used to focus on resolving specific points of contention.","audio":"audio/8aad786b969ac392.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-offer-some-perspective","text":"To offer some perspective","kind":"collocation","form":"to offer some perspective","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To present a particular viewpoint or way of looking at a situation.","example":"It's easy to feel overwhelmed, but to offer some perspective, many companies face similar challenges.","plain":"It's easy to feel overwhelmed, but to give you a different viewpoint, many companies face similar challenges.","note":"Helps to broaden understanding or alleviate concerns by showing a wider view.","audio":"audio/8d65fc5232828128.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-paint-a-picture-of","text":"to paint a picture of","kind":"frame","form":"Could you paint a picture of","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To describe a situation or scenario vividly.","example":"Could you paint a picture of a typical day in this role for me?","plain":"Could you describe a typical day in this role for me?","note":"Invites a detailed and descriptive answer.","audio":"audio/35d0262959cc6d60.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-phrase-it-differently","text":"To phrase it differently","kind":"chunk","form":"To phrase it differently","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To express the same idea using different words for clarity.","example":"The data suggests a trend. To phrase it differently, consumer habits are evolving rapidly.","plain":"The data suggests a trend; consumer habits are evolving rapidly.","note":"Useful for rephrasing a complex idea to ensure audience understanding.","audio":"audio/59d9933d60430175.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-pick-up-on","text":"To pick up on","kind":"chunk","form":"To pick up on","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To refer back to something previously mentioned and continue discussing it.","example":"To pick up on what Sarah said earlier, collaboration is indeed key to our success.","plain":"As Sarah said earlier, collaboration is indeed key to our success.","note":"A natural way to refer to a previous speaker's point; sounds engaged.","audio":"audio/0bad0d6fca60790c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-pick-up-on-that","text":"to pick up on that","kind":"chunk","form":"To pick up on that","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","calls"],"meaning":"To continue discussing or elaborate on a point that someone else has just made.","example":"To pick up on that point about the budget, we need to consider the long-term costs.","plain":"Regarding your point about the budget, we must consider long-term costs.","note":"A useful discourse marker to show you are connecting your statement to a previous one.","audio":"audio/72acfaafe58b6f2e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-proceed-with","text":"to proceed with","kind":"collocation","form":"to proceed with","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To continue with a plan or action.","example":"Are we ready to proceed with the revised terms, or do we need further discussion?","plain":"Are we ready to continue with the revised terms, or do we need further discussion?","note":"Formal. Used to ask if it's time to move forward.","audio":"audio/d7fd9d47b2c4091f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-provide-some-clarity","text":"to provide some clarity","kind":"collocation","form":"to provide some clarity","level":"C1","situations":["presenting","explaining"],"meaning":"To make something clearer or easier to understand.","example":"I'd like to provide some clarity on the recent changes to our remote work policy.","plain":"I'd like to make the recent changes to our remote work policy clearer.","note":"Professional and helpful, often used when addressing confusion.","audio":"audio/0ce9f45ad6b0b754.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-provide-some-context","text":"To provide some context","kind":"collocation","form":"to provide some context","level":"C1","situations":["presenting","explaining"],"meaning":"To offer background information that helps in understanding a situation or statement.","example":"Before I explain the new strategy, I need to provide some context about our recent performance.","plain":"Before I explain the new strategy, I need to give you some background information about our recent performance.","note":"Often used at the beginning of an explanation to set the scene.","audio":"audio/cb1f0d18aa075b6f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-provide-some-input","text":"to provide some input","kind":"chunk","form":"to provide some input","level":"C1","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To give one's thoughts, ideas, or feedback on a topic.","example":"I'd like to take a moment to provide some input on the proposed marketing strategy.","plain":"I'd like to take a moment to give my thoughts on the proposed marketing strategy.","note":"Professional and collaborative, invites participation in a discussion.","audio":"audio/742c4141f7b91852.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-a-cap-on","text":"to put a cap on","kind":"collocation","form":"to put a cap on","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To set an upper limit on an amount or quantity.","example":"We need to put a cap on the total expenses for this phase of the project.","plain":"We need to set an upper limit on the total expenses for this phase of the project.","note":"Common in financial negotiations. Used to limit costs.","audio":"audio/5aa6e082eb760c8e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-a-hold-on","text":"to put a hold on","kind":"collocation","form":"to put a hold on","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To temporarily stop or postpone something.","example":"We need to put a hold on further discussions until we have the updated figures.","plain":"We need to temporarily stop further discussions until we have the updated figures.","note":"Used to pause a process or discussion.","audio":"audio/ae37dccd47a8cd8c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-a-proposal-forward","text":"to put a proposal forward","kind":"collocation","form":"to put a proposal forward","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To formally suggest a plan or idea for consideration.","example":"I'd like to put a proposal forward for adjusting the payment schedule.","plain":"I'd like to formally suggest a plan for adjusting the payment schedule.","note":"Formal and proactive. Used when presenting an idea for discussion.","audio":"audio/f3d751faf2865431.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-forward-an-offer","text":"to put forward an offer","kind":"collocation","form":"to put forward an offer","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To formally present a proposal or price.","example":"We'd like to put forward an offer for your consideration regarding the new contract.","plain":"We'd like to formally present a proposal for your consideration regarding the new contract.","note":"Formal. Used when making a proposal.","audio":"audio/1eb88fad2cb57ff6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-it-concisely","text":"to put it concisely","kind":"chunk","form":"To put it concisely","level":"C1","situations":["presenting","explaining"],"meaning":"To express something briefly and clearly, using few words.","example":"To put it concisely, our new strategy focuses on digital transformation and customer experience.","plain":"Our new strategy focuses on digital transformation and customer experience.","note":"A slightly more formal way to indicate brevity and clarity.","audio":"audio/2b9b32507a2ce7e2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-it-concisely-the-goal-is","text":"To put it concisely, the goal is","kind":"frame","form":"To put it concisely, the goal is","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To state the objective briefly and directly.","example":"To put it concisely, the goal is to achieve sustainable growth.","plain":"The goal is to achieve sustainable growth.","note":"Used for a brief and clear statement of purpose.","audio":"audio/aa4716c29620ea7b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-it-differently","text":"to put it differently","kind":"chunk","form":"To put it differently","level":"C1","situations":["academic","explaining"],"meaning":"To rephrase a statement using alternative words, often to make it clearer or offer a new perspective.","example":"The project is underfunded. To put it differently, we don't have enough resources to finish it.","plain":"The project is underfunded; we don't have enough resources to finish it.","note":"A common phrase for offering an alternative explanation or perspective.","audio":"audio/f912502f1f989e01.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-it-in-a-nutshell","text":"to put it in a nutshell","kind":"chunk","form":"To put it in a nutshell","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To summarize something very briefly and concisely, getting straight to the main point.","example":"To put it in a nutshell, the new policy aims to improve work-life balance for everyone.","plain":"The new policy aims to improve work-life balance for everyone.","note":"Informal and effective for quick summaries, similar to \"in a nutshell.\"","audio":"audio/52b98c535e16456a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-it-in-context","text":"To put it in context","kind":"chunk","form":"To put it in context","level":"C1","situations":["academic","presenting","explaining"],"meaning":"Introduces background information necessary for understanding a particular point or situation.","example":"To put it in context, this region has historically faced significant socio-economic disparities.","plain":"To understand this, remember this region has historically faced significant socio-economic disparities.","note":"Used to provide relevant background information.","audio":"audio/2479d02a53f0ef79.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-it-in-context-this-happened","text":"To put it in context, this happened","kind":"frame","form":"To put it in context, this happened","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To provide background information to help understand a specific event.","example":"To put it in context, this happened during a period of significant market volatility.","plain":"This happened during a period of significant market volatility.","note":"Helps explain the circumstances surrounding a particular event.","audio":"audio/454d0762d512cefe.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-it-in-laymans-terms","text":"to put it in layman's terms","kind":"chunk","form":"To put it in layman's terms","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain a technical or complex subject using simple language that anyone can understand.","example":"The algorithm uses machine learning. To put it in layman's terms, it learns from data.","plain":"The algorithm uses machine learning; it learns from data.","note":"Used when simplifying technical jargon for a non-expert audience.","audio":"audio/ddbd01c7ec3b94a7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-it-in-perspective","text":"To put it in perspective","kind":"chunk","form":"To put it in perspective","level":"C1","situations":["academic","explaining"],"meaning":"Provides additional information to help understand the true importance or context of something.","example":"To put it in perspective, this represents a 10% increase over last year's figures.","plain":"To help understand its true importance, this is a 10% increase over last year.","note":"Used to clarify the scale or significance of a statement.","audio":"audio/c6f0261c57452fb9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-it-in-simple-terms","text":"to put it in simple terms","kind":"chunk","form":"To put it in simple terms","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain something using easy-to-understand language, avoiding complexity.","example":"To put it in simple terms, the new software automates the entire reporting process.","plain":"The new software automates the entire reporting process.","note":"Similar to \"in simple terms,\" but emphasizes the act of simplifying.","audio":"audio/021c323931c5fb82.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-it-in-simpler-terms","text":"To put it in simpler terms","kind":"chunk","form":"To put it in simpler terms","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To rephrase an explanation using language that is easier for a general audience to understand.","example":"The process involves stochastic modeling. To put it in simpler terms, we're using probability to predict outcomes.","plain":"The process involves stochastic modeling. To explain it using easier words, we're using probability to predict outcomes.","note":"Often used when explaining technical concepts to a non-technical audience.","audio":"audio/8069f9289f36526f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-it-more-simply","text":"To put it more simply","kind":"chunk","form":"To put it more simply","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain something again using simpler language or fewer complex ideas.","example":"The algorithm optimizes data flow. To put it more simply, it makes our network faster.","plain":"The algorithm optimizes data flow. To explain it in simpler language, it makes our network faster.","note":"Used when the initial explanation might have been too technical or complex.","audio":"audio/f7b1e3153d2317cc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-it-plainly","text":"to put it plainly","kind":"chunk","form":"To put it plainly","level":"C1","situations":["presenting","explaining"],"meaning":"To state something directly and clearly, without using complicated language or euphemisms.","example":"To put it plainly, we don't have the budget for this ambitious new initiative.","plain":"We don't have the budget for this ambitious new initiative.","note":"Used for direct, unambiguous communication, often when delivering unwelcome news.","audio":"audio/2052ceb050175a13.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-it-plainly-we-failed","text":"To put it plainly, we failed","kind":"frame","form":"To put it plainly, we failed","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To state a negative outcome clearly and without euphemism.","example":"To put it plainly, we failed to meet our sales targets this quarter.","plain":"We failed to meet our sales targets this quarter.","note":"Used for direct, unambiguous communication of a negative result.","audio":"audio/66177dbcf97e66c5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-it-simply-and-clearly","text":"to put it simply and clearly","kind":"chunk","form":"To put it simply and clearly","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain something in a straightforward, unambiguous, and easy-to-understand manner.","example":"To put it simply and clearly, we need to increase our sales by 15% this quarter.","plain":"We need to increase our sales by 15% this quarter.","note":"Combines simplicity with an emphasis on clarity.","audio":"audio/b0a4909d77d4e7ca.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-it-simply-its-about","text":"To put it simply, it's about","kind":"frame","form":"To put it simply, it's about","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To explain the core essence of a topic in an easy-to-understand way.","example":"To put it simply, it's about connecting with our customers on a deeper level.","plain":"It's about connecting with our customers on a deeper level.","note":"A common way to simplify the main purpose or goal of something.","audio":"audio/1f980288c74d7d69.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-it-succinctly","text":"to put it succinctly","kind":"chunk","form":"To put it succinctly","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To express something briefly and clearly, getting straight to the main point.","example":"To put it succinctly, our goal is to increase market share by 20% this quarter.","plain":"Our goal is to increase market share by 20% this quarter.","note":"A more formal alternative to \"in a nutshell,\" emphasizing conciseness.","audio":"audio/0e72ba2b7588374c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-my-experience-to-use","text":"to put my experience to use","kind":"collocation","form":"put my experience to use","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To apply one's past knowledge and skills in a practical way.","example":"I'm looking for a role where I can truly put my experience to use and make an impact.","plain":"I'm looking for a role where I can truly apply my experience and make an impact.","note":"Shows a desire for meaningful contribution.","audio":"audio/bb658145739a365c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-my-finger-on","text":"to put my finger on","kind":"chunk","form":"put my finger on","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To identify or pinpoint something precisely.","example":"I can't quite put my finger on what makes this team so effective, but it works.","plain":"I can't quite identify what makes this team so effective, but it works.","note":"Often used in the negative. Shows nuanced observation.","audio":"audio/bac0c73c15f41177.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-my-skills-to-use","text":"to put my skills to use","kind":"collocation","form":"to put my skills to use","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To apply one's abilities and expertise in a practical way.","example":"I'm eager to put my skills to use in a challenging new environment.","plain":"I'm eager to apply my abilities in a challenging new environment.","note":"Common for interviewees to express motivation and readiness to contribute.","audio":"audio/ab6f1b3c184f8c75.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-pressure-on","text":"to put pressure on","kind":"collocation","form":"to put pressure on","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To try to persuade or force someone to do something.","example":"They are trying to put pressure on us to agree quickly, but we need more time.","plain":"They are trying to force us to agree quickly, but we need more time.","note":"Can be negative. Describes an attempt to influence through force or urgency.","audio":"audio/c50fbb29c03e75ac.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-things-in-perspective","text":"to put things in perspective","kind":"chunk","form":"put things in perspective","level":"C1","situations":["interviews","presenting","explaining","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To help someone understand the true importance or significance of a situation.","example":"Let me put things in perspective: this challenge is significant, but manageable.","plain":"Let me explain the true importance: this challenge is significant, but manageable.","note":"Useful for managing expectations or reframing a problem.","audio":"audio/96a61e0b2959cf05.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-your-mind-at-ease","text":"To put your mind at ease","kind":"chunk","form":"To put your mind at ease","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To say or do something that helps someone stop worrying or feeling anxious.","example":"To put your mind at ease, we've already secured the necessary funding for the project.","plain":"To stop you worrying, we've already secured the necessary funding for the project.","note":"Used when you want to reassure someone who has concerns. Empathetic and calming.","audio":"audio/7e3970b1bf683ff4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-reach-a-compromise","text":"to reach a compromise","kind":"collocation","form":"to reach a compromise","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To arrive at an agreement where both sides give up something to meet in the middle.","example":"We need to reach a compromise on the payment terms that works for both parties.","plain":"We need to arrive at an agreement on the payment terms where both sides give up something.","note":"Collaborative. Used when seeking a middle ground.","audio":"audio/03490d4f98631fdc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-reach-a-settlement","text":"to reach a settlement","kind":"collocation","form":"to reach a settlement","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To achieve a formal agreement, especially after a dispute or negotiation.","example":"Our aim is to reach a settlement on the contract details by the end of the week.","plain":"Our aim is to achieve a formal agreement on the contract details by the end of the week.","note":"Formal. Often used in legal or complex business contexts.","audio":"audio/0eb06b9044e442c5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-recap-quickly","text":"to recap quickly","kind":"chunk","form":"To recap quickly","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"To briefly summarise the main points that have been discussed.","example":"To recap quickly, we've agreed on the budget and the project timeline.","plain":"To summarise briefly, we've agreed on the budget and project timeline.","note":"Used to provide a brief summary, often before moving to the next topic or ending the call.","audio":"audio/11110d9845240d24.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-recap-what-weve-discussed","text":"To recap what we've discussed","kind":"frame","form":"To recap what we've discussed","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to summarize the main points or decisions that have been made in a conversation or meeting.","example":"To recap what we've discussed, we'll proceed with option A and review progress next week.","plain":"To summarize, we'll proceed with option A and review progress next week.","note":"Ensures everyone is aligned and confirms understanding of outcomes. Professional and clear.","audio":"audio/a3218a56bd1229f3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-recap-then","text":"To recap, then","kind":"chunk","form":"To recap, then","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a short summary of what has just been discussed or presented.","example":"To recap, then, we've covered the historical context and the theoretical framework.","plain":"To summarize, we've covered the historical context and the theoretical framework.","note":"Useful for reminding the audience of key points before moving on.","audio":"audio/dc941c152e6570e5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-reiterate-my-main-point","text":"To reiterate my main point","kind":"frame","form":"To reiterate my main point","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To repeat the most important idea for emphasis or clarity.","example":"To reiterate my main point, customer satisfaction is our top priority.","plain":"To repeat my most important idea, customer satisfaction is our top priority.","note":"Used to reinforce a key message, often at the end of a section.","audio":"audio/b4d2f352c4e12ebe.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-reiterate-my-point","text":"To reiterate my point","kind":"collocation","form":"to reiterate my point","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","academic","presenting","explaining"],"meaning":"To repeat a point or statement, often for emphasis or to ensure it is understood.","example":"Just to reiterate my point, timely submission is absolutely critical for this project.","plain":"Just to repeat my point, timely submission is absolutely critical for this project.","note":"Used when a point needs to be reinforced or might have been missed.","audio":"audio/c9db09110f2c38e3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-reiterate-my-position","text":"To reiterate my position","kind":"chunk","form":"To reiterate my position","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Introducing a restatement of one's stance or opinion.","example":"To reiterate my position, I believe we should proceed with caution.","plain":"To repeat my stance, I believe we should proceed with caution.","note":"Used to emphasize or clarify a previously stated viewpoint.","audio":"audio/8d9fa036988b7219.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-reiterate-then","text":"To reiterate, then","kind":"chunk","form":"To reiterate, then","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Used to repeat a main point for emphasis or clarity, often before moving to a conclusion.","example":"To reiterate, then, interdisciplinary collaboration is key to addressing complex global issues.","plain":"To repeat, interdisciplinary collaboration is key to addressing complex global issues.","note":"A formal way to restate a crucial point, often in a summary.","audio":"audio/d3ecb6fb14f48b99.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-say-the-least","text":"To say the least","kind":"chunk","form":"to say the least","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Indicates that a statement is an understatement, implying the reality is more extreme.","example":"The journey was eventful, to say the least, with several unexpected detours.","plain":"The journey was very eventful, with several unexpected detours.","note":"Similar to \"to put it mildly,\" emphasizes an understatement.","audio":"audio/f1b73f481d6a7d8b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-seal-the-deal","text":"to seal the deal","kind":"chunk","form":"to seal the deal","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To finalize an agreement or negotiation successfully.","example":"I think we're ready to seal the deal on this project if we can agree on the timeline.","plain":"I think we're ready to finalize the agreement on this project if we can agree on the timeline.","note":"Informal and positive. Used when an agreement is imminent.","audio":"audio/da6ff19a62bda9b9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-secure-an-agreement","text":"to secure an agreement","kind":"collocation","form":"to secure an agreement","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To successfully obtain or finalize an agreement.","example":"Our goal is to secure an agreement on all terms by the end of the day.","plain":"Our goal is to successfully finalize an agreement on all terms by the end of the day.","note":"Formal. Used when the objective is to conclude negotiations.","audio":"audio/7a1217697112b26b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-set-the-scene","text":"To set the scene","kind":"chunk","form":"To set the scene","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling","presenting"],"meaning":"To provide necessary context or background information before discussing a topic.","example":"To set the scene, let's look at the current market conditions.","plain":"Let's look at the current market conditions to provide context.","note":"Similar to \"by way of background,\" but often more about current circumstances.","audio":"audio/119cb2536ba73f0e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-shed-light-on","text":"to shed light on","kind":"collocation","form":"to shed light on","level":"C1","situations":["interviews","explaining"],"meaning":"To help explain or clarify something that was previously unclear or mysterious.","example":"This new research really helps to shed light on the causes of the phenomenon.","plain":"This new research really helps to explain the causes of the phenomenon.","note":"Implies revealing information that makes something understandable.","audio":"audio/f250dc22b23ea447.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-simplify","text":"to simplify","kind":"chunk","form":"To simplify","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To make something easier to understand or do, often by removing complexity.","example":"To simplify, the new software automates the entire reporting process.","plain":"The new software automates the entire reporting process.","note":"A direct way to indicate you are making an explanation less complex.","audio":"audio/022c64dd9d6b607d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-smooth-things-over","text":"to smooth things over","kind":"chunk","form":"to smooth things over","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To make a difficult or awkward situation less tense or problematic, often through diplomacy.","example":"I had a chat with the client to smooth things over after the misunderstanding about the invoice.","plain":"I had a chat with the client to make the situation less problematic after the misunderstanding about the invoice.","note":"Focuses on resolving interpersonal friction or minor issues diplomatically. Informal.","audio":"audio/1b0d6f3700b05b04.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-spell-it-out","text":"to spell it out","kind":"chunk","form":"to spell it out","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain something in a very clear, detailed, and explicit way, leaving no room for doubt.","example":"I'll try to spell it out for you: we need to reduce our carbon footprint by 20% this year.","plain":"I'll try to explain it very clearly for you: we need to reduce our carbon footprint by 20% this year.","note":"Informal. Used when you need to be extremely explicit, sometimes with a hint of impatience.","audio":"audio/9cf24e0c80bbc500.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-stay-abreast-of","text":"to stay abreast of","kind":"collocation","form":"to stay abreast of","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To keep up-to-date with the latest information or developments.","example":"It's essential to stay abreast of industry trends to remain competitive.","plain":"It's essential to keep up-to-date with industry trends to remain competitive.","note":"Professional, emphasizes continuous learning and awareness.","audio":"audio/2eb5b1a0a8d70113.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-strike-a-balance","text":"to strike a balance","kind":"collocation","form":"strike a balance","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To find a satisfactory compromise between two opposing things.","example":"It's important to strike a balance between innovation and practical implementation.","plain":"It's important to find a compromise between innovation and practical implementation.","note":"Shows ability to manage competing priorities.","audio":"audio/9c84464588bb6b9c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-summarise-our-discussion","text":"to summarise our discussion","kind":"frame","form":"To summarise our discussion","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I will briefly restate the main points and conclusions of our conversation.","example":"To summarise our discussion, we'll proceed with option A and review it next month.","plain":"In summary, we'll go with option A and check it again next month.","note":"A formal and clear way to recap key outcomes at the end of a meeting or call.","audio":"audio/5cbb6d6a2fe40f62.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-summarise-then","text":"To summarise then","kind":"chunk","form":"To summarise then","level":"C1","situations":["academic","presenting"],"meaning":"Introduces a concluding summary of the key points discussed.","example":"To summarise then, the evidence strongly supports the need for immediate policy intervention.","plain":"In conclusion, the evidence strongly supports the need for immediate policy intervention.","note":"A common way to signal the end of a section or presentation.","audio":"audio/ff740e7fae238b70.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-summarise-then-2","text":"To summarise, then","kind":"chunk","form":"To summarise, then","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a concluding summary of the key points discussed.","example":"To summarise, then, the evidence strongly supports the need for immediate policy intervention.","plain":"In conclusion, the evidence strongly supports the need for immediate policy intervention.","note":"A common way to signal the end of a section or presentation.","audio":"audio/2306b727078fae12.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-sweeten-the-deal","text":"to sweeten the deal","kind":"chunk","form":"to sweeten the deal","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To make an offer more attractive or appealing to the other party.","example":"We could extend the warranty to sweeten the deal, if that helps you decide.","plain":"We could extend the warranty to make the offer more attractive, if that helps you decide.","note":"Informal but common in business. Used when making an additional offer.","audio":"audio/a4082e922af9174f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-sweeten-the-pot","text":"to sweeten the pot","kind":"chunk","form":"to sweeten the pot","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To make an offer more appealing by adding extra benefits or value.","example":"We might need to sweeten the pot with a longer support period to get them to agree.","plain":"We might need to make the offer more appealing with a longer support period to get them to agree.","note":"Informal. Similar to 'sweeten the deal', often implies adding more money or value.","audio":"audio/d3021f8e8d15d847.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-take-a-firm-stance","text":"to take a firm stance","kind":"collocation","form":"to take a firm stance","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To adopt a resolute and unyielding position on an issue.","example":"We need to take a firm stance on this issue to protect our long-term interests.","plain":"We need to adopt a resolute position on this issue to protect our long-term interests.","note":"Formal. Used to describe a determined position.","audio":"audio/f9c524af0207d071.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-take-a-step-back","text":"to take a step back","kind":"chunk","form":"take a step back","level":"C1","situations":["interviews","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To pause and consider a situation from a broader perspective.","example":"Sometimes it's helpful to take a step back and look at the bigger picture.","plain":"Sometimes it's helpful to pause and look at the bigger picture.","note":"Useful for problem-solving or strategic thinking.","audio":"audio/8c2ec5946e40e7e5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-take-into-account","text":"to take into account","kind":"collocation","form":"take into account","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation","interviews","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To consider particular facts, circumstances, or feelings when making a decision.","example":"We need to take into account the team's current workload before assigning new tasks.","plain":"We need to consider the team's current workload before assigning new tasks.","note":"Shows careful planning and empathy.","audio":"audio/8e09749a19045351.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-take-ownership-of","text":"to take ownership of","kind":"collocation","form":"take ownership of","level":"C1","situations":["interviews","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To accept responsibility for something, including its successes and failures.","example":"I always encourage my team members to take ownership of their tasks and outcomes.","plain":"I always encourage my team members to accept responsibility for their tasks and outcomes.","note":"Highly valued in professional settings. Shows maturity and accountability.","audio":"audio/0b6b2e562a2e65a0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-take-the-initiative","text":"to take the initiative","kind":"collocation","form":"take the initiative","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To act independently and proactively, without being told what to do.","example":"I always encourage my team members to take the initiative in problem-solving.","plain":"I always encourage my team members to act proactively in problem-solving.","note":"Highly valued professional trait.","audio":"audio/8f77de1dbbac6744.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-talk-you-through","text":"To talk you through","kind":"chunk","form":"talk you through","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"Similar to \"walk you through,\" to explain something verbally in a step-by-step manner.","example":"I can talk you through the setup process over the phone if you like.","plain":"I can explain the setup process step-by-step over the phone if you like.","note":"Often used for verbal instructions or explanations, especially remotely.","audio":"audio/8c375fd51e8b7afa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-touch-on","text":"to touch on","kind":"collocation","form":"touch on","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To mention or briefly discuss a topic without going into great detail.","example":"I'd like to touch on my experience with data analysis, as it's relevant here.","plain":"I'd like to briefly mention my experience with data analysis, as it's relevant here.","note":"Good for introducing a point without committing to a long explanation.","audio":"audio/c8df40e3cf16bcb2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-underscore-the-importance","text":"To underscore the importance","kind":"collocation","form":"to underscore the importance","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To emphasize how significant or crucial something is.","example":"I want to underscore the importance of meeting this deadline for our client.","plain":"I want to emphasize how important it is to meet this deadline for our client.","note":"A more formal way to stress the significance of something.","audio":"audio/3cd928eb0f151b8b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-underscore-the-importance-of","text":"To underscore the importance of","kind":"frame","form":"To underscore the importance of","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Highlights why something is particularly significant or crucial.","example":"To underscore the importance of ethical considerations, we included a dedicated section.","plain":"To emphasize why ethical considerations are important, we included a dedicated section.","note":"A slightly more formal way to stress significance.","audio":"audio/d734af9a48fb59a7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-underscore-the-point","text":"To underscore the point","kind":"chunk","form":"To underscore the point","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To emphasize a particular idea or statement, making it more noticeable or important.","example":"To underscore the point, our competitors are already adopting similar technologies.","plain":"To emphasize the point, our competitors are already adopting similar technologies.","note":"A formal way to add emphasis to an argument or statement. Often used before a reinforcing fact.","audio":"audio/77de18c1e57285be.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-walk-me-through","text":"to walk me through","kind":"chunk","form":"walk me through","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To explain a process or sequence of events step by step.","example":"Could you walk me through the typical onboarding process for new hires?","plain":"Could you explain the typical onboarding process for new hires step by step?","note":"Common request in interviews. Polite and direct.","audio":"audio/d5ea92c05c147df1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-walk-you-through","text":"To walk you through","kind":"chunk","form":"walk you through","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To guide someone step-by-step through a process, explanation, or demonstration.","example":"The new software has many features. Let me walk you through the main functions.","plain":"The new software has many features. Let me guide you step-by-step through the main functions.","note":"Implies a patient, detailed, and guided explanation.","audio":"audio/026abe02f4acb4aa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-weigh-in-on","text":"to weigh in on","kind":"collocation","form":"weigh in on","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To offer an opinion or contribute to a discussion.","example":"I'd be happy to weigh in on the proposed marketing strategy if you'd like.","plain":"I'd be happy to offer my opinion on the proposed marketing strategy if you'd like.","note":"Shows willingness to contribute.","audio":"audio/ad7cc21f164c3aab.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-weigh-up-the-pros-and-cons","text":"to weigh up the pros and cons","kind":"chunk","form":"to weigh up the pros and cons","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To carefully consider the advantages and disadvantages of a particular course of action or decision.","example":"Before making a final decision, we need to weigh up the pros and cons of each proposed solution.","plain":"Before making a final decision, we need to consider the advantages and disadvantages of each proposed solution.","note":"A standard phrase for decision-making, emphasizing a balanced and thoughtful approach.","audio":"audio/bb1b5799a909f1a2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.touch-base","text":"touch base","kind":"chunk","form":"touch base","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To briefly connect with someone to discuss something.","example":"Let's touch base next week to see how the implementation is progressing.","plain":"Let's talk next week to see how the implementation is progressing.","note":"Informal but widely used in professional settings.","audio":"audio/846c809339822eb1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.touch-base-on","text":"touch base on","kind":"collocation","form":"touch base on","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","feedback"],"meaning":"To briefly connect with someone to discuss a specific topic or update.","example":"Let's touch base on the feedback from the client meeting tomorrow morning.","plain":"Let's discuss the feedback from the client meeting tomorrow morning.","note":"A common business idiom for a short follow-up or check-in. Informal but professional.","audio":"audio/50357e39f4d741de.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.touch-base-with","text":"touch base with","kind":"chunk","form":"touch base with","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To briefly communicate with someone, often to update or check in.","example":"I'll touch base with you next week to see how the implementation is progressing.","plain":"I'll briefly communicate with you next week to see how the implementation is progressing.","note":"Informal, common in professional communication.","audio":"audio/729991165f1cb868.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.touch-wood","text":"touch wood","kind":"chunk","form":"Touch wood","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to ward off bad luck after mentioning something good or positive.","example":"The project is going smoothly, touch wood, we haven't had any major issues.","plain":"The project is going smoothly, we haven't had any major issues.","note":"Common in British English; equivalent to 'knock on wood' in American English.","audio":"audio/83750197ae7e4fbb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.travel-light","text":"travel light","kind":"collocation","form":"travel light","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To take only a small amount of luggage when travelling.","example":"For this short trip, I decided to travel light with just a backpack.","plain":"For this short trip, I decided to take only a small amount of luggage.","note":"Useful advice for easier travel, especially with budget airlines.","audio":"audio/5e33b6b513cc26a0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.unfortunately-im-not-able-to","text":"Unfortunately, I'm not able to","kind":"frame","form":"Unfortunately, I'm not able to","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite and slightly more formal way to decline an invitation or request.","example":"Unfortunately, I'm not able to attend the meeting next week.","plain":"Sadly, I cannot attend the meeting next week.","note":"Suitable for both formal and informal contexts, conveying regret.","audio":"audio/ce11b6644a6b8fc9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-can-achieve-this-by","text":"We can achieve this by","kind":"frame","form":"We can achieve this by","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To explain the method or strategy for reaching a goal.","example":"We can achieve this by optimizing our internal processes and reducing waste.","plain":"We can reach this goal by optimizing our internal processes and reducing waste.","note":"Provides a clear path forward, making a proposal actionable.","audio":"audio/6c363e35c0bbbadb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-cant-budge-on","text":"we can't budge on","kind":"chunk","form":"we can't budge on","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Indicates that a particular term or condition is non-negotiable and cannot be changed.","example":"I'm afraid we can't budge on the core functionality requirements.","plain":"I'm afraid we cannot change the core functionality requirements.","note":"A firm statement, signaling a fixed position on a specific issue.","audio":"audio/415862f7cf0e34b5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-could-also","text":"We could also","kind":"frame","form":"We could also","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To introduce an additional option or alternative course of action.","example":"We could also explore partnerships with local businesses to expand our reach.","plain":"Another option is to explore partnerships with local businesses to expand our reach.","note":"Offers flexibility and shows openness to different approaches.","audio":"audio/26bb2967cc91d6b6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-could-explore-options-like","text":"We could explore options like","kind":"frame","form":"We could explore options like","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to suggest specific alternatives or possibilities for consideration.","example":"We could explore options like extending the deadline or reassigning some team members.","plain":"We could consider alternatives such as extending the deadline or reassigning some team members.","note":"Encourages brainstorming and offers concrete examples of potential solutions.","audio":"audio/17832d990190dc07.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-a-chance-to","text":"We have a chance to","kind":"frame","form":"We have a chance to","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To highlight an opportunity for positive change or achievement.","example":"We have a chance to redefine our market position with this innovative product.","plain":"We can redefine our market position with this innovative product.","note":"Similar to 'We have an opportunity to,' but slightly more informal, emphasizing possibility.","audio":"audio/8a6593f6786e48da.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-a-clear-path-to","text":"We have a clear path to","kind":"frame","form":"We have a clear path to","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To indicate a well-defined strategy for achieving a goal.","example":"We have a clear path to achieving our sustainability targets by 2030.","plain":"We have a definite plan to achieve our sustainability targets by 2030.","note":"Conveys clarity and direction, making a proposal seem well-thought-out.","audio":"audio/889915dd38bdbbec.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-a-collective-goal-to","text":"We have a collective goal to","kind":"frame","form":"We have a collective goal to","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To appeal to a common objective that unites all parties.","example":"We have a collective goal to reduce our carbon footprint by 20%.","plain":"We all want to reduce our carbon footprint by 20%.","note":"Emphasizes unity and common purpose, fostering collaboration.","audio":"audio/7700a687982fb98b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-a-collective-responsibility-to","text":"We have a collective responsibility to","kind":"frame","form":"We have a collective responsibility to","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To appeal to a shared sense of duty or obligation among the group.","example":"We have a collective responsibility to uphold the company's ethical standards.","plain":"We all have a duty to uphold the company's ethical standards.","note":"A strong appeal to group accountability and shared moral compass.","audio":"audio/da2b2eb7a1f4b684.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-a-common-purpose-to","text":"We have a common purpose to","kind":"frame","form":"We have a common purpose to","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To appeal to a shared fundamental reason for action or existence.","example":"We have a common purpose to serve our community with integrity and excellence.","plain":"We all share the goal of serving our community with integrity and excellence.","note":"Emphasizes a unifying mission or overarching objective.","audio":"audio/de947d4925161c8d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-a-common-understanding-that","text":"We have a common understanding that","kind":"frame","form":"We have a common understanding that","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To appeal to a shared interpretation or agreement on a topic.","example":"We have a common understanding that customer satisfaction is our top priority.","plain":"We all agree that customer satisfaction is our top priority.","note":"Establishes common ground by confirming mutual comprehension.","audio":"audio/e9ddb729a22aed99.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-a-common-vision-for","text":"We have a common vision for","kind":"frame","form":"We have a common vision for","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To appeal to a shared future aspiration or ideal.","example":"We have a common vision for a more sustainable and equitable future.","plain":"We share the same idea for a more sustainable and equitable future.","note":"Inspires unity by focusing on a collective, desired future state.","audio":"audio/e718637d4cd2161a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-a-duty-to","text":"We have a duty to","kind":"frame","form":"We have a duty to","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To emphasize a moral or professional obligation.","example":"We have a duty to protect our customers' privacy and data security.","plain":"We must protect our customers' privacy and data security.","note":"A more formal and stronger version of 'responsibility to,' often used for ethical arguments.","audio":"audio/f852f53a7616a9f8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-a-responsibility-to","text":"We have a responsibility to","kind":"frame","form":"We have a responsibility to","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To appeal to a sense of duty or obligation.","example":"We have a responsibility to ensure the well-being of our employees.","plain":"We must ensure the well-being of our employees.","note":"Used to invoke ethical considerations or shared duties, often for a moral argument.","audio":"audio/8db8d2ecc48aac61.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-a-shared-belief-in","text":"We have a shared belief in","kind":"frame","form":"We have a shared belief in","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To appeal to a common conviction or principle among all parties.","example":"We have a shared belief in the importance of continuous learning and development.","plain":"We all believe in the importance of continuous learning and development.","note":"Emphasizes mutual conviction, fostering unity and shared purpose.","audio":"audio/9ad919a00026279b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-a-shared-commitment-to","text":"We have a shared commitment to","kind":"frame","form":"We have a shared commitment to","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To appeal to a common dedication or promise among all parties.","example":"We have a shared commitment to innovation and continuous improvement.","plain":"We are all dedicated to innovation and continuous improvement.","note":"Highlights mutual dedication, fostering unity and shared purpose.","audio":"audio/bf42154d7cbc6bfe.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-a-shared-goal-of","text":"We have a shared goal of","kind":"frame","form":"We have a shared goal of","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To appeal to a common objective that unites all parties.","example":"We have a shared goal of delivering exceptional value to our clients.","plain":"We all want to deliver exceptional value to our clients.","note":"Emphasizes unity and common purpose, fostering collaboration.","audio":"audio/147b61dd16eed554.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-a-shared-interest-in","text":"We have a shared interest in","kind":"frame","form":"We have a shared interest in","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To appeal to common goals or benefits among all parties.","example":"We have a shared interest in ensuring the long-term sustainability of this project.","plain":"We all want to ensure the long-term sustainability of this project.","note":"Emphasizes common ground, fostering collaboration and mutual understanding.","audio":"audio/df7427a2987155f4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-a-shared-objective-to","text":"We have a shared objective to","kind":"frame","form":"We have a shared objective to","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To appeal to a common aim or target that unites all parties.","example":"We have a shared objective to deliver this project under budget.","plain":"We all aim to deliver this project under budget.","note":"Emphasizes unity and common purpose, fostering collaboration.","audio":"audio/6b1e7e4f4a3d3146.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-a-shared-understanding-of","text":"We have a shared understanding of","kind":"frame","form":"We have a shared understanding of","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To appeal to a common interpretation or agreement on a topic.","example":"We have a shared understanding of the challenges we face in the current market.","plain":"We all agree on the challenges we face in the current market.","note":"Establishes common ground by confirming mutual comprehension.","audio":"audio/e2b0a075d84f1638.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-a-shared-vision-of","text":"We have a shared vision of","kind":"frame","form":"We have a shared vision of","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To appeal to a common future aspiration or ideal.","example":"We have a shared vision of becoming the leading innovator in our field.","plain":"We all share the same idea of becoming the leading innovator in our field.","note":"Inspires unity by focusing on a collective, desired future state.","audio":"audio/6cab477d3ed44261.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-a-strong-argument-for","text":"We have a strong argument for","kind":"frame","form":"We have a strong argument for","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To state that there is compelling reasoning to support a position.","example":"We have a strong argument for prioritizing employee well-being in our budget.","plain":"There is good reasoning to prioritize employee well-being in our budget.","note":"Similar to 'strong case for,' but emphasizes the logical reasoning behind a stance.","audio":"audio/b20be0e039a90ca6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-a-strong-case-for","text":"We have a strong case for","kind":"frame","form":"We have a strong case for","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To state that there is compelling evidence or reasoning to support something.","example":"We have a strong case for increasing our investment in research and development.","plain":"There is good reason to increase our investment in research and development.","note":"Indicates that the argument is well-supported and likely to be persuasive.","audio":"audio/168f6630b2f7fff2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-a-strong-commitment-to","text":"We have a strong commitment to","kind":"frame","form":"We have a strong commitment to","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To emphasize a firm dedication to a principle or goal.","example":"We have a strong commitment to ethical practices in all our business dealings.","plain":"We are very dedicated to ethical practices in all our business dealings.","note":"Highlights a core value or unwavering dedication of the group.","audio":"audio/e60d84464fbbc6f7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-a-strong-foundation-for","text":"We have a strong foundation for","kind":"frame","form":"We have a strong foundation for","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To state that there is a solid basis for future development or action.","example":"We have a strong foundation for expanding into new markets next year.","plain":"We have a solid basis for expanding into new markets next year.","note":"Suggests stability and readiness for growth, making future plans seem achievable.","audio":"audio/7e6619b6d562159e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-a-unique-opportunity-to","text":"We have a unique opportunity to","kind":"frame","form":"We have a unique opportunity to","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To highlight a special or rare chance for success.","example":"We have a unique opportunity to capture a significant share of this emerging market.","plain":"We have a special chance to capture a significant share of this emerging market.","note":"Emphasizes the distinctiveness and value of the current situation.","audio":"audio/339246eb07dbe4b5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-an-opportunity-to","text":"We have an opportunity to","kind":"frame","form":"We have an opportunity to","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To point out a chance for positive development or advantage.","example":"We have an opportunity to set a new industry standard with this innovative product.","plain":"We can set a new industry standard with this innovative product.","note":"Frames a situation as a chance for growth or success, encouraging action.","audio":"audio/a4831cd29826107e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-the-potential-to","text":"We have the potential to","kind":"frame","form":"We have the potential to","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To highlight an inherent capability or undeveloped possibility.","example":"We have the potential to become market leaders if we embrace this innovation.","plain":"We can become market leaders if we embrace this innovation.","note":"Inspires ambition and encourages leveraging existing strengths for future success.","audio":"audio/2edea192dd6f2102.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-to-factor-in","text":"We have to factor in","kind":"frame","form":"We have to factor in","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to emphasize the necessity of considering an additional element or circumstance.","example":"We have to factor in the potential for unexpected delays when setting the project timeline.","plain":"We must consider the potential for unexpected delays when setting the project timeline.","note":"Highlights the importance of including all relevant variables in planning or decision-making.","audio":"audio/b24a411f2265bbad.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-must-do-this-again-soon","text":"We must do this again soon","kind":"chunk","form":"we must do this again soon","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing a strong desire to repeat the social activity or meeting in the near future.","example":"That was a lovely evening; we must do this again soon.","plain":"That was a lovely evening; we should meet again soon.","note":"A warm and friendly closing remark, indicating enjoyment and future intent.","audio":"audio/3df922e6f7de4e89.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-must-ensure-that","text":"We must ensure that","kind":"frame","form":"We must ensure that","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To state that it is absolutely necessary to make certain that something happens or is true.","example":"We must ensure that all team members receive adequate training on the new system.","plain":"We need to make certain that all team members receive adequate training on the new system.","note":"Used to highlight a critical responsibility or requirement. Direct and firm.","audio":"audio/7d7f8103b0cd8026.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-must-not-forget","text":"We must not forget","kind":"frame","form":"We must not forget","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To strongly emphasize a point that needs to be kept in mind.","example":"We must not forget the human element when implementing new technologies.","plain":"We need to remember the human element when implementing new technologies.","note":"A strong way to highlight a crucial consideration; sounds serious.","audio":"audio/f3cc311e9a6478ad.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-must-take-into-account","text":"We must take into account","kind":"frame","form":"We must take into account","level":"C1","situations":["academic","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To state the absolute necessity of considering particular facts or circumstances.","example":"We must take into account the feedback from our pilot users.","plain":"It is absolutely necessary to consider the feedback from our pilot users.","note":"Strong and direct, used to ensure important factors are not overlooked.","audio":"audio/9b99273b529492bb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-account-for","text":"We need to account for","kind":"frame","form":"We need to account for","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To state that a particular element must be considered or explained.","example":"We need to account for the increased demand during the holiday season.","plain":"We must consider the increased demand during the holiday season.","note":"Used to ensure all relevant aspects are included in planning.","audio":"audio/2efbbcbfe12871cb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-acknowledge","text":"We need to acknowledge","kind":"frame","form":"We need to acknowledge","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To state that a particular fact or issue must be accepted or dealt with, often a difficult one.","example":"We need to acknowledge the challenges involved before we can move forward effectively.","plain":"We must accept the challenges before we can move forward effectively.","note":"Used to bring a potentially uncomfortable truth or difficulty into the open. Direct and responsible.","audio":"audio/40e6a46fcb5ab5b5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-align-on","text":"we need to align on","kind":"collocation","form":"we need to align on","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To ensure that all parties agree on or are in harmony regarding a specific point.","example":"We need to align on the project's key performance indicators.","plain":"We need to agree on the project's key performance indicators.","note":"Emphasizes the importance of shared understanding and agreement.","audio":"audio/fb501a144c5f894c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-be-clear-on","text":"we need to be clear on","kind":"frame","form":"We need to be clear on","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Emphasizes the importance of having a precise understanding of a specific point.","example":"We need to be clear on the exact deliverables before we proceed.","plain":"We must have a precise understanding of the exact deliverables before we proceed.","note":"Used to ensure no ambiguity remains on critical aspects.","audio":"audio/6e7c19cc2721720e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-be-flexible-on","text":"we need to be flexible on","kind":"frame","form":"We need to be flexible on","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Emphasizes the necessity of being adaptable regarding a specific point.","example":"We need to be flexible on the exact specifications to stay within budget.","plain":"We must be adaptable regarding the exact specifications to stay within budget.","note":"Suggests a willingness to adjust, often to achieve a larger goal.","audio":"audio/7a1754011b343250.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-be-mindful-of","text":"we need to be mindful of","kind":"frame","form":"We need to be mindful of","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","negotiation","academic","presenting","persuading","problem-solving"],"meaning":"Emphasizes the importance of being aware of and considering certain factors.","example":"We need to be mindful of the budget constraints throughout this process.","plain":"We must be aware of the budget constraints throughout this process.","note":"Highlights a crucial consideration that should not be overlooked.","audio":"audio/75b96f89d93b1f96.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-be-realistic","text":"we need to be realistic","kind":"chunk","form":"We need to be realistic","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation","persuading"],"meaning":"Emphasizes the importance of considering practical limitations and possibilities.","example":"We need to be realistic about what we can achieve within this timeframe.","plain":"We need to consider what is practically achievable within this timeframe.","note":"Used to temper expectations or introduce a dose of practicality.","audio":"audio/377881b0c05a30d2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-bridge-the-gap","text":"we need to bridge the gap","kind":"collocation","form":"we need to bridge the gap","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To find a way to reconcile differences or disagreements between parties.","example":"We need to bridge the gap between our expectations and their capabilities.","plain":"We need to find a way to reconcile our expectations and their capabilities.","note":"Emphasizes the need to find common ground and overcome differences.","audio":"audio/7121caf7e159d285.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-consider-all-angles","text":"we need to consider all angles","kind":"collocation","form":"we need to consider all angles","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To examine a situation or proposal from every possible perspective.","example":"Before making a final decision, we need to consider all angles of this proposal.","plain":"Before making a final decision, we need to examine every perspective of this proposal.","note":"Emphasizes thoroughness and a comprehensive approach to decision-making.","audio":"audio/0ae8c1df2d9d44ed.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-consider-the-implications","text":"we need to consider the implications","kind":"collocation","form":"we need to consider the implications","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To think about the possible future effects or consequences of a decision or action.","example":"We need to consider the implications of this change on our long-term strategy.","plain":"We need to think about the possible future effects of this change on our long-term strategy.","note":"Emphasizes a cautious and forward-thinking approach.","audio":"audio/f3c86b04f409c7e0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-differentiate-between","text":"We need to differentiate between","kind":"frame","form":"We need to differentiate between","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To state the necessity of recognizing the differences between two things.","example":"We need to differentiate between urgent tasks and important tasks.","plain":"We must recognize the differences between urgent tasks and important tasks.","note":"Used to clarify distinctions for better decision-making.","audio":"audio/d6f51f7a4a76d3aa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-ensure-that","text":"we need to ensure that","kind":"frame","form":"We need to ensure that","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation","persuading","problem-solving"],"meaning":"Emphasizes the necessity of guaranteeing a particular outcome or condition.","example":"We need to ensure that all legal requirements are met before signing.","plain":"We must guarantee that all legal requirements are met before signing.","note":"Highlights a critical requirement that must be satisfied.","audio":"audio/80cc08f27bee3d47.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-factor-in","text":"we need to factor in","kind":"collocation","form":"we need to factor in","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To include a particular element or consideration when making calculations or plans.","example":"We need to factor in the potential for delays due to supply chain issues.","plain":"We need to include the potential for delays due to supply chain issues.","note":"Highlights the importance of considering all relevant variables.","audio":"audio/fd24d46346ce2ac7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-figure-out-how-to","text":"We need to figure out how to","kind":"frame","form":"We need to figure out how to","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to identify a problem that requires a solution or a method to achieve a goal.","example":"We need to figure out how to scale our operations without compromising quality.","plain":"We need to find a way to scale our operations without compromising quality.","note":"Highlights a challenge that requires collective problem-solving. Common in team discussions.","audio":"audio/3f16f68ba208db3a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-find-a-middle-ground","text":"we need to find a middle ground","kind":"collocation","form":"we need to find a middle ground","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To discover a compromise or a solution that satisfies both parties to some extent.","example":"We need to find a middle ground between your budget and our service level.","plain":"We need to discover a compromise between your budget and our service level.","note":"Emphasizes the need for compromise and mutual adjustment.","audio":"audio/45b1c1e83225dcbf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-find-a-solution","text":"we need to find a solution","kind":"frame","form":"We need to find a solution","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Emphasizes the necessity of resolving a problem or challenge.","example":"We need to find a solution to the integration issues quickly.","plain":"We must resolve the integration issues quickly.","note":"Focuses on problem-solving and moving forward.","audio":"audio/542c16a7d53b91f5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-find-a-way-to","text":"we need to find a way to","kind":"frame","form":"We need to find a way to","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Expresses the necessity of discovering a method or solution for a challenge.","example":"We need to find a way to reduce costs without compromising quality.","plain":"We must discover a method to reduce costs without compromising quality.","note":"Encourages collaborative problem-solving and innovation.","audio":"audio/15f4f8f4a8afe31f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-should-also-consider","text":"We should also consider","kind":"frame","form":"We should also consider","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To introduce an additional point or perspective for discussion.","example":"We should also consider the environmental impact of our manufacturing processes.","plain":"We should also think about the environmental impact of our manufacturing processes.","note":"Broadens the scope of the discussion, ensuring all relevant aspects are covered.","audio":"audio/e1e09d5eaa895ec3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-should-catch-up-properly","text":"We should catch up properly","kind":"chunk","form":"we should catch up properly","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Suggesting a more in-depth meeting to discuss recent events and news.","example":"This is too quick; we should catch up properly over coffee soon.","plain":"This is too quick; we should meet for a longer chat over coffee soon.","note":"Implies that a brief encounter isn't enough to share everything.","audio":"audio/ad4adc1f0cf26f80.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-should-definitely","text":"We should definitely","kind":"chunk","form":"We should definitely","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing a strong suggestion or intention to do something together.","example":"We should definitely try that new restaurant everyone's talking about.","plain":"We must try that new restaurant everyone's talking about.","note":"Enthusiastic and encouraging. Often used when making future plans.","audio":"audio/097b9e604112d9e2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-stand-to-gain","text":"We stand to gain","kind":"frame","form":"We stand to gain","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To explain the potential benefits or advantages for the group.","example":"By investing in training, we stand to gain a more skilled and motivated workforce.","plain":"By investing in training, we will get a more skilled and motivated workforce.","note":"Emphasizes the positive outcomes for the collective, appealing to shared interest.","audio":"audio/e2b98af98ed9ca95.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.wed-be-delighted-if","text":"We'd be delighted if","kind":"frame","form":"We'd be delighted if","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A formal and very polite way to express pleasure at someone's potential attendance.","example":"We'd be delighted if you could attend our anniversary celebration.","plain":"We would be very happy if you could attend our anniversary celebration.","note":"Quite formal, suitable for significant events or when addressing esteemed guests.","audio":"audio/dcd6c6bf6b03aca4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.well-delve-into","text":"We'll delve into","kind":"collocation","form":"we'll delve into","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To explore a topic in detail and depth.","example":"Later, we'll delve into the specific challenges we faced during implementation.","plain":"Later, we will examine the specific challenges we faced during implementation in detail.","note":"Suggests a thorough and in-depth discussion.","audio":"audio/5e3ff10a50be545b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.well-explore-the-implications-of","text":"We'll explore the implications of","kind":"collocation","form":"we'll explore the implications of","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To examine the potential consequences or effects of something.","example":"Next, we'll explore the implications of these policy changes for our operations.","plain":"Next, we will examine the effects of these policy changes for our operations.","note":"Indicates a discussion about future impacts or consequences.","audio":"audio/87d9aafba39c6f9c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.were-largely-in-agreement","text":"We're largely in agreement","kind":"chunk","form":"We're largely in agreement","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Emphasizes common ground before introducing a minor point of divergence.","example":"We're largely in agreement on the overall goals, but the implementation details need more discussion.","plain":"We mostly agree on the overall goals, but the implementation details need more discussion.","note":"Highlights shared understanding, making any subsequent disagreement seem less significant.","audio":"audio/660dad083f801aba.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.were-prepared-to-absorb","text":"we're prepared to absorb","kind":"frame","form":"We're prepared to absorb","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Indicates a willingness to take on costs or responsibilities that might otherwise fall to the other party.","example":"We're prepared to absorb the initial training costs for your team.","plain":"We are willing to cover the initial training costs for your team.","note":"A significant concession, often used to sweeten a deal or build trust.","audio":"audio/98d8fd5e646f036c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.were-prepared-to-adjust","text":"we're prepared to adjust","kind":"frame","form":"We're prepared to adjust","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Indicates a willingness to make changes to one's position or proposal.","example":"We're prepared to adjust the scope if it helps meet your budget.","plain":"We are willing to change the scope if it helps meet your budget.","note":"Shows flexibility and a desire to accommodate the other party's needs.","audio":"audio/3cf7e8d46c8a7f14.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.were-prepared-to-be-flexible-on","text":"we're prepared to be flexible on","kind":"frame","form":"We're prepared to be flexible on","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Indicates a willingness to adapt or change one's position on a specific point.","example":"We're prepared to be flexible on the exact specifications if the budget is tight.","plain":"We are willing to adapt on the exact specifications if the budget is tight.","note":"Shows a cooperative attitude and a desire to find a workable solution.","audio":"audio/e262d35db45c06ab.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.were-prepared-to-compromise-on","text":"we're prepared to compromise on","kind":"frame","form":"We're prepared to compromise on","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Indicates a willingness to make concessions on a specific point.","example":"We're prepared to compromise on the delivery date if the price is firm.","plain":"We are willing to make concessions on the delivery date if the price is firm.","note":"Shows flexibility and a desire to find a middle ground.","audio":"audio/2a607199a64550b2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.were-prepared-to-concede","text":"we're prepared to concede","kind":"frame","form":"We're prepared to concede","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Indicates a willingness to give up a point or demand in a negotiation.","example":"We're prepared to concede on the minor features to secure the core deal.","plain":"We are willing to give up the minor features to secure the core deal.","note":"A direct way to signal a concession, often to move the negotiation forward.","audio":"audio/bc0898a18e371514.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.were-prepared-to-explore","text":"we're prepared to explore","kind":"frame","form":"We're prepared to explore","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Indicates openness to investigating different possibilities or solutions.","example":"We're prepared to explore alternative technologies if the current one is too costly.","plain":"We are willing to investigate alternative technologies if the current one is too costly.","note":"Shows flexibility and a proactive approach to problem-solving.","audio":"audio/8e062175660f7cf9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.were-prepared-to-go-as-far-as","text":"we're prepared to go as far as","kind":"frame","form":"We're prepared to go as far as","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Indicates the maximum extent of a concession or offer one is willing to make.","example":"We're prepared to go as far as a 15% discount, but no further.","plain":"We are willing to offer up to a 15% discount, but no more.","note":"Sets a clear limit on concessions, signaling a firm boundary.","audio":"audio/23fa86fc79c4a657.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.were-prepared-to-make-a-concession","text":"we're prepared to make a concession","kind":"frame","form":"We're prepared to make a concession","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Indicates a readiness to yield on a point to facilitate agreement.","example":"We're prepared to make a concession on the payment terms for a quicker sign-off.","plain":"We are willing to yield on the payment terms for a quicker sign-off.","note":"A formal way to signal flexibility and a desire to move forward.","audio":"audio/167fd085cc322f48.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.were-prepared-to-make-a-trade-off","text":"we're prepared to make a trade-off","kind":"frame","form":"We're prepared to make a trade-off","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Indicates a willingness to exchange one benefit for another, accepting a compromise.","example":"We're prepared to make a trade-off on features for a faster delivery.","plain":"We are willing to exchange some features for a faster delivery.","note":"Highlights a strategic decision to prioritize one aspect over another.","audio":"audio/5d26533ff90568ef.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.were-prepared-to-make-an-adjustment","text":"we're prepared to make an adjustment","kind":"frame","form":"We're prepared to make an adjustment","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Indicates a willingness to modify a proposal or position slightly.","example":"We're prepared to make an adjustment to the delivery schedule if needed.","plain":"We are willing to modify the delivery schedule if needed.","note":"Shows flexibility and a readiness to fine-tune details.","audio":"audio/c584c57e630f3ed0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.were-prepared-to-make-an-exception","text":"we're prepared to make an exception","kind":"frame","form":"We're prepared to make an exception","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Indicates a willingness to deviate from standard rules or procedures for a specific case.","example":"We're prepared to make an exception for your company on the payment terms.","plain":"We are willing to deviate from our standard payment terms for your company.","note":"A significant concession, highlighting the special nature of the offer.","audio":"audio/15a5f37f2e74b228.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.were-prepared-to-offer","text":"we're prepared to offer","kind":"frame","form":"We're prepared to offer","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Indicates a specific concession or benefit that one is willing to provide.","example":"We're prepared to offer a 10% discount for an upfront payment.","plain":"We are willing to give a 10% discount for an upfront payment.","note":"A direct way to present a concession, often conditional on another action.","audio":"audio/4b676b3bfc7d8f50.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.were-prepared-to-offer-a-discount","text":"we're prepared to offer a discount","kind":"frame","form":"We're prepared to offer a discount","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Indicates a willingness to reduce the price as a concession.","example":"We're prepared to offer a discount for a long-term commitment.","plain":"We are willing to reduce the price for a long-term commitment.","note":"A common negotiation tactic to incentivize agreement.","audio":"audio/d99287cc65d9fc60.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.were-prepared-to-revisit","text":"we're prepared to revisit","kind":"frame","form":"We're prepared to revisit","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Indicates a willingness to re-examine a previously discussed topic or decision.","example":"We're prepared to revisit the timeline if new information comes to light.","plain":"We are willing to re-examine the timeline if new information comes to light.","note":"Shows flexibility and openness to adapting to changing circumstances.","audio":"audio/b381bd7e488e4fcd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.were-willing-to-consider","text":"we're willing to consider","kind":"frame","form":"We're willing to consider","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Indicates openness to discussing or accepting alternative ideas or proposals.","example":"We're willing to consider a different payment structure if it helps.","plain":"We are open to discussing a different payment structure if it helps.","note":"Shows flexibility and a desire to find a mutually beneficial solution.","audio":"audio/51ed618f7147565b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.weve-considered-that","text":"We've considered that","kind":"chunk","form":"We've considered that","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To indicate that a particular point or objection has already been thought about and addressed.","example":"We've considered that potential risk and have developed a contingency plan for it.","plain":"We have thought about that potential risk and have developed a contingency plan for it.","note":"A concise way to show preparedness and anticipate objections. Professional and proactive.","audio":"audio/b35356d8bbd14226.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.weve-covered-a-lot","text":"we've covered a lot","kind":"collocation","form":"we've covered a lot","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"We have discussed many topics or a significant amount of information during our conversation.","example":"I think we've covered a lot today, so let's aim to meet again next week.","plain":"We discussed many things today, so let's plan to meet again next week.","note":"Used to acknowledge the breadth of discussion, often as a lead-in to wrapping up.","audio":"audio/0a98c89a7a87e926.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-a-nightmare","text":"What a nightmare","kind":"chunk","form":"What a nightmare","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Expresses strong sympathy or commiseration for a very difficult experience.","example":"Your flight was cancelled and you lost your luggage? What a nightmare!","plain":"Your flight was cancelled and you lost your luggage? That's a terrible situation!","note":"Informal, used to express strong empathy for a bad experience.","audio":"audio/495640ec15fa3510.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-a-nuisance","text":"What a nuisance","kind":"chunk","form":"What a nuisance","level":"C1","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses mild annoyance or frustration about something bothersome.","example":"The printer's jammed again? What a nuisance, especially when I'm in a hurry.","plain":"The printer's jammed again? That's annoying, especially when I'm in a hurry.","note":"Used for minor, irritating problems; can be said to oneself or others.","audio":"audio/0be83924a436ca4c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-a-pleasant-surprise","text":"what a pleasant surprise","kind":"chunk","form":"What a pleasant surprise","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","reacting"],"meaning":"Used to express happiness or delight at an unexpected event or encounter.","example":"Oh, it's you! What a pleasant surprise to see you here.","plain":"Oh, it's you! I'm happy to see you here unexpectedly.","note":"A warm and positive reaction to an unexpected meeting or good news.","audio":"audio/92d765b28930fb24.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-about-the-possibility","text":"What about the possibility","kind":"frame","form":"What about the possibility","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Introduces an alternative scenario or outcome that might challenge the current view.","example":"What about the possibility of unforeseen delays due to supply chain issues?","plain":"We should consider the possibility of unforeseen delays due to supply chain issues.","note":"Encourages consideration of other factors, often negative ones, to prompt caution.","audio":"audio/df87f250383a3637.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-are-your-plans-for","text":"what are your plans for","kind":"frame","form":"What are your plans for","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A common question to ask about someone's upcoming activities or intentions.","example":"It's almost Friday! What are your plans for the weekend?","plain":"It's almost Friday! What will you do this weekend?","note":"A good way to keep a conversation going by looking ahead.","audio":"audio/5a7a048daad8da9e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-are-your-thoughts","text":"what are your thoughts","kind":"chunk","form":"What are your thoughts","level":"C1","situations":["meetings","calls"],"meaning":"I'm asking for your opinion or perspective on the topic being discussed.","example":"We've discussed my proposal; now, what are your thoughts on the implementation?","plain":"I've presented my idea; what do you think about implementing it?","note":"A direct and inclusive way to invite others to contribute to the conversation.","audio":"audio/84fee6cfa3650196.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-are-your-thoughts-on-that","text":"What are your thoughts on that","kind":"frame","form":"What are your thoughts on that","level":"C1","situations":["socialising","calls"],"meaning":"Asking for someone's opinion or perspective on a particular topic.","example":"We're thinking of going to the new Italian place, what are your thoughts on that?","plain":"We're considering the new Italian restaurant, what do you think about it?","note":"A polite way to invite input and ensure everyone's opinion is considered.","audio":"audio/3b237c34c0eb01de.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-brings-you-here","text":"what brings you here","kind":"frame","form":"What brings you here","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to ask someone why they are at a particular event or location.","example":"So, what brings you here to the conference this year?","plain":"So, why are you at the conference this year?","note":"Good for starting conversations with strangers at events; can be slightly formal.","audio":"audio/d00c37faa177edb1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-do-you-make-of","text":"what do you make of","kind":"frame","form":"What do you make of","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to ask someone for their opinion or interpretation of something, often something new or unusual.","example":"Have you seen the new proposal? What do you make of it?","plain":"Have you seen the new proposal? What do you think of it?","note":"A common way to ask for an opinion, especially on something that requires thought.","audio":"audio/2761ca57106aaa82.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-happened-next-was","text":"What happened next was","kind":"frame","form":"What happened next was","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Transitions the narrative to the subsequent event or development.","example":"I waited for hours, and what happened next was completely unexpected.","plain":"I waited for hours, and the next event was completely unexpected.","note":"Builds anticipation for the next part of the story.","audio":"audio/f8fccec3201f5769.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-i-mean-by-that-is","text":"What I mean by that is","kind":"frame","form":"what I mean by that is","level":"C1","situations":["academic","presenting"],"meaning":"To clarify a previous statement by rephrasing or explaining it.","example":"We need to be agile; what I mean by that is we must adapt quickly to market changes.","plain":"We need to be agile, which means we must adapt quickly to market changes.","note":"Helps ensure the audience understands a potentially complex or vague statement.","audio":"audio/748dd851a32cd81c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-i-mean-to-say-is","text":"What I mean to say is","kind":"frame","form":"What I mean to say is","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Introducing a rephrasing or further explanation of one's thoughts.","example":"What I mean to say is that the budget constraints are tighter than expected.","plain":"The budget constraints are tighter than expected.","note":"A common frame to elaborate or correct a previous statement.","audio":"audio/04b516afca34c508.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-im-driving-at-is","text":"What I'm driving at is","kind":"frame","form":"What I'm driving at is","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To state the underlying point or ultimate intention of one's argument or explanation.","example":"We need to improve efficiency. What I'm driving at is that we should automate more processes.","plain":"We need to improve efficiency. My ultimate point is that we should automate more processes.","note":"Implies a deeper or less obvious point that the speaker is leading towards.","audio":"audio/09d1b2a9f2a7c169.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-if-we-considered","text":"What if we considered","kind":"frame","form":"What if we considered","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Introduces a new idea or perspective as a question, inviting exploration rather than direct opposition.","example":"What if we considered the environmental impact more closely before making a final decision?","plain":"We should consider the environmental impact more closely before making a final decision.","note":"A collaborative way to introduce a new factor or alternative, encouraging discussion.","audio":"audio/121f44284de7ffeb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-if-we-looked-at","text":"What if we looked at","kind":"frame","form":"What if we looked at","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Proposes examining an issue from another angle or considering a new factor.","example":"What if we looked at the problem from the customer's perspective for a change?","plain":"Let's look at the problem from the customer's perspective for a change.","note":"Encourages a shift in perspective, often leading to new insights or solutions.","audio":"audio/2d6a750085fcd0f9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-if-we-were-to","text":"what if we were to","kind":"frame","form":"What if we were to","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation","problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to introduce a hypothetical suggestion or alternative proposal for consideration.","example":"What if we were to extend the project by two weeks and keep the original budget?","plain":"How about we extend the project by two weeks and keep the original budget?","note":"A soft way to float a new idea, inviting discussion rather than demanding agreement.","audio":"audio/d4d31d37ce49131a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-it-boils-down-to","text":"what it boils down to","kind":"frame","form":"What it boils down to","level":"C1","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To summarize the main point or essential truth of a complex situation or argument.","example":"There are many factors, but what it boils down to is a lack of clear communication.","plain":"There are many factors, but the main point is a lack of clear communication.","note":"Similar to \"the bottom line is,\" but often used for underlying causes or core issues.","audio":"audio/a802948a76d831d4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-sets-me-apart-is","text":"What sets me apart is","kind":"frame","form":"What sets me apart is","level":"C1","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Used to highlight a unique quality or skill that makes you stand out.","example":"What sets me apart is my ability to quickly adapt to diverse team environments.","plain":"My ability to quickly adapt to diverse team environments makes me unique.","note":"Excellent for interviewees to emphasize their unique value proposition.","audio":"audio/2eea40e809fbf4c7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-time-suits-you","text":"What time suits you","kind":"chunk","form":"what time suits you","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Asking someone to specify their preferred time for a meeting or activity.","example":"I'm free all afternoon, so what time suits you for our coffee?","plain":"I'm free all afternoon, so tell me what time is best for our coffee.","note":"A direct but polite way to ask about someone's availability.","audio":"audio/93ce6ab9b38a51f9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.whats-on-your-mind","text":"what's on your mind","kind":"frame","form":"What's on your mind","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A friendly and open question asking someone what they are thinking or worrying about.","example":"You seem a bit quiet today. What's on your mind?","plain":"You seem a bit quiet today. What are you thinking about?","note":"Shows concern and invites a deeper conversation, if appropriate.","audio":"audio/b79b8bd6aaf127f2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.whats-our-next-course-of-action","text":"What's our next course of action","kind":"frame","form":"What's our next course of action","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to ask about the specific plan or steps that will be taken next.","example":"Given the client's feedback, what's our next course of action for revising the proposal?","plain":"Given the client's feedback, what's our next plan for revising the proposal?","note":"A more formal version of 'Where do we go from here?', focusing on concrete steps.","audio":"audio/60d10dff182ff49d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.whats-really-important-is","text":"What's really important is","kind":"frame","form":"What's really important is","level":"C1","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To highlight the most significant aspect of a situation or discussion.","example":"What's really important is that we learn from our mistakes and improve.","plain":"The most important thing is that we learn from our mistakes and improve.","note":"Emphasises a core value or lesson.","audio":"audio/4b4f2e532abbcb49.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.whats-the-best-way-to-get-around","text":"What's the best way to get around","kind":"chunk","form":"What's the best way to get around","level":"C1","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Asking for advice on the most efficient or convenient mode of transport.","example":"We're staying downtown; what's the best way to get around the city?","plain":"We're staying downtown; what's the most efficient transport in the city?","note":"Useful question when arriving in a new city or area.","audio":"audio/64cd83565244acc4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.whats-the-consensus","text":"What's the consensus","kind":"frame","form":"What's the consensus","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To ask what the general agreement or shared opinion is among the group.","example":"We've heard all perspectives, so what's the consensus on the best approach?","plain":"We've heard all perspectives, so what is the general agreement on the best approach?","note":"Used to gauge the group's overall agreement.","audio":"audio/a3fc8204f1a707f6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.whats-the-latest-with","text":"What's the latest with","kind":"chunk","form":"What's the latest with","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Asking for the most recent news or developments concerning a particular person or situation.","example":"What's the latest with your sister's job search? Has she found anything?","plain":"What is the newest information about your sister's job search?","note":"A common, informal way to ask for updates.","audio":"audio/e0c5e508a36c6bdf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.whats-your-availability-like","text":"What's your availability like","kind":"chunk","form":"what's your availability like","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Asking someone about their free time or when they can meet.","example":"Before I suggest a date, what's your availability like next week?","plain":"Before I suggest a date, when are you free next week?","note":"A polite and slightly more formal way to inquire about someone's schedule.","audio":"audio/0bef4f7424af0e98.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.whats-your-perspective","text":"What's your perspective","kind":"chunk","form":"What's your perspective","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Asking for someone's viewpoint or way of looking at a situation.","example":"We've heard from the team, but what's your perspective on the challenges?","plain":"We've heard from the team, but what is your view on the challenges?","note":"A polite way to ask for an individual's unique viewpoint.","audio":"audio/72dc23b86519daf9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.whats-your-take-on-this","text":"What's your take on this","kind":"chunk","form":"What's your take on this","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Asking for someone's opinion or viewpoint on a specific issue.","example":"We've discussed my ideas, but what's your take on this proposal?","plain":"We've discussed my ideas, but what do you think about this proposal?","note":"An informal but common way to solicit opinions.","audio":"audio/556536912643f97f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.where-do-we-go-from-here","text":"Where do we go from here","kind":"frame","form":"Where do we go from here","level":"C1","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to ask about the next steps or future course of action after a discussion or decision.","example":"Okay, we've identified the problem. So, where do we go from here in terms of solutions?","plain":"Okay, we've identified the problem. So, what are our next steps in terms of solutions?","note":"A common way to prompt for action planning and move a discussion forward.","audio":"audio/e1876b5b0f95f499.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.where-do-we-stand","text":"where do we stand","kind":"chunk","form":"Where do we stand","level":"C1","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Asks for an update on the current status or position in a negotiation.","example":"So, after our discussion, where do we stand on the project deliverables?","plain":"So, after our discussion, what is our current position on the project deliverables?","note":"A direct question to assess progress and clarify the current situation.","audio":"audio/50a1a79df4fa707d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.where-i-differ-is","text":"Where I differ is","kind":"frame","form":"Where I differ is","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Clearly states the specific point of disagreement, making it easy to understand the divergence.","example":"Where I differ is on the proposed timeline; I think it's unrealistic given our current workload.","plain":"I disagree on the proposed timeline; I think it's unrealistic given our current workload.","note":"A direct yet polite way to pinpoint the exact area of disagreement.","audio":"audio/dc0617e8a3a676b4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.where-im-struggling-is-with","text":"Where I'm struggling is with","kind":"frame","form":"Where I'm struggling is with","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Explains the specific aspect of an idea or plan that causes difficulty or disagreement.","example":"Where I'm struggling is with the assumption that we can achieve these results without additional resources.","plain":"I'm having trouble with the assumption that we can achieve these results without additional resources.","note":"Personalizes the disagreement, making it sound like a personal difficulty rather than a direct attack.","audio":"audio/d684f5ae97726ce8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.where-was-i","text":"where was I","kind":"chunk","form":"Where was I","level":"C1","situations":["presenting","calls"],"meaning":"I'm asking to be reminded of the point I was making before an interruption or digression.","example":"Sorry, I got sidetracked. Where was I before the phone rang?","plain":"Apologies, I lost my train of thought. What was I saying before the call?","note":"A common informal phrase used to get back on track after an interruption.","audio":"audio/ae58c121953f403a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.where-were-we","text":"where were we","kind":"chunk","form":"Where were we","level":"C1","situations":["small-talk","socialising"],"meaning":"Used to ask for a reminder of the last point discussed after an interruption or digression.","example":"Sorry, my phone rang. Where were we before that?","plain":"Sorry, my phone rang. What were we talking about before that?","note":"Helps to smoothly resume a conversation after a pause or interruption.","audio":"audio/ab73a2831881dc78.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.while-i-agree-with","text":"While I agree with","kind":"frame","form":"While I agree with","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"States a point of agreement before introducing a contrasting idea or reservation.","example":"While I agree with the need for cost-cutting, I'm concerned about the impact on our core services.","plain":"I agree with the need for cost-cutting, but I'm concerned about the impact on our core services.","note":"A common way to establish common ground before gently diverging.","audio":"audio/35666cc4aabec773.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.while-i-appreciate-that","text":"While I appreciate that","kind":"frame","form":"While I appreciate that","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"Acknowledges a valid point or effort, but then introduces a contrasting or limiting factor.","example":"While I appreciate that you worked late, the report still needs significant revisions.","plain":"I appreciate that you worked late, but the report still needs significant revisions.","note":"A polite way to soften criticism or introduce a caveat.","audio":"audio/946289d01d107b2e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.while-i-understand","text":"While I understand","kind":"frame","form":"While I understand","level":"C1","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Acknowledges the other person's viewpoint or feelings before presenting a different perspective.","example":"While I understand your frustration, we still need to follow the established protocol.","plain":"I understand your frustration, but we still need to follow the established protocol.","note":"A diplomatic way to show empathy before stating a contrasting fact or rule.","audio":"audio/358adf8b47679c94.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.while-thats-true","text":"While that's true","kind":"frame","form":"While that's true","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Acknowledges the truth of a statement before introducing a contrasting or qualifying point.","example":"While that's true for developed nations, the situation in developing countries is quite different.","plain":"Although that's true for developed nations, the situation in developing countries is quite different.","note":"Used to concede a point before presenting a counter-argument or nuance.","audio":"audio/ad0f89ca2a526505.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.with-a-view-to","text":"With a view to","kind":"frame","form":"with a view to","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To state the purpose or intention behind an action, indicating what you hope to achieve.","example":"We are implementing new training programs with a view to enhancing staff skills.","plain":"We are implementing new training programs to enhance staff skills.","note":"A formal way to express purpose or objective. Often used in written reports or formal speeches.","audio":"audio/f174df29960e2816.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.with-the-caveat-that","text":"With the caveat that","kind":"chunk","form":"with the caveat that","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a specific condition or limitation that applies to a preceding statement.","example":"We can proceed with the analysis, with the caveat that the data quality is variable.","plain":"We can proceed with the analysis, but be aware that the data quality is variable.","note":"Formal and precise, often used in academic or professional contexts.","audio":"audio/2b7e70d492fbd320.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.work-towards-a-common-goal","text":"Work towards a common goal","kind":"collocation","form":"work towards a common goal","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To cooperate and direct energy towards achieving an objective shared by everyone.","example":"Let's all work towards a common goal of improving customer satisfaction scores this quarter.","plain":"Let's all cooperate to improve customer satisfaction scores this quarter.","note":"Encourages teamwork and shared responsibility. Positive and motivating.","audio":"audio/e6603cfcd75a51c5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.would-you-be-interested-in","text":"Would you be interested in","kind":"frame","form":"Would you be interested in","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Asking if someone wants to do something or join an activity.","example":"Would you be interested in joining us for dinner next Saturday?","plain":"Do you want to join us for dinner next Saturday?","note":"A polite and open-ended way to invite someone, suggesting an option rather than a demand.","audio":"audio/6ed4074923fbf25d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.would-you-care-to-join-us","text":"Would you care to join us","kind":"frame","form":"Would you care to join us","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite way to invite someone to an activity or event.","example":"We're heading to the new gallery opening; would you care to join us?","plain":"We're going to the gallery opening; would you like to come?","note":"Very polite and slightly formal, suitable for new acquaintances or more formal invitations.","audio":"audio/ae111e0156e03114.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.wrap-things-up","text":"wrap things up","kind":"chunk","form":"wrap things up","level":"C1","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To conclude a discussion or meeting.","example":"We're almost out of time, so let's start to wrap things up.","plain":"We're almost out of time, so let's start to conclude.","note":"Informal but very common way to signal the end of a meeting.","audio":"audio/4769000b458a51b5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.x-argues-that","text":"X argues that","kind":"frame","form":"Dr. Chen argues that","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a specific claim or contention made by a particular author or researcher.","example":"Dr. Chen argues that current economic models fail to account for informal labor markets.","plain":"Dr. Chen claims current economic models fail to account for informal labor markets.","note":"A standard academic phrase for presenting an author's main point.","audio":"audio/1796bb6dd60019b4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.you-can-reach-me-at","text":"you can reach me at","kind":"frame","form":"You can reach me at","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"You can contact me using the following phone number or email address.","example":"If you have any further questions, you can reach me at my office number.","plain":"If you have more questions, you can contact me using my office number.","note":"A common way to provide contact information, often at the end of a message.","audio":"audio/4c89c53ade688bd9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.you-have-a-point","text":"You have a point","kind":"chunk","form":"You have a point","level":"C1","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Acknowledges that another person's argument or observation is reasonable and well-founded.","example":"You have a point about the need for more qualitative data in our analysis.","plain":"Your argument about the need for more qualitative data in our analysis is valid.","note":"A slightly less formal but still polite way to acknowledge a valid argument.","audio":"audio/233c85ff1834997f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.you-know-what-they-say","text":"You know what they say","kind":"chunk","form":"You know what they say","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Introduces a common proverb, saying, or piece of conventional wisdom.","example":"You know what they say, 'When in Rome, do as the Romans do.'","plain":"As the saying goes, 'When in Rome, do as the Romans do.'","note":"Used to relate a situation to a well-known piece of advice.","audio":"audio/cf2baa5fe421ae2a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.you-might-be-thinking","text":"You might be thinking","kind":"frame","form":"You might be thinking","level":"C1","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"Anticipates a potential question or objection from the audience.","example":"You might be thinking this is too ambitious, but I assure you it's achievable.","plain":"Perhaps you are thinking this is too ambitious, but I assure you it's achievable.","note":"Used to pre-empt objections and address them proactively.","audio":"audio/bedc549d9a817277.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.youre-freezing","text":"you're freezing","kind":"chunk","form":"You're freezing","level":"C1","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Your video image is temporarily stuck or paused on the screen during a video call.","example":"Oh, you're freezing on my screen; I think your internet connection might be unstable.","plain":"Your video is stuck; your internet connection seems to be unstable.","note":"Used exclusively for video calls when someone's image stops moving due to connection issues.","audio":"audio/7c460b3214ba580b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.youre-not-kidding","text":"You're not kidding","kind":"chunk","form":"You're not kidding","level":"C1","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Expresses strong agreement, often with a sense of shared experience or difficulty.","example":"The traffic was terrible this morning. \"You're not kidding, I was stuck for an hour.\"","plain":"The traffic was terrible this morning. \"I agree, I was stuck for an hour.\"","note":"Informal, emphatic agreement, often used when something is obvious or difficult.","audio":"audio/b4a41f1e1d48b5f5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.youre-welcome-to-join","text":"You're welcome to join","kind":"chunk","form":"you're welcome to join","level":"C1","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Extending an open invitation, indicating that someone is free to participate.","example":"If you're free, you're welcome to join us for brunch on Sunday.","plain":"If you're free, you can join us for brunch on Sunday.","note":"Friendly and inclusive, often used for informal gatherings.","audio":"audio/64f64d66571b15c6.m4a"}]}}